.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/11/30/primary-sources-2/', 'snippet': 'Thomas B. Edsall’s article on the Democratic party, the Obama coalition and the white working class caused a ruckus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few days ago, we posted an article by Thomas B. Edsall analyzing the arguments of Democratic strategists who suggest that the Democratic Party has lost its chance to win over a majority of the white working class and that party leaders should concentrate their efforts on other constituencies. The response was swift and fierce. Readers posted nearly five hundred comments. Edsall’s column was picked up, dissected, lambasted and praised in dozens of blog posts and newspaper articles, with headlines like “Different Flavor Kool-Aid For Democrats,” “Is Obama ‘Abandoning’ the White Working Class?” and “NY Times: Democrats Not Interested in Voters Who Work, Target Losers Instead” (thank you, Rush Limbaugh).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Primary Sources', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Gail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edsall, Thomas B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldberg, Jonah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Limbaugh, Rush', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weigel, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T02:08:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3940d77-afe3-52bc-b9ea-9e851854b5a8', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3940d77-afe3-52bc-b9ea-9e851854b5a8'}, {'abstract': 'What makes Bobby Valentine’s selection as Boston’s new manager so intriguing is his curiosity and his daring, best exemplified by his time spent managing in Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/sports/baseball/bobby-valentine-bold-and-brilliant-in-any-language.html', 'snippet': 'What makes Bobby Valentine’s selection as Boston’s new manager so intriguing is his curiosity and his daring, best exemplified by his time spent managing in Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in his tenure as manager of the Chiba Lotte Marines in Japan, Bobby Valentine decided to run a drill to practice pickoff moves at second base. He instructed his pitchers to throw behind the runner, but for some reason they refused to do as he asked.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/01/sports/jpVALENTINE1/jpVALENTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/01/sports/jpVALENTINE1/jpVALENTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/01/sports/jpVALENTINE1/jpVALENTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/01/sports/jpVALENTINE1/jpVALENTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/01/sports/jpVALENTINE1/jpVALENTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brilliant and Bold,