ing the United States and the Soviet Union, signed a treaty in Washington, which designated Antarctica as a scientific preserve, free from military activity.The signatories included the seven nations to have claimed control over a part of Antarctica: Argentina, Australia, Britain, Chile, France, Norway and New Zealand. The treaty did not affect these claims though it did ban all future claims. The New York Times noted that overlapping claims by Britain, Argentina and Chile had caused tension and that many hoped the treaty would “ultimately permit the claims issue to die a natural death.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dec. 1, 1959 | Antarctica Is Set Aside as Scientific Preserve', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T09:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/664fdf78-859c-5507-8826-a892a8bb82a2', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/664fdf78-859c-5507-8826-a892a8bb82a2'}, {'abstract': 'Can you put five sentences in the correct order?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/test-yourself-english-dec-1-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you put five sentences in the correct order?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the DealBook article “Wall Street’s ‘CrackBerry’ Withdrawal.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Dec. 1, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T09:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'orig