, 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T19:40:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Trebay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6983bff-906c-5df5-b728-cf716b01a865', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6983bff-906c-5df5-b728-cf716b01a865'}, {'abstract': 'Quarantine the comforter, stow the TV and always ask to see the third room first.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/travel/check-in-tips.html', 'snippet': 'Quarantine the comforter, stow the TV and always ask to see the third room first.', 'lead_paragraph': ' SEE THE THIRD ROOM FIRST It’s anyone’s guess why the first room that check-in clerks invariably offer is the cubbyhole with a romantic view of the air handling system. No matter how travel fatigued, I make it a point to apply the advice of a friend with decades of international travel experience. “Show me the third room first,” this woman says at check-in, telegraphing a willingness to keep at it until she’s offered a room she likes. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Six Ways to Master Your Hotel Stay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Check-In Tips', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T19:41:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Guy Trebay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Guy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e210face-94fd-5cc5-97a3-03e1006ef1b5', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e210face-94fd-5cc5-97a3-03e1006ef1b5'}, {'abstract': 'Growing up, I knew them as places of romance and intrigue. Now they are islands of cosseted repose, where life’s clutter is kept at bay and strangers know what I like to drink.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/travel/my-life-in-hotels.html', 'snippet': 'Growing up, I knew them as places of romance and intrigue. Now they are islands of cosseted repose, where life’s clutter is kept at bay and strangers know what I like to drink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF you travel a lot, it happens that you are expected to keep a list of dream destinations. Friends are understandably curious to learn where somebody fortunate enough to have visited a fair number of places would voluntarily go if given the chance. It surprises people, then, to learn that where I would most like to be is often a great hotel.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04HOTEL_395/04HOTEL_395-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04HOTEL_395/04HOTEL_395-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04HOTEL_395/04HOTEL_395-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Life in Hotels', 'kicke