 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75e6b48c-3b1f-5100-837e-ba18e954f9cc', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75e6b48c-3b1f-5100-837e-ba18e954f9cc'}, {'abstract': 'The valley, in Hawaii, is home to thousands of acres of tropical rain forest, as well as archaeological finds. Now guided tours are available to areas previously off-limits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/travel/hiking-in-hawaiis-waimea-valley.html', 'snippet': 'The valley, in Hawaii, is home to thousands of acres of tropical rain forest, as well as archaeological finds. Now guided tours are available to areas previously off-limits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S not often that a hike begins with a ceremonial chant. We were standing outside the visitor center at Waimea Valley, on the North Shore of Oahu, when our guide, Kahokulea Haiku, started our excursion by singing an oli, a traditional Hawaiian chant that honors the history of the land and its people, and can be used as a blessing or to give thanks. As bright morning light shone down on us, Mr. Haiku’s voice resonated across the gardens that surround the center. And with that, we were off. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04NEXT2_SPAN/04NEXT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04NEXT2_SPAN/04NEXT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 200}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/travel/04NEXT2_SPAN/04NEXT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 200, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},