ocument_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/878cb259-02fe-5cde-a28a-2b8772e90b55', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/878cb259-02fe-5cde-a28a-2b8772e90b55'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Feinstein and Barbara Cook shared musical lore and popular standards onstage at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/arts/music/michael-feinstein-and-barbara-cook-at-feinsteins-review.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Feinstein and Barbara Cook shared musical lore and popular standards onstage at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To watch Michael Feinstein and Barbara Cook share musical lore onstage is the next best thing to attending a master class on how to sing popular standards. Ms. Cook, who actually conducts master classes on “living” inside a song while singing it, never hits a false emotional note. And Mr. Feinstein is the ultimate singing professor, whose perfect pitch coincides with a photographic memory for lyrics. Woe to the performer who takes liberties with words that he views almost as sacred texts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/FEINSTEINS/FEINSTEINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/FEINSTEINS/FEINSTEINS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/FEINSTEINS/FEINSTEINS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/FEINSTEINS/FEINSTEINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/FEINSTEINS/FEINSTEINS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Benevolent Monarchs Of the Songbook Realm', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Benevolent Monarchs Of the Songbook Realm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cook, Barbara', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:43:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c02abeb2-2331-5b00-94fe-1390a8307dad', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c02abeb2-2331-5b00-94fe-1390a8307dad'}, {'abstract': 'At the debut concert of the New York Women’s Ensemble at Carnegie Hall on Tuesday, the program was almost wholly devoted to canonical pieces by Mozart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/arts/music/new-york-womens-ensemble-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'At the debut concert of the New York Women’s Ensemble at Carnegie Hall on Tuesday, the program was almost wholly devoted to canonical pieces by Mozart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not so long ago female musicians’ effort to gain entry to major professional orchestras was a subject for hot debate. Today, however, women constitute a healthy proportion among most American orchestras and a majority in more than a few. Progress remains to be made, not least on the conducting podium. But for the most part the situation appears to have improved dramatically for rank-and-file players and even for principals.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN/ENSEMBLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN/02ENSEMBLE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disparate Players, Evoking Familiar Sounds', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disparate Players, Evoking Familiar Sounds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:46:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/11e4932c-4425-5e7e-81eb-46c04d124b37', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11e4932c-4425-5e7e-81eb-46c04d124b37'}, {'abstract': 'A resurgent Newt Gingrich tops the polls in the campaign to be the Republican candidate for president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/12/01/us/politics/01caucus_pod.html', 'snippet': 'A resurgent Newt Gingrich tops the polls in the campaign to be the Republican candidate for president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A resurgent Newt Gingrich tops the polls in the campaign to be the Republican candidate for president.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newt 2.0', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/7ee910fa-eea9-5731-a835-11499195977e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/7ee910fa-eea9-5731-a835-11499195977e'}, {'abstract': 'More than 10,600 people submitted written comments on the controversial drilling method,  the state Department of Environmental Conservation said.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/state-fracking-hearings-drew-6000-n-y-says/', 'snippet': 'More than 10,600 people submitted written comments on the controversial drilling method,  the state Department of Environmental Conservation said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Proposed regulations governing the natural gas drilling process known as horizontal hydraulic fracturing drew 6,000 people to four public hearings in New York State and have so far elicited more than 10,600 written public comments, the state Department of Environmental Conservation said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'State Fracking Hearings Drew 6,000, N.Y. Says', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MARTENS, JOE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Environmental Conservation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1eef6430-b724-5e61-a4c4-44404f6af5a2', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eef6430-b724-5e61-a4c4-44404f6af5a2'}, {'abstract': '“Moïse et Pharaon,” which the Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra performed at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday, is only boring if you can’t adjust to its stately pacing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/arts/music/moise-et-pharaon-at-carnegie-hall-review.html', 'snippet': '“Moïse et Pharaon,” which the Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra performed at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday, is only boring if you can’t adjust to its stately pacing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some people still find Rossini’s serious operas a little, well, tedious. Even the writer of the program synopsis for “Moïse et Pharaon” (“Moses and Pharaoh”), which the Collegiate Chorale and the American Symphony Orchestra performed at Carnegie Hall under James Bagwell on Wednesday evening, evinced frustration with the opera’s plot. “It takes another three acts to let the people go,” he or she dryly summarized after describing the opening.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02CHORALE_SPAN/02CHORALE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02CHORALE_SPAN/02CHORALE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02CHORALE_SPAN/CHORALE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02CHORALE_SPAN/02CHORALE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02CHORALE_SPAN/02CHORALE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Illicit Romance, a Revenge Wrought and an Escape From Egypt', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Illicit Romance, a Revenge Wrought and an Escape From Egypt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SYMPHONY ORCHESTRA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rossini, Gioacchino', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Collegiate Chorale', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:51:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30aa86e6-ed8c-5853-9c0f-53a746c1e764', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30aa86e6-ed8c-5853-9c0f-53a746c1e764'}, {'abstract': 'Rumble is part of a new wave of hardcore social games startup companies trying to make games that are equal to console games in quality. Founded by a group of executives from Electronic Arts and Activision, it has raised $15 million from Google’s venture capital arm and Khosla Ventures.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/hard-core-gamers-try-building-social-games/', 'snippet': 'Rumble is part of a new wave of hardcore social games startup companies trying to make games that are equal to console games in quality. Founded by a group of executives from Electronic Arts and Activision, it has raised $15 million from Google’s ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Social game makers like Zynga have gotten hundreds of millions of people to play their games, but one of the big raps on FarmVille, CityVille and other Facebook time-wasters is that they’re too shallow to appeal to hard-core players.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/01/blogs/rumble/rumble-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hard-Core Gamers Try Building Social Games', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Khosla Ventures', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:56:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2bf43a2-a513-55b8-9a3c-bcee5affa45e', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2bf43a2-a513-55b8-9a3c-bcee5affa45e'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Browning plays Lucy, who is paid to fall into a drugged slumber in “Sleeping Beauty.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/movies/julia-leighs-sleeping-beauty-review.html', 'snippet': 'Emily Browning plays Lucy, who is paid to fall into a drugged slumber in “Sleeping Beauty.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sexuality can be a puzzle — just ask Carl Jung, the kinky and conflicted psychoanalyst played by Michael Fassbender in “A Dangerous Method,” or Brandon, the sex addict played by Michael Fassbender in “Shame.” Or, if you insist on exploring the complexities of eros in a new movie not starring Mr. Fassbender, you might check out “Sleeping Beauty,” Julia Leigh’s sly and elusive film, which is itself something of a puzzle.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02SLEEP1_SPAN/02SLEEP1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02SLEEP1_SPAN/02SLEEP1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02SLEEP1_SPAN/JPSLEEP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02SLEEP1_SPAN/02SLEEP1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/02/arts/02SLEEP1_SPAN/02SLEEP1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Objectification Is Also in the Eye of the Beheld', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Sleeping Beauty'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Objectification Is Also In the Eye of the Beheld', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leigh, Julia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browning, Emily', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sleeping Beauty (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-01T23:57:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/31e92576-ec75-57f7-9a40-b7c28f49559d', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31e92576-ec75-57f7-9a40-b7c28f49559d'}, {'abstract': 'The Financial Industry Regulatory Authority has quietly built a fledgling fraud task force that has entered the front lines to fight insider trading.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/for-wall-street-watchdog-all-grunt-work-little-glory/', 'snippet': 'The Financial Industry Regulatory Authority has quietly built a fledgling fraud task force that has entered the front lines to fight insider trading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROCKVILLE, Md. — Last week, when a former Boston Red Sox infielder faced accusations of insider trading, the Securities and Exchange Commission got credit for levying a $100,000 fine. The Department of Justice in May heralded a guilty plea from a one-time senior executive at the Nasdaq Stock Market who traded on secret information. And earlier this year, after a lawyer was accused of stealing insider tidbits, federal prosecutors in New Jersey boasted of the arrest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Wall Street Watchdog, All Grunt Work, Little Glory', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Industry Regulatory Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T00:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ca3029f-6121-55a1-8cf4-db3975170d13', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ca3029f-6121-55a1-8cf4-db3975170d13'}, {'abstract': 'Clog-danci