ticle', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Lacey and Dan Frosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lacey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e233bb43-9077-5dae-aaf9-a3f66cd676d2', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e233bb43-9077-5dae-aaf9-a3f66cd676d2'}, {'abstract': 'Why did the president release scores of State Department correspondence to the public in 1861?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/01/lincolns-p-r-coup/', 'snippet': 'Why did the president release scores of State Department correspondence to the public in 1861?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In addition to the domestic challenge it presented, the fledgling Confederacy posed a foreign policy dilemma for Abraham Lincoln’s administration. The Confederacy hoped to use its position as a crucial source of cotton to secure recognition from foreign powers and boost its legitimacy, and it sent commissioners abroad to sway other countries into supporting its cause. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lincoln’s P.R. Coup', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T01:30:27+0000', 'documen