e in life. His reply: “What if I bite out the eyes and scream ‘Stop staring at me!‘?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday: Ones Who Might Get You Into Hot Water', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T03:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4166047b-3d53-50fe-8616-21dcfbdf2f64', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4166047b-3d53-50fe-8616-21dcfbdf2f64'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations-backed tribunal investigating the killing of a former Lebanese prime minister said it had received the long-overdue half of its budget from Beirut, about $32 million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/world/middleeast/lebanon-sends-funds-to-court.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations-backed tribunal investigating the killing of a former Lebanese prime minister said it had received the long-overdue half of its budget from Beirut, about $32 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United Nations-backed tribunal investigating the killing of a former Lebanese prime minister said Thursday that it had received the long-overdue half of its budget from Beirut, about $32 million. Many in Lebanon’s government, dominated by the militant group Hezbollah, had opposed cooperating with the court. The court has indicted four Hezbollah members as suspects in the 2005 killing of former Prime Minister Rafik Hariri. Hezbollah’s leader, Hassan Nasrallah, said Thursday that although he disagreed with the payment, his party and its allies would not stop it. “We reject the legality and constitutionality of financing the court or cooperating with it in any way, but we will not cause a problem in the country, and we will serve the higher national interest,” he said in a televised address. He has promised, however, that the government will never arrest the indicted Hezbollah members. The court is debating whether to try them in absentia.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/world/02BRIEFS/02BRIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 669, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/world/02BRIEFS/02BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/02/world/02BRIEFS/02BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lebanon Sends Funds to Court', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lebanon Sends Funds to Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hezbollah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nasrallah, Hassan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beirut (Lebanon)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hariri, Rafik', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T03:05:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e31129f8-f52a-5791-af34-39e121f44f61', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e31129f8-f52a-5791-af34-39e121f44f61'}, {'abstract': 'America’s extraordinary intervention was timely, and it should impress upon the Europeans that it is high time they stopped sitting on their hands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/opinion/the-fed-and-the-euro.html', 'snippet': 'America’s extraordinary intervention was timely, and it should impress upon the Europeans that it is high time they stopped sitting on their hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Federal Reserve’s move on Wednesday to make it easier for European banks to acquire dollars shows that American policy makers understand the gravity of Europe’s turmoil and will do what they can to prevent a financial collapse across the Atlantic. European leaders, however, seem paralyzed\xa0and, even at this point, fail to share the Fed’s sense of urgency. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fed and the Euro', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fed and the Euro', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Euro (Currency)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T03:07:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5439a846-c341-55fc-b21d-95c454f249d2', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5439a846-c341-55fc-b21d-95c454f249d2'}, {'abstract': 'A prosecutor said that while there was evidence against other defendants accused in the public corruption case that ensnared State Senator Carl Kruger, for Aaron Malinsky there was not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/nyregion/in-transcript-details-on-why-developer-aaron-malinsky-was-dropped-from-kruger-case.html', 'snippet': 'A prosecutor said that while there was evidence against other defendants accused in the public corruption case that ensnared State Senator Carl Kruger, for Aaron Malinsky there was not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A newly released court transcript offers an unusually frank statement by a federal prosecutor in Manhattan about why the government agreed to drop charges against a real estate developer who was a co-defendant in the broad public corruption case that ensnared State Senator Carl Kruger.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Insight Into Dropping of Charges for Developer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Insight Into Dropping of Charges for Developer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malinsky, Aaron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kruger, Carl', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T03:11:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7079e02-5a1f-5e10-8829-3da52353a56f', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7079e02-5a1f-5e10-8829-3da52353a56f'}, {'abstract': 'Carmelo Anthony was one of only two Knicks who turned up for the first day of league-sanctioned workouts and was promptly asked about Chris Paul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/sports/basketball/for-knicks-anthony-a-lonely-workout.html', 'snippet': 'Carmelo Anthony was one of only two Knicks who turned up for the first day of league-sanctioned workouts and was promptly asked about Chris Paul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREENBURGH, N.Y. — When a team official entered the press room at the Knicks’ training center shortly after noon on Thursday and informed everyone that Carmelo Anthony was in the building, about half of the two dozen reporters who were waiting for some Knick, any Knick, to talk to jumped up in anticipation. And then they waited some more.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/02/sports/y-jp-beck/y-jp-beck-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam