ubject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Des Moines Register', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T12:47:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63a9655f-4859-55e2-8d34-e5cb5c72ae8d', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63a9655f-4859-55e2-8d34-e5cb5c72ae8d'}, {'abstract': 'Soliciting your questions and recommendations on India’s labor market.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/your-questions-for-indias-temp-workforce-guru/', 'snippet': 'Soliciting your questions and recommendations on India’s labor market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A recent article by Vikas Bajaj about India’s labor market has sparked a fierce debate about the difference between American and the Indian capitalism. We would like to know what you think and invite you to pose questions to Manish Sabharwal, the chairman of TeamLease, the company profiled in the article.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Your Questions for India’s Temp Workforce Guru', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabharwal, Manish', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T12:52:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1463d434-7ac5-5ff2-9580-c8199644b5e5', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1463d434-7ac5-5ff2-9580-c8199644b5e5'}, {'abstract': 'Misha Glenny staked out the profane, male-dominated Internet underworld, where cybercriminals take credit card fraud to new heights.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/now-reading-darkmarket/', 'snippet': 'Misha Glenny staked out the profane, male-dominated Internet underworld, where cybercriminals take credit card fraud to new heights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If style, per Gore Vidal, is about not giving a damn, then one might say cybercriminals have lots of style. “DarkMarket” (Knopf, $27), the new book by the T contributor Misha Glenny, disturbingly catalogs the capabilities of these nefarious Internet trolls — imagine, as Glenny does, that they could not only clog your in-box and steal your financial identity but attack your government, cripple your power grid or disable your national banking system. Still, the most common illicit activity on the Interwebs —\xa0 pornography aside\xa0 —\xa0 seems to be some form of personal credit card fraud. In fact, Glenny’s title, “DarkMarket,” comes from the name of a Web site where thousands of hackers coordinated the cloning and selling of credit card numbers and pins, often culled from illegally placed “skimmers” in A.T.M.’s and card machines. The site was infiltrated by an F.B.I. agent and taken down in 2008, resulting in many arrests.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Reading | ‘DarkMarket’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glenny, Misha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Knopf, Alfred A, Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T13:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4180586-277b-5f79-a312-8c5e6ef6bd62', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4180586-277b-5f79-a312-8c5e6ef6bd62'}, {'abstract': 'The best public spaces encourage diverse urban experiences, from people watching to protesting, daydreaming to handball, eating, reading and sunbathing to strolling and snoozing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/12/04/arts/design/20111204-SPACES.html', 'snippet': 'The best public spaces encourage diverse urban experiences, from people watching to protesting, daydreaming to handball, eating, reading and sunbathing to strolling and snoozing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best public spaces encourage diverse urban experiences, from people watching to protesting, daydreaming to handball, eating, reading and sunbathing to strolling and snoozing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/20111204-SPACES-slide-1OMS/20111204-SPACES-slide-1OMS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/1