'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/business/economy/03jobs-span/03jobs-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/03/business/economy/03jobs-span/03jobs-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobless Rate Dips to Lowest Level in More Than 2 Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobless Rate Dips To Lowest Level For Last 2 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T13:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79b4eac4-cd6e-53e1-896b-b99f7c96b926', 'word_count': 1092, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79b4eac4-cd6e-53e1-896b-b99f7c96b926'}, {'abstract': 'A prototype of Buckminster Fuller’s “Fly’s Eye Dome” and a reconstruction of his Dymaxion car are on display during Art Basel Miami Beach.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/design-miami-back-to-the-future/', 'snippet': 'A prototype of Buckminster Fuller’s “Fly’s Eye Dome” and a reconstruction of his Dymaxion car are on display during Art Basel Miami Beach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among Buckminster Fuller’s visionary designs was the “Fly’s Eye Dome,” a prefabricated fiberglass structure that was meant to be mass-produced and energy-efficient. This 24-foot prototype of the dome, which is now part of the Craig Robins Collection, is on display during Art Basel Miami Beach and Design Miami at the Palm Lot, at 140 Northeast 39th Street in the Miami Design District, which Robins developed; the dome will ultimately be installed in a pedestrian plaza in the district. It’s being shown along with a reconstruction, by the British architect Norman Foster, of the Dymaxion car, Fuller’s “omnidirectional transport system,” in the exhibition “Architecting the Future: Buckminster Fuller and Norman Foster,” curated by Daniel Reiser and Elizabeth Thompson. Here’s a time-lapse film that shows the dome’s installation in Miami. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Design Miami | Back to the Future', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/335804f1-c8aa-5570-99e9-baa4fb1767b7', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/335804f1-c8aa-5570-99e9-baa4fb1767b7'}, {'abstract': 'UPDATED | In a talk at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology this week, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg said he would choose higher quality teachers over smaller class sizes: “If I had the ability, which nobody does really, to just design a system and say, ex cathedra, \'this is what we’re going to do,’ you would cut the number of teachers in half, but you would double the compensation of them, and you would weed out all the bad ones and just have good teachers. And double the class size with a better teacher is a good deal for the students."', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/02/bloomberg-says-large-classes-are-fine-by-him/', 'snippet': 'UPDATED | In a talk at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology this week, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg said he would choose higher quality teachers over smaller class sizes: “If I had the ability, which nobody does really, to just design a system ...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If Teaching Improves, Bloomberg Says Large Classes Are Fine by Him', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARY ANN GIORDANO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Ann', 'lastname': 'GIORDANO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/645b64a8-5f0b-5e64-b11f-9e40e78c6fe3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/645b64a8-5f0b-5e64-b11f-9e40e78c6fe3'}, {'abstract': 'Packing cookies in a tray. That is the analogy a Stuyvesant High School senior, Brian Kim, of Bayside, Queens, uses to describe the math project that has put him in the finals of the annual Siemens Competition in Math, Science and Technology. Brian, whose parents are Korean immigrants, will compete this weekend in Washington for the top prize of a $100,000 college scholarship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/02/cookie-tray-theory-propels-student-to-d-c/', 'snippet': 'Packing cookies in a tray. That is the analogy a Stuyvesant High School senior, Brian Kim, of Bayside, Queens, uses to describe the math project that has put him in the finals of the annual Siemens Competition in Math, Science and Technology. Bria...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cookie Tray Theory Propels Student to D.C.', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By BETH FERTIG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FERTIG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dfb5414-7970-5ea9-aee5-d8f59c3e40b2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dfb5414-7970-5ea9-aee5-d8f59c3e40b2'}, {'abstract': 'Research in Motion said its BlackBerry PlayBook tablet was finally selling. But the steep discounts that prompted consumer interest in the device have forced the company to restate its earnings expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/business/blackberry-maker-cuts-estimates-again.html', 'snippet': 'Research in Motion said its BlackBerry PlayBook tablet was finally selling. But the steep discounts that prompted consumer interest in the device have forced the company to restate its earnings expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — Research In Motion said on Friday that its previously unwanted BlackBerry PlayBook tablet was finally selling. But the steep discounts that prompted consumer interest in the device have forced the company to restate its earnings expectations.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'RIM Says It Will Take a $485 Million Writedown on Heavy Discounts for Its PlayBook Tablets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'RIM Says It Will Take a $485 Million Writedown on Heavy Discounts for Its PlayBook Tablets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Research In Motion Ltd', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:25:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cf6f695-82ed-557e-850c-509574b29a65', 'word_count': 662, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cf6f695-82ed-557e-850c-509574b29a65'}, {'abstract': 'Verizon says the 122 spectrum licenses being acquired will  allow it to improve its 4G products and could serve a potential 259 million additional customers.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/verizon-wireless-buys-spectrum-from-comcast-and-others-for-3-6-billion/', 'snippet': 'Verizon says the 122 spectrum licenses being acquired will  allow it to improve its 4G products and could serve a potential 259 million additional customers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Verizon Wireless said Friday it had agreed to purchase spectrum licenses for $3.6 billion from a group of companies that included Comcast and Time Warner Cable, part of a push to meet growing consumer demand for wireless services.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Verizon Wireless Buys Spectrum for $3.6 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio Spectrum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bright House', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comcast Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:28:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af44b5d4-8d91-5b47-bd36-cace40ee3fbe', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af44b5d4-8d91-5b47-bd36-cace40ee3fbe'}, {'abstract': 'The Bagger talks with Tilda Swinton, star of “We Need to Talk About Kevin.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/if-this-acting-thing-doesnt-work-out-tilda-swinton-has-a-plan-b/', 'snippet': 'The Bagger talks with Tilda Swinton, star of “We Need to Talk About Kevin.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Bagger was lucky enough to interview Tilda Swinton the other night, for a TimesTalk, and sartorially and conversationally, she was a thrill, as always. Among La Swinton’s revelations: she hates the theater; doesn’t believe in the notion of fixed identity; prefers to think of herself as a “schizophrenic” writer-performer rather than an actor; and used to make a bundle betting on horses. (That’s her Career Plan B.) Ms. Swinton also talked about her work in “We Need to Talk About Kevin,” Lynne Ramsay’s controversial adaptation of Lionel Shriver’s novel about the mother of a son who goes on a Columbine-style rampage. In this clip, Ms. Swinton explains why she believes the film, which recently won the top prize at the London BFI Film Festival, is about more than the familiar facts of that story. “It’s about the inside of someone’s mind,” she said. “If you go into the film expecting that, you are much more likely to have a good trip.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If This Acting Thing Doesn’t Work Out, Tilda Swinton Has a Plan B', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb58f75-338c-5696-ae6b-66ee85e871c9', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb58f75-338c-5696-ae6b-66ee85e871c9'}, {'abstract': 'In Friday’s New York Times, Claire Cain Miller and Nick Bilton report that Google is developing a package delivery service geared toward near-instant gratification. The service would let people order items on the Web from nearby stores and receive them within a day.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/daily-report-google-may-step-on-amazons-turf/', 'snippet': 'In Friday’s New York Times, Claire Cain Miller and Nick Bilton report that Google is developing a package delivery service geared toward near-instant gratification. The service would let people order items on the Web from nearby stores and receive...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Friday’s New York Times, Claire Cain Miller and Nick Bilton report that Google is developing a package delivery service geared toward near-instant gratification. The service would let people order items on the Web from nearby stores and receive them within a day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Google May Step on Amazon’s Turf', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:34:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5729e2c3-6c88-5bf6-afd7-91fa0c30cd59', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5729e2c3-6c88-5bf6-afd7-91fa0c30cd59'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/what-were-reading-323/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hitchhiking to Heaven: Quince are in season, people: the ones I bought yesterday at the Greenmarket are destined for this recipe. And yes, they are supposed to have a downy gray fuzz on the outside. — Julia Moskin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:42:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8691d30-2d23-5446-9bdd-d551fbc64b2f', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8691d30-2d23-5446-9bdd-d551fbc64b2f'}, {'abstract': 'In a Bronx home, Christmas decorations herald the season, and a sense of pride of place.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/its-more-than-holiday-spirit-its-having-a-place-to-call-home/', 'snippet': 'In a Bronx home, Christmas decorations herald the season, and a sense of pride of place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the afternoon sun sinks behind a row of two-family cookie-cutter houses on White Plains Road, Ana Jimenez’s front yard stirs to life. Within minutes, pancake-flat plastic blobs plump up with air, revealing jiggling Santas and a Nativity scene rising alongside wire-frame robo-reindeer, all ringed by strands of twinkling lights.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s More Than Holiday Spirit; It’s Having a Place to Call Home', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas Trees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:48:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2e7438f-598a-5130-af5f-b3a5653bdfbf', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2e7438f-598a-5130-af5f-b3a5653bdfbf'}, {'abstract': 'Why Tom Glocer is out at Thomson Reuters, Google mulls a new local delivery service and David Pogue gets feedback from Barnes & Noble.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-breakfast-meeting-for-dec-2/', 'snippet': 'Why Tom Glocer is out at Thomson Reuters, Google mulls a new local delivery service and David Pogue gets feedback from Barnes & Noble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news and financial services giant Thomson Reuters announced a shakeup in its leadership late Thursday afternoon, Amy Chozick reported, replacing its chief executive, Thomas H. Glocer, after he had struggled to turn around the company in an increasingly competitive market. The Wall Street Journal put those declining fortunes starkly, quoting the projected 2011 breakdown of the $24 billion financial-data market by Burton-Taylor International Consulting: Thomson Reuters at 31.4 percent of the market, down from 37.4 percent in 2005; Bloomberg LP at 30.8 percent of the market, up from 25.1 percent in 2005.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting for Dec. 2', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, James C', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barnes & Noble Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Thomson Reuters Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:51:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b26b4a2b-ee57-579e-b4e5-5fb397269650', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b26b4a2b-ee57-579e-b4e5-5fb397269650'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans are hopeful, even confident, that 1992 is a template (albeit with party roles reversed) for 2012. Here’s a matchup of 1992 versus 2012 — and a look at where the parallels could benefit one party or the other.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/parties-can-look-to-92-as-a-guide-for-2012/', 'snippet': 'Republicans are hopeful, even confident, that 1992 is a template (albeit with party roles reversed) for 2012. Here’s a matchup of 1992 versus 2012 — and a look at where the parallels could benefit one party or the other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was the last time a challenger defeated an incumbent to take the White House. The campaign was dominated by unemployment and the budget deficit. The party out of power coalesced around a nominee and drove him to victory only after a tumultuous primary season and complaints that the best potential candidates were sitting it out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parties Can Look to ’92 as a Guide for 2012', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerrey, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perot, Ross', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shrum, Robert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T14:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard W. Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/835d17c9-2e0b-5c66-8b80-0cadb2ec8b05', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/835d17c9-2e0b-5c66-8b80-0cadb2ec8b05'}, {'abstract': 'T’s cocktail columnist on this year’s best boozy gifts:\xa0aged gin, retro jiggers, hand-cranked juicers and the smartest, cheapest shaker setup he’s ever come across.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/case-study-gifts-for-drinkers/', 'snippet': 'T’s cocktail columnist on this year’s best boozy gifts:\xa0aged gin, retro jiggers, hand-cranked juicers and the smartest, cheapest shaker setup he’s ever come across.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the season draws nigh, the calls begin from friends asking what they should get their bibulous spouses. Gifting, as a verb, needs a strategy, of course, and the one at work here supposes that most people have a semi-bar at home, a bit of this and a bottle of that, but could use another well-chosen item to connect the dots, to add a dash of panache to the occasional drink. That’s what 6 p.m. was made for, right?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Case Study | Gifts for Drinkers', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toby Cecchini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cecchini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffb9fc35-a5cf-516d-b924-933472f65c4a', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffb9fc35-a5cf-516d-b924-933472f65c4a'}, {'abstract': 'A host of added features are aimed at enriching the group conversation.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/posting-comments-gets-better/', 'snippet': 'A host of added features are aimed at enriching the group conversation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new day has dawned in the comment-sphere on City Room and across nytimes.com, as many of you may have noticed when you’ve reached the bottom of an article or post, with a host of added features aimed at enriching the group conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Commenting Shifts Into a New Gear', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:02:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/192502de-8b2f-560d-9438-3cb5bf678f88', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/192502de-8b2f-560d-9438-3cb5bf678f88'}, {'abstract': 'A former Tibetan monk set fire to himself on Thursday as an anti-China political protest in Tibet’s Chamdo township.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/asia/twelfth-tibetan-a-former-monk-self-immolates-but-survives.html', 'snippet': 'A former Tibetan monk set fire to himself on Thursday as an anti-China political protest in Tibet’s Chamdo township.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A former Tibetan monk set fire to himself as an anti-China political protest in Tibet’s Chamdo township on Thursday but survived, according to reports on Friday by Tibet advocacy groups. It was the 12th self-immolation by a Tibetan this year and the first inside Tibet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twelfth Tibetan Self-Immolates, but Survives', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Twelfth Tibetan Self-Immolates, but Survives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buddhism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monasteries and Monks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immolation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:13:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4500664b-9c2c-53c6-b0a6-b5d35e2ff127', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4500664b-9c2c-53c6-b0a6-b5d35e2ff127'}, {'abstract': 'Alexander Garvin, a veteran urban planner, has been emphasizing the notion that government should zone more – and more wisely — for public space, as opposed to private development.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/a-personal-walk-through-public-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Alexander Garvin, a veteran urban planner, has been emphasizing the notion that government should zone more – and more wisely — for public space, as opposed to private development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For my Arts & Leisure essay on public space in New York, I talked to Alexander Garvin. At “Zoning the City,” a recent conference held on the occasion of the 50th anniversary of the city’s landmark 1961 zoning resolution, Mr. Garvin, a veteran urban planner, kept hammering away at the notion that government should zone more – and more wisely — for public space, as opposed to private development. Mr. Garvin is well known as a big fan of Paris, which Baron Haussmann largely demolished and redesigned for Napoleon III in the 19th century around grand boulevards, streets and pedestrian-friendly spaces. Paris today has become a museum city, for all its glories. Still, Mr. Garvin thinks Haussmann’s priorities provide a lesson for New York.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Personal Walk Through Public New York', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Zoning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garvin, Alexander', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:15:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9714ad9-70ad-536a-807a-6e1d40cf3fdd', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9714ad9-70ad-536a-807a-6e1d40cf3fdd'}, {'abstract': 'The Long Island investigation into SAT and ACT cheating continues, revealing evidence against at least 40 students.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/sat-cheating/', 'snippet': 'The Long Island investigation into SAT and ACT cheating continues, revealing evidence against at least 40 students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jenny Anderson and Peter Applebome take a deeper look in Friday’s Times at the SAT cheating scandal on Long Island.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SAT Cheating Investigation Continues on Long Island', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:22:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e863075d-a7dc-59ef-ac93-f986d46cca36', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e863075d-a7dc-59ef-ac93-f986d46cca36'}, {'abstract': 'A layman’s guide to oil and gas leases, concerns raised about phone monitoring software, dogs and post-traumatic stress disorder and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/friday-reading-a-laymans-guide-to-oil-and-gas-leases/', 'snippet': 'A layman’s guide to oil and gas leases, concerns raised about phone monitoring software, dogs and post-traumatic stress disorder and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: A Layman’s Guide to Oil and Gas Leases', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d8a8303-9ab1-56bd-89fd-4512bcee5cc0', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d8a8303-9ab1-56bd-89fd-4512bcee5cc0'}, {'abstract': 'In a first, a senior expert with a Chinese government think tank says that Beijing is likely to agree to a quantified target to limit its greenhouse gas emissions after 2020.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/on-our-radar-china-hints-at-emissions-timetable/', 'snippet': 'In a first, a senior expert with a Chinese government think tank says that Beijing is likely to agree to a quantified target to limit its greenhouse gas emissions after 2020.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time, a Chinese official suggests that Beijing is “likely” to agree to a target for limiting its greenhouse gas emissions after 2020. But that depends on the outcome of international climate change negotiations in Durban, South Africa, he says. [China Daily]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: China Hints at Emissions Timetable', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:39:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99dc6906-8fb2-59a8-a24e-2b25b1e2d761', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99dc6906-8fb2-59a8-a24e-2b25b1e2d761'}, {'abstract': 'Leading figures in the American Jewish community have expressed anger at an ad campaign paid for by Israel which seeks to convince Israelis living in the United States to return home by suggesting that living among Americans is a sad and lonely experience.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/american-jews-angered-by-israeli-ads-aimed-at-luring-expats-home-from-u-s/', 'snippet': 'Leading figures in the American Jewish community have expressed anger at an ad campaign paid for by Israel which seeks to convince Israelis living in the United States to return home by suggesting that living among Americans is a sad and lonely ex...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | 12:37 p.m. In response to anger from leading figures in the American Jewish community, on Friday Israel’s government halted an ad campaign that aimed to persuade Israelis living in the United States to return home by suggesting that living among Americans is a sad and lonely experience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel Cancels Ad Campaign Aimed at Luring Expats Home From U.S.', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foxman, Abraham H', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4005b2ca-480b-52e6-9a65-7675a430c047', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4005b2ca-480b-52e6-9a65-7675a430c047'}, {'abstract': 'Autumn Unfolds: The loss of leaves has opened a vista in Inwood Hill Park in Upper Manhattan, and the city has come to us.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-city-comes-into-view/', 'snippet': 'Autumn Unfolds: The loss of leaves has opened a vista in Inwood Hill Park in Upper Manhattan, and the city has come to us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See a larger version of the forest from this week. Compare with the enlarged version of last week’s image. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Leaves Gone, the City Comes Into View', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fungi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'INWOOD HILL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:53:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marielle Anzelone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anzelone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bbddf7e-ccf9-54bc-b4f1-39be896b2818', 'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bbddf7e-ccf9-54bc-b4f1-39be896b2818'}, {'abstract': 'Do you let your children make a holiday wish list, and do they think it’s a list of what they’re going to get?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/should-kids-write-their-own-holiday-gift-lists/', 'snippet': 'Do you let your children make a holiday wish list, and do they think it’s a list of what they’re going to get?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing to Santa is an iconic holiday tradition, and one of the few that I dislike. Sure, that carefully penned letter — Dear Santa, please bring me a pony — is cute, what with the backward letters and the lopsided picture of a sleigh and such. But why should Santa, whose gifts come from the goodness of his heart, be told what to give? And how can someone whose toys are made by elves in a workshop be expected to produce a Wii? Then, there’s the obvious fact that a pony will never fit in the sleigh. (Please don’t ask for a puppy.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Children Write Their Own Holiday Gift Lists?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T15:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dac9756-5672-5a53-912e-ca9682d81fcf', 'word_count': 594, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dac9756-5672-5a53-912e-ca9682d81fcf'}, {'abstract': 'Vivian Toy has visited nearly 600 Manhattan co-ops and condos, mainly for the Real Estate section’s “On the Market” page. Here she casts a critical eye on a typical Manhattan two-bedroom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/04/realestate/20111204-pano-vivian-toy.html', 'snippet': 'Vivian Toy has visited nearly 600 Manhattan co-ops and condos, mainly for the Real Estate section’s “On the Market” page. Here she casts a critical eye on a typical Manhattan two-bedroom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vivian Toy has visited nearly 600 Manhattan co-ops and condos, mainly for the Real Estate section’s “On the Market” page. Here she casts a critical eye on a typical Manhattan two-bedroom.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pros and Cons of a Two-Bedroom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toy, Vivian S', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date