rank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c68d31eb-c7ba-5719-b076-c821636db28e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c68d31eb-c7ba-5719-b076-c821636db28e'}, {'abstract': 'They’re called ninja miners not for their stealth but for the ubiquitous green panning bowls they sling over their backs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/04/magazine/ninja-gold-miners-mongolia.html', 'snippet': 'They’re called ninja miners not for their stealth but for the ubiquitous green panning bowls they sling over their backs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They’re called ninja miners not for their stealth but for the ubiquitous green panning bowls they sling over their backs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ninja Gold Miners of Mongolia', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mongolia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:49:53+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/78854f8c-abd6-5445-a088-12fca92b79e3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/78854f8c-abd6-5445-a088-12fca92b79e3'}, {'abstract': 'He got as close as any writer ever has to being a rock star. His last act won’t be any different.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/nik-cohn-fever-dream.html', 'snippet': 'He got as close as any writer ever has to being a rock star. His last act won’t be any different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barely a month after his 22nd birthday, the British reporter, novelist and pop critic Nik Cohn hunkered down in a cottage in Connemara, on Ireland’s craggy western coast. It was the spring of 1968. Political storms were whipping up in Prague and Paris and America. Connemara couldn’t have been farther away from it all, and that was the idea: writing 10 hours a day, no distractions and no breaks, and, at the end of a mere seven weeks, a book.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04cohn1/04cohn1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 979, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04cohn1/04cohn1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04cohn1/04cohn1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nik Cohn’s Fever Dream', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fever Dream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohn, Nik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Rozzo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rozzo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c94cd08-f5e9-5ddd-baad-0d8bc290fc63', 'word_count': 3627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c94cd08-f5e9-5ddd-baad-0d8bc290fc63'}, {'abstract': 'A healthy 29-year-old man wakes up with excruciating abdominal pain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/diagnosis-nerves-or-something-worse.html', 'snippet': 'A healthy 29-year-old man wakes up with excruciating abdominal pain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It felt like a knife slicing through his right side. The young man lay in bed and tried not to move. He’d had this pain off and on for years — usually when he was nervous, and he was very nervous that Sunday morning. He was supposed to start a new job the next day.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04diagnosis2/04diagnosis2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 267, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04diagnosis2/04diagnosis2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04diagnosis2/04diagnosis2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nerves or Something Worse?', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nerves or Something Worse?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Calcium', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tumors', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3443423c-5d2f-571f-a193-654db5eef91e', 'word_count': 1485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3443423c-5d2f-571f-a193-654db5eef91e'}, {'abstract': 'Returning home after the war, a Bosnian runs into the neighbor who turned on his family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/lives-the-reckoning.html', 'snippet': 'Returning home after the war, a Bosnian runs into the neighbor who turned on his family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After reading an article on Bosnia’s tourism boom, my brother, Eldin, and I decided it was time to face down our past. We reasoned that we were really doing this for our 72-year-old father, Senahid. If he didn’t see the country of his birth or his childhood friends soon, he never would. Yet within days I became obsessed with creating a to-do list for our trip: 1) Take a picture of the concentration camp my brother and father survived; 2) visit the cemetery where the karate coach who betrayed us was buried; 3) confront Petra, the neighbor who stole from my mother.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04lives/04lives-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04lives/mag-04Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04lives/mag-04Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Reckoning', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Reckoning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenan Trebincevic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trebincevic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d185fd40-d00d-5d32-b306-e1c8b221505d', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d185fd40-d00d-5d32-b306-e1c8b221505d'}, {'abstract': 'Business as usual.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/the-ethicist-caveat-vendor.html', 'snippet': 'Business as usual.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I work for a small retail company that is on the verge of going under. Some suppliers have already cut us off for nonpayment. The owner has asked me to place large orders with our remaining vendors, to buy some time to turn the company around. I think it’s more likely that these vendors (many of whom are sole proprietors or small businesses themselves) will never be paid. Placing those orders without telling the vendors about our situation isn’t illegal, but I think it’s unethical. What should I do? NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Caveat Vendor', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caveat Vendor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/24750d8b-1ed2-55ee-84ff-6f88b1e92848', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24750d8b-1ed2-55ee-84ff-6f88b1e92848'}, {'abstract': 'Also: Matt Bai on F.D.R.-lust and Adam Sternbergh on things neither hot nor not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/04/magazine/diablo-cody-and-adult-onesies.html', 'snippet': 'Also: Matt Bai on F.D.R.-lust and Adam Sternbergh on things neither hot nor not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Also: Matt Bai on F.D.R.-lust and Adam Sternbergh on things neither hot nor not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diablo Cody and Adult Onesies', 'kicker': 'The One-Page Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cody, Diablo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roosevelt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/e2697a2b-5aba-5c57-a801-3be06c759aac', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/e2697a2b-5aba-5c57-a801-3be06c759aac'}, {'abstract': 'As the temperature drops, the Beagle in the East Village offers four kinds of toddies made with whiskey: hot toddy, whiskey skin, brick and hot buttered rum — and the Negus, made with port or Madeira.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/hot-toddies-at-the-beagle-in-the-east-village/', 'snippet': 'As the temperature drops, the Beagle in the East Village offers four kinds of toddies made with whiskey: hot toddy, whiskey skin, brick and hot buttered rum — and the Negus, made with port or Madeira.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a snap in the air, the classic cocktail revival turns to traditional warmers like hot toddies. “In the old days, they’d warm the drink by putting a hot poker into the glass,” said Matthew Piacentini, who owns the Beagle in the East Village, which just started serving what he calls “twilight toddies.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hot Toddies at the Beagle in the East Village', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14fcb63f-c8fc-5914-bda8-79743c3b8ebf', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14fcb63f-c8fc-5914-bda8-79743c3b8ebf'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/reply-all-elizabeth-warren.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elizabeth Warren may not be able to get huge, important bills through. That is not under the control of one person, even a senator. But imagine a really good law professor asking the questions in hearing after hearing after hearing. So many of the current members of Congress spend their time listing dull talking points. A good law professor knows how to call out a student who tries to duck a question. I want to see her do the same thing with a few bankers.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heaven Is a Place Called Elizabeth Warren', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heaven Is a Place Called Elizabeth Warren', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa55224d-0db6-5541-ae31-aa5130886660', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa55224d-0db6-5541-ae31-aa5130886660'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/reply-all-teaching-good-sex.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a 17-year-old girl, I have always found the traditional approach to sex ed, both in school and from parents, to be rather counterproductive. They aim to educate you in a manner that will allow you to have safe sex, but I feel that it teaches girls to feel bad about sex. It becomes a question of pleasing your parents or your boyfriend and not yourself. This article literally had me shedding tears of joy to think that an educated adult recognizes the confusion surrounding sex and seeks to address it in a manner that is free from shame or the pressure to do the “right thing,” whatever that may be. It’s not that I’m going to go out and sleep with someone tomorrow; I simply feel that I am free to operate how I see fit, regardless of cultural stereotypes that could make sex a negative experience.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teaching Good Sex', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teaching Good Sex', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Abstinence (Sexual)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/cada2496-164c-5dee-b26e-2b57c46eeaaf', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cada2496-164c-5dee-b26e-2b57c46eeaaf'}, {'abstract': 'From the Diner’s Club to the Google Wallet, the charge card has come a long way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/the-cardboard-beginnings-of-the-credit-card.html', 'snippet': 'From the Diner’s Club to the Google Wallet, the charge card has come a long way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Looking Backward,” Edward Bellamy’s 1888 novel about a socialist utopia more than a century hence, citizens are issued “credit cards” entitling them to shares of the national wealth. In today’s dystopian reality, people use a different sort of card to buy stuff on the Internet. Yet according to Robert Manning, a historian and author of “Credit Card Nation,” plastic remains “one of the top 10 innovations in the post-World War II period.” Even if it owes its ubiquity, in part, to a New York businessman named Frank X. McNamara, who forgot to bring cash to a lunch meeting. In 1950, McNamara introduced a cardboard charge card. He called it the Diner’s Club.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04wmt_span/04wmt_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 301, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04wmt_span/04wmt_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 301}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04wmt_span/04wmt_span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04wmt_span/04wmt_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04wmt_span/04wmt_span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cardboard Beginnings of the Credit Card', 'kicker': 'Makers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Credit Card)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e9c1e2-21c8-5179-8780-87cc8de445bb', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e9c1e2-21c8-5179-8780-87cc8de445bb'}, {'abstract': 'Ralph Waldo Emerson’s famous call to independent thinking gave rise to every faddish movement in America, from birthers to Etsy to “Blink.” But enough is enough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/magazine/riff-ralph-waldo-emerson.html', 'snippet': 'Ralph Waldo Emerson’s famous call to independent thinking gave rise to every faddish movement in America, from birthers to Etsy to “Blink.” But enough is enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My first exposure to the high-flown pap of Ralph Waldo Emerson’s “Self-Reliance” came in a basement classroom at the private boys’ school where I enrolled to learn the secrets of discipline and because I wanted, at age 14, to wear a tie. The class was early American literature, the textbook an anthology with the heft of a volume of the Babylonian Talmud; a ribbon for holding your place between “Rip Van Winkle,” by Washington Irving, and “Young Goodman Brown,” by Nathaniel Hawthorne; and a slick hardcover the same shade of green as the backside of a dollar bill.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04riff-2/04riff-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 689, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04riff-2/04riff-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/magazine/04riff-2/04riff-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Foul Reign of Emerson’s ‘Self-Reliance’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Foul Reign of ‘Self-Reliance’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Walden Pond (Concord, Mass)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emerson, Ralph Waldo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:50:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Anastas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anastas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a86983c-d080-5e52-819f-56fc9c1bc27a', 'word_count': 1879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a86983c-d080-5e52-819f-56fc9c1bc27a'}, {'abstract': 'The E.P.A. announces a slight watering down of proposed rules on toxic air pollution that were significantly scaled back earlier this year.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/new-pollution-rules-for-boilers-and-incinerators/', 'snippet': 'The E.P.A. announces a slight watering down of proposed rules on toxic air pollution that were significantly scaled back earlier this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Environmental Protection Agency on Friday released its much-delayed and oft-revised air quality regulations for industrial boilers and incinerators. The proposal is a modest refashioning of boiler rules introduced earlier this year, which were themselves a major revision of a 2010 plan by the agency that drew heavy political and industry opposition.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Pollution Rules for Boilers and Incinerators', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clean Air Act', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN LUNG ASSN', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82b48f3d-74e3-52a6-b370-71aa0972209a', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82b48f3d-74e3-52a6-b370-71aa0972209a'}, {'abstract': 'One of the final topics that came up over the course of many sit-downs with Justin Vernon of Bon Iver when I was working on my magazine profile of him was the Grammy Awards. Or more precisely, his discomfort with them, and his incredulity that he’d ever be in contention for one.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-bon-iver-grammy-quandary/', 'snippet': 'One of the final topics that came up over the course of many sit-downs with Justin Vernon of Bon Iver when I was working on my magazine profile of him was the Grammy Awards. Or more precisely, his discomfort with them, and his incredulity that he’...', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the final topics that came up over the course of many sit-downs with Justin Vernon of Bon Iver when I was working on my magazine profile of him was the Grammy Awards. Or more precisely, his discomfort with them, and his incredulity that he’d ever be in contention for one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bon Iver Grammy Quandary', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cave, Nick (1957- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vernon, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bon Iver', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:53:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87cbc179-6e1e-5b1e-8b99-3a5c15a7e6f5', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87cbc179-6e1e-5b1e-8b99-3a5c15a7e6f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Justice Department ended a six-month investigation of Google’s move to buy Admeld, a display advertising company, for $400 million. Regulators approved the deal, saying that they determined that Web publishers rely on many display advertising tools and that there is unlikely to be one dominant company.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/justice-department-approves-googles-acquisition-of-admeld/', 'snippet': 'The Justice Department ended a six-month investigation of Google’s move to buy Admeld, a display advertising company, for $400 million. Regulators approved the deal, saying that they determined that Web publishers rely on many display advertising ...', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Justice Department on Friday approved Google’s roughly $400 million acquisition of Admeld, an online display advertising company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Department Approves Google’s Purchase of Admeld', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:56:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/020f15e4-87c7-5c52-9264-a5317e6ab0e0', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/020f15e4-87c7-5c52-9264-a5317e6ab0e0'}, {'abstract': 'For the first five years of my twin boys’ lives, I visited our town’s library each week, but only to scoop up heaps of picture books, diversions, when all else failed, that filled the time and calmed down their fighting. Now the boys are in kindergarten, learning to read, and so, in a way, am I again.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/you-must-read-this/', 'snippet': 'For the first five years of my twin boys’ lives, I visited our town’s library each week, but only to scoop up heaps of picture books, diversions, when all else failed, that filled the time and calmed down their fighting. Now the boys are in kinder...', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first five years of my twin boys’ lives, I visited our town’s library each week, but only to scoop up heaps of picture books, diversions, when all else failed, that filled the time and calmed down their fighting. Now the boys are in kindergarten, learning to read, and so, in a way, am I again. On a recent Saturday, I let my sons page through books on their own in the children’s area of the library while I finally wandered among the tall shelves of fiction, which is where I stumbled on Penelope Lively’s “Moon Tiger,” a Booker Prize winner in 1987. Just a few days earlier, I had heard the writer Moni Mohsin on NPR describe the book on a terrific weekly segment called “You Must Read This.” So inspired had she been by the novel’s ambitious, selfish, literary hero, Claudia Hampton, Mohsin, at age 22, had quit the “worthy, dreary job” she had accepted shortly after finishing university and jumped into a job at an upstart newspaper. “Like Claudia,” she said, “I was determined to lead my life, not be led by it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Mothers and Worse Mothers', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lively, Penelope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldman, Ayelet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambridge (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T16:57:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dominus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a1effe0-4f97-5678-881a-eabcaa212a0f', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a1effe0-4f97-5678-881a-eabcaa212a0f'}, {'abstract': 'Measures by Turkey, the Arab League and others are already unraveling the most significant change of President Bashar al-Assad’s tenure: linking Syria to the global economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/middleeast/syrians-say-they-are-feeling-grip-of-economic-sanctions.html', 'snippet': 'Measures by Turkey, the Arab League and others are already unraveling the most significant change of President Bashar al-Assad’s tenure: linking Syria to the global economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAMASCUS, Syria — The walls are suddenly closing in around enterprising young Syrians who bought into the idea of a modernized economy promised by President Bashar al-Assad — their simplest money transfers are blocked, and their credit cards are useless outside Syria as the growing list of international sanctions darkens their financial future. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/world/03damascus-span/03damascus-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/03/world/03damascus-span/03damascus-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/world/03damascus-span/03damascus-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/world/03damascus-span/03damascus-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/03/world/03damascus-span/03damascus-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrians Say They Are Feeling the Grip of Sanctions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sanctions Against Syria Are Keenly Felt, From Shops to Oil Fields', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARAB LEAGUE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc3e170f-feb9-56b0-9d0e-94063b7036b1', 'word_count': 1457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc3e170f-feb9-56b0-9d0e-94063b7036b1'}, {'abstract': 'Does it matter that the Browns have lost 11 consecutive coin tosses, why do the Harbaugh brothers love to kick off in the first half, and other questions about the random contests that proceed each professional football game.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-strategy-and-consequences-of-the-n-f-l-coin-toss/', 'snippet': 'Does it matter that the Browns have lost 11 consecutive coin tosses, why do the Harbaugh brothers love to kick off in the first half, and other questions about the random contests that proceed each professional football game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are many ways to measure the lucklessness of the Cleveland Browns. The team has suffered through freakishly botched fourth-quarter field goals in each of the last three games, losing two of them as a result. Peyton Hillis, arguably the team’s best player, has come down with a severe case of the Madden Curse, and it seems likely that he will play for another team next season. During a trip to Indianapolis in September, the team showed up four hours later than expected after its plane got stuck in mud at the Cleveland airport.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Strategy and Consequences of the N.F.L. Coin Toss', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Luck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Browns', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:10:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brustein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/893dd3e2-20f8-5a85-9239-7c289258f17f', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/893dd3e2-20f8-5a85-9239-7c289258f17f'}, {'abstract': 'The latest jobs report is the best in a long while, but it has a dark side.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-despair-index/', 'snippet': 'The latest jobs report is the best in a long while, but it has a dark side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama never expected to campaign for re-election on lowering the jobless rate to pre-recession levels; all he really needs is a trend in the right direction. He got that this morning with the announcement that unemployment had fallen to 8.6 percent from its sticky 9 percent level most of the year, and you can expect to hear about that drop from the president and his surrogates for some time. It’s one of the best job reports in a long while.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Despair Index', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:10:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c729b100-afa9-5b89-8cfa-39eaf11db146', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c729b100-afa9-5b89-8cfa-39eaf11db146'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer Paul Taylor is collaborating with the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater’s artistic director, Robert Battle, on a staging of Mr. Taylor’s “Arden Court.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/arts/dance/paul-taylor-and-robert-battle-collaborate-on-arden-court.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer Paul Taylor is collaborating with the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater’s artistic director, Robert Battle, on a staging of Mr. Taylor’s “Arden Court.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a recent afternoon at the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater, where dancers were about to perform a run-through of “Arden Court,” the sunny studio was a little too glaring for comfort. The choreographer Paul Taylor, 81, his scruffiness offset by a pair of twinkling blue eyes, watched as state-of-the-art automatic blinds worked their magic. “My new studio has an elevator,” he told Robert Battle. “I’m not even sure where it is.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/04ARDEN1/04ARDEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/04ARDEN1/04ARDEN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/04ARDEN1/04ARDEN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/04ARDEN1/04ARDEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/arts/04ARDEN1/04ARDEN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ailey’s Torch Bearer Honors Another of His Inspirations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ailey’s Torch Bearer Honors Another Of His Inspirations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AILEY, ALVIN', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, Carolyn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AILEY, ALVIN, AMERICAN DANCE THEATER', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Battle, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Paul', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:10:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4279c8e4-71bb-5151-b308-3f9906fd2d01', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4279c8e4-71bb-5151-b308-3f9906fd2d01'}, {'abstract': 'Samuel Aranda worked anonymously for weeks in Yemen, where he was the only Western photographer shooting for a number of weeks. Last week, his name began to appear with his images published in The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/in-yemen-a-photographer-with-no-name/', 'snippet': 'Samuel Aranda worked anonymously for weeks in Yemen, where he was the only Western photographer shooting for a number of weeks. Last week, his name began to appear with his images published in The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been hard much of this year for Western photojournalists to enter Yemen to cover the political turmoil of the Arab Spring. It took Samuel Aranda more than a month to find a safe way into the country, giving him plenty of time to ponder what it would be like working in the deeply divided nation, where hundreds have died in protests.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Yemen, a Photographer With No Name', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saleh, Ali Abdullah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sana (Yemen)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiz (Yemen)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yemen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:10:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin and David Furst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52b6ab71-d09c-5830-bfbe-1140e7c2fafb', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52b6ab71-d09c-5830-bfbe-1140e7c2fafb'}, {'abstract': 'We’re teaming up with FantasyPros.com to bring you Week 13’s toughest start/sit calls, based on the selections of more than 50 fantasy football experts on the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/fantasy-football-week-13s-toughest-picks/', 'snippet': 'We’re teaming up with FantasyPros.com to bring you Week 13’s toughest start/sit calls, based on the selections of more than 50 fantasy football experts on the Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jason and Justin Sablich are here to help you with your fantasy football team. The Sablich brothers will provide fantasy football advice throughout the season on this blog and on Twitter (@5thDownFantasy). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Football: Week 13’s Toughest Picks', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benson, Cedric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burress, Plaxico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gerhart, Toby', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greene, Shonn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harvin, Percy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs, Brandon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Andre', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Steve', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Matt', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palmer, Carson', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roethlisberger, Ben', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wayne, Reggie', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, DeAngelo', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:19:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Sablich and Jason Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8d5212c-febe-52e4-ab67-9dda086aefde', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8d5212c-febe-52e4-ab67-9dda086aefde'}, {'abstract': 'Quarterback news kept writers busy, with the Eagle’s Vince Young throwing four interceptions at Seattle and the Vikings releasing Donovan McNabb.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/sports/quarterbacks-in-motion-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'Quarterback news kept writers busy, with the Eagle’s Vince Young throwing four interceptions at Seattle and the Vikings releasing Donovan McNabb.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vince Young threw four interceptions in Thursday’s loss to the Seahawks, a figure that matches the Eagles’ victory total this season. The Vikings released quarterback Donovan McNabb, who could go to the Bears, who, with Caleb Hanie at quarterback in place of the injured Jay Cutler, play the Chiefs on Sunday. Kansas City will have Tyler Palko (yes, the Tyler Palko) at quarterback, unless the recent arrival Kyle Orton starts. Orton, of course, was released by the Broncos after he lost his starting job to Tim Tebow. He used to be a Bear — before Cutler, who once started for the Broncos himself. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quarterbacks in Motion', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quarterbacks in Motion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N