et': 'This week, the editors of the Book Review on the 100 Notable Books of 2011; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Andrew Graham-Dixon talks about his new biography, “Caravaggio: A Life Sacred and Profane”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news....', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, the editors of the Book Review on the 100 Notable Books of 2011; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; Andrew Graham-Dixon talks about his new biography, “Caravaggio: A Life Sacred and Profane”; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 100 Notable Books of 2011', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T17:29:20+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/a12167dc-bfb5-56c7-9901-ddc78051b707', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/a12167dc-bfb5-56c7-9901-ddc78051b707'}, {'abstract': 'In their second round of sampling at the Jicotea site in Costa Rica, scientists investigating mammal diversity on coffee farms find some intrepid animals and tricky plant life.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/plants-that-attack-and-a-well-traveled-rat/', 'snippet': 'In their second round of sampling at the Jicotea site in Costa Rica, scientists investigating mammal diversity on coffee farms find some intrepid animals and tricky plant life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'S. Amanda Caudill, a doctoral student at the University of Rhode Island, writes from Costa Rica, where she is assessing mammal diversity on coffee farms. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Plants That Attack, and a Well-Traveled Rat', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': Non