 the country to the point of threatening those lifelines. By training locals to act as stewards of ecosystems, the Wildlife Conservation Society hopes to pro...', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Wildlife Conservation Society scientists talk about their work in Afghanistan, they typically draw puzzled reactions. “Wildlife conservation in Afghanistan?” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nurturing Wildlife in War-Torn Afghanistan', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:36:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18c227d8-44c6-55d5-b2f7-ca69d140b3df', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18c227d8-44c6-55d5-b2f7-ca69d140b3df'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of art and design books, on Saul Bass’s logos, pavement art, neon signs and cigar box labels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/visuals.html', 'snippet': 'A roundup of art and design books, on Saul Bass’s logos, pavement art, neon signs and cigar box labels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These are indeed happy holidays for film-title fans like me, who have spent sleepless nights anticipating the arrival of SAUL BASS: A Life in Film and Design (Laurence King, $75), by Jennifer Bass and Pat Kirkham. Weighing in at 424 pages, with a foreword by Martin Scorsese, this collection is gratifyingly complete. It ought to be: Jennifer Bass is Saul Bass’s daughter, and she has unlimited access to the treasures of his archive.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Heller-1/Heller-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Heller-1/Heller-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Heller-1/Heller-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Heller-1/Heller-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Heller-1/Heller-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Visuals', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Visuals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bass, Saul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e29d4c3c-87a4-537d-bc09-d654a99cd496', 'word_count': 2973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e29d4c3c-87a4-537d-bc09-d654a99cd496'}, {'abstract': 'This season’s cookbooks embrace American regionalism and home cooking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/cooking.html', 'snippet': 'This season’s cookbooks embrace American regionalism and home cooking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See America First. Or, better, Cook America First. And cook at home. That’s how chefs, food writers and publishers are reacting to the economy this year: with continuing exploration of American regionalism and a near-universal embrace of home cooking, even by chefs who are so modernist — the new preferred term for molecular gastronomy — that you might’ve assumed they didn’t have home kitchens, or at least not stoves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cooking/Cooking-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 952, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cooking/Cooking-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cooking/Cooking-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Embracing Home: Books About Cooking', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corby Kummer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kummer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbbab3ec-8610-56b4-8eb1-80a296935392', 'word_count': 3002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbbab3ec-8610-56b4-8eb1-80a296935392'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books for ice and death fans chronicle the exploration of the South Pole and the Transantarctic Mountains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-antarctic.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books for ice and death fans chronicle the exploration of the South Pole and the Transantarctic Mountains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this day in 1911 Robert Falcon Scott, en route to the South Pole, was on the Ross Ice Shelf near the gateway to the Beardmore Glacier. “One has a horrid feeling that this is a real bad season,” he wrote in his diary. It would get worse. When he finally reached the pole on Jan. 18, 1912, he found that his party had been beaten by Roald Amundsen’s Norwegian expedition, which had arrived a month before. By the end of March, Scott and his four companions would have perished on the return journey, only 11 miles from a lifesaving depot.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04HARRIS/04HARRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04HARRIS/04HARRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04HARRIS/04HARRIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04HARRIS/04HARRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04HARRIS/04HARRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Antarctic', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Antarctic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Pole', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Robert Falcon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, David M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:42:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ROBERT R. HARRIS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'HARRIS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/019778c8-b9fa-5507-9ffe-373ffeaa5e14', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/019778c8-b9fa-5507-9ffe-373ffeaa5e14'}, {'abstract': 'The inside story of New York’s High Line, a helpful guide to the cactus, a spruced-up edition of a gardening bible and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/gardening.html', 'snippet': 'The inside story of New York’s High Line, a helpful guide to the cactus, a spruced-up edition of a gardening bible and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'W