g Graham Greene’s steps in Africa and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/travel.html', 'snippet': 'Searching for tribes in the Amazon, exploring Machu Picchu, retracing Graham Greene’s steps in Africa and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A passion for adventure in some of the world’s harshest environments courses through many of this season’s travel books. Following in the footsteps of polar explorers, wandering novelists and tropical archaeologists, writers test their mettle in spots as varied as Greenland, Peru and Liberia. Along the way, they offer a wealth of cautionary tips to would-be extreme adventurers: don’t slip even the smallest ancient artifact into your backpack, be careful of unlabeled cans of food and never wear only one pair of socks when hiking through the jungle.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Travel/Travel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Travel/Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Travel/Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harsh Adventures: Books About Travel', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:42:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d8658ce-459a-583d-85a0-fe3a1499d2a7', 'word_count': 2393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d8658ce-459a-583d-85a0-fe3a1499d2a7'}, {'abstract': 'Two pioneering artists, Alex Steinweiss and R. Crumb, see their designs for record covers collected between book covers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/album-covers.html', 'snippet': 'Two pioneering artists, Alex Steinweiss and R. Crumb, see their designs for record covers collected between book covers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As strange as it may now seem, not only were albums once the state-of-the-art format for recorded music, but they were once literally albums: booklike objects, similar to photo or stamp albums, that contained multiple 78-r.p.m. discs — and whose covers rarely bore anything more than words. Presumably someone would eventually have thought of decorating those covers with original illustrations if Alex Steinweiss hadn’t, if only because the idea made so much sense from a marketing standpoint. But Steinweiss was the person who got the ball rolling, and who showed the world that album covers could be works of art in their own right.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Keepnews/Keepnews-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 861, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Keepnews/Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Keepnews/Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Album Covers', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Album Covers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinweiss, Alex', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crumb, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:42:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b100827b-dd33-5f73-b28c-85830e49e839', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b100827b-dd33-5f73-b28c-85830e49e839'}, {'abstract': 'Kiplinger’s Personal Finance Magazine has assembled a list of ten private colleges whose graduates incur average student debt far lower than the national average of $28,000.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/college-debt/', 'snippet': 'Kiplinger’s Personal Finance Magazine has assembled a list of ten private colleges whose graduates incur average student debt far lower than the national average of $28,000.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the average college student graduates with about $28,000 in personal debt and two out of every three students borrow money for school, according to the College Board, the extent to which a student is in the red at graduation can vary dramatically by institution.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Private Colleges That Keep the Average Student Debt Low', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/842d2235-f168-5612-963c-54e4ed316586', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/842d2235-f168-5612-963c-54e4ed316586'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at some of the best mysteries reviewed this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/notable-crime-books-of-2011.html', 'snippet': 'A look back at some of the best mysteries reviewed this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m always stumped when someone asks me to find them “a good mystery,” because I might recommend a serial killer thriller like Jo Nesbo’s fiendishly clever novel THE SNOWMAN (Knopf, $25.95) to someone hankering for a civilized British detective story like Peter Lovesey’s STAGESTRUCK (Soho Crime, $25)  So let’s play favorites — but pick your poison first.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 788, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Notable Crime Books of 2011', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Favorite Crime', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:43:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdfd76be-08ad-543a-9566-b5112b9b788e', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdfd76be-08ad-543a-9566-b5112b9b788e'}, {'abstract': 'The ticket brokerage firm, long criticized for the size of its fees and surcharges, will offer credits to consumers as part of a settlement of a class-action lawsuit.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/ticketmaster-to-offer-redress-for-fees/', 'snippet': 'The ticket brokerage firm, long criticized for the size of its fees and surcharges, will offer credits to consumers as part of a settlement of a class-action lawsuit.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:30 p.m. | Updated Ticketmaster’s various fees and surcharges, which sometimes add 40 percent or more to the cost of a ticket, have long infuriated its customers. But next year, thanks to a recent class-action settlement, many of those fans will be able to get some money back.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ticketmaster Offers Credits to Settle Lawsuit Over Fees', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ticketmaster', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Parcel Service Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:43:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a402104-1093-5553-b91f-a224555058ea', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a402104-1093-5553-b91f-a224555058ea'}, {'abstract': 'Three new children’s books about the story of Hanukkah.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/festival-of-lights.html', 'snippet': 'Three new children’s books about the story of Hanukkah.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I believe in scaring children a little. My daughter has never totally forgiven me for reading to her when she was not yet 3 Maurice Sendak’s “Outside Over There,” with its faceless goblins and their theft of a little girl’s baby sister. I like to think, though, that being haunted by that macabre book led to my daughter’s fanatical devotion to fairy tales, with which she now alarms her dolls. So I was disposed to adore “The Golem’s Latkes,” by Eric A. Kimmel, a Hanukkah story entwined with the legend of the golem, the Jewish Frankenstein. And I like the book fine. But it would be too tame for my daughter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Shulevitz/Shulevitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Shulevitz/Shulevitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Shulevitz/Shulevitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Shulevitz/Shulevitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Shulevitz/Shulevitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Picture Books About Hanukkah', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Festival of Lights...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmel, Eric A', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adler, David A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosen, Michael J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabuda, Robert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Shulevitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shulevitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/83e4ba69-2042-5ac2-9a03-fc311e5333d8', 'word_count': 1091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83e4ba69-2042-5ac2-9a03-fc311e5333d8'}, {'abstract': 'Two new picture books about the celebration of Christmas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/gifts-of-grace.html', 'snippet': 'Two new picture books about the celebration of Christmas.', 'lead_paragraph': ' What’s with Christmas? So anxious. So self-conscious. Always fretting about its “true spirit.” Always taking its own temperature or rummaging around for “the real meaning of.” Increasingly paranoid about the “war against.” Begging, inevitably, to be saved — December after December after December. Does any other holiday demand magic when a mere day off and a pleasant dinner would suffice? If Christmas were a friend, I’d beg her to go on Zoloft or maybe Thorazine — at least talk to someone, for God’s sake. Or eat some fudge and shut up.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Handy2/Handy2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 243, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Handy2/Handy2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 243}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Handy2/Handy2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Handy2/Handy2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Handy2/Handy2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main':