 '2011-12-02T19:46:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/484de561-2552-5454-8715-67e46b665ab5', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/484de561-2552-5454-8715-67e46b665ab5'}, {'abstract': 'The Doors, with the charismatic but tormented lead singer Jim Morrison, were instrumental in the swift transformation of rock ’n’ roll from brash diversion to serious genre.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-doors-a-lifetime-of-listening-to-five-mean-years-by-greil-marcus-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Doors, with the charismatic but tormented lead singer Jim Morrison, were instrumental in the swift transformation of rock ’n’ roll from brash diversion to serious genre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Within an electrifying few years during the 1960s, rock ’n’ roll was transformed from a brash diversion of antsy teenagers into a serious genre that threatened to rival the traditional fine arts. Instrumental in this swift development was a Los Angeles band, the Doors, whose charismatic but tormented and self-\xaddestructive lead singer, Jim Morrison, attained cultlike status after his mysterious death at age 27 in Paris in 1971, only four years after the release of their first album.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PAGLIA/04PAGLIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PAGLIA/04PAGLIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PAGLIA/04PAGLIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 972, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PAGLIA/04PAGLIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PAGLIA/04PAGLIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Listening to the Doors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rock Prophets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Doors, The', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Greil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morrison, Jim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:47:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Camille Paglia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Camille', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paglia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5198dd72-ccbf-5987-9b88-f78da93184be', 'word_count': 1276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5198dd72-ccbf-5987-9b88-f78da93184be'}, {'abstract': 'The singer Judy Collins reveals the harder facts of her life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/sweet-judy-blue-eyes-my-life-in-music-by-judy-collins-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The singer Judy Collins reveals the harder facts of her life.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Gentle voice amid the strife” is how Life magazine described the folk singer Judy Collins on the cover of its May 2, 1969, issue. The label accompanied a photo of a young woman whose distant, blue-eyed gaze hinted at strife of her own. Just months before, Collins had scored a top-10 hit with Joni Mitchell’s pained lament “Both Sides Now.” The record broke through in an age of Vietnam protesters and social revolutionaries, out to save the world but often floundering personally; of young Americans caught between the conformity of their parents’ generation and the pressure to rebel. Collins spoke to the lost soul in all of them when she sang, in Mitchell’s words, “I really don’t know life at all.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27GAVIN/27GAVIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27GAVIN/27GAVIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27GAVIN/27GAVIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Judy Collins: Sweet Soprano', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Just Folk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Judy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:47:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gavin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gavin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce92981e-7af5-50a7-ba81-0d02d4b57ea2', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce92981e-7af5-50a7-ba81-0d02d4b57ea2'}, {'abstract': 'A member of the hit-generating band Chic and a producer for Diana Ross and others, Nile Rodgers recounts his life and career in music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/le-freak-an-upside-down-story-of-family-disco-and-destiny-by-nile-rodgers-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A member of the hit-generating band Chic and a producer for Diana Ross and others, Nile Rodgers recounts his life and career in music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although Nile Rodgers makes the grade as a B-list celebrity, he isn’t as famous as the musical record warrants. A longtime co-leader of the so-called disco band Chic, he’s best known for the bass line his partner Bernard Edwards devised for the monster single “Good Times,” which later drove the seminal “Rapper’s Delight” and several lesser records. But many A-listers have capitalized on his talents and quite a few valued his friendship. And as this eventful and engaging autobiography emphasizes, that was how he and Edwards planned it. Chic cultivated anonymity. They saw their way to success as an “opening act.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04CHRISTGAU/04CHRISTGAU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04CHRISTGAU/04CHRISTGAU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04CHRISTGAU/04CHRISTGAU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04CHRISTGAU/04CHRISTGAU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04CHRISTGAU/04CHRISTGAU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rock Days of Disco', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disco Days', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Nile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:47:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Christgau', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christgau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/83338947-f6a8-5639-9fc6-8898d1139cab', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83338947-f6a8-5639-9fc6-8898d1139cab'}, {'abstract': 'A commemorative picture book justifies Def Jam as not only a record label, but a bona fide chapter in pop-culture history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-age-of-def-jam.html', 'snippet': 'A commemorative picture book justifies Def Jam as not only a record label, but a bona fide chapter in pop-culture history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Never mind Kevin Bacon. Let’s play six degrees of Def Jam.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Dreisinger/Dreisinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 813, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Dreisinger/Dreisinger-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Dreisinger/Dreisinger-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Age of Def Jam', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hip-Hop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Def Jam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:48:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Baz Dreisinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Baz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dreisinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe0b8467-4276-5633-a04b-549d606b7f1d', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe0b8467-4276-5633-a04b-549d606b7f1d'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of gorgeous, memorable photos from Life magazine’s archive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/75-years-the-very-best-of-life-illustrated-224-pp-life-books-36-95-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of gorgeous, memorable photos from Life magazine’s archive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The frenzy that usually erupts when a new iPhone hits stores — wild anticipation, long lines, jacked-up resale prices — was more than matched 75 years ago in the exuberant public reaction to the launching of an innovative (how quaint it sounds today) print magazine. When the first issue of Life appeared on Nov. 23, 1936, the 200,000 newsstand copies sold out within 24 hours, leaving disappointed customers and vendors clamoring for more. Defying expectations in the midst of the Depression, the magazine’s circulation rapidly soared to nearly two million readers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '57', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SUBGORDON/04SUBGORDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SUBGORDON/04SUBGORDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SUBGORDON/04SUBGORDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Look of Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Photojournalism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Life (Magazine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:49:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meryl Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5bc5160-0897-5b67-a598-029d7b81c14d', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5bc5160-0897-5b67-a598-029d7b81c14d'}, {'abstract': 'A portrait of the movie star as a real human being.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/spencer-tracy-a-biography-by-james-curtis-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A portrait of the movie star as a real human being.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re going to hunker down with a thousand-page biography of Spencer Tracy — specifically, James Curtis’s “Spencer Tracy: A Biography” — you may be somewhat interested in Tracy’s youth and school years, his career as a young actor in stock theater, his courtship of, and subsequent marriage to, his fellow actor Louise Treadwell. But what you really want to hear about is Tracy’s complex and sometimes fraught relationship with Katharine Hepburn, an open secret that spanned some 25 years, even though Tracy never divorced his wife.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '55', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27ZACHAREK/27ZACHAREK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27ZACHAREK/27ZACHAREK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27ZACHAREK/27ZACHAREK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27ZACHAREK/27ZACHAREK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/27/books/review/27ZACHAREK/27ZACHAREK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spencer Tracy: Leading Man', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leading Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hepburn, Katharine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tracy, Spencer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Curtis, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:49:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Zacharek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zacharek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9905150f-ef00-5590-8fab-3d0e58991bac', 'word_count': 1356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9905150f-ef00-5590-8fab-3d0e58991bac'}, {'abstract': 'Prepare for a fresh dose of whiplash with your climate headlines', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/on-the-climate-high-end-methane-time-bombs/', 'snippet': 'Prepare for a fresh dose of whiplash with your climate headlines', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most attention on climate science is focused on the new and the provocative, while the picture of growing risk from rising levels of greenhouse gases is best delineated by decades of work that is old and not contentious. That makes for a bad fit between this scientific enterprise and the world of public opinion, policy and politics. It can also produce a chronic feeling of intellectual whiplash.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Climate High End, Methane Time Bombs and the Lure of the New', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:50:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43977d48-c5bf-5b2d-8155-ae4888c105e4', 'word_count': 2591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43977d48-c5bf-5b2d-8155-ae4888c105e4'}, {'abstract': 'A history of caricature and satire from the 15th century onward.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-history-of-caricature.html', 'snippet': 'A history of caricature and satire from the 15th century onward.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody’s sure who invented the caricature. It might have been Leonardo da Vinci, whose studies of “bizarre heads” led his admirers to the realization that distorted aspects of physiognomy could serve a satirical purpose more directly than strict representation, or so INFINITE JEST: Caricature and Satire From Leonardo to Levine (Metropolitan Museum of Art/Yale University, $45), by the Met curators Constance C. McPhee and Nadine M. Orenstein, suggests. “Scholars still puzzle over the function of these drawings, which appear to have had a comic intent,” Orenstein deadpans in her commentary on an etching of a pair of grotesque heads copied from Leonardo. Or it might have been some kid who noticed that if you draw somebody’s nose and chin bigger it looks pretty funny.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The History of Caricature', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caricature', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:50:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Wolk', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolk', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e9a6f5c-8510-54c9-b1d6-e6ee9e7319fa', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e9a6f5c-8510-54c9-b1d6-e6ee9e7319fa'}, {'abstract': 'A bumper-car ride down memory lane for habitués of the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-joys-of-bam.html', 'snippet': 'A bumper-car ride down memory lane for habitués of the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-five years ago my husband and I moved to Brooklyn, lured by the promise of more space than we could afford in Manhattan. We loved our big, sunny apartment and its proximity to Prospect Park, but if we wanted to eat in a restaurant that was not a pizzeria, or see a movie whose protagonist was not a superhero, we had to return to our old neighborhood, the Village.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DIXLER/04DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 444, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DIXLER/04DIXLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 444}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DIXLER/04DIXLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 793, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DIXLER/04DIXLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DIXLER/04DIXLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Joys of BAM', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Academy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:51:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Dixler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dixler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae78f61a-f329-591e-ae34-3dd7cb477a9d', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae78f61a-f329-591e-ae34-3dd7cb477a9d'}, {'abstract': 'This book celebrates the 120th birthday of Carnegie Hall, whose very name is synonymous with musical excellence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/getting-to-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'This book celebrates the 120th birthday of Carnegie Hall, whose very name is synonymous with musical excellence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To celebrate the 120th birthday of Carnegie Hall, whose very name is synonymous with musical excellence, its overseers have put together a lavishly illustrated volume and accompanying trove of memorabilia. CARNEGIE HALL TREASURES (Harper Design/HarperCollins, $75), by Tim Page and the Carnegie Hall staff, is a sort of glorified publicity kit — the kind of thing you might give donors as a souvenir or sell to members of the public who wish they had been there. Do not look for complexity, skepticism or critical analysis, much less sober institutional history. What we have here is an orgy of self-congratulation that is both awkward and touching.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Lopate/Lopate-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Lopate/Lopate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Lopate/Lopate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting to Carnegie Hall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Musical Memorabilia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memorabilia and Souvenirs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Page, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:51:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Lopate', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lopate', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cd49e9c-4eeb-5b3a-8dd4-adb8ec1aab19', 'word_count': 1003, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cd49e9c-4eeb-5b3a-8dd4-adb8ec1aab19'}, {'abstract': 'This book offers morality tales from the N.F.L. with larger-than-life heroes and a few signature battles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-nfls-highlight-reel.html', 'snippet': 'This book offers morality tales from the N.F.L. with larger-than-life heroes and a few signature battles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010, The Wall Street Journal reported that after you take away commercials, clock stoppages, huddles and just about everything else, a typical three-hour National Football League game contains merely “10 minutes and 43 seconds of actual playing time.” The average play, between the snap and the whistle, lasts but four seconds and, The Journal found, “the ratio of inaction to action is approximately 10 to 1.” Which helps explain why nobody likes to watch professional football on television. Heh.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '49', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04TRACY/04TRACY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04TRACY/04TRACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04TRACY/04TRACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The N.F.L.’s Highlight Reel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pro Football', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Costas, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:51:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Tracy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tracy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6d594df-a2a5-514d-816a-12ec89debe1b', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6d594df-a2a5-514d-816a-12ec89debe1b'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Ribowsky’s biography charts Howard Cosell’s rise from obscurity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/howard-cosell-the-man-the-myth-and-the-transformation-of-american-sports-by-mark-ribowsky-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Ribowsky’s biography charts Howard Cosell’s rise from obscurity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once upon a time, Howard Cosell roamed television draped in the \xadcanary-colored blazer of ABC Sports, smoking a cigar the length of a sequoia, covering his baldness with a toupee the size of a featherweight boxer and speaking of sports in a way no one ever had. He was loud, audacious, obnoxious, perspicacious, brilliant, narcissistic, provocative and haughty. He would doubtlessly agree with those descriptions — and add more if only to prove that he was, as he was wont to enunciate slowly, “HOW-id Cyo-SELL.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SANDOMIR/04SANDOMIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SANDOMIR/04SANDOMIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SANDOMIR/04SANDOMIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Howard Cosell: Admired and Abhorred', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sportscasting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cosell, Howard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:51:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc3ec535-2182-5a96-b201-3f5b302371ed', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc3ec535-2182-5a96-b201-3f5b302371ed'}, {'abstract': 'This coffee-table book showcases some of the finest photographs the high-society favorite took during his many sojourns in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/cecil-beaton-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'This coffee-table book showcases some of the finest photographs the high-society favorite took during his many sojourns in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost anyone would have hankered to have a personal portrait shot by Cecil Beaton. He made his subjects, many of them distinguished or celebrated to begin with, seem impeccably elegant and disarmingly good-looking, seldom employing any of the irony or social commentary that other photographers often used.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BERGER/04BERGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 814, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BERGER/04BERGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BERGER/04BERGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cecil Beaton in New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Photography', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beaton, Cecil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albrecht, Donald', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:51:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f316eff-9749-573d-8a40-206bf1f06c28', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f316eff-9749-573d-8a40-206bf1f06c28'}, {'abstract': 'Hundreds of raw images from some 70 photographers are included in this compilation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-magic-of-magnum.html', 'snippet': 'Hundreds of raw images from some 70 photographers are included in this compilation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not often that a lavish coffee-table book comes trailing equally lavish doubts about the wisdom of its existence. “It’s generally rather depressing to look at my contacts,” Elliott Erwitt tells Kristen Lubben, the editor of MAGNUM CONTACT SHEETS (Thames & Hudson, $150), a magnificent compendium of raw images from the photography cooperative. “One always has great expectations, and they’re not always fulfilled.” Henri Cartier-\xadBresson, a Magnum founder, so hated the idea of someone pawing through his outtakes that he once bragged about throwing out his negatives “in the same way as one cuts one’s nails.” Contact sheets “are mostly a waste of money,” the photographer Leonard Freed once declared, though he added: “Because it is a waste of money, I love them.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHUESSLER/04SCHUESSLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHUESSLER/04SCHUESSLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHUESSLER/04SCHUESSLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Magic of Magnum', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outtakes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Magnum Photos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f7a7ba5-a4e6-59f3-95f0-737250932a9c', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f7a7ba5-a4e6-59f3-95f0-737250932a9c'}, {'abstract': 'Joan Didion, Carly Simon, Karl Lagerfeld and others share their enthusiasm for archival fashion photos in this collection of essays from Vogue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/looking-back-in-vogue.html', 'snippet': 'Joan Didion, Carly Simon, Karl Lagerfeld and others share their enthusiasm for archival fashion photos in this collection of essays from Vogue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fashion world is, with apologies to William Butler Yeats, no country for old men — or for anything old, really. It traffics in the new because it has to: if styles never changed, Barneys and Bergdorf would have nothing to sell us. But its \xadsingle-minded celebration of the “now” (the latest look, the fresh new face, the next big thing) also betrays a philosophical bias against the “then.” Perhaps nowhere has this bias been more memorably expressed than in a quip often attributed to Karl Lagerfeld, whose career with Chanel has consisted precisely in reinventing and refreshing an aesthetic the company’s founder developed almost a century ago: “The worst is when friends say, ‘Remember the good old days?’ Forget about the good old days! . . . What is interesting is now.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WEBER/04WEBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WEBER/04WEBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WEBER/04WEBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Back in Vogue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fashion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacSweeney, Eve', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vogue', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wintour, Anna', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Weber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/39cd021c-d6ad-573a-ab31-081597427166', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39cd021c-d6ad-573a-ab31-081597427166'}, {'abstract': 'An enormous coffee-table book fixes the work of paparazzi with an almost ennobling glaze.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-golden-age-of-the-paparazzi.html', 'snippet': 'An enormous coffee-table book fixes the work of paparazzi with an almost ennobling glaze.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometime in the last couple of decades, paparazzi became not so much the buzzing mosquitoes their namesakes in “La Dolce Vita” were meant onomatopoeically to conjure, as the predatory peckers of “The Birds.” Since descending on Princess Diana’s crashed limousine in 1997, they seem to have entered a cruel purgatory: the endless stakeout of reality-show contestants — Kendra! Amber! Over here! — in the parking lots of Whole Foods. And now that anyone can snap a “gotcha” picture with his smartphone, what possible status remains in being a professional celebrity chaser?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04JACOBS/04JACOBS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04JACOBS/04JACOBS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04JACOBS/04JACOBS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04JACOBS/04JACOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04JACOBS/04JACOBS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Golden Age of the Paparazzi', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eye For Fashion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9fe54e9-e21a-5407-98db-1cea98a4c306', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9fe54e9-e21a-5407-98db-1cea98a4c306'}, {'abstract': 'This book, commemorating the 25th anniversary of “Maus,” includes an expansive interview with Art Spiegelman and an exhaustive collection of archival material.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-making-of-maus.html', 'snippet': 'This book, commemorating the 25th anniversary of “Maus,” includes an expansive interview with Art Spiegelman and an exhaustive collection of archival material.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Survival is having children even if they hate you,” Art Spiegelman wrote in his notebook in 1985, while still working on his two-volume comics masterpiece, “Maus,” which would eventually win a Pulitzer Prize. Subtitled “A Survivor’s Tale,” “Maus” recreated the horrors of the Holocaust — as experienced by Art’s father, Vladek, and mother, Anja — casting Jews as mice and Germans as cats. But it also explored the difficult relationship between tetchy skinflint Vladek and his resentful son with touching and discomfiting honesty.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '53', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Kois/Kois-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Kois/Kois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Kois/Kois-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Making of ‘Maus’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Making of ‘Maus’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiegelman, Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Kois', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kois', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a6686f2-ba46-5bf3-b883-e8eb3417eb4a', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a6686f2-ba46-5bf3-b883-e8eb3417eb4a'}, {'abstract': 'John Berger offers his sketches and writing alongside excerpts of Spinoza’s philosophy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/spinoza-plums-and-why-we-draw.html', 'snippet': 'John Berger offers his sketches and writing alongside excerpts of Spinoza’s philosophy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 2008, while sketching at the National Gallery in London, John Berger rested his satchel on an empty chair. A security guard approached Berger and asked him to remove it. Berger placed the bag between his feet, but the guard was not satisfied, and insisted that the bag be carried. Berger became obstinate and was thrown out of the museum.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cole/Cole-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cole/Cole-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Cole/Cole-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spinoza, Plums, and Why We Draw', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spinoza, Baruch', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berger, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teju Cole', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teju', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cole', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b06ae86d-8f6c-5185-b9f8-6f35f56a36e2', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b06ae86d-8f6c-5185-b9f8-6f35f56a36e2'}, {'abstract': 'The brainchild of a group of unnamed editors, this book is an encyclopedic art museum between covers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/a-museum-in-a-book.html', 'snippet': 'The brainchild of a group of unnamed editors, this book is an encyclopedic art museum between covers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The more popular digital books become, the bigger print books grow, or so it seems, especially around holiday time when jumbo is at a premium. THE ART MUSEUM (Phaidon, $200), the size and weight of a suitcase filled with rocks, is the latest piece of material evidence. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04COTTER/04COTTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04COTTER/04COTTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04COTTER/04COTTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04COTTER/04COTTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04COTTER/04COTTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Museum in a Book', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exhibitions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:52:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b4816cd-adf8-53de-8bfa-01b2211d52a2', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b4816cd-adf8-53de-8bfa-01b2211d52a2'}, {'abstract': 'A provocatively honest memoir by a self-sufficient star of the big screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/then-again-by-diane-keaton-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A provocatively honest memoir by a self-sufficient star of the big screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Diane Keaton is admired for her ease in performing the difficult and for making the sensible glamorous. She minted a wildly original style of dressing while moviegoers were just learning her name. For three and a half decades, she’s starred in films spanning from younger woman’s nightmare (“Looking for Mr. Goodbar”) to older woman’s fantasy (“Something’s Gotta Give”), while somehow not being punished for the fact that no role ever quite equaled the luster of her Best Actress-winning turn in “Annie Hall.” She sashayed into arty endeavor — working on books about architecture and photography, directing a documentary on concepts of heaven — without waxing aloof. She rode out the “age thing” (she’s 65) that all female stars face, with minimum humiliation. And she’s been self-sufficient. She forthrightly says, “I never found a home in the arms of a man.” Yet she’s furnished an awfully big home in these pages.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WELLER/04WELLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WELLER/04WELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04WELLER/04WELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Diane Keaton: Soulful, Unselfish Maturity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leading Lady', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keaton, Diane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Celebrities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Weller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/628bd2e0-2a92-5d15-bb84-f799e0e59b71', 'word_count': 1340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/628bd2e0-2a92-5d15-bb84-f799e0e59b71'}, {'abstract': 'The Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association and the International Swaps and Derivatives Association contend that the Commodity Futures Trading Commission failed to evaluate the economic impact of its position limits rule.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/wall-street-groups-sue-regulator-over-dodd-frank/', 'snippet': 'The Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association and the International Swaps and Derivatives Association contend that the Commodity Futures Trading Commission failed to evaluate the economic impact of its position limits rule.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:06 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall St. Groups Sue Regulator to Challenge New Trading Rule', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Swaps and Derivatives Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities Industry and Financial Markets Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/449dccec-4e8e-52ba-8d46-0fbe10916a8f', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/449dccec-4e8e-52ba-8d46-0fbe10916a8f'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the cultural phenomenon of dessert, which has its roots in savory-sweet 16th-century dishes like eel in marzipan and goose-liver macaroons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/how-we-got-to-dessert.html', 'snippet': 'A look at the cultural phenomenon of dessert, which has its roots in savory-sweet 16th-century dishes like eel in marzipan and goose-liver macaroons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eel in marzipan and goose-liver macaroons may sound like outtakes from Monty Python’s “Crunchy Frog” skit, but in SWEET INVENTION: A History of Dessert (Chicago Review Press, $24.95), Michael Krondl tells us that the eel was offered at a 16th-century Italian banquet while the cookies, known more elegantly as foie gras macaroons, are the creation of a 21st-century Parisian pastry chef. These two dishes show how the relationship between sweet and savory has come full circle — from medieval and Renaissance Europe, when there was no division between them, to their segregation during the 20th century and back again to the current era, in which cutting-edge chefs delight in toying with our expectations.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DRZAL/04DRZAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 769, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DRZAL/04DRZAL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04DRZAL/04DRZAL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How We Got to Dessert', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweet And Savory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desserts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dawn Drzal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dawn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drzal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b657467e-bb68-55a2-9994-e96a383ebca1', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b657467e-bb68-55a2-9994-e96a383ebca1'}, {'abstract': 'In this collection of essays, Adam Gopnik offers a serious examination of food and taste.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/think-before-you-eat.html', 'snippet': 'In this collection of essays, Adam Gopnik offers a serious examination of food and taste.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three subjects in its subtitle are sure to propel Adam Gopnik’s new essay collection, THE TABLE COMES FIRST: Family, France, and the Meaning of Food (Knopf, $25.95) stockingward. But a word of advice to those seeking a book of heartwarming tales and wry culinary observations in the vein of Gopnik’s popular 2000 collection, “From Paris to the Moon” — babies and baguettes it is not. This is Gopnik as gastronomist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '63', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Think Before You Eat', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gastronomy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gopnik, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Muhlke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Muhlke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a093269d-3dba-54d2-9c3a-bd92eac276e7', 'word_count': 725, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a093269d-3dba-54d2-9c3a-bd92eac276e7'}, {'abstract': 'An exploration of Balzac as the first writer to bring “meals into literature, in all their diversity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-novelist-who-loved-food.html', 'snippet': 'An exploration of Balzac as the first writer to bring “meals into literature, in all their diversity.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Balzac’s Eugénie Grandet falls in love, she defies her maniacally miserly father — for the first time — by taking the sugar he’s hidden and returning it to the breakfast table so her beloved can take another lump. “Most certainly,” we are told, “the Parisian lady who holds up a silken ladder with her fragile arms to help her lover escape shows no greater courage.” For Balzac, food expresses character and class; and meals provide high drama.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Novelist Who Loved Food', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Food History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Kline', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kline', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2965da6-4fc2-5268-93b7-990cf3ed9113', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2965da6-4fc2-5268-93b7-990cf3ed9113'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Hughes examines the Eternal City through its history, politics and art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/rome-a-cultural-visual-and-personal-history-by-robert-hughes-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Hughes examines the Eternal City through its history, politics and art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If we could be reborn wherever we chose, how crowded Rome would be, populated by souls who had spent their previous lives longing to inhabit a villa on the Janiculum Hill or a room overlooking the Piazza Navona. I intend to be among them. And Robert Hughes’s ambitious, panoramic paean to the Italian capital suggests he may have similar plans for the afterlife, despite his complaints — the noise, the traffic, the tourist hordes in the Sistine Chapel! — about the 21st-century version of the Eternal City.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PROSE/04PROSE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 282, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PROSE/04PROSE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 282}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PROSE/04PROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PROSE/04PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PROSE/04PROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Hughes Tours Rome', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Eternal City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francine Prose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0417f3af-7b33-5789-bd70-fd67b85aa721', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0417f3af-7b33-5789-bd70-fd67b85aa721'}, {'abstract': 'The historian David Gilmour argues that the 1861 unification of Italy was a mistake.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-pursuit-of-italy-a-history-of-a-land-its-regions-and-their-peoples-by-david-gilmour-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The historian David Gilmour argues that the 1861 unification of Italy was a mistake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recent fissures in the European Union remind us that no political union is historically inevitable. Such federations are human creations, established either for expediency or through expansionism, frequently artificial: sometimes so much so that, as in the case of Yugoslavia, they simply fall apart.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy’s Fragile Union', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlusconi, Silvio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilmour, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:53:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/26b82634-692d-5eae-9b07-98a120ed5bdd', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26b82634-692d-5eae-9b07-98a120ed5bdd'}, {'abstract': 'With their empire in tatters, postwar Britons were desperate for a source of renewal and needed grand projects to restore national pride. They looked eastward, and up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/into-the-silence-the-great-war-mallory-and-the-conquest-of-everest-by-wade-davis-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'With their empire in tatters, postwar Britons were desperate for a source of renewal and needed grand projects to restore national pride. They looked eastward, and up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing endures quite like a mystery frozen in time at the top of the world. The story of George Mallory’s pursuit of Mount Everest in the 1920s has done just that. But behind the legend, of which most of us know only the bullet points, is a heap of story: the gruesome realities and bitter fallout of a nation gutted by World War I, the character of hegemony in pursuit of a peak, the gasping of empire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04MORRIS/04MORRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04MORRIS/04MORRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04MORRIS/04MORRIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 776, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04MORRIS/04MORRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04MORRIS/04MORRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lure of Everest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lure of Everest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wade', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mount Everest', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountain Climbing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2fb1f15-32ef-53ef-9d82-44a4b79ef25e', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2fb1f15-32ef-53ef-9d82-44a4b79ef25e'}, {'abstract': 'The artist and filmmaker Miranda July presents portraits of the sad, joyous and weird lives of Los Angeles residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/miranda-july-is-in-your-house.html', 'snippet': 'The artist and filmmaker Miranda July presents portraits of the sad, joyous and weird lives of Los Angeles residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PennySaver is a classified rag that arrives weekly in American mailboxes. It inspired the artist and filmmaker Miranda July’s latest book, IT CHOOSES YOU (McSweeney’s, $24), a revolving adventure that takes July and the photographer Brigitte Sire into strange Los Angeles homes and the sad, joyous, weird lives of the people who dwell in them — like Michael, a 60-something man in a magenta blouse who’s going through a gender transformation and happens to be selling a large black leather jacket for $10.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '43', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Koppel/Koppel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Koppel/Koppel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Koppel/Koppel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Koppel/Koppel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Koppel/Koppel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Miranda July Is in Your House', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classified Lives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'July, Miranda', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Koppel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koppel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b76cd2e-da31-5e1c-b841-171e70634683', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b76cd2e-da31-5e1c-b841-171e70634683'}, {'abstract': 'P. J. O’Rourke’s latest collection of global dispatches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/holidays-in-heck-by-p-j-orourke-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'P. J. O’Rourke’s latest collection of global dispatches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March 1998, right here in the pages of the Book Review, the humorist P. J. O’Rourke wrote a satire de force entitled “Putting the Moi Back in Memoir.” It contained jokes like: “My parents also neglected to abuse me. They’re gone now, alas. . . . I’ve thought about asking my wife’s parents to abuse me, but it seems too little, too late. I did have a stepfather who bowled.” And: “I’ve had to endure enormous prejudice. True, since I’m a middle-aged white male Republican, most of the prejudice came from me. But I still had to endure it.” And two dozen or so others we don’t have time for.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '61', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHEFT/04SCHEFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 966, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHEFT/04SCHEFT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04SCHEFT/04SCHEFT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'P. J. O’Rourke on the Road Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Humor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Rourke, P J", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Scheft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scheft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/06fd8373-8cf4-53ef-96ba-5119e38622fc', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06fd8373-8cf4-53ef-96ba-5119e38622fc'}, {'abstract': 'Kenneth Gross offers poetic and scholarly insights into the strange, archaic world of puppets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/puppet-an-essay-on-uncanny-life-by-kenneth-gross-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Kenneth Gross offers poetic and scholarly insights into the strange, archaic world of puppets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No one better illustrates the evolution of academic literary criticism into poetry than Kenneth Gross. An admired Shakespeare scholar (as well as the author of a kind of precursor to this new book called “The Dream of the Moving Statue”), he moves in “Puppet” way out there beyond mere scholarship. There are myriad internal references (“as Susan Stewart suggests”), back-of-the-book footnotes and a generous bibliography, yet Gross rises serenely above his apparatus, shaping his own vision. He dreams and muses, offering endless insights into the strange and archaic world of puppets, inanimate things breathed to life. This is a book of literary mysticism, rich with accrued culture yet never weighed down by it. It’s a summation of what Gross calls the “vast literature” about puppets, yet never didactic or preachy. It makes arguments and tells stories about wildly diverse puppet traditions, but it can also be dipped into like a book of poetry, savored for its lapidary insights on every page.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04ROCKWELL/04ROCKWELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04ROCKWELL/04ROCKWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04ROCKWELL/04ROCKWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pinocchio’s Tribe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pinocchio’s Tribe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puppets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Rockwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rockwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35890b4d-d7ba-5b48-9e45-d709539bd32b', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35890b4d-d7ba-5b48-9e45-d709539bd32b'}, {'abstract': 'The exploits of Arthur and his Knights of the Round Table, based on the 15th-century classic “Le Morte d’Arthur.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/the-death-of-king-arthur-thomas-malorys-le-morte-darthur-retold-by-peter-ackroyd-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The exploits of Arthur and his Knights of the Round Table, based on the 15th-century classic “Le Morte d’Arthur.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' Peter Ackroyd’s vigorous retelling of the Arthur legends, based on Thomas Malory’s 15th-century classic “Le Morte d’Arthur,” begins with a reptilian king, “a dragon in wrath as well as in power,” lasciviously whispering in the ear of another man’s wife. “This dragon will not bite,” he assures her, but she’s grossed out anyway — who wouldn’t be? — and complains to her husband. Kings tend to get what they wish for, especially if Merlin is available to make the magic happen. The husband is killed off, and three hours later the dragon, assuming the dead man’s shape, sleeps with the oblivious woman. Presto, Arthur is conceived, not quite a bastard, as Merlin explains, on a technicality clear only to him. The price for Merlin’s matchmaking is baby Arthur, whom Merlin entrusts to a rustic knight. Only when Arthur, in search of a sword for his foster brother, Sir Kay, conveniently finds one stuck in a stone, is his special destiny disclosed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '65', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BENFEY/04BENFEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 573, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BENFEY/04BENFEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04BENFEY/04BENFEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Camelot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Camelot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ACKROYD, PETER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cadbf04-659c-5643-becf-51b7c7d17de5', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cadbf04-659c-5643-becf-51b7c7d17de5'}, {'abstract': 'A 16-year-old develops superhero powers after a 32-gigabyte iPhone 3GS is embedded in his skull.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/iboy-by-kevin-brooks-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A 16-year-old develops superhero powers after a 32-gigabyte iPhone 3GS is embedded in his skull.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The setting for “iBoy,” in the projects and high-rises of South London, as well as its violence, meted out by marauding pubescent gangs, cannot help cutting close to the bone after this past summer’s riots in England. Kevin Brooks’s “iBoy,” his latest gritty sci-fi thriller (“Black Rabbit Summer,” “The Road of the Dead”), is a vengeance tale that even offers a philosophical take on the criminal actions of these young offenders, a prescient view currently shared by many British politicians: “The application in my iBrain doesn’t care why they do it. It doesn’t care if they’re poor or uneducated or bored or addicted or troubled or lonely or if they simply don’t know any better.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The iPhone, Weaponized', 'kicker': 'Young Adult', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Digital Defender', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Beale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/98ca4a02-40f4-529f-b301-7d995a2832f8', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98ca4a02-40f4-529f-b301-7d995a2832f8'}, {'abstract': 'This debut novel set in post-apocalyptic Los Angeles is told from the alternating perspectives of two teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/legend-by-marie-lu-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'This debut novel set in post-apocalyptic Los Angeles is told from the alternating perspectives of two teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Overpackaged, over\xadhyped first novels are done a disservice by their publishers, with untested authors expected somehow to justify all the gloss and glitter of a full-bore prepublication promotional blitz. Too many just don’t merit the attention. I could only stand up and cheer, therefore, for Marie Lu’s “Legend.” A fine example of commercial fiction with razor-sharp plotting, depth of character and emotional arc, “Legend” doesn’t merely survive the hype, it deserves it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PEARSON/04PEARSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PEARSON/04PEARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/04PEARSON/04PEARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Post-Apocalyptic Teenagers in Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dystopia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ridley Pearson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ridley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pearson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/987bfc6d-ef53-5009-a366-3a94015348a1', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/987bfc6d-ef53-5009-a366-3a94015348a1'}, {'abstract': 'A duplex penthouse apartment overlooking Union Square, with an outdoor fireplace and an infinity pool on its more than 2,000 square feet of terrace space, was the biggest apartment sale of the week, according to city records.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/big-ticket-sold-for-11-5-million/', 'snippet': 'A duplex penthouse apartment overlooking Union Square, with an outdoor fireplace and an infinity pool on its more than 2,000 square feet of terrace space, was the biggest apartment sale of the week, according to city records.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A duplex penthouse apartment overlooking Union Square, with an outdoor fireplace and an infinity pool on its more than 2,000 square feet of terrace space, was the biggest apartment sale of the week, at $11.5 million, according to city records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $11.5 Million', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8698483-5aa5-5d30-8251-8f3fa6fe6896', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8698483-5aa5-5d30-8251-8f3fa6fe6896'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Giovanni Belzoni, a 6-foot-6 “giant” who earned the undying enmity of his successors in a field that only later became known as archaeology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/books/review/belzoni-the-giant-archaeologists-love-to-hate-by-ivor-noel-hume-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Giovanni Belzoni, a 6-foot-6 “giant” who earned the undying enmity of his successors in a field that only later became known as archaeology.', 'lead_paragraph': ' His introduction to the wonders of the ancient world could hardly have been less auspicious. While in Cairo in the summer of 1815, awaiting an audience with Mohammed Ali Pasha, Turkish viceroy of Egypt, the Italian monk-turned-peddler-turned-hydrologist-turned-circus impresario Giovanni Belzoni paid a visit to the Great Pyramid and became so tightly wedged in one of its internal passages that his guides had to forcibly extract him. It was merely the first of many indignities endured by this 6-foot-6 “giant,” whose adventures in the Nile Valley would yield some of the most imposing treasures in the British Museum. They would also earn him the undying enmity of his successors in a field that only later acquired the polish of a professional discipline — archaeology.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Becker/Becker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Becker/Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/04/books/review/Becker/Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Archaeologist as Titan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Archaeology', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Egyptian Civilization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T19:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad97f4a6-c10e-5fff-b6ca-ae75aa46632f', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad97f4a6-c10e-5fff-b6ca-ae75aa46632f'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican race for president focuses on Newt Gingrich and Mitt Romney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/politics/100000001203974/the-caucus--two-man-race.html', 'snippet': 'The Republican race for president focuses on Newt Gingrich and Mitt Romney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Republican race for president focuses on Newt Gingrich and Mitt Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur