y University of New York’s Center for Urban Research, also found that the financial industry’s work force was becoming more diverse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To get rich on Wall Street, it may help to be white and male.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Says Wall Street Pay Is Tilted Toward White Men', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Urban Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:01:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1875f30-3309-5c08-8390-a9245dcf586d', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1875f30-3309-5c08-8390-a9245dcf586d'}, {'abstract': 'A popular sleep medication may have found a second life as a drug that stirs consciousness in patients in a near-vegetative state.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/awakenings-caused-by-sleeping-pills/', 'snippet': 'A popular sleep medication may have found a second life as a drug that stirs consciousness in patients in a near-vegetative state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ambien is one of the nation’s most popular sleep medications, widely used to treat insomnia. But this week’s New York Times Magazine explains how the drug is being used to help awaken patients in comalike states. One of them is Chris Cox, a 26-year-old who suffered brain damage after an accidental overdose of pain medication.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Awakenings Caused by Sleeping Pills', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:11:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bd54d91-0dfa-5010-9ec1-0bad8a850228', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bd54d91-0dfa-5010-9ec1-0bad8a850228'}, {'abstract': 'Pizzas delivered to the station house never reached their intended recipients. “An honest mistake,” the police say. The protesters have their doubts.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/pizza-brutality-police-devour-protesters-pies/', 'snippet': 'Pizzas delivered to the station house never reached their intended recipients. “An honest mistake,” the police say. The protesters have their doubts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First there was pepper spray. Then came the batons. But on Thursday night, bad relations between the police and protesters entered a new dimension. While arrested demonstrators sat in their cells at a Lower East side station house, the police, protesters say, stole their pizza and drank all their soda pop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pizza Brutality: Police Devour Protesters’ Pies', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pizza', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing Works', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:14:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jillian Dunham and Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f354507f-8d31-530f-9a18-5ec8e64ce64b', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f354507f-8d31-530f-9a18-5ec8e64ce64b'}, {'abstract': 'The mayor praised Matthew P. Sapolin’s accomplishments during his nine years leading the Mayor’s Office for People With Disabilities. Mr. Sapolin helped develop guidelines for architects to build accessible spaces and led an effort to freeze rents for some disabled New Yorkers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/bloomberg-eulogizes-aide-who-led-office-for-disabled/', 'snippet': 'The mayor praised Matthew P. Sapolin’s accomplishments during his nine years leading the Mayor’s Office for People With Disabilities. Mr. Sapolin helped develop guidelines for architects to build accessible spaces and led an effort to freeze rents...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg paid tribute on Friday to a close aide, Matthew P. Sapolin, his disabilities commissioner, who died of cancer on Tuesday at age 41. At a funeral at St. Patrick’s Cathedral, the mayor praised Mr. Sapolin, who was blind from the age of 5 as a result of his cancer, as someone who refused to feel sorry for himself and who fought up until the end of his life to improve the lives of disabled New Yorkers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bloomberg Eulogizes Aide Who Led Office for Disabled', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sapolin, Matthew P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Mayor's Office for People With Disabilities (NYC)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:18:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d44b33fe-c1c3-51bb-a795-4f551eae32ba', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d44b33fe-c1c3-51bb-a795-4f551eae32ba'}, {'abstract': 'An ethical quandary for parents: when another child accidentally breaks your child’s expensive gadget.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/parental-quandary-the-broken-ipod/', 'snippet': 'An ethical quandary for parents: when another child accidentally breaks your child’s expensive gadget.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Parenting, as so many have so famously said, isn’t something we do alone. It takes a village, and in that village we sometimes find ourselves faced with, well, situations. Sticky situations. Dilemmas, of a perplexing ethical nature. Moments when we’re not sure how to do right by our kids, other kids, our fellow parents, and the people around us.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Quandary: The Broken iPod', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:19:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4638f05d-e2f0-5042-82ec-d4f71175a58e', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4638f05d-e2f0-5042-82ec-d4f71175a58e'}, {'abstract': 'Yankees General Manager Brian Cashman had challenged the new Red Sox manager, Bobby Valentine, to a little friendly rappelling.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/valentine-to-cashman-its-on/', 'snippet': 'Yankees General Manager Brian Cashman had challenged the new Red Sox manager, Bobby Valentine, to a little friendly rappelling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe they will call it High Noon. When Brian Cashman went to the top of a 22-story building in Stamford, Conn., on Friday to practice what will be his second annual holiday descent down the building’s side, he issued a mischievous, but friendly, challenge to Bobby Valentine: come and join me next year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Valentine to Cashman: It’s On', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cashman, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Valentine, Bobby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Red Sox', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T20:21:15+0000', 'document_type': 'art