], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T22:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3822aa0c-a1e6-55e9-b64e-361b391af688', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3822aa0c-a1e6-55e9-b64e-361b391af688'}, {'abstract': 'You don’t need to roll out the dough.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/easier-linzer-cookies/', 'snippet': 'You don’t need to roll out the dough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cutting out cookie rings to expose the marmalade filling makes for very pretty linzer cookies. But if rolling out cookies isn’t your thing, there is an easier way. Simply form the dough into logs instead of disks, and wrap it in plastic. Chill until quite firm, at least 6 hours or up to 5 days. Then unwrap the logs and use a sharp knife to slice off 3/16-inch-thick rounds of the dough. Bake as directed for in the recipe, then form sandwich cookies with the marmalade filling. Sprinkle with confectioners’ sugar. Just as tasty, much quicker. You could even skip the marmalade. But I wouldn’t.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Easier Linzer Cookies', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-02T22:19:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaed3976-3984-513f-87ce-1fc7a647ca56', 'word_count': 106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaed3976-3984-513f-87ce-1fc7a647ca56'}, {'abstract': 'This week: Jeff Sommer with David Gillen on the No