ection_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/66b8bbb8-7470-5d1b-80fa-21d1062b58ff', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66b8bbb8-7470-5d1b-80fa-21d1062b58ff'}, {'abstract': 'At Oaxaca Kitchen in New Haven, Prasad Chirnomula interprets traditional Mexican cuisine through the bright contemporary lens that characterizes the food at his innovative Indian restaurants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/nyregion/an-innovative-chef-looks-south-to-oaxaca.html', 'snippet': 'At Oaxaca Kitchen in New Haven, Prasad Chirnomula interprets traditional Mexican cuisine through the bright contemporary lens that characterizes the food at his innovative Indian restaurants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE 2000, diners have come to know Prasad Chirnomula as the chef and owner of the innovative Thali Indian restaurants in New Canaan, Ridgefield and New Haven, as well as two casual, vegetarian eateries, called Thali Too, in New Haven and Westport. Last year, he turned his considerable energies to Mexican cuisine. The result is Oaxaca Kitchen, in New Haven. Here, Mr. Chirnomula interprets traditional Mexican cuisine through the bright contemporary lens that characterizes the food at his other restaurants. Some of his dishes work better than others, but given the chef’s creativity and the excellent service, the experience is well worth the adventure.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/nyregion/04SUBDINECT3/04SUBDINECT3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/04/nyregion/04SUBDINECT3/04SUBDINECT3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thum