: 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/nyregion/ACORN1/ACORN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/03/nyregion/ACORN1/ACORN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/03/nyregion/ACORN1/ACORN1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Lean Acorn Crop in Northeast, Even People May Feel the Effects', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Lean Acorn Crop In Northeast, Even People May Feel the Effects', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ticks (Insects)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acorns', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Northeastern States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mice', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lyme Disease', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T01:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ritchie S. King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ritchie', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca1eb657-3107-50b2-9fc6-a61259b4da13', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca1eb657-3107-50b2-9fc6-a61259b4da13'}, {'abstract': 'The government is trying to limit the force of the Privacy Act, which would allow agencies to act improperly with impunity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/opinion/the-true-costs-of-privacy-invasion.html', 'snippet': 'The government is trying to limit the force of the Privacy Act, which would allow agencies to act improperly with impunity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Privacy Act of 1974 allows a person to sue a federal agency for intentionally disclosing personal, confidential information without permission. But the government is trying to limit the force of that law and make it harder to hold agencies accountable for such violations. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The True Costs of Privacy Invasion', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The True Costs of Privacy Invasion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Aviation Administration', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T02:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bf26fe3-3660-505c-8d43-6247626f19ad', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bf26fe3-3660-505c-8d43-6247626f19ad'}, {'abstract': 'Lincoln, in his first “annual message” to the nation 150 years ago, found some hope amid the grimness.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/the-state-of-the-union-is-bad/', 'snippet': 'Lincoln, in his first “annual message” to the nation 150 years ago, found some hope amid the grimness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 3, 1861, President Abraham Lincoln delivered his first Annual Message, the equivalent of our modern State of the Union. In the 19th century, presidents summarized progress at the end of the year rather than the beginning, which gives a little more perspective on things. The phrase “the State of the Union” dates from 1934, when Franklin D. Roosevelt used it for the first time. Nor was it spoken, as it has been, generally, since Woodrow Wilson chose to travel down Pennsylvania Avenue and deliver his message verbally in 1913. (Jimmy Carter sent his in in 1981, a confession that things had not gone so well.) Instead, Lincoln composed a long written document, and sent it to Congress. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The State of the Union Is Bad', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T02:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted Widmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Widmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67d78e55-aba0-59f1-9eb3-3507e17c2a7a', 'word_count': 1750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67d78e55-aba0-59f1-9eb3-3507e17c2a7a'}, {'abstract': 'The potential for trading on insider information from the halls of Congress is undeniable and needs to be policed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/opinion/profit-taking-inside-congress.html', 'snippet': 'The potential for trading on insider information from the halls of Congress is undeniable and needs to be policed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ghost of George Washington Plunkitt, the 19th-century Tammany Hall boss who cynically defined “honest graft,” is in the air as bipartisan panic suddenly sweeps Congress over an ethical matter that is old news on Wall Street: insider trading. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Profit-Taking Inside Congress', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Profit-Taking Inside Congress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pelosi, Nancy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slaughter, Louise M', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachus, Spencer', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congress (US)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_dat