 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T04:55:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/9257480b-361c-5cfd-9414-02ba49512052', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9257480b-361c-5cfd-9414-02ba49512052'}, {'abstract': 'The Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant largely withstood the March 11 earthquake, but succumbed to the tsunami that followed, according to an internal study released by the facility’s operator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/asia/japan-reactor-withstood-quake-power-company-report-says.html', 'snippet': 'The Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant largely withstood the March 11 earthquake, but succumbed to the tsunami that followed, according to an internal study released by the facility’s operator.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant largely withstood the March 11 earthquake, but succumbed to the tsunami that followed, according to an internal study released Friday by the plant’s operator, Tokyo Electric Power. The report concluded that there was no evidence to suggest that the main facilities at the site were compromised by the quake before the tsunami waves hit. The company has been eager to show that the site was relatively unscathed by the quake. A finding that the quake had caused the damage would heighten concerns about nuclear plants in other areas, where big quakes are far more frequent than tsunamis. A panel of experts that reviewed the study for the company said it had been unprepared for a tsunami of that scale, which slowed its response and made the disaster worse. The government is also conducting its own investigation. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan: Reactor Withstood Quake, Power Company Report Says', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Reactor Withstood Quake, Power Company Report Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tokyo Electric Power Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earthquakes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tidal Waves and Tsunamis', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T04:59:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6828be6f-36e4-573a-a636-8bdc626ef5ac', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6828be6f-36e4-573a-a636-8bdc626ef5ac'}, {'abstract': 'Out of 3,868 tests on baseball players last season, there was only one positive test resulting in discipline, one fewer than the previous year.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/02/totals-change-little-in-baseballs-annual-drug-report/', 'snippet': 'Out of 3,868 tests on baseball players last season, there was only one positive test resulting in discipline, one fewer than the previous year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The annual report issued by baseball’s independent drug program administrator, Bryan Smith, showed no drastic changes in the number of positive tests from the previous year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Totals Change Little in Baseball’s Annual Drug Report', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramirez, Manny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Bryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Colorado Rockies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T04:59:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af7f2ee6-8734-5fe8-bee9-f63a226528ac', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af7f2ee6-8734-5fe8-bee9-f63a226528ac'}, {'abstract': 'Humberto Moreira, the president of the party that ruled Mexico for seven decades, resigned Friday in the face of a financial scandal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/americas/mexico-humberto-moreira-party-official-resigns.html', 'snippet': 'Humberto Moreira, the president of the party that ruled Mexico for seven decades, resigned Friday in the face of a financial scandal.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The president of the party that ruled Mexico for seven decades resigned Friday in the face of a financial scandal that threatened to taint the party’s prospects for reclaiming the presidency next year. Humberto Moreira stepped down from his post at the Institutional Revolutionary Party, saying news media coverage connecting him to a large debt and loan irregularities in his home state, Coahuila, which he governed until January, were hurting the party. The party’s candidate, Enrique Peña Nieto, is the front-runner in most presidential polls, and it is striving to convince voters that it has moved past a history of corruption. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Party Official Resigns', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Party Official Resigns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Institutional Revolutionary Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T05:02:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karla Zabludovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zabludovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b14ccde-5853-5425-bc3a-857b35f6801a', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b14ccde-5853-5425-bc3a-857b35f6801a'}, {'abstract': 'Duke was outplayed in the first half, but the Blue Devils got some breaks and wound up winning easily.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/duke-reaches-womens-soccer-final/', 'snippet': 'Duke was outplayed in the first half, but the Blue Devils got some breaks and wound up winning easily.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a battle of old conference foes playing the biggest game in the rivalry’s history, Atlantic Coast Conference powerhouses Duke and Wake Forest put on a performance worthy of the occasion — one that featured five goals, penalties, yellow cards and more than a few game-saving tackles. In the end, though, it was the A.C.C.’s regular-season champion that claimed a spot in the national championship game. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Duke Reaches Women’s Soccer Final', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NCAA Soccer Championships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlantic Coast Conference', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duke University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wake Forest University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T05:07:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d932d056-6053-5375-b13e-b60627076c54', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d932d056-6053-5375-b13e-b60627076c54'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of an attack on British diplomatic facilities in Tehran on Tuesday, Iranian diplomats facing a Friday expulsion deadline vacated Britain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/middleeast/iran-diplomats-leave-london.html', 'snippet': 'In the wake of an attack on British diplomatic facilities in Tehran on Tuesday, Iranian diplomats facing a Friday expulsion deadline vacated Britain.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Facing a Friday expulsion deadline, Iranian diplomats vacated Britain. Britain’s Foreign Office, which ordered the expulsion and the closing of Iran’s embassy after the attack on British diplomatic facilities in Tehran on Tuesday, said the Iranians had left in a chartered aircraft. The Iranian authorities, meanwhile, warned the European Union against elevating sanctions against Iran over its disputed nuclear program. They also denounced the Security Council for siding with Britain’s protest of the embassy attack, which has reduced Iran-Britain relations to their lowest point in years. “If you have just a bit of wisdom, you won’t tie your rope to the rotten rope of Britain,” the militant cleric Ayatollah Ahmad Khatami, above, warned the West in a speech at Tehran University, news agencies reported. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Diplomats Leave London', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Diplomats Leave London', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T05:12:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/161ac55b-32b6-5634-a5b6-bbda0fba638a', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/161ac55b-32b6-5634-a5b6-bbda0fba638a'}, {'abstract': 'The Spanish government is looking to prosecute 15 former Salvadoran military officers in the 1989 murders of six Jesuit priests, their housekeeper and her teenage daughter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/03/world/europe/spain-asks-us-and-el-salvador-to-extradite-murder-suspects.html', 'snippet': 'The Spanish government is looking to prosecute 15 former Salvadoran military officers in the 1989 murders of six Jesuit priests, their housekeeper and her teenage daughter.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Spanish government asked the United States and El Salvador Friday to extradite 15 former Salvadoran military officers in the 1989 murders of six Jesuit priests, their housekeeper and her teenage daughter. The arrest warrants were issued in May by Judge Eloy Velasco of the Spanish National Court in a case brought under Spain’s universal jurisdiction law. The case was one of the most notorious of El Salvador’s civil war. Two of the defendants are in the United States and one of them, Inocente Orlando Montano, a former vice minister of public security, is in custody, facing immigration charges. The remaining 13 defendants are in El Salvador. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spain Asks U.S. and El Salvador to Extradite Murder Suspects', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spain Asks U.S. and El Salvador to Extradite Murder Suspects', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Salvador', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Velasco, Eloy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T05:19:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'p