ource': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Voting Rights and Texas', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Voting Rights and Texas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-03T22:29:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/16f87914-5aed-5bda-a15f-53ffccdfec36', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16f87914-5aed-5bda-a15f-53ffccdfec36'}, {'abstract': 'The word “apology” has become so politicized in our own discourse that no president can admit to doing it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/opinion/sunday/im-sorry-the-scariest-words-in-politics.html', 'snippet': 'The word “apology” has become so politicized in our own discourse that no president can admit to doing it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE of the great tropes of Republican criticism of Barack Obama is that the president goes around the world apologizing for America’s past misdeeds. “Have we ever had a president,” Mitt Romney asked in a foreign policy speech this summer, “who was so eager to address the world with an apology on his lips and doubt in his heart?” Mr. Romney repeated the allegation at last month’s national security debate. He even embedded his claim in the title of his ca