g past polling to caucus results suggests Newt Gingrich may be well positioned in Iowa.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/how-safe-is-gingrichs-lead-in-iowa/', 'snippet': 'Comparing past polling to caucus results suggests Newt Gingrich may be well positioned in Iowa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two new surveys out this morning, from the Des Moines Register and NBC News, show Newt Gingrich with the polling lead for the Iowa Republican caucuses. Although these results are broadly in line with other recent polls of the state, it is good to get the confirmation since these are extremely high-quality surveys. On average, Mr. Gingrich is at 25 percent in the two polls, with Mitt Romney and Ron Paul each at 17 percent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Safe Is Gingrich’s Lead in Iowa?', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dean, Howard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Gary', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Des Moines Register', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC News', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T16:52:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce99c255-e20b-53f4-abb6-04f1e2c6a339', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce99c255-e20b-53f4-abb6-04f1e2c6a339'}, {'abstract': 'While Chinese leaders speak in favor of political reform, local authorities routinely deny voters the chance to choose their own political representative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/world/asia/china-clamps-down-on-even-a-by-the-book-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'While Chinese leaders speak in favor of political reform, local authorities routinely deny voters the chance to choose their own political representative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Periodic elections to neighborhood People’s Congresses are as close to participatory democracy as this nation comes. Of the many grass-roots candidates running here this year, Qiao Mu, an energetic 41-year-old journalism professor in the capital, seemed one of the better bets. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/world/asia/05beijing1/05beijing1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/05/world/asia/05beijing1/05beijing1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/world/asia/05beijing1/05beijing1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/world/asia/05beijing1/05beijing1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/05/world/asia/05beijing1/05beijing1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alarmed by Independent Candidates, Chinese Authorities Crack Down', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alarmed by Independent Candidates, Chinese Authorities Crack Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T17:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon LaFraniere', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaFraniere', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13ee8ab6-1cfe-5618-80d2-7dbed8749a28', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13ee8ab6-1cfe-5618-80d2-7dbed8749a28'}, {'abstract': '“Leonardo da Vinci: Painter at the Court of Milan” has created a ticket frenzy and thick crowds at the National Gallery in London, where the show emphasizes that master’s importance as a painter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/arts/design/leonardo-da-vinci-blockbuster-at-national-gallery-in-london.html', 'snippet': '“Leonardo da Vinci: Painter at the Court of Milan” has created a ticket frenzy and thick crowds at the National Gallery in London, where the show emphasizes that master’s importance as a painter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — It’s hard to think of anything that Alexander McQueen and Leonardo da Vinci have in common. One made clothes out of feathers, flowers, shells and lace. The other painted intensely naturalistic religious scenes and animated portraits. But both, it seems, have the rare power to please modern crowds. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/05LEONARDO/05LEONARDO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/05LEONARDO/05LEONARDO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/05LEONARDO/05LEONARDO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/05LEONARDO/05LEONARDO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/05LEONARDO/05LEONARDO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Lines and Scalpers: Rock Star? No, Leonardo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Lines and Scalpers: Rock Star? No, Leonardo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Gallery (London)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Vinci, Leonardo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T17:37:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b6f39ce-388d-5c78-9013-10e7949c8735', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b6f39ce-388d-5c78-9013-10e7949c8735'}, {'abstract': '“Breaking Dawn — Part 1,” the most recent entry in this popular vampire movie franchise, continues to impress at the box office, where holdover movies dominated the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/04/latest-twilight-remains-leader-at-box-office/', 'snippet': '“Breaking Dawn — Part 1,” the most recent entry in this popular vampire movie franchise, continues to impress at the box office, where holdover movies dominated the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a weekend of Thanksgiving leftovers at the multiplex. With no new broad-audience movies arriving in theaters, “The Twilight Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 1” (Summit Entertainment) repeated as the No. 1 picture in North America for the third weekend, taking in an estimated $16.9 million for a new total of $247.3 million. “The Muppets” (Walt Disney) was second with about $11.2 million in ticket sales, bringing its two-week total to $56.1 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box office statistics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Latest ‘Twilight’ Remains Leader at Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T17:43:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05534c46-dddb-5ae7-bfdc-4f08d1d481bd', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05534c46-dddb-5ae7-bfdc-4f08d1d481bd'}, {'abstract': 'Global emissions of carbon dioxide from fossil-fuel burning jumped by 5.9 percent in 2010, upending the notion that a brief decline during the recession might persist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/science/earth/record-jump-in-emissions-in-2010-study-finds.html', 'snippet': 'Global emissions of carbon dioxide from fossil-fuel burning jumped by 5.9 percent in 2010, upending the notion that a brief decline during the recession might persist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Global emissions of carbon dioxide from fossil-fuel burning jumped by the largest amount on record last year, upending the notion that the brief decline during the recession might persist through the recovery.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carbon Emissions Show Biggest Jump Ever Recorded', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Global Carbon Dioxide Emissions in 2010 Show the Biggest Jump Ever Recorded', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T18:01:49+0000',