 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a431502-c241-574b-8ddb-c1a69c9d701d', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a431502-c241-574b-8ddb-c1a69c9d701d'}, {'abstract': 'The Kalichstein-Laredo-Robinson Trio presented a lively new work by Ellen Taaffe Zwilich at Zankel Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/arts/music/kalichstein-laredo-robinson-trio-at-zankel-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Kalichstein-Laredo-Robinson Trio presented a lively new work by Ellen Taaffe Zwilich at Zankel Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Kalichstein-Laredo-Robinson Trio, long in effect the resident chamber group at the 92nd Street Y, is touring some of New York’s other halls this season and bringing new music to each stop. Having already offered the premiere of a Stanley Silverman work there in September, the trio turned up at Zankel Hall on Tuesday evening with a lively new work by Ellen Taaffe Zwilich. Still to come are performances at Town Hall, with a fresh Richard Danielpour score, and at Alice Tully Hall (as guests of the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center) with the latest from André Previn. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/trio/trio-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/trio/trio-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/arts/trio/trio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bent Notes Suffused With Blues', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bent Notes Suffused With Blues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zwilich, Ellen Taaffe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kalichstein-Laredo-Robinson Trio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T23:02:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/913c69a1-1eff-5e03-a550-8dbcc55567cb', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/913c69a1-1eff-5e03-a550-8dbcc55567cb'}, {'abstract': 'Brian Frons, the ABC executive who angered many soap opera fans this year by canceling “All My Children” and “One Life to Live,” will leave the network when his contract expires next month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/arts/television/abc-daytime-chief-to-leave-network.html', 'snippet': 'Brian Frons, the ABC executive who angered many soap opera fans this year by canceling “All My Children” and “One Life to Live,” will leave the network when his contract expires next month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brian Frons, the ABC executive who angered many soap opera fans this year by canceling “All My Children” and “One Life to Live,” will leave the network when his contract expires next month. The Disney/ABC Television Group said that Vicki Dummer, ABC’s senior vice president for current series and specials, would take over Mr. Frons’s responsibilities for daytime programming. The soap opera “General Hospital” and talk shows like “The View” will be incorporated into Times Square Studios, a division of the ABC/Disney Television Group that will manage and create daytime and syndicated shows. Ms. Dummer’s title will be executive vice president of Times Square Studios. “General Hospital,” which has an uncertain fate as ABC’s remaining soap opera, looks like the odd one out at Times Square Studios: a news release on Friday said the division would mostly develop “nonscripted lifestyle and health” programming. Along with “The View,” it will manage the two shows that are replacing the soap operas — “The Chew” and “The Revolution” — as well as the game show “Who Wants to Be a Millionaire” and Katie Couric’s forthcoming syndicated talk show. “The Chew,” which features a panel of five chefs and foodies who cook and drink and chat, began in September in place of “All My Children”; “The Revolution,” described by ABC as a talk show “about health and lifestyle transformations,” is to begin in January in place of “One Life to Live.” Mr. Frons has headed the daytime hours of ABC since 2002. “While my decision to try something new was not arrived at easily, nine years is a long time in television terms,” he said in a statement.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ABC Daytime Chief to Leave Network', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ABC Daytime Chief To Leave Network', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T23:03:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/baca3a20-56b7-5831-adb6-cae520727ad1', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baca3a20-56b7-5831-adb6-cae520727ad1'}, {'abstract': 'The “30 Rock” comedian and Tony Award winner Jane Krakowski is set to return to the stage for a one-woman show as part of Town Hall’s 91st anniversary season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/arts/music/town-hall-season-is-announced.html', 'snippet': 'The “30 Rock” comedian and Tony Award winner Jane Krakowski is set to return to the stage for a one-woman show as part of Town Hall’s 91st anniversary season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “30 Rock” comedian and Tony Award winner Jane Krakowski, below,  is set to return to the stage for a one-woman show as part of Town Hall’s 91st anniversary season, the theater announced. Ms. Krakowski will perform a range of musical numbers on June 8 in a season that will also include solo concerts from Lea Salonga (“Miss Saigon”) on May 4 and the Grammy Award winner Loudon Wainwright III on May 18. Other highlights include the musical-comedy troupe Capitol Steps (March 23) and a double bill of Judy Collins and Madeleine Peyroux on June 1. Information: the-townhall-nyc.org.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Town Hall Season Is Announced', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Town Hall Season Is Announced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krakowski, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-04T23:05:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title'