lue': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T05:42:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Botta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Botta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8deaeb58-8390-573d-bd95-142b4fc9b97b', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8deaeb58-8390-573d-bd95-142b4fc9b97b'}, {'abstract': 'This week on “The Good Wife,” the affair between Will and Alicia, which had made for tepid viewing, proved compelling when it came to the breakup. But some other turns may have been a little too perfect.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/good-wife-watch-the-end-of-the-affair/', 'snippet': 'This week on “The Good Wife,” the affair between Will and Alicia, which had made for tepid viewing, proved compelling when it came to the breakup. But some other turns may have been a little too perfect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There had been some dread in the “Good Wife” community before Sunday night’s episode, “Parenting Made Easy” (otherwise known as the kidnap episode), but it turned out there was nothing to worry about. Grace’s temporary disappearance — a plot device to crystallize all the troubles in Alicia’s life, bringing together Alicia, Peter, Will, Kalinda and both of the children (only Jackie was absent, conspicuously) — was suspenseful but not overplayed; no sharks were jumped.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Good Wife’ Watch: The End of the Affair', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T06:01:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/972d7ba5-4fc6-5c3c-a4ca-35ed4cb207a6', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/972d7ba5-4fc6-5c3c-a4ca-35ed4cb207a6'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney’s deceptive ad about President Obama’s economic policies is only the latest moment in a long history of political sleight-of-hand that has its roots in the 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/the-reinvention-of-political-morality/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney’s deceptive ad about President Obama’s economic policies is only the latest moment in a long history of political sleight-of-hand that has its roots in the 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Struggling to justify a recent television spot that reached new heights of deception, a top operative in Mitt Romney’s 2012 presidential campaign put it plainly, while insisting on anonymity:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Reinvention of Political Morality', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeLay, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T06:07:06+0000', 'document_type': 'artic