dit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/05/nyregion/05southbury1/05southbury1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/05/nyregion/05southbury1/05southbury1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Graying Town Builds a Life Beyond Bingo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Graying Town Builds a Life Beyond Bingo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:13:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Applebome', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Applebome', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aa505ae-7524-57af-aa8a-5b41abc49e2a', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aa505ae-7524-57af-aa8a-5b41abc49e2a'}, {'abstract': 'The Packers remain undefeated. L.S.U. and Alabama will play again. And the Mets continue to be the Mets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/sports/leading-off-right-back-where-we-started.html', 'snippet': 'The Packers remain undefeated. L.S.U. and Alabama will play again. And the Mets continue to be the Mets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For all the action of the weekend, all the dramatic games and frantic finishes, today feels a bit anticlimactic. Because for everything that felt like a crazy twist and turn, we are essentially left back where we started. The Packers are still undefeated. The Bowl Championship Series is still designed to keep the power conference teams on top. Fighting is still a part of the N.H.L., although the more people who read about Derek Boogaard’s heart-rending fate, the less defensible that is, and the Mets’ fortunes are still sinking like a rock.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Right Back Where We Started', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Right Back Where We Started', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Alabama', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana State University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8db70dd2-f5e5-5e0e-8a29-8b4d6ada4c93', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8db70dd2-f5e5-5e0e-8a29-8b4d6ada4c93'}, {'abstract': 'Why the Mets may not miss Jose Reyes, and why the Marlins are taking a very big risk.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/at-106-million-mets-were-wise-to-pass-on-reyes/', 'snippet': 'Why the Mets may not miss Jose Reyes, and why the Marlins are taking a very big risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After news broke Sunday that the Miami Marlins and Jose Reyes had agreed on a contract, Mets General Manager Sandy Alderson made a revelation of his own: the team had lost $70 million last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At $106 Million, Mets Were Wise to Pass on Reyes', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALDERSON, SANDY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Heath', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crawford, Carl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reyes, Jose', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Florida Marlins', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:26:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e83ef122-fad0-5a19-bf71-f47cb78a583e', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e83ef122-fad0-5a19-bf71-f47cb78a583e'}, {'abstract': 'Howard Beck, the N.B.A. writer for The Times, will answer questions from readers.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/have-n-b-a-questions-howard-beck-has-answers/', 'snippet': 'Howard Beck, the N.B.A. writer for The Times, will answer questions from readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are the Heat the favorites for the N.B.A. championship this season? Will Chris Paul stay in New Orleans? Do the Nuggets have anyone or anything left? Howard Beck, the N.B.A. reporter for The New York Times, will answer questions from readers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have N.B.A. Questions? Howard Beck Has Answers', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beck, Howard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2cff573-83da-5186-a475-36cbf333047b', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2cff573-83da-5186-a475-36cbf333047b'}, {'abstract': 'A trip to the nation’s capital for a night of politicians doing stand-up proved the old truism: Washington is rather humorless.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/senators-and-congressmen-all-trying-for-a-laugh/', 'snippet': 'A trip to the nation’s capital for a night of politicians doing stand-up proved the old truism: Washington is rather humorless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I was working on my argument about political parody on “Saturday Night Live,” I traveled to our nation’s capital to see if the humor of our political elite was also suffering from malaise. Wednesday was the 18th annual Funniest Celebrity in Washington Contest, a benefit whose proceeds go to the Susan G. Komen for the Cure, which fights breast cancer. The evening featured a bipartisan bill of stand-up performances by politicians, journalists and lobbyists who all seem to agree on one thing: People from Washington are not funny.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senators and Congressmen, All Trying for a Laugh', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norquist, Grover G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'West, Allen B', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICANS FOR TAX REFORM', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Politico, The', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfe4ac78-8479-5111-8f0b-292219a9740c', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfe4ac78-8479-5111-8f0b-292219a9740c'}, {'abstract': 'The Commodity Futures Trading Commission’s new rule will limit how the brokerage industry can invest customer money, largely barring firms from using client funds to buy foreign sovereign debt.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/regulators-approve-mf-global-rule/', 'snippet': 'The Commodity Futures Trading Commission’s new rule will limit how the brokerage industry can invest customer money, largely barring firms from using client funds to buy foreign sovereign debt.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:04 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MF Global’s Collapse Spurs Curbs on Brokers', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MF Global Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:32:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb55a54d-2372-5063-8c85-f3be9bfe03ad', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb55a54d-2372-5063-8c85-f3be9bfe03ad'}, {'abstract': 'Bubbles always seem obvious in retrospect, but the next one probably won’t look much like any backwards-looking models for bubble spotting that we develop today.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/the-risky-sport-of-bubble-spotting/', 'snippet': 'Bubbles always seem obvious in retrospect, but the next one probably won’t look much like any backwards-looking models for bubble spotting that we develop today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Richards is a certified financial planner in Park City, Utah. His sketches are archived here on the Bucks blog. His new book, “The Behavior Gap,” will be out in January.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Risky Sport of Bubble Spotting', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fear (Emotion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:41:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9045f852-829b-5a12-a09e-7c2e70a88a93', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9045f852-829b-5a12-a09e-7c2e70a88a93'}, {'abstract': 'The director of the Yamaguchi prefecture’s traffic police called the convoy of exotic cars “a gathering of narcissists.”', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/in-japan-8-ferraris-reportedly-involved-in-sunday-smash-up/', 'snippet': 'The director of the Yamaguchi prefecture’s traffic police called the convoy of exotic cars “a gathering of narcissists.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of supercars traveling on rain-slicked roads in Yamaguchi Prefecture, Japan, were involved in a crash Sunday, in what many have called one of the most expensive automotive accidents ever recorded.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Japan, 8 Ferraris Reportedly Involved in Sunday Smash-Up', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ferrari SpA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T16:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5bd2b46-7a8c-5e55-9e07-12c67a6b589c', 'word_c