'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Curling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T19:13:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/37013d7a-e87b-5d2b-ac40-ed6f535c3e8a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/37013d7a-e87b-5d2b-ac40-ed6f535c3e8a'}, {'abstract': 'Through Week 13 last season, 18.8 percent of kickoffs resulted in touchbacks. This year, the figure is 45.9, above the 30 percent the N.F.L. anticipated when it moved kickoffs forward 5 yards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/sports/football/rule-change-leads-to-more-touchbacks-than-anticipated-nfl-fast-forward.html', 'snippet': 'Through Week 13 last season, 18.8 percent of kickoffs resulted in touchbacks. This year, the figure is 45.9, above the 30 percent the N.F.L. anticipated when it moved kickoffs forward 5 yards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Green Bay Packers’ Randall Cobb returned a kickoff 108 yards for a touchdown in the N.F.L. season opener in September, it played out as a rejoinder to the hand-wringing over a rules change that seemed destined to nearly eliminate returns by moving the kick to the 35-yard line from the 30. The adjustment was made to improve player safety — few hits are as explosive as the ones delivered at full speed on kickoff returns — and the N.F.L. figured it was willing to sacrifice the thrill of a possible highlight for fewer concussions. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/sports/football/06forward-pic/06forward-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201