pe_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3f0b48f5-8978-5c1b-8351-2f45aaa98c8a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3f0b48f5-8978-5c1b-8351-2f45aaa98c8a'}, {'abstract': 'Until now, it has been hard to see how to make individualized education affordable. But advances in technology may provide a path to this goal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/science/daphne-koller-technology-as-a-passport-to-personalized-education.html', 'snippet': 'Until now, it has been hard to see how to make individualized education affordable. But advances in technology may provide a path to this goal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our education system is in a state of crisis. Among developed countries, the United States is 55th in quality rankings of elementary math and science education, 20th in high school completion rate and 27th in the fraction of college students receiving undergraduate degrees in science or engineering.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD2/06HEAD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 256, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD2/06HEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD2/06HEAD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Death Knell for the Lecture: Technology as a Passport to Personalized Education', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Death Knell for the Lecture: Technology as a Passport to Personalized Education', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T20:53:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daphne Koller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daphne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02a23c53-5d7a-561e-a716-511772a2538c', 'word_count': 1036, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02a23c53-5d7a-561e-a716-511772a2538c'}, {'abstract': 'As many as 1 percent of the votes cast in New York State elections last year were voided by election officials because people unintentionally cast their ballots for more than one candidate, according to a study being released this week.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/study-finds-voters-erred-often-in-using-new-machines/', 'snippet': 'As many as 1 percent of the votes cast in New York State elections last year were voided by election officials because people unintentionally cast their ballots for more than one candidate, according to a study being released this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As many as 60,000 of the votes cast in New York State elections last year were voided because people unintentionally cast their ballots for more than one candidate, according to a study being released this week. The excess-voting was highest in predominantly black and Hispanic neighborhoods, including two Bronx election districts where 40 percent of the votes for governor were disqualified.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Finds Voters Erred Often in Using New Machines', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Machines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brennan Center for Justice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T20:56:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33fad7b8-e7d1-584e-af76-704a49536ea1', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33fad7b8-e7d1-584e-af76-704a49536ea1'}, {'abstract': 'From scheduling conference rooms to rooting out incipient tumors, computers that can go to the information that we care greatly about', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/science/drew-endy-better-computing-for-the-things-we-care-about-most.html', 'snippet': 'From scheduling conference rooms to rooting out incipient tumors, computers that can go to the information that we care greatly about', 'lead_paragraph': 'The value of computers is often measured with terms like gigahertz, petaflop and exabyte — the speed, scale and efficiency with which they perform computations. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD4/06HEAD4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 257, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD4/06HEAD4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06