results in the Russian capital, chanting, among other things, “Putin’s a Thief!”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Updated | Tuesday | 8:05 p.m. As my colleagues Michael Schwirtz and David Herszenhorn reports from Moscow, on Monday evening thousands of demonstrators gathered to protest election results in the Russian capital, chanting, among other things, “Putin’s a Thief!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chants of ‘Putin’s a Thief!’ Ring Out in Moscow', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:20:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ad209c0-1734-5ab2-a487-34dfd8b155b6', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ad209c0-1734-5ab2-a487-34dfd8b155b6'}, {'abstract': 'A Tchaikovsky recital presented by the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center was a mixture of rising and established artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/arts/music/chamber-music-society-of-lincoln-center-at-kaplan-penthouse-review.html', 'snippet': 'A Tchaikovsky recital presented by the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center was a mixture of rising and established artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lincoln Center doesn’t have a permanent performance hall quite like Le Poisson Rouge or Galapagos Art Space, where you sit at tables and can order wine (or harder stuff) while you listen to the music. But the center and its constituent organizations do what they can with the smaller, more malleable rooms in the Rose Building, which looms over the Juilliard School.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tchaikovsky Rendered Anew, Steadily and Elegantly', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tchaikovsky Rendered Anew, Steadily and Elegantly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:21:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e5ac118-591d-57ac-9e43-c65740167592', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e5ac118-591d-57ac-9e43-c65740167592'}, {'abstract': 'Laurent Gbagbo, who nearly pushed his country into civil war after refusing to accept his defeat in an election last year, faces charges of crimes against humanity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/world/africa/ivory-coasts-laurent-gbagbo-appears-before-hague-court.html', 'snippet': 'Laurent Gbagbo, who nearly pushed his country into civil war after refusing to accept his defeat in an election last year, faces charges of crimes against humanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Seven months after artillery fire from United Nations and French troops drove him from his fortified compound, Laurent Gbagbo, the former president of Ivory Coast, appeared in public for the first time on Monday, as he faced courtroom cameras and a panel of international judges in The Hague.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/world/HAGUE/HAGUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/06/world/HAGUE/HAGUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/world/HAGUE/HAGUE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/world/HAGUE/HAGUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/world/HAGUE/HAGUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Former Ivory Coast Strongman Appears at Hague Court', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ivory Coast’s Ex-Leader Faces Court at Hague', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ivory Coast', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ouattara, Alassane D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gbagbo, Laurent', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:23:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marlise Simons and Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marlise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c5eac88-7756-57e6-ab10-79489fe83266', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c5eac88-7756-57e6-ab10-79489fe83266'}, {'abstract': 'The Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra’s staging of Handel’s “Messiah” found symbols in Americana.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/arts/music/messiah-by-the-pittsburgh-symphony-orchestra-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra’s staging of Handel’s “Messiah” found symbols in Americana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PITTSBURGH — Since the elements of oratorio — recitative, aria, chorus, sinfonia — and its overall scale resemble those of opera, and since Handel was eminently fluent in both, it might seem a simple matter to stage a Handel oratorio, and periodic attempts have been made. Unlike most of his operas, after all, the oratorios are in English.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/messiah-1/messiah-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/messiah-1/messiah-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/messiah-1/messiah-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/messiah-1/messiah-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/messiah-1/messiah-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Handel, Illustrated With Americana', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Handel, Illustrated With Americana', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Honeck, Manfred', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh (Pa)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:25:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b127944-3671-5822-9332-cf4111b766b0', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b127944-3671-5822-9332-cf4111b766b0'}, {'abstract': 'The N.F.L. has a “Music Boot Camp” to train players for life after football.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/after-playing-music-as-a-career-option-for-n-f-l-veterans/', 'snippet': 'The N.F.L. has a “Music Boot Camp” to train players for life after football.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many football players struggle to find meaningful work after they stop playing. The N.F.L.’s latest attempt to help them develop a post-retirement career will take place in February when the 20 current and former football players will join the first-ever Business of Music Boot Camp.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Playing, Music as a Career Option for N.F.L. Veterans', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Clive', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vincent, Troy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:28:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd79811b-f18f-5c32-9fb0-e6b840b31b66', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd79811b-f18f-5c32-9fb0-e6b840b31b66'}, {'abstract': 'Open platforms, low development costs and a huge and growing market put China in line to emerge as a leader in computing innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/science/kai-fu-lee-china-is-poised-for-an-it-golden-age.html', 'snippet': 'Open platforms, low development costs and a huge and growing market put China in line to emerge as a leader in computing innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Chinese universities graduate more than 600,000 engineering students a year. China has consistently placed at or near the top of programming competitions. And while we have not seen China become a leader in information technology and computing, I expect that this will change in the coming decade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Is Poised for an I.T. Golden Age', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tencent', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:28:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kai-Fu Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kai-fu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b26024ea-5c64-5bac-af64-418218786172', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b26024ea-5c64-5bac-af64-418218786172'}, {'abstract': 'On Monday, President Obama gathered 12 higher-education leaders at the White House to discuss ways to make college more accessible and affordable for all.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/05/president-gathers-college-leaders-to-discuss-access-and-affordability/', 'snippet': 'On Monday, President Obama gathered 12 higher-education leaders at the White House to discuss ways to make college more accessible and affordable for all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Continuing his calls for college affordability, President Obama met privately with university presidents and chancellors at the White House on Monday. According to The Chronicle of Higher Education, the group gathered to discuss opportunities to bring down “overall campus costs” across the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'President Gathers College Leaders to Discuss Access and Affordability', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35e8622e-7007-5ad6-a8fa-d1b57b55af5e', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35e8622e-7007-5ad6-a8fa-d1b57b55af5e'}, {'abstract': 'Computer scientists may have the best skills — they can use machines, algorithms and the wisdom of the crowd — to fight cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/science/david-patterson-enlist-computer-scientists-in-cancer-fight.html', 'snippet': 'Computer scientists may have the best skills — they can use machines, algorithms and the wisdom of the crowd — to fight cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The war against cancer is increasingly moving into cyberspace. Computer scientists may have the best skills to fight cancer in the next decade — and they should be signing up in droves.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD6/06HEAD6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 257, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD6/06HEAD6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/science/06HEAD6/06HEAD6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Computer Scientists May Have What It Takes to Help Cure Cancer', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Computer Scientists May Have What It Takes to Help Cure Cancer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:32:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By DAVID PATTERSON', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'PATTERSON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab9a2409-f55d-5b47-9adb-21fd52529fa6', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab9a2409-f55d-5b47-9adb-21fd52529fa6'}, {'abstract': 'In “Loves Goes to Buildings on Fire,” Will Hermes has isolated the years 1973 to 1977 as a crucial, if sometimes awkward, period of transition in New York and in American music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/books/love-goes-to-buildings-on-fire-by-will-hermes-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Loves Goes to Buildings on Fire,” Will Hermes has isolated the years 1973 to 1977 as a crucial, if sometimes awkward, period of transition in New York and in American music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You have to admire Will Hermes’s “Love Goes to Buildings on Fire” — the reference is to the first single by Talking Heads — for refusing to oversell the importance of its subject. True, the subtitle calls the period from 1973 to 1977 “Five Years in New York that Changed Music Forever,” but that’s actually a modest claim: what five years of the 20th century didn’t change music forever? The five years previous, for instance, saw “Bitches Brew,” “Abbey Road,” “Exile on Main Street,” the Velvet Underground and James Brown’s “Sex Machine.” The subsequent five produced “Off the Wall” and “Thriller”; hip-hop’s first hit, the Sugar Hill Gang’s “Rapper’s Delight”; and the debuts of R.E.M., the Replacements and Hüsker Dü. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/BOOK-1/BOOK-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/BOOK-1/BOOK-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/BOOK-1/BOOK-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/BOOK-1/BOOK-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/arts/BOOK-1/BOOK-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Dreamers Were Breaking the Music Apart', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Dreamers Were Breaking the Music Apart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hermes, Will', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-05T23:38:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': 