sts-breach-nuclear-plants.html', 'snippet': 'Activists from the environmental group Greenpeace infiltrated two nuclear power plants on Monday, and breached or tried to breach security at least two other nuclear sites, authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Activists from the environmental group Greenpeace infiltrated two nuclear power plants on Monday, and breached or tried to breach security at least two other nuclear sites, authorities said. Nine activists were arrested at a site in Nogent-sur-Seine, southeast of Paris, and two in Cruas, in the south. Greenpeace called security measures at the plants “insufficient,” and called for French leaders to shut down the country’s 58 nuclear reactors, which account for more than three quarters of the electricity consumed in France. French officials acknowledged a need to reinforce security around nuclear facilities. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France: Activists Breach Nuclear Plants', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'France: Activists Breach Nuclear Plants', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenpeace', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T06:07:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Sayare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sayare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/101e9f99-54be-5784-b711-b6a0a6c529bc', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/101e9f99-54be-5784-b711-b6a0a6c529bc'}, {'abstract': 'If we had a supercomputer that could predict the future, we would tell what’s next for technology. Then again, if the past is any guide, the predictions would certainly be wrong. A special report of articles and essays takes a many-faceted look at a set of technologies that are changing the world in more ways than could ever have been foreseen.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/the-future-of-computing/', 'snippet': 'If we had a supercomputer that could predict the future, we would tell what’s next for technology. Then again, if the past is any guide, the predictions would certainly be wrong. A special report of articles and essays takes a many-faceted look at...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A special report on the future of technology:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Future of Computing', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supercomputers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dyson, George', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stephenson, Neal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T06:11:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b39d0bf-4c88-5085-8130-5a8b01099564', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b39d0bf-4c88-5085-8130-5a8b01099564'}, {'abstract': 'The leading candidate in next year’s presidential election drew ridicule from critics when, at a book fair, he was unable to completely name three books that had influenced him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/world/americas/mexico-presidential-candidate-is-asked-to-name-influential-books-oops.html', 'snippet': 'The leading candidate in next year’s presidential election drew ridicule from critics when, at a book fair, he was unable to completely name three books that had influenced him.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The leading candidate in next year’s presidential election drew ridicule from critics over the weekend when, in response to a question at Latin America’s biggest book fair, he was unable to completely name three books that had influenced him. The stumble played into the image that the candidate, Enrique Peña Nieto of the Institutional Revolutionary Party, has tended more to his looks than his books. He mentioned “parts of” the Bible, erred on the author of another book and was not able to come up with a third. On Monday, the gaffe was compounded when his teenage daughter sent out a Twitter message calling his critics “a bunch of idiots who form part of the proletariat.” Mr. Peña Nieto apologized for the tweet, calling it an “emotional reaction.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Presidential Candidate Is Asked to Name Influential Books. Oops!', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Presidential Candidate Is Asked To Name Influential Books. Oops!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pena Nieto, Enrique', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Institutional Revolutionary Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T06:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karla Zabludovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zabludovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c516ba7-1d96-5811-b40b-6455654c038c', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c516ba7-1d96-5811-b40b-6455654c038c'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on December 6, 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/pageoneplus/corrections-december-6-2011.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on December 6, 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: December 6', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T06:58:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b71ec54-89bd-5d32-88e6-764b0fc2663f', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b71ec54-89bd-5d32-88e6-764b0fc2663f'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/news-quiz-dec-6-2011/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | Dec. 6, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T07:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenan Christiansen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christiansen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ca6c0cd-1802-5a81-ad04-43629eb625cc', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ca6c0cd-1802-5a81-ad04-43629eb625cc'}, {'abstract': 'Madison Square Garden was buzzing on Monday night as the Rangers fell to Toronto, 4-2, with news of an N.H.L. realignment, a camera crew from HBO starting round-the-clock coverage of the Rangers, and, of course, the requisite brawls.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/rangers-streak-ends-on-a-busy-night-for-hockey/', 'snippet': 'Madison Square Garden was buzzing on Monday night as the Rangers fell to Toronto, 4-2, with news of an N.H.L. realignment, a camera crew from HBO starting round-the-clock coverage of the Rangers, and, of course, the requisite brawls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was an odd evening at Madison Square Garden Monday. So many things converging at once, but what went practically unnoticed was perhaps the biggest thing of all – the news that Derek Boogaard’s brain had extensive damage from chronic traumatic encephalopathy when he died last spring of an accidental drug overdose, that Boogaard’s father reported seeing several pill bottles of painkillers prescribed by Rangers doctors at his drug-addicted son’s Manhattan apartment, and that, according to Boogaard’s father, Boogaard once received a call from the Rangers warning him of a random drug test coming in four days’ time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rangers’ Streak Ends on a Busy Night for Hockey', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boogaard, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T08:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7524421-2276-53cc-9e68-13bee084aa29', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7524421-2276-53cc-9e68-13bee084aa29'}, {'abstract': 'On Dec. 6, 1923, the first presidential address was broadcast on the radio.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/dec-6-1923-calvin-coolidge-delivers-first-presidential-address-on-radio/', 'snippet': 'On Dec. 6, 1923, the first presidential address was broadcast on the radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 6, 1923, the first presidential address was broadcast on the radio. President Calvin Coolidge delivered what is now known as the State of the Union address to a joint session of Congress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dec. 6, 1923 | Calvin Coolidge Delivers First Presidential Address on Radio', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T09:02:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/beccb8b4-3a24-5348-8523-828940a432e9', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/beccb8b4-3a24-5348-8523-828940a432e9'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the best antonym for a word used in context?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/test-yourself-english-dec-6-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the best antonym for a word used in context?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the article “A Connecticut Town Adjusts to a Graying Population.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Dec. 6, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T09:15:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad494721-703f-5965-9168-b9f262f70bdc', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad494721-703f-5965-9168-b9f262f70bdc'}, {'abstract': 'Bombers struck Shiite religious observances on Tuesday, in the first such sectarian attacks since the fall of the Taliban.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/world/asia/suicide-bombers-attack-shiite-worshipers-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Bombers struck Shiite religious observances on Tuesday, in the first such sectarian attacks since the fall of the Taliban.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — A Pakistan-based extremist group claimed responsibility for a series of coordinated bombings aimed at Afghan Shiites on Tuesday, in what many feared was an attempt to further destabilize Afghanistan by adding a new dimension of strife to a country that, though battered by a decade of war, has been free of sectarian conflict. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/world/asia/AFGHAN/AFGHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/07/world/asia/AFGHAN/AFGHAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/world/asia/AFGHAN/AFGHAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/world/asia/AFGHAN/AFGHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/07/world/asia/AFGHAN/AFGHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rare Attacks on Shiites Kill Scores in Afghanistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sectarian Attack Kills at least 60 in Afghan Cities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lashkar-e-Jhangvi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kandahar (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mazar-i-Sharif (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shiite Muslims', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T09:51:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rod Nordland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nordland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e25d338-dc6a-53f8-956d-0f7b6305fd9d', 'word_count': 1206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e25d338-dc6a-53f8-956d-0f7b6305fd9d'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | How can people visit the remains of the Titanic?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/visit-the-titantic-on-its-100th-birthday/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | How can people visit the remains of the Titanic?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN did the Titanic sink?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Visit the Titanic on Its 100th Birthday', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T09:57:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah Griffee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griffee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fe050e4-b653-5f69-9a2c-cdfb35bf863d', 'word_count': 73, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fe050e4-b653-5f69-9a2c-cdfb35bf863d'}, {'abstract': 'André Cypriano wanted to show the dignity of the people who live in Rocinha, one of Rio de Janeiro’s largest slums. Fernanda Santos, who grew up nearby, talked to Mr. Cypriano about his experience photographing the area.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/in-brazil-finding-dignity-in-horror/', 'snippet': 'André Cypriano wanted to show the dignity of the people who live in Rocinha, one of Rio de Janeiro’s largest slums. Fernanda Santos, who grew up nearby, talked to Mr. Cypriano about his experience photographing the area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I must have been 11, maybe 12 years old. I was staring out the big glass window in the big apartment I lived in Rio de Janeiro, looking at homes made out of wood and broken pieces of plastic and, sometimes, clay bricks, impossibly perched on the hillside ahead. It was a leg of one of the city’s largest slums, Rocinha, which had draped over the mountain’s top, crossing from one neighborhood to the next.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Brazil, Finding Dignity in Horror', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salgado, Sebastiao', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T10:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fernanda Santos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fernanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb7efb3-a6c6-5950-a993-8c7024a526e0', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb7efb3-a6c6-5950-a993-8c7024a526e0'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Tell us what you think about the candidates for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/which-republican-candidate-will-win-the-presidential-nomination/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Tell us what you think about the candidates for the Republican presidential nomination.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Newt Gingrich is ahead of the other potential Republican presidential nominees in recent polls. After Herman Cain suspended his campaign, many of his supporters rallied behind Mr. Gingrich. However, eight other candidates  remain in the field.  Who do you think will eventually win the nomination?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Republican Candidate Will Win the Presidential Nomination?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T10:04:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susannah Griffee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susannah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griffee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/918fd8ff-5f72-5f11-bdf0-85a7c15be33d', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/918fd8ff-5f72-5f11-bdf0-85a7c15be33d'}, {'abstract': 'If the wealthy  are not taxed more, then everyone else will have to be, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/raising-taxes-on-the-rich-not-whether-but-how/', 'snippet': 'If the wealthy  are not taxed more, then everyone else will have to be, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul. He is the author of the forthcoming book “The Benefit and the Burden.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Raising Taxes on the Rich: Not Whether, but How', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congressional Budget Office', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/228cfd31-21ac-55fd-b42b-2eee21603b11', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/228cfd31-21ac-55fd-b42b-2eee21603b11'}, {'abstract': 'Ray Nicosia, director of testing integrity for the Educational Testing Service, answers questions about cheating and security measures surrounding the SAT and ACT.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/questions-for-the-sat-top-cop/', 'snippet': 'Ray Nicosia, director of testing integrity for the Educational Testing Service, answers questions about cheating and security measures surrounding the SAT and ACT.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In response to the standardized-test cheating scandal on New York’s Long Island — which began with arrests related to the SAT, and has since come to encompass the ACT, too — I initiated an e-mail exchange last week with Ray Nicosia of the Educational Testing Service. Mr. Nicosia is director of testing integrity for E.T.S., which administers the SAT. What follows are edited excerpts from our electronic conversation. (At the end of this post, readers will find contact information for Mr. Nicosia’s office as well as a short cut to post comments on this blog in response to his remarks.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions for the SAT’s Top Cop', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e8f550c-6c25-5a90-bc27-a712b10d627f', 'word_count': 1463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e8f550c-6c25-5a90-bc27-a712b10d627f'}, {'abstract': 'Even in a bellwether state like California, availability of hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles may exceed the capacity of hydrogen filling stations.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/in-u-s-hydrogen-cars-may-line-up-with-few-places-to-fill-up/', 'snippet': 'Even in a bellwether state like California, availability of hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles may exceed the capacity of hydrogen filling stations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week at the Tokyo auto show, Toyota unveiled its FCV-R concept. According to a Toyota spokesman, John Hanson, the vehicle was designed to give the public an impression of the hydrogen-powered fuel-cell sedan it expected to make available in 2015. With a projected range of more than 400 miles, a production version of the FCV-R would surely dodge the range-anxiety stigma that afflicts battery electric vehicles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In U.S., Hydrogen Cars May Line Up With Few Places to Fill Up', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim'