m/2011/12/06/the-early-word-gingrich/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Gingrich', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd9e6258-f0c8-55a0-8140-01a5808d4c11', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd9e6258-f0c8-55a0-8140-01a5808d4c11'}, {'abstract': 'Some states are working hard to move elderly residents from nursing homes into the community.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/an-exodus-from-nursing-homes/', 'snippet': 'Some states are working hard to move elderly residents from nursing homes into the community.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve helped an older relative move into a nursing home in the past year, you’ve likely heard a nurse or administrator pose The Question: “Do you want to talk to someone about the possibility of returning to the community?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Slow Exodus From Nursing Homes', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab6ced52-99b8-5eb3-ab15-04ea5da2e00d', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab6ced52-99b8-5eb3-ab15-04ea5da2e00d'}, {'abstract': 'Manish Sabharwal, chairman of the temporary-employment agency TeamLease answers questions posed by India Ink readers.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/manish-sabharwal-answers-readers-questions/', 'snippet': 'Manish Sabharwal, chairman of the temporary-employment agency TeamLease answers questions posed by India Ink readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'India Ink asked Manish Sabharwal, chairman of the temporary-employment agency TeamLease, who was profiled in a recent New York Times article, to answer readers’ questions about the Indian labor market and his company’s philosophy. Readers posed tough queries about his business model, his competitors and the hurdles that employers and job seekers face in India. The questions and Mr. Sabharwal’s answers have been edited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manish Sabharwal Answers Readers’ Questions', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:27:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28eefe49-9e1f-553d-8858-24dcd8f20380', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28eefe49-9e1f-553d-8858-24dcd8f20380'}, {'abstract': 'How to finance a blockbuster film, political spending in Iowa, MTV looks for a “Jersey Shore” in West Virginia and rethinking Donald Trump as a debate moderator.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/the-breakfast-meeting-dec-6/', 'snippet': 'How to finance a blockbuster film, political spending in Iowa, MTV looks for a “Jersey Shore” in West Virginia and rethinking Donald Trump as a debate moderator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The production of “Cloud Atlas,” a film version of the David Mitchell novel that has a $100 million budget, is an example of how sophisticated filmmakers “are turning to a globe-straddling independent finance system for their most expensive projects,” rather than American studios, Nicholas Kulish and Michael Cieply report. The talent connected to the project includes Andy and Lana Wachowski, the American-born team behind the “Matrix” movies, and Tom Hanks and Halle Berry. But investors from China, South Korea, Singapore and beyond contributed roughly $35 million, without which the film could not have been made. German subsidies account for $18 million more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting, Dec. 6', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanks, Tom', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Larry', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stephenson, Neal', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Times', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Virginia', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T11:33:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdc97575-a352-56f7-8a48-f37a309ec88b', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdc97575-a352-56f7-8a48-f37a309ec88b'}, {'abstract': 'A survey by The New York Times/CBS News shows that likely Republican caucus-goers are fairly split over whether Newt Gingrich or Mitt Romney have a better chance of beating President Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/new-iowa-poll-who-can-beat-obama/', 'snippet': 'A survey by The New York Times/CBS News shows that likely Republican caucus-goers are fairly split over whether Newt Gingrich or Mitt Romney have a better chance of beating President Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just weeks before the first votes are cast in the 2012 presidential campaign, likely Republican caucus-goers in Iowa are closely divided over which candidate has the best chance of defeating President Obama, the latest New York Times/CBS News poll finds. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Iowa Poll: Who Can Beat Obama?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': ''