rir Square protesters’ eyes. When Shennawy’s photo came out, the city gave a collective gasp at his youth. He looks about 15. Is it really possible that that those rosy cheeks were fed by such icy blood?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clinging to the Egyptian Army', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T16:29:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Graeme Wood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Graeme', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4821c9c-77d7-5def-903e-d0964014a7cb', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4821c9c-77d7-5def-903e-d0964014a7cb'}, {'abstract': 'Iraq is no longer the Iraq of 2003 to 2008, when Humvees and armored convoys were omnipresent, dominating every highway they traversed, and demanding priority.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/on-the-streets-of-baghdad-an-invisible-american-withdrawl/', 'snippet': 'Iraq is no longer the Iraq of 2003 to 2008, when Humvees and armored convoys were omnipresent, dominating every highway they traversed, and demanding priority.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — Only a few miles to the west of here, Camp Victory, the vast American military headquarters, was handed over to the Iraqis just a few days ago and the remaining American bases, soldiers and equipment will follow soon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Streets of Baghdad, an Invisible American Withdrawal', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T16:33:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c5e6f07-760d-50de-a498-152884bd3d98', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c5e6f07-760d-50de-a498-152884bd3d98'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott discusses paranoia in John Frankenheimer’s political thriller.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/critics-picks-video-the-manchurian-candidate/', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott discusses paranoia in John Frankenheimer’s political thriller.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The democratic process can often seem like the theater of the absurd,” A. O. Scott says in this week’s Critics’ Picks video, which looks back at John Frankenheimer’s political thriller “The Manchurian Candidate.” The film tells the story of Raymond Shaw (Laurence Harvey),  a soldier who is given the Congressional Medal of Honor for his heroism in Korea, but more about what happened to him in battle soon emerges. Shaw and his troop were kidnapped and brainwashed by Chinese and Soviet Communists who sent Shaw back to the United States as a pawn for their plans of world domination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks Video: ‘The Manchurian Candidate’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frankenheimer, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T16:33:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2463eed-829b-50e6-a770-bfbf6726039f', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2463eed-829b-50e6-a770-bfbf6726039f'}, {'abstract': 'Web site WhiteFence aims to help you find better deals for residential services. But can you dodge the upgrade pitch from providers when you try to switch?', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/shopping-for-a-better-deal-on-home-services-with-whitefence/', 'snippet': 'Web site WhiteFence aims to help you find better deals for residential services. But can you dodge the upgrade pitch from providers when you try to switch?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of my least favorite tasks is dealing with utilities and other service providers, like the cable company, to see if I can get a better deal. It’s unpleasant, but something that needs to be done periodically — especially when you notice your monthly bills ticking inexorably upwards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shopping for a Better Deal on Home Services With WhiteFence', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Geographic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_