en to buy them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/web-site-offers-help-getting-deals-on-electronics.html', 'snippet': 'A Web site and app for mobile devices mines transactions to determine the best price on electronics, and when to buy them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF only shopping for electronics were as easy as buying a car.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/DECIDE2/DECIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/DECIDE2/DECIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/DECIDE2/DECIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/DECIDE2/DECIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/DECIDE2/DECIDE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Secret Weapon for Electronics Shoppers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Weapon for Electronics Shoppers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Decide Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:10:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SpecialSections', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damon Darlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Darlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/719dfb43-8aaf-5821-8720-366b5716e418', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/719dfb43-8aaf-5821-8720-366b5716e418'}, {'abstract': 'Adam Sisman’s new biography of the historian Hugh Trevor-Roper, “An Honourable Englishman,” is a rescue mission of sorts: it will not persuade you to admire him, exactly, but you will warm to his bumptious company.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/books/adam-sismans-honourable-englishman-on-hugh-trevor-roper.html', 'snippet': 'Adam Sisman’s new biography of the historian Hugh Trevor-Roper, “An Honourable Englishman,” is a rescue mission of sorts: it will not persuade you to admire him, exactly, but you will warm to his bumptious company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The English historian Hugh Trevor-Roper (1914-2003) is best remembered, if at all, for a humiliating blunder. He was the Oxford and Cambridge scholar who, in 1983, was called upon to authenticate diaries, discovered by the German magazine Stern, said to be Hitler’s. Trevor-Roper didn’t merely pronounce the Hitler diaries genuine. He also declared them “the most important historical discovery of the decade.” He maintained that they were, in an odd comparison, “a scoop of Watergate proportions.” The diaries turned out to be crude forgeries, and his reputation was shattered. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 737, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reputation Staked, and Shattered, on the Forged Diaries of Hitler', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Reputation Staked, and Shattered, On the Forged Diaries of Hitler', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sisman, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hitler, Adolf', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eca0e1c4-bdd4-5fce-be87-b8a74cac390c', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eca0e1c4-bdd4-5fce-be87-b8a74cac390c'}, {'abstract': 'Checking in on the new Travel Channel series “Hidden City.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/channel-surfing-hidden-city/', 'snippet': 'Checking in on the new Travel Channel series “Hidden City.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early part of the century, one of my primary guilty pleasures was A&E’s “City Confidential,” a true-crime show that was the closest thing on television to a dirty little B-movie. There’s been no lack of docu-reality crime shows since it went off the air in 2006, but none has had the same combination of regional flavor, hard-boiled attitude and lurid, literate narration. “City Confidential” made your skin crawl in the best way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Channel Surfing: ‘Hidden City’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:14:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95ac04e9-6a31-5dea-ab25-75a6453fd619', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95ac04e9-6a31-5dea-ab25-75a6453fd619'}, {'abstract': 'By gaining more than 450 books from Marshall Cavendish Children’s Books for an undisclosed sum, Amazon is beginning its first significant push into the category. Amazon says it plans to convert all of the titles into e-books.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/amazon-acquires-childrens-book-titles/', 'snippet': 'By gaining more than 450 books from Marshall Cavendish Children’s Books for an undisclosed sum, Amazon is beginning its first significant push into the category. Amazon says it plans to convert all of the titles into e-books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amazon expanded its publishing ambitions on Tuesday by acquiring more than 450 titles from Marshall Cavendish Children’s Books, a niche publisher.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multime