 and won every half-hour in total viewership and in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic for a ratings sweep. “How I Met Your Mother” started the night off with its largest audience since Jan. 12, 2009, (11.7 million total viewers), followed by “2 Broke Girls” and its largest audience (12.8 million) since its premiere. “Two and a Half Men” was the most-watched program of the night with 15 million viewers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Wins Monday Night Ratings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:23:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/076f4e46-1d96-5bc8-93af-9d012b8f7c67', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/076f4e46-1d96-5bc8-93af-9d012b8f7c67'}, {'abstract': 'Chalk it up to a need for a little sartorial dignity in an overly confessional culture; or to the grass-is-greener yearning for a cold-weather garment when 60-degree temperatures have steadily been reported on the East Coast; or even to Steve Jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow.html', 'snippet': 'Chalk it up to a need for a little sartorial dignity in an overly confessional culture; or to the grass-is-greener yearning for a cold-weather garment when 60-degree temperatures have steadily been reported on the East Coast; or even to Steve Jobs...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chalk it up to a need for a little sartorial dignity in an overly confessional culture; or to the grass-is-greener yearning for a cold-weather garment when 60-degree temperatures have steadily been reported on the East Coast; or even to Steve Jobs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/06/fashion/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6/08noticedslideshow-slide-2VY6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Turtleneck Comes Out of Its Shell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:24:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0c9c4631-93db-5483-96a4-3c6e746e732e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0c9c4631-93db-5483-96a4-3c6e746e732e'}, {'abstract': 'It is discouraging that ten years after the Sept. 11th attacks our communities are still seen as disloyal, foreign, automatically suspect and un-American.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/when-will-we-stop-the-scapegoating/', 'snippet': 'It is discouraging that ten years after the Sept. 11th attacks our communities are still seen as disloyal, foreign, automatically suspect and un-American.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the 2012 presidential election approaches, it has been difficult for me to listen to the political scapegoating of Muslims and those perceived to be Muslims. A decade ago, as an attorney at the Department of Justice, I worked with colleagues in the civil rights division to address the unprecedented backlash in the wake of the 9/11 attacks. We investigated complaints of brutal hate crimes in neighborhoods, vandalism at places of worship, bullying at schools, and discrimination in the workplace aimed primarily at those who traced their origins to South Asia and the Middle East, or those who practiced Islam, Sikhism and Hinduism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Will We Stop the Scapegoating?', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:24:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deepa Iyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deepa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Iyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0230eab-cc0d-5f4f-8541-8770ba25a85b', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0230eab-cc0d-5f4f-8541-8770ba25a85b'}, {'abstract': 'Whether they prefer Angry Birds, FarmVille or Sims Social, it seems that almost everyone plays some kind of electronic game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/video-games-for-all-tastes-and-attention-spans.html', 'snippet': 'Whether they prefer Angry Birds, FarmVille or Sims Social, it seems that almost everyone plays some kind of electronic game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It wasn’t long ago that video games were on the verge of being marginalized in American culture — like comic books. (When was the last time you saw an adult reading a comic book, err, excuse me, a “graphic novel,” in public? Never?) ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/07GAME/07GAME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Video Games for All Tastes and Attention Spans', 'kicker': 'Video Game Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Video Games for All Tastes and Attention Spans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Xbox 360 (Video Game System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'PlayStation 3 (Video Game System)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wii (Video Game System)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nintendo Co Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:24:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/794313b4-0066-5715-9738-3b92022eaf95', 'word_count': 2415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/794313b4-0066-5715-9738-3b92022eaf95'}, {'abstract': 'New devices, rigged with sensors to supply metrics like the number of steps walked and calories burned, can help transform even a mundane workout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/for-the-health-conscious-fitness-for-the-holidays.html', 'snippet': 'New devices, rigged with sensors to supply metrics like the number of steps walked and calories burned, can help transform even a mundane workout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOOKING for something to place under the tree for the health buff in the family? Skip the Zumba DVDs and consider a fitness gadget. They can make a great present for anyone on your list, whether that person is an ardent athlete or simply wants a little help working off holiday indulgences like eggnog and pecan pie.\xa0', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/technology/personaltech/FITNESS3/FITNESS3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': N