209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ea7c61c-651e-5311-8e19-31c5682515ea'}, {'abstract': 'Hundreds of thousands of kids have a deployed parent, and schools in military districts are learning to help them cope.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/when-daddys-cafeteria-is-in-afghanistan/', 'snippet': 'Hundreds of thousands of kids have a deployed parent, and schools in military districts are learning to help them cope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marine stoicism is no longer expected from the kindergartners at schools on the Marine base at Camp Lejeune, N.C., who are waiting for Dad or Mom (women make up 11 percent of forces serving in Iraq and Afghanistan, and 40 percent of them have children) to come home. Instead, an impressive array of high-quality support services are available for children with parents at war. How much can any club or counseling do to help a child manage the hopes and fears and confusion that come with watching a parent get on that plane, or come home injured or changed (or not at all)? Maybe not much, but the military is trying. In 2008-9, it spent $6 million to train 420 school counselors and psychologists in methods for helping children affected by parents serving in wartime. Michael Winerip’s recent article in The Times, “Lessons in Coping for Children of Deployed,” is a reminder that not all sacrifices come from our troops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Daddy’s Cafeteria Is in Afghanistan', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T21:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_i