'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a51e094d-d6be-5cb3-a879-320f15881d5b', 'word_count': 7, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a51e094d-d6be-5cb3-a879-320f15881d5b'}, {'abstract': 'Blake Nelson’s sequel to his beloved 1994 teenage bildungsroman, “Girl,” owes its publication, at least partly, to our current fascination with that halcyon age of mix tapes and Doc Martens.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/the-stuff-that-dream-school-is-made-of/', 'snippet': 'Blake Nelson’s sequel to his beloved 1994 teenage bildungsroman, “Girl,” owes its publication, at least partly, to our current fascination with that halcyon age of mix tapes and Doc Martens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The touchstones seem to be everywhere these days: the plaid-wearing, goateed bicycle messengers of “Portlandia”; the grunge heiress Frances Bean Cobain’s recent Hedi Slimane photo shoot; the chortling return of “Beavis and Butthead” to MTV. Yes, the ‘90s are totally back — so much so that some culture observers are probably busy deciding at this very moment that they’re already over again. And there’s no doubt that “Dream School” (Figment), Blake Nelson’s sequel to his beloved 1994 teenage bildungsroman, “Girl,” owes its publication, at least partly, to our current fascination with that halcyon age of mix tapes and Doc Martens. Nelson himself said, “I finished writing the book in 2000, but nobody would publish it, so it sat in my drawer for 10 years.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stuff That ‘Dream School’ Is Made Of', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-06T22:19:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Naomi Fry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Naomi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organiza