alifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/431dc04b-154f-58ef-8add-3bafe0e90539', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/431dc04b-154f-58ef-8add-3bafe0e90539'}, {'abstract': 'The real question for religious conservatives isn’t whether they can trust Newt Gingrich; it’s whether they can afford to be associated with him.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/06/the-tempting-of-the-christian-right/', 'snippet': 'The real question for religious conservatives isn’t whether they can trust Newt Gingrich; it’s whether they can afford to be associated with him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than any other Republican constituency, religious conservatives have good reasons to be wary of Newt Gingrich. As the leader of a right-wing insurgency in the early 1990s, he often kept their causes at arm’s length — deliberately excluding issues like abortion and school prayer from the Contract With America, for instance. As Speaker of the House, he undercut their claim to the moral high ground by carrying on an extramarital affair even as his party was impeaching Bill Clinton for lying under oath about adultery.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tempting of the Christian Right', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southern Baptist Convention', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value':