Tattoo,” the director David Fincher, a specialist in alienation, alters what is for many a text as sacred as the Harry Potter novels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/movies/david-fincher-directs-girl-with-the-dragon-tattoo.html', 'snippet': 'For his version of “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo,” the director David Fincher, a specialist in alienation, alters what is for many a text as sacred as the Harry Potter novels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN anticipation of the movie version of Stieg Larsson’s “Girl With the Dragon Tattoo,” which stars Daniel Craig and Rooney Mara and opens Dec. 21, Knopf has printed two different tie-in editions of the original novel, more than a million copies all told.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11JPFINCHER1/11JPFINCHER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11JPFINCHER1/11JPFINCHER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11JPFINCHER1/11JPFINCHER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obsession, Reignited', 'kicker': 'Arts & Leisure', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obsession, Reignited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mara, Rooney', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fincher, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larsson, Stieg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T13:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/599c6675-778f-5b09-b582-4e3ead675d42', 'word_count': 1862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/599c6675-778f-5b09-b582-4e3ead675d42'}, {'abstract': 'Advertisers are bullish on video, networks are anxious about the NFL and Mitt Romney changes his mind about Fox News.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/the-breakfast-meeting-dec-7/', 'snippet': 'Advertisers are bullish on video, networks are anxious about the NFL and Mitt Romney changes his mind about Fox News.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the takeaways from the annual UBS global media and communications conference in Midtown Manhattan, Stuart Elliott and Amy Chozick report, is the primacy of video content “regardless of the type of screen on which it is watched.” Based on the talks there, “marketers remain keen on buying commercial time during video programming despite the uncertain outlook for the economy.” Spending on TV ads around the world next year will increase 6.7 percent from 2011, said one forecaster, Vincent Létang, as compared with a predicted overall 5 percent increase in advertising. (Online ads will have the greatest growth, he predicted, 11.2 percent.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting, Dec. 7', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 