'2011-12-07T15:55:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac1915a3-ab0c-5e94-bf99-460eead51604', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac1915a3-ab0c-5e94-bf99-460eead51604'}, {'abstract': 'Capturing the elegant quail in Mexico requires time, patience and more quail-friendly traps.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/goodbye-to-quail-for-now/', 'snippet': 'Capturing the elegant quail in Mexico requires time, patience and more quail-friendly traps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jennifer Calkins, a biologist who teaches at Evergreen State College in Olympia, Wash., writes from Mexico, where she is studying the ecology, behavior and evolution of the elegant quail.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goodbye to Quail, for Now', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T15:58:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Calkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/510fa23f-17cc-5c34-99c1-e40983daaa93', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/510fa23f-17cc-5c34-99c1-e40983daaa93'}, {'abstract': 'The New Museum has raised admission prices to $16, from $12, to pay for the extra staff needed to shepherd museumgoers through Mr. Höller’s carnival-like pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/crowds-at-holler-exhibition-force-price-hike/', 'snippet': 'The New Museum has raised admission prices to $16, from $12, to pay for the extra staff needed to shepherd museumgoers through Mr. Höller’s carnival-like pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Participatory art, as it turns out, is not all fun and games. The New Museum, whose exhibition of the work of the Belgian-born artist Carsten Höller has been packing in crowds since it opened last month, has raised its general admission price to $16, from $12. The higher price will help it pay for the extra staff needed to shepherd museumgoers through Mr. Höller’s carnival-like pieces, which take up all the museum’s floors: goggles that turn the world upside down; a three-story tubular slide; and a heavily salinated sensory deprivation tank (complete with a shower and hooks for the clothes of those who decide to undress and get in for a float).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crowds at Höller Exhibition Force Price Increase', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holler, Carsten', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T15:58:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/649852f5-e141-52a2-b8a9-229b8959f641', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/649852f5-e141-52a2-b8a9-229b8959f641'}, {'abstract': 'Stanley Ng was accused of providing inside information about the Marvell Technology Group to Winifred Jiau, a former consultant with Primary Global Research.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/former-marvell-employee-expected-to-plead-guilty-to-insider-trading/', 'snippet': 'Stanley Ng was accused of providing inside information about the Marvell Technology Group to Winifred Jiau, a former consultant with Primary Global Research.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:23 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Former Marvell Employee Pleads Guilty to Insider Trading', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jiau, Winifred', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvell Technology Group Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T16:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/354e2ecf-5e17-56e4-8307-1b50b41d3b9a', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/354e2ecf-5e17-56e4-8307-1b50b41d3b9a'}, {'abstract': 'Apple allows its iTunes software to be controlled from beyond the computer, either with a remote control designed to work with iTunes, or by special software installed on a mobile device.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/qa-controlling-itunes-remotely/', 'snippet': 'Apple allows its iTunes software to be controlled from beyond the computer, either with a remote control designed to work with iTunes, or by special software installed on a mobile device.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Controlling iTunes Remotely', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPod', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T16:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c83cebf3-6a27-5f2f-8799-2d0916544625', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c83cebf3-6a27-5f2f-8799-2d0916544625'}, {'abstract': 'New research shows that heading the ball in soccer is damaging—if you do it more than a thousand times in a year.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/how-dangerous-is-heading-a-soccer-ball/', 'snippet': 'New research shows that heading the ball in soccer is damaging—if you do it more than a thousand times in a year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soccer parents, watching their children use their head to send the ball rocketing in an age of increased worry about the long-term effects of minor sports concussions, have been arguing for years about the practice. On the Well blog, Gretchen Reynolds reports on new research showing frontal lobe damage and weaker verbal and visual recall among players who had headed a ball more than a thousand times in the last year, even those who reported no concussion, and among college players who were frequent headers in a Division 2 program. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Dangerous Is Heading a Soccer Ball?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T16:13:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7146c879-c93a-5962-8da7-c9ee58c897c0', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7146c879-c93a-5962-8da7-c9ee58c897c0'}, {'abstract': 'After two floods, Pulling Down the Moon, which offers yoga classes and other services to women undergoing fertility treatments, asks itself whether it should stay or go.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/business/smallbusiness/a-business-tries-to-decide-whether-its-location-is-perfect-or-a-disaster.html', 'snippet': 'After two floods, Pulling Down the Moon, which offers yoga classes and other services to women undergoing fertility treatments, asks itself whether it should stay or go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pulling Down the Moon provides services like yoga classes, acupuncture, massage therapy and nutritional counseling to women undergoing fertility treatments. Based in Chicago, with satellite locations in Arlington Heights, Ill., and Rockville, Md., the nine-year-old company has grown to 35 employees with $1.3 million in annual revenue. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/business/Sbiz/Sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/08/business/Sbiz/Sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 