_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfc232af-a7f5-50cc-aafa-88ae585fd7bf', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfc232af-a7f5-50cc-aafa-88ae585fd7bf'}, {'abstract': 'Economic and demographic information, as well as this week’s polling data, can be a reminder to voters and candidates that these early nominating contests are quite different than the rest of the country.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/iowa-and-new-hampshire-stand-apart/', 'snippet': 'Economic and demographic information, as well as this week’s polling data, can be a reminder to voters and candidates that these early nominating contests are quite different than the rest of the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With much of the political field focused on the testing grounds of Iowa and New Hampshire, it’s easy to forget that these two early states — in economic and demographic makeup — are markedly different from much of the rest of the country. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iowa and New Hampshire Stand Apart', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T17:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allison Kopicki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kopicki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff1332d1-5576-566f-b15c-3c4f430b4901', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff1332d1-5576-566f-b15c-3c4f430b4901'}, {'abstract': 'The sound designer and composer Kristian Eidnes Andersen, with the help of Wagner, created the sounds of the end of the world in “Melancholia.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/below-the-line-the-world-ending-sounds-of-melancholia/', 'snippet': 'The sound designer and composer Kristian Eidnes Andersen, with the help of Wagner, created the sounds of the end of the world in “Melancholia.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'What might the moment before world annihilation sound like? While most would prefer not to experience the sound firsthand, it is one that has been imagined and portrayed cinematically in several ways, from a whimper (Sidney Lumet’s “Fail-Safe”) to a bang (the 1983 TV movie “The Day After,” which didn’t depict the end of everything, exactly, but did portray the unthinkable). Lars von Trier’s “Melancholia,” the apocalyptic drama that has received as much critical praise as its director has received public scrutiny, chooses to end the world with Wagner.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Below the Line: The World-Ending Sounds of ‘Melancholia’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T17:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': 