al)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T21:08:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Powell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fd559b2-8981-5df4-8e5f-b8783f8a8d55', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fd559b2-8981-5df4-8e5f-b8783f8a8d55'}, {'abstract': 'Along with “New Girl,” “Glee” appears to hit a season low among the 18-to-49 set.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/a-good-night-for-fox-despite-little-joy-from-glee/', 'snippet': 'Along with “New Girl,” “Glee” appears to hit a season low among the 18-to-49 set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Based on early Nielsen estimates, “Glee” and “New Girl” dropped to season lows in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic on Tuesday night but Fox was still able to claim the top spot in the advertiser-beloved category over all. “Glee” drew 7.1 million total viewers, a far cry from the 11.1 million that tuned in to a comparable episode a year ago, while “New Girl” fell to 6.8 million viewers, a series low. CBS finished first in total viewers, with repeats from its “NCIS” franchise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Good Night for Fox Despite Little Joy From ‘Glee’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buble, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank'