nnounced Wednesday that it was closing nearly one quarter of its stores and had received a loan from a private equity firm, as the struggling clothing retailer looks to shore up its finances as it heads into the heart of the holiday shopping season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pacific Sunwear Gets Private Equity Loan and Closes Stores', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pacific Sunwear of California Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa46e65d-1b5e-5722-af94-ea11ec8a4e03', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa46e65d-1b5e-5722-af94-ea11ec8a4e03'}, {'abstract': 'Lana Del Rey, notorious Web sensation, finally played her first proper New York show, at Bowery Ballroom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/arts/music/lana-del-rey-at-bowery-ballroom-review.html', 'snippet': 'Lana Del Rey, notorious Web sensation, finally played her first proper New York show, at Bowery Ballroom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lana Del Rey is a singer of songs that are very popular on the Internet. Stop. Rewind. That’s not quite it. Let’s try again.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/ray/ray-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/ray/ray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/07/arts/ray/ray-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finally Taking the Stage, Direct From the Internet', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finally Taking the Stage, Direct From the Internet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bowery Ballroom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:04:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/54306c58-7abc-5779-a2e0-26f1c65e1fa5', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54306c58-7abc-5779-a2e0-26f1c65e1fa5'}, {'abstract': 'Despite a technical glitch, the broadcast from Milan was shown at Big Cinemas Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/watching-a-la-scala-opening-on-the-big-screen/', 'snippet': 'Despite a technical glitch, the broadcast from Milan was shown at Big Cinemas Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don Giovanni had just gone to hell, and the rest of the characters in Mozart’s opera of that name were singing of his final moments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Watching a La Scala Opening on the Big Screen', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La Scala', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:05:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0a38fc7-ea9d-542f-9f9e-7e35d6640bf9', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0a38fc7-ea9d-542f-9f9e-7e35d6640bf9'}, {'abstract': 'This year, the mythical calendar was photographed for the first time by a native son of Italy, Mario Sorrenti.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/now-exposing-the-2012-pirelli-calendar/', 'snippet': 'This year, the mythical calendar was photographed for the first time by a native son of Italy, Mario Sorrenti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For most every year since 1964, Pirelli, the Italian tire maker, has bankrolled a very unusual, very expensive promotional calendar. It typically involves a coterie of the world’s hottest models jetting to an exquisitely remote clime — the Seychelles, say, or Botswana — to be photographed, nude, by an elite fashion photographer. The calendar began inauspiciously as a pinup that grease monkeys would post at continental service stations. But just like in the case of Michelin’s influential guidebook, it took on a life of its own and now its connection to motoring seems completely incidental. (One must give props to all these European tire makers for their enduring contributions to world culture; the best thing our own Goodyear has come up with so far is a blimp.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Exposing | The 2012 Pirelli Calendar', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jovovich, Milla', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kikuchi, Rinko', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moss, Kate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aec3c8c-dd3c-5c92-a1af-bdc7793c2363', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aec3c8c-dd3c-5c92-a1af-bdc7793c2363'}, {'abstract': 'Art Deco motifs, it seems, are sometimes a reference point for designers whether they are aware of it or not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/fashion/a-ticket-to-east-egg-please.html', 'snippet': 'Art Deco motifs, it seems, are sometimes a reference point for designers whether they are aware of it or not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU could be forgiven for thinking that the spring 2012 fashion collections were an open call for costumes for the Baz Luhrmann remake of “The Great Gatsby,” in theaters late next year. Flapper shapes and Art Deco lines lindy-hopped down runways from New York to Milan. There was flapper fringe at Etro and Marchesa, Glad-wrapped chorus girls at Marc Jacobs, zigzagging patterns at J. Mendel, BCBG and Alberta Ferretti. And at Gucci there were so many flapper numbers, their architectural patterns outlined in black and gold, that the show was a study in Jazz Age opulence.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/fashion/08finepoints/08finepoints-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1016, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/fashion/08finepoints/08finepoints-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/08/fashion/08finepoints/08finepoints-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ticket to East Egg, Please', 'kicker': 'Fine Points', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Ticket to East Egg, Please', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handbags and Purses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ART DECO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Twenties', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:15:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Joy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/987f7f1d-2e18-5df1-a5ba-e22ef1c788ae', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/987f7f1d-2e18-5df1-a5ba-e22ef1c788ae'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon, Barnes & Noble and Apple have made it possible to send personalized gifts to their products.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/technology/personaltech/quick-and-easy-shopping-for-tablet-users.html', 'snippet': 'Amazon, Barnes & Noble and Apple have made it possible to send personalized gifts to their products.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those days of dashing through the mall on Christmas Eve, searching for the perfect book or DVD, may be history. Procrastinators this year can sit at home, alone and unruffled, and send books, music and even video in time for Christmas delivery — on tablets like the Kindle Fire, the Nook and the iPad.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/business/08-BASICS/08-BASICS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 645, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/business/08-BASICS/08-BASICS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/08/business/08-BASICS/08-BASICS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Short Work of Shopping for Tablet Users', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Short Work of Shopping for Tablet Users', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Barnes & Noble Nook', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:17:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mickey Meece', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mickey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meece', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/850d148f-32e9-50ac-9512-c2c88514e5d1', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/850d148f-32e9-50ac-9512-c2c88514e5d1'}, {'abstract': 'An ancient apple tree takes root in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/07/for-the-big-apple-an-ancient-gift/', 'snippet': 'An ancient apple tree takes root in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They plunged the roots beneath the surface of the city, the din of an elevated train drowning out the shovels smacking against the dirt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For the Big Apple, a Gift That Grows', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APPLES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kazakhstan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:18:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94c0b09a-a387-589a-9ea4-f59e71aad0c0', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94c0b09a-a387-589a-9ea4-f59e