nizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:50:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0229d67f-3bc5-5208-84e5-3bab423291ee', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0229d67f-3bc5-5208-84e5-3bab423291ee'}, {'abstract': '“The Help” is two feel-good stories in one: the movie itself, which preaches empowerment over oppression, and the story of making the film, in which rejection leads to triumph.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/movies/awardsseason/for-tate-taylor-making-the-help-was-a-tale-of-adversity.html', 'snippet': '“The Help” is two feel-good stories in one: the movie itself, which preaches empowerment over oppression, and the story of making the film, in which rejection leads to triumph.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the winter of 2009 Tate Taylor decided to take a road trip with a few friends across the Mississippi Delta. Mark Ricker, a production designer who had known Mr. Taylor since the early 1990s, when both were production assistants — and occasional models — for the J. Crew catalog, turned up in Memphis with Rena DeAngelo, a decorator. Joined by two producers and led by a Southerner with an eye for period detail, they went in search of the setting for what would become one of this year’s blockbuster movies, “The Help.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/arts/bagger/bagger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/08/arts/bagger/bagger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/arts/bagger/bagger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/08/arts/bagger/bagger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/08/arts/bagger/bagger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Director’s Kinship With the Deep South', 'kicker': 'The Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Director’s Kinship With the Deep South', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Viola', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-07T23:51:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7165d6c-1dbe-51d2-b94b-1064bf23310d', 'word_count': 1314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7165d6c-1dbe-51d2-b94b-1064bf23310d'}, {'abstract': 'Letter writers respond to a news article and an editorial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/opinion/obamas-kansas-speech-about-income-inequality.html', 'snippet': 'Letter writers respond to a news article and an editorial.', 'lead_parag