Cruz', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALDERSON, SANDY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beato, Pedro', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emaus, Brad', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pagan, Angel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Astros', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:12:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0db42448-bf75-5c1f-b21b-a839c58c2a5e', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0db42448-bf75-5c1f-b21b-a839c58c2a5e'}, {'abstract': 'A new report shows some breakfast cereals are more than 50 percent sugar. But you knew that, didn’t you?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/kids-want-some-cereal-with-your-sugar/', 'snippet': 'A new report shows some breakfast cereals are more than 50 percent sugar. But you knew that, didn’t you?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The key to a healthy cereal breakfast is to roll down the cereal aisle, grab your tried-and-true box of Cheerios, and get out before your child even asks about Tony the Tiger or that toucan dude on the box of Froot Loops. Of course you can say no. But it’s all too easy to agree to a little experimentation and come home with a box of Honey Nut Cheerios, only to realize that a serving contains more sugar than three Chips Ahoy cookies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kids, Want Some Cereal With Your Sugar?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:13:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69a45ac5-d819-5f9c-90b7-492ecbc0f4e0', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69a45ac5-d819-5f9c-90b7-492ecbc0f4e0'}, {'abstract': 'Albert Pujols’s contract will be compared to Alex Rodriguez’s deal with the Rangers in 2001, then a record 10 years and $252 million.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/signing-for-rodriguez-money-is-the-easy-part/', 'snippet': 'Albert Pujols’s contract will be compared to Alex Rodriguez’s deal with the Rangers in 2001, then a record 10 years and $252 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appears as if the Albert Pujols sweepstakes is over and that Arte Moreno and the Angels have landed baseball’s greatest active hitter for a whopping 10 years and at least $250 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Signing for Rodriguez Money Is the Easy Part', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pujols, Albert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Angels of Anaheim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:17:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11fd6c2c-ef9c-53c4-a1d8-54e82998fd94', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11fd6c2c-ef9c-53c4-a1d8-54e82998fd94'}, {'abstract': 'Louis Nemeth, 93, was a publicity photographer with a remarkable back story that took him all the way to Buchenwald and left him in Hungarian Yorkville, just three blocks from where he’d grown up.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/radio-had-a-voice-louie-gave-it-a-face/', 'snippet': 'Louis Nemeth, 93, was a publicity photographer with a remarkable back story that took him all the way to Buchenwald and left him in Hungarian Yorkville, just three blocks from where he’d grown up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Someone had to take the promotional picture for the Mutual Broadcasting System’s “Deadline” program, showing the host, Louise Snyder, holding a large turkey. (It was Thanksgiving, after all.) Mutual’s publicity photographer in the 1950s and ’60s was Louis Nemeth, who had a much greater talent — and a much more interesting story — than his 8-by-10 glossies ever revealed. Over the course of 93 years, he moved all of three blocks in Hungarian Yorkville, from East 68th Street to East 65th Street. But his career also took him to the Buchenwald concentration camp during World War II. There, in April 1945, as a member of the 165th Signal Photo Company, he spent three days documenting the horrors uncovered after the camp’s liberation. Maybe he used the next decades to create so many innocuous photo fantasies because he had already seen enough reality to last a lifetime. A video and a longer article are on Lens.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Radio Had a Voice. Louie Gave It a Face.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:19:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f21944a4-494c-52e2-9e12-ae73cf5fc4ef', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f21944a4-494c-52e2-9e12-ae73cf5fc4ef'}, {'abstract': 'Owners and players are working to ratify a new labor agreement for the N.B.A.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/new-n-b-a-deal-is-expected-to-be-approved-thursday/', 'snippet': 'Owners and players are working to ratify a new labor agreement for the N.B.A.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The second-longest labor crisis in N.B.A. history is expected to reach its conclusion Thursday afternoon, with a vote to adopt a new collective bargaining agreement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New N.B.A. Deal Is Expected to Be Approved Thursday', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Battier, Shane', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Dwight', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Chris', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern,