as holiday lights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Children love to wake up to a house full of presents during the winter holidays. But for the grown-ups, one of the best gifts of all is waking up to a clean house. Today’s Home section offers advice from extreme cleaners.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Extreme Cleaning for the Holidays', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:21:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1379187e-ce89-51fe-a77d-e66d81b0a723', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1379187e-ce89-51fe-a77d-e66d81b0a723'}, {'abstract': 'Restore Our Future, a well-financed but largely unseen political group supporting Mitt Romney, surfaces in a big way.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/romney-super-pac-enters-the-fray/', 'snippet': 'Restore Our Future, a well-financed but largely unseen political group supporting Mitt Romney, surfaces in a big way.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:31 p.m. | Updated A well-financed but largely unseen independent political group supporting Mitt Romney surfaced on Thursday in a big way, saying it was putting $3.1 million down on advertising in Iowa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Super PAC Assails Gingrich', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Politico, The', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Restore Our Future', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:26:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a9dbe2c-3dd1-5387-b9f0-ae1d3c4d3ee9', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a9dbe2c-3dd1-5387-b9f0-ae1d3c4d3ee9'}, {'abstract': 'Food drives may be inefficient, but stopping them altogether means missing out on some important teaching moments.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/the-case-against-ending-food-drives/', 'snippet': 'Food drives may be inefficient, but stopping them altogether means missing out on some important teaching moments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over on Slate yesterday, the new Moneybox columnist Matthew Yglesias sounded off on food drives. Having to pick up or sort your donations of random cans is highly inefficient. Much of the stuff that people remove from their pantries is unusable because it’s old or it’s junk food, he said. Plus, most food banks, he noted, can buy highly discounted bulk food in a variety of ways.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case Against Ending Food Drives', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Banks and Pantries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Lieber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lieber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d5d45d5-1a16-5dc9-b95b-c8986f2c0c39', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d5d45d5-1a16-5dc9-b95b-c8986f2c0c39'}, {'abstract': 'A culinary jaunt across Barbados, quite possibly the foodiest of Caribbean isles.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/dispatch-eating-in-barbados/', 'snippet': 'A culinary jaunt across Barbados, quite possibly the foodiest of Caribbean isles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are worse fates than being stuck in Barbados for a long weekend, as I was last month, having tagged along with my lady friend to a conference at the University of the West Indies. I had been told that Barbados, the most British of Caribbean isles, is also among the most delicious, so I resolved to eat my way across it, a journey that involved much driving on roads with no signs and much sipping of Mount Gay Extra Old rum, but of course never at the same time. (If you visit Barbados, you will be extolled the virtues of house-made rum punch — it is often poured out of bottles that look as if they once held bleach — but this cloying potion guarantees a terrible hangover. Instead, drink the local rums neat or with fresh juice.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dispatch | Eating in Barbados', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0073113f-415e-5f4c-b01f-9de67fa8c62f', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0073113f-415e-5f4c-b01f-9de67fa8c62f'}, {'abstract': 'One man’s exit from a nursing home.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/the-nursing-home-exodus-pt-2/', 'snippet': 'One man’s exit from a nursing home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, when he was only 91, Edwin Murphy awoke in a hospital in Albuquerque, N.M., and learned that he’d had a heart attack.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Nursing Home Exodus, Part 2', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T17:32:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e58a3f43-76bd-55da-a5ad-b0684d45acaf', 'word_count': 850, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e58a3f43-76bd-55da-a5ad-b0684d45acaf'}, {'abstract': 'The 592 suspender cables, part of the bridge’s original equipment, are well past their prime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/nyregion/george-washington-bridge-cables-to-be-replaced.html', 'snippet': 'The 592 suspender cables, part of the bridge’s original equipment, are well past their prime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The George Washington Bridge is a cathedral of steel cables and beams, a stark, glimmering span that has become a civil engineering landmark, not to mention the busiest vehicular bridge in the world.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/nyregion/09bridge-span/09bridge-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/09/nyregion/09bridge-span/09bridge-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/nyregion/09bridge-span/09bridge-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/nyregion/09bridge-span/09bridge-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/nyregion/09bridge-span/09bridge-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Now 80, George Washington Bridge Will Undergo Repairs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now 80 George Washington Will Soon Undergo The Bridge Equivalent Of Hip Replacement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'George Washington Bridge', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridges and Tunnels', 'rank'