170-85fc-cadcb61583c1'}, {'abstract': 'Casting of Larry Bird in “Magic/Bird” announced', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/magicbird-gets-its-larry-bird/', 'snippet': 'Casting of Larry Bird in “Magic/Bird” announced', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 6’9’’ leading man who could match the height of basketball great Larry Bird was probably too much to hope for in “Magic/Bird,” the new play whose producers are aiming for Broadway in the spring of 2012. But 6’5’’ will do. The producers, Fran Kirmser and Tony Ponturo, said in an interview Thursday that they have cast actor (and former college basketball player) Tug Coker in the role of Bird, the former Boston Celtic forward whose on-court rivalry and off-court friendship with Magic Johnson, the Los Angeles Lakers point guard, are the heart of the play.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Magic/Bird’ Gets Its Larry Bird', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T21:53:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aff96df6-9002-5b74-94a3-838b34cdb20f', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aff96df6-9002-5b74-94a3-838b34cdb20f'}, {'abstract': 'New York walking tours are laid out as phone-based treasure hunts, with texted clues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/nyregion/new-york-walking-tours-by-phone-and-a-local-business-app.html', 'snippet': 'New York walking tours are laid out as phone-based treasure hunts, with texted clues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Committing to a love affair with New York is hard work for a Miser. As with any long-term relationship, you have to keep discovering new ways to get to know each other if you want to preserve the thrill. That’s what Avi Millman’s “New York, the Game” is all about: rediscovering the city in a way that’s new and fun. To play, you need $12 and a cellphone. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09MISER_SPAN/09MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09MISER_SPAN/09MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09MISER_SPAN/09MISER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 774, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09MISER_SPAN/09MISER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09MISER_SPAN/09MISER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Miser', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weekend Miser', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T21:57:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54e6d2eb-9e22-5359-b604-d2adc68b24d6', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54e6d2eb-9e22-5359-b604-d2adc68b24d6'}, {'abstract': 'Plant advice, given from the designer’s impeccably potted apartment.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/the-happy-houseplant-indoor-gardening-with-richard-chai/', 'snippet': 'Plant advice, given from the designer’s impeccably potted apartment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What Richard Chai calls his orchid cemetery is a narrow, north-facing window sill in the kitchen area of his Financial District loft. There, five flowerless orchid plants in uniform terracotta pots are getting indirect sunlight and enough water to keep the soil moist most of the time, and that’s it. “Just ignore them,” says Chai, who is literally –– a word he uses quite frequently –– bringing the gentle plants back to life (maybe rehab is a better word for the facility). “I used to obsess about orchids. But if you ignore them, they’re so much happier.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Happy Houseplant | Indoor Gardening with Richard Chai', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:05:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0373d402-8892-58f7-aafc-b1b9661d67a9', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0373d402-8892-58f7-aafc-b1b9661d67a9'}, {'abstract': 'The Manhattan School of Music production shifts Mozart’s comedy of fidelity from 18th-century Naples to North Africa just before World War I.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/arts/music/cosi-fan-tutte-at-manhattan-school-of-music-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Manhattan School of Music production shifts Mozart’s comedy of fidelity from 18th-century Naples to North Africa just before World War I.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an essay about Mozart’s “Così Fan Tutte” in his book “On Opera,” the English philosopher Bernard Williams wrote: “Nothing is denied of what has happened; that is what is sad in this comedy, that all that dimension of feeling exists, but the world will rumble on as though it did not.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09COSI_SPAN/COSI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09COSI_SPAN/COSI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09COSI_SPAN/COSI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09COSI_SPAN/COSI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09COSI_SPAN/COSI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Soldiers, Their Girls, Those Doubts', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Soldiers, Their Girls, Those Doubts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan School of Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan School of Music Opera Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:10:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c01d4068-82a6-544b-96b4-d54feb0576dc', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c01d4068-82a6-544b-96b4-d54feb0576dc'}, {'abstract': 'Patent models on view; Art Deco posters by Roger Broders at auction; Meissen porcelain in a reopened Frick Collection portico; and a show of Tiffany paintings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/arts/design/patent-models-roger-broders-posters-meissen-porcelain.html', 'snippet': 'Patent models on view; Art Deco posters by Roger Broders at auction; Meissen porcelain in a reopened Frick Collection portico; and a show of Tiffany paintings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American inventors in the 19th century shipped operable miniatures by the hundreds of thousands to the federal government, as models for potential patent approval. Tinkerers in every realm, from piano keyboards to collapsible lifeboats, were required to submit three-dimensional scale prototypes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09ANTIQUES2_SPAN/ANTIQUES2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/201