}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Talent, James M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:22:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3d55f1c-1e30-57ca-8a0a-5cd3aefa59a4', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3d55f1c-1e30-57ca-8a0a-5cd3aefa59a4'}, {'abstract': 'The Jewish Museum’s retrospective of the work of Ezra Jack Keats tells the story of how a white Jew created a black character who helped change the face of children’s books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/arts/design/the-snowy-day-art-of-ezra-jack-keats-at-jewish-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The Jewish Museum’s retrospective of the work of Ezra Jack Keats tells the story of how a white Jew created a black character who helped change the face of children’s books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1962 Ezra Jack Keats started a quiet revolution that in its own way had as much influence as some of the decade’s louder protests. An author and illustrator, Keats published “The Snowy Day,” about a small boy’s delight in his first snowstorm. Nothing radical there. But the story differed profoundly from virtually all the mainstream American picture books preceding it: Peter, the little boy, was black.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SNOWY_SPAN/09SNOWY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SNOWY_SPAN/09SNOWY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SNOWY_SPAN/09SNOWY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SNOWY_SPAN/09SNOWY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SNOWY_SPAN/09SNOWY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Homage to a Picture-Book Rebel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homage to a Picture-Book Rebel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keats, Ezra Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jewish Museum (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd86b409-c0cc-5de2-a5e5-b5d6ee4ca744', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd86b409-c0cc-5de2-a5e5-b5d6ee4ca744'}, {'abstract': 'Not for the first time, Senator Tom Coburn has stopped shaving.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/a-new-look-for-senator-coburn/', 'snippet': 'Not for the first time, Senator Tom Coburn has stopped shaving.', 'lead_paragraph': ' There is a great deal occurring in Congress right now, but let us stop for a moment to ponder Senator Tom Coburn’s beard. For weeks now, the usually baby-faced Republican from Oklahoma has been strolling about the Capitol with a new look, his push-broom head of hair complemented by a slightly scruffy goatee. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Look for Senator Coburn', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coburn, Tom', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:23:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4d8cb9e-bfd3-5185-85e9-a85347c433ef', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4d8cb9e-bfd3-5185-85e9-a85347c433ef'}, {'abstract': 'Guests on this week’s podcast include Daniel Mohan of ID Watchdog, who explains how people’s identities can be stolen (and what to do about it), and Will Lucas, who discusses Thankyouaga.in, a new social app for positive posts.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/tech-talk-podcast-say-nice-things/', 'snippet': 'Guests on this week’s podcast include Daniel Mohan of ID Watchdog, who explains how people’s identities can be stolen (and what to do about it), and Will Lucas, who discusses Thankyouaga.in, a new social app for positive posts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the holiday shopping season in full swing, credit-card numbers and other personal bits of information are flying around the Web in a flurry. But while some shoppers may be nervous about identity theft, the problem goes far beyond just getting your credit-card number stolen, as Daniel Mohan explains. Mr. Mohan, the president and chief operating officer of ID Watchdog, describes what else thieves are after and how his company’s identity-monitoring service works. He also offers some tips for staying safer online.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Say Nice Things', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identity Fraud', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:25:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a1ea96b-0bb0-5c95-ac3a-90702aa3a97c', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a1ea96b-0bb0-5c95-ac3a-90702aa3a97c'}, {'abstract': 'Canada says tightrope walker Nik Wallenda can’t cross Niagara Falls.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/derring-dont/', 'snippet': 'Canada says tightrope walker Nik Wallenda can’t cross Niagara Falls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It looks like the tightrope walker Nik Wallenda won’t able to cheat death at Niagara Falls, at least not the way he originally proposed — on 1,800 feet of cable between the American and Canadian sides. On Wednesday, Canada said no. Its Niagara Parks Commission rejected Mr. Wallenda’s request, in keeping with its 100-year history of rejecting daredevil acts at the falls.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Derring-Don’t', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallenda, Nik', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Niagara Falls (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:26:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6c556e0-c9f5-599a-ad2f-35aef60e9722', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6c556e0-c9f5-599a-ad2f-35aef60e9722'}, {'abstract': 'Sales of luxury condominiums geared to affluent empty nesters have been strong in Bergen County.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/realestate/new-jersey-in-the-region-demand-for-estate-condominiums.html', 'snippet': 'Sales of luxury condominiums geared to affluent empty nesters have been strong in Bergen County.', 'lead_paragraph': 'REAL ESTATE optimists have been picking up blips on the radar in Bergen County lately, most recently this one: 24 of 68 “attached manor” condominiums with prices starting at $1.25 million sold in a single weekend — before they went up, and before a sales office had time to open. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/realestate/11NJZONE/NJZONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 273, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/realestate/11NJZONE/NJZONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 273}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/realestate/11NJZONE/NJZONE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/realestate/11NJZONE/NJZONE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/realestate/11NJZONE/NJZONE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Demand for ‘Estate’-Like Condos', 'kicker': 'In the Region | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Demand for &#8216;Estate&#8217;-Like Condos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bergen County (NJ)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antoinette Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antoinette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4ec52d6-c790-5b81-b183-7b2109ef9c33', 'word_count': 785, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4ec52d6-c790-5b81-b183-7b2109ef9c33'}, {'abstract': 'The current production of Puccini’s “Madama Butterfly” at the Metropolitan Opera is an inspired vision of mirrored ceiling, paper lanterns, fluttering petals and sliding panels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/arts/music/madama-butterfly-at-the-metropolitan-opera-review.html', 'snippet': 'The current production of Puccini’s “Madama Butterfly” at the Metropolitan Opera is an inspired vision of mirrored ceiling, paper lanterns, fluttering petals and sliding panels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In any successful opera performance, all the various parts of the presentation — music, text, singing, playing and staging — add up to more than the sum of the parts. The stage being by its nature a precarious and dangerous place, true parity among all those elements is a rare and difficult achievement. But a successful evening can still result when, on balance, the stronger aspects of a performance rise to compensate for the weaker ones.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN/BUTTERFLY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 989, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN/09BUTTERFLY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Potently Moving Music and Imagery to Match', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Potently Moving Music And Imagery to Match', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Domingo, Placido', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Puccini, Giacomo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3028f195-31e8-5d86-b7e9-982310f3ce01', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3028f195-31e8-5d86-b7e9-982310f3ce01'}, {'abstract': 'Elizabeth Warren, Wall Street stooge?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/slavery-is-freedom-and-all-that/', 'snippet': 'Elizabeth Warren, Wall Street stooge?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of my many radicalizing moments during the Bush years came when a number of the usual suspects began attacking John Kerry because he was, wait for it, too rich. Even with all the cynicism I’d developed over the previous four years, the idea that Republican operatives would invoke class envy for political advantage — and that voters would fall for it — caught me off guard.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Slavery Is Freedom, And All That', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-08T22:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba2659ec-9a53-50be-8995-33bc1ae6