t it had found no evidence that decades of live fire and bombing exercises by the Navy on the Puerto Rican island of Vieques had caused health problems documented among its residents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Finds No Link Between Vieques Bombings and Health Risks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Rejects Health Risks On Vieques', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vieques Island (Puerto Rico)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Superfund', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T02:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/448548a6-47af-5098-9f27-a418b12a0bea', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/448548a6-47af-5098-9f27-a418b12a0bea'}, {'abstract': 'Water pumps and wells in the developing world often fall into disrepair. One program teaches villagers how to maintain them on their own.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/08/keeping-the-water-flowing-in-rural-villages/', 'snippet': 'Water pumps and wells in the developing world often fall into disrepair. One program teaches villagers how to maintain them on their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keeping projects in business for the long term has been a constant theme of the Fixes column, and if sustainability has a poster child, it would be a water pump.\xa0\xa0 Travel anywhere in Africa or South Asia or Central America, and you will find a landscape dotted with the rusting skeletons of dead water pumps or wells..', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping the Water Flowing in Rural Villages', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tanzania', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uttar Pradesh State (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T02:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5b548a8-a89c-566b-baf7-4f327f446661', 'word_count': 1452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5b548a8-a89c-566b-baf7-4f327f446661'}, {'abstract': '“The Sitter,” an on-and-off funny rehash of “Adventures in Babysitting,” centers on a college dropout who reluctantly agrees to mind three children for pay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/movies/the-sitter-with-jonah-hill-review.html', 'snippet': '“The Sitter,” an on-and-off funny rehash of “Adventures in Babysitting,” centers on a college dropout who reluctantly agrees to mind three children for pay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Noah, the nobody’s fool played by Jonah Hill in the breezily indifferent comedy “The Sitter,” is a character in search of a movie, a setup in search of a punch line. Less directed than doodled by David Gordon Green, this on-and-off funny rehash of “Adventures in Babysitting” centers on a college dropout who, at his mother’s urging, reluctantly agrees to mind three children for pay. Somewhat of a big baby himself, Noah sets off with a pout, apparently unaware that most American excursions in comedy these days come with mandatory opportunities for growth.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SITTER/SITTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SITTER/SITTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/arts/09SITTER/SITTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Bother Checking In With This Baby Sitter', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Sitter'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Bother Checking In With This Baby Sitter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Jonah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, David Gordon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockwell, Sam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T02:30:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f550d281-a43e-5b62-9c51-6875d1e94ab6', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f550d281-a43e-5b62-9c51-6875d1e94ab6'}, {'abstract': 'Marketers and media companies have started buzzing about the potential to reach Latin women, and among the most recent initiatives is a new publication, Cosmopolitan Latina.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/business/media/media-companies-set-their-sights-on-latin-women.html', 'snippet': 'Marketers and media companies have started buzzing about the potential to reach Latin women, and among the most recent initiatives is a new publication, Cosmopolitan Latina.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH the Census Bureau counting nearly 25 million Latin women in the United States, marketers and media companies have started getting excited about the potential to reach them. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/business/Adco/Adco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 802, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/business/Adco/Adco-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marketers, and Media Companies, Set Their Sights on Latin Women', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Marketers, and Media Companies, Set Their Sights on Latin Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Spanish Language', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cosmopolitan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T02:30:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a01e067-39dc-5c92-9cc1-1ca664a9e77f', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a01e067-39dc-5c92-9cc1-1ca664a9e77f'}, {'abstract': 'Newt Gingrich may be big-government conservatism’s leading messenger, but his temperament and character distort the effect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/opinion/brooks-the-gingrich-tragedy.html', 'snippet': 'Newt Gingrich may be big-government conservatism’s leading messenger, but his temperament and character distort the effect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the major Republicans, the one who comes closest to my worldview is Newt Gingrich. Despite his erratically shifting views and odd phases, he continually returns to this core political refrain: He talks about using government in energetic but limited ways to increase growth, dynamism and social mobility.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-brooks-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/david-brooks/david-br