a Kolkata hospital, killing scores of patients who were trapped inside the smoke-filled building as rescue workers struggled to tame the blaze. The fire, which took place at a 161-bed private hospital, broke out in the basement, according to rescue officials, and sent huge plumes of smoke throughout the building. More than 70 people died in the blaze, many due to smoke inhalation, according to television news reports from the scene. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kolkata Hospital Fire Kills Dozens', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kolkata (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T09:50:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ff7ff33-d4d4-5d9e-8518-03277d325302', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ff7ff33-d4d4-5d9e-8518-03277d325302'}, {'abstract': 'The ratings agency Moody’s Investors Service on Friday downgraded the three largest banks in France, and said the French government was likely to step in if conditions worsened.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/business/global/moodys-downgrades-top-french-banks.html', 'snippet': 'The ratings agency Moody’s Investors Service on Friday downgraded the three largest banks in France, and said the French government was likely to step in if conditions worsened.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — In another sign of how Europe’s debt crisis is rippling through the banking system, Moody’s Investors Service on Friday downgraded the three largest banks in France, saying there was a “very high” probability that the French government would step in to support them if conditions worsened. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moody’s Downgrades Top French Banks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Big French Banks Downgraded by Moody’s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Societe Generale', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BNP Paribas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Credit Agricole d'Ile-de-France", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Moody's Investors Service Inc", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Ratings and Credit Rating Agencies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T09:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef0df492-98f9-52cd-8d6c-7591b4217f2c', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef0df492-98f9-52cd-8d6c-7591b4217f2c'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | What does the new musical iPhone app for Central Park by the group Bluebrain do?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/central-park-soundtrack/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | What does the new musical iPhone app for Central Park by the group Bluebrain do?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO are Bluebrain?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Central Park Soundtrack', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T09:53:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/163e51d0-669d-501c-b91e-328b34d50b50', 'word_count': 81, 'uri': 'nyt://article/163e51d0-669d-501c-b91e-328b34d50b50'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Read the related articles and tell us what you think about recent decision by the United States secretary of health.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/should-the-morning-after-pill-be-sold-over-the-counter-to-people-under-17/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Read the related articles and tell us what you think about recent decision by the United States secretary of health.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States secretary of health publicly overruled the Food and Drug Administration this week, refusing to allow emergency contraception known as the “morning-after pill” to be sold without a prescription to people under 17. (If taken soon after unprotected sex, the pill halves the chances of a pregnancy.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should the Morning-After Pill Be Sold Over the Counter to People Under 17?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T10:22:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4404a8e3-595f-5ff3-8a0c-fd07ee8834a8', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4404a8e3-595f-5ff3-8a0c-fd07ee8834a8'}, {'abstract': 'With psychedelic colors and elaborate sets, Manjari Sharma is creating a photographic series about Indian deities — a return to her cultural heritage and also a new acceptance of her profession by her family, who participated in the project.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/the-beauty-and-chaos-of-the-gods/', 'snippet': 'With psychedelic colors and elaborate sets, Manjari Sharma is creating a photographic series about Indian deities — a return to her cultural heritage and also a new acceptance of her profession by her family, who participated in the project.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Darshan” is a Sanskrit term meaning sight, in the Hindu sense of beholding a deity and making an immediate spiritual connection. Overwhelmingly in India, Hindu gods and goddesses are rendered in paintings or sculpted from stone and clay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Beauty and Chaos of the Gods', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T10:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niko Koppel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koppel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e485f65-658b-515f-b24c-86022e70fa50', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e485f65-658b-515f-b24c-86022e70fa50'}, {'abstract': 'While pursuing their 14th straight New York City basketball championship, the girls on the Murry Bergtraum team must deal with the killing in September of Tayshana Murphy, a star teammate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/nyregion/at-murry-bergtraum-high-school-playing-for-tayshana-murphy.html', 'snippet': 'While pursuing their 14th straight New York City basketball championship, the girls on the Murry Bergtraum team must deal with the killing in September of Tayshana Murphy, a star teammate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DIONNE COE has a sweet outside shot and eyes that light up when she sees a loose ball on the court. Shequana Harris has quick hands and an explosive first step to the basket. Both seniors, they arrived at school in September confident that this would be their year — college scouts, scholarship offers, a brilliant star teammate and a chance to lead their school, Murry Bergtraum High School for Business Careers, toward its 14th consecutive city championship. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/nyregion/11BERGTRAUM1/11BERGTRAUM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 757, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/nyregion/11BERGTRAUM1/11BERGTRAUM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/nyregion/11BERGTRAUM1/11BERGTRAUM1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing With Dedication', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playing With Dedication', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morningside Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grezinsky, Ed', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bergtraum, Murry, High School', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Tayshana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T10:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abbdb7de-7fa7-5374-b506-04a74b5c5e0a', 'word_count': 2599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abbdb7de-7fa7-5374-b506-04a74b5c5e0a'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans are dangerously close to having none of their candidates be acceptable to rank-and-file voters and the party establishment. Because finding a nominee who is broadly acceptable to different party constituencies is the foremost goal of any party during its nomination process, it seems distinctly possible that Republicans might begin to look elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/the-real-g-o-p-dark-horse-none-of-the-above/', 'snippet': 'Republicans are dangerously close to having none of their candidates be acceptable to rank-and-file voters and the party establishment. Because finding a nominee who is broadly acceptable to different party constituencies is the foremost goal of a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two of my favorite analysts, Rhodes Cook and Josh Putnam, have a good debate going about just how plausible it is that a Republican who is not currently running for president could enter the race later and potentially win it (probably necessitating a brokered convention). Those of you who follow my Twitter feed will know that I think Mr. Cook has the stronger side of the argument; I think there is a small but nontrivial chance that the Republican nominee could be someone like Jeb Bush, Paul Ryan, Mitch Daniels, Tim Pawlenty or Chris Christie. (In fact, I was speculating about these scenarios as long as a month ago.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Real G.O.P. Dark Horse: None of the Above', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T10:38:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7d19246-41d1-5ffe-a7b0-9ae8d0bdf6a8', 'word_count': 1378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7d19246-41d1-5ffe-a7b0-9ae8d0bdf6a8'}, {'abstract': 'After a high-scoring early season, N.F.L. offenses have entered a period of market correction.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/week-14-matchups-offenses-tighten-their-belts/', 'snippet': 'After a high-scoring early season, N.F.L. offenses have entered a period of market correction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Texans (9-3) at Bengals (7-5)Sunday, 1 p.m.Line: Bengals by 3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 14 Matchups: Offenses Tighten Their Belts', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T11:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d32c3da-62ea-5114-b9a4-05f769ff5cb2', 'word_count': 2345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d32c3da-62ea-5114-b9a4-05f769ff5cb2'}, {'abstract': 'Tiësto, the Dutch D.J., will get his own music channel on Sirius XM satellite radio early next year. Tiësto’s Club Life Radio will be at Channel 340 on Edge Satellite radios and on the Sirius XM Internet radio site.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/tiestos-club-life-finds-a-new-home-on-sirius-xm/', 'snippet': 'Tiësto, the Dutch D.J., will get his own music channel on Sirius XM satellite radio early next year. Tiësto’s Club Life Radio will be at Channel 340 on Edge Satellite radios and on the Sirius XM Internet radio site.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tiësto, long a major figure in European electronic dance music, will begin hosting a music channel on Sirius XM satellite radio early next year, a spokesman for the radio network said. The channel will broadcast dance music in a variety of styles selected by Tiësto, the Dutch D.J. who is known for his six-hour live sets and his memorable performance during the opening ceremony of the 2004 Olympics in Athens. A master of trance and progressive house music, he has sold more than 761,000 records in the last decade. His new channel, called Tiësto’s Club Life Radio, will feature Tiësto’s own mixes and music by artists on his label, Musical Freedom, which he started in late 2010. “It will be an awesome way to showcase the incredible artists on my own label, play new material of my own as well as other music that I’m really passionate about,” said Tiësto, whose real name is Tijs Verwest. Tiësto currently hosts a Saturday show on Sirius’s Electric Area channel, but that program will be moved to the new dial setting: Channel 340 on Edge satellite radios and on the Sirius XM Internet radio site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tiësto’s Club Life Finds a New Home on Sirius XM', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tiesto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'XM Satellite Radio Holdings Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T11:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5886687-6ccf-5690-9f85-131612fd075d', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5886687-6ccf-5690-9f85-131612fd075d'}, {'abstract': 'The United States might learn a good deal from Germany’s approach to health care insurance, which provides both a broad net and significant individual choice, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/social-insurance-and-individual-freedom/', 'snippet': 'The United States might learn a good deal from Germany’s approach to health care insurance, which provides both a broad net and significant individual choice, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uwe E. Reinhardt is an economics professor at Princeton. He has some financial interests in the health care field.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Social Insurance and Individual Freedom', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T11:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uwe E. Reinhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uwe', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Reinhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8283005-5901-590d-9d1a-7a650149e641', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8283005-5901-590d-9d1a-7a650149e641'}, {'abstract': 'The outgoing Econoline is a fixture of utility crews, metal-scrapping outfits and rock bands. Its Europe-derived replacement, the Transit, has a big wheelbase to fill.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/ford-e-series-requiem-for-the-band-van/', 'snippet': 'The outgoing Econoline is a fixture of utility crews, metal-scrapping outfits and rock bands. Its Europe-derived replacement, the Transit, has a big wheelbase to fill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Ford Econoline, marketed by Ford in its later years as the E-Series, is being replaced by the Transit, a model developed by Ford’s European division and undergoing tests at the automaker’s proving grounds in Michigan. But as the Econoline nameplate goes, so goes a legacy steeped in rock ‘n’ roll — and in exceptional cases, chlorine and pizza sauce.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford E-Series: Requiem for the Band Van', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T11:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64832860-03a6-5c7a-b169-bef3c93050b8', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64832860-03a6-5c7a-b169-bef3c93050b8'}, {'abstract': 'Musée de la Chasse et de la Nature in Paris features works by the contemporary artist Françoise Pétrovitch.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/whimsical-works-join-a-museums-menagerie-in-paris/', 'snippet': 'Musée de la Chasse et de la Nature in Paris features works by the contemporary artist Françoise Pétrovitch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Musée de la Chasse et de la Nature (62, rue des Archives; 33-1-53-01- 92-40, www.chassenature.org) is a singular space. Dedicated to exploring man’s interaction with nature, it has a permanent collection featuring hunting scene tapestries, ornately carved firearms, curiosity cabinets and taxidermied animals. And now, the museum is juxtaposing its rather sober items with whimsical pieces by the contemporary artist Françoise Pétrovitch, whose works will be on display through Jan. 22.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whimsical Works Join a Museum’s Menagerie in Paris', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T11:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Moroz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moroz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85600eeb-0cbe-5fd1-9f60-ecaa9ed5e2e7', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85600eeb-0cbe-5fd1-9f60-ecaa9ed5e2e7'}, {'abstract': 'MMG Worldwide and Y Partnership are combining to create what they describe as the largest travel marketing firm.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/merger-planned-between-travel-focused-ad-agencies/', 'snippet': 'MMG Worldwide and Y Partnership are combining to create what they describe as the largest travel marketing firm.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Two agencies that specialize in campaigns for travel advertisers are combining to create what they describe as the largest travel marketing firm. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Merger Planned Between Travel-Focused Ad Agencies', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T12:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7d2b41f-dc22-5339-98a9-7fd25ff6fd1c', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7d2b41f-dc22-5339-98a9-7fd25ff6fd1c'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike many of its competitors, Johnson Security does not try to compete on price.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/resolved-to-continue-growing-in-the-new-year/', 'snippet': 'Unlike many of its competitors, Johnson Security does not try to compete on price.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jessica Johnson knows she must continue to innovate if she wants her business, Johnson Security Bureau, to maintain its impressive growth rate in 2012. This year, the company’s annual sales have doubled — just as they did in 2010.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Refusing to Compete on Price', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Jessica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T12:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5318af6-14d0-5fe6-ba62-73c8a0724ab3', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5318af6-14d0-5fe6-ba62-73c8a0724ab3'}, {'abstract': 'Japanese financial regulators called on  Friday for penalties against Citigroup and UBS, accusing them of trying to manipulate interest rates at which they borrow from each other.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/japan-calls-for-action-on-citigroup-and-ubs/', 'snippet': 'Japanese financial regulators called on  Friday for penalties against Citigroup and UBS, accusing them of trying to manipulate interest rates at which they borrow from each other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Japanese financial regulators called on Friday for penalties against Citigroup and UBS, accusing them of trying to manipulate interest rates at which they borrow from each other, broadening an international inquiry into some of the world’s biggest banks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Calls for Action Against Citigroup and UBS', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UBS A.G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T12:29:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c875933-d8e6-56a7-9eb0-3042bc58c81f', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c875933-d8e6-56a7-9eb0-3042bc58c81f'}, {'abstract': 'Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and other international leaders spoke out in favor of Internet freedom at a conference in the Netherlands.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/daily-report-clinton-pushes-for-internet-freedom/', 'snippet': 'Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and other international leaders spoke out in favor of Internet freedom at a conference in the Netherlands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the opening of a two-day conference on digital freedom in the Netherlands, Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and other international leaders argued that countries and businesses should fight increasing efforts to restrict access to the Internet by repressive governments and even some democratic ones, reported Steven Lee Myers and Heather Timmons in Friday’s New York Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'