cle/f31eea1c-9327-56b3-924e-e78f83a2bd18', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f31eea1c-9327-56b3-924e-e78f83a2bd18'}, {'abstract': 'The rapid creation of a set piece for Ryan Gosling’s performance as an Invisible Man.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/painting-ryan-gosling-the-video/', 'snippet': 'The rapid creation of a set piece for Ryan Gosling’s performance as an Invisible Man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For our video of Ryan Gosling as the Invisible Man, the director’s sister, Vanessa Prager, created a painting of the actor. Gosling liked it so much, he bought it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Painting Ryan Gosling, the Video', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosling, Ryan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:43:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36bfaeef-e1bb-51e6-99cd-e2cefe42a362', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36bfaeef-e1bb-51e6-99cd-e2cefe42a362'}, {'abstract': 'An inventive and economical restaurant experiment in Windsor Terrace, Brooklyn, has ended.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/a-coffee-shop-loses-its-nighttime-alter-ego/', 'snippet': 'An inventive and economical restaurant experiment in Windsor Terrace, Brooklyn, has ended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anybody want to buy a custom-made portable bar, cheap? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Coffee Shop Loses Its Nighttime Alter Ego', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Time-Sharing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Windsor Terrace (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Farmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35a51aed-46c3-5934-b6f1-32a2ec31793f', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35a51aed-46c3-5934-b6f1-32a2ec31793f'}, {'abstract': 'New classical composers are finding a home in clubs. So what does that mean for traditional musical institutions?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/arts/music/as-new-composers-flourish-where-will-they-be-heard.html', 'snippet': 'New classical composers are finding a home in clubs. So what does that mean for traditional musical institutions?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE critics have long argued vehemently that if major musical institutions hope to be regarded as vital, modern institutions, they must keep their listeners (and performers) in touch with the ideas, trendy or otherwise, that excite the composers of their time. But truth be told, we are a little bipolar on that subject. Though we criticize the big organizations for fostering a museum culture, we actually value the museums they have become. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11ALT2_SPAN/11ALT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11ALT2_SPAN/11ALT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'