': 'His muse, the Gowanus, was an unlikely one. The Lens blog published an appreciation of the Brooklyn photographer Jose Gaytan, who died this week.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/tell-them-that-i-love-photography/', 'snippet': 'His muse, the Gowanus, was an unlikely one. The Lens blog published an appreciation of the Brooklyn photographer Jose Gaytan, who died this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jose Gaytan wore lots of hats — dog lover, camera salesman, guitarist. But he topped off all of that by being a dedicated photographer who chronicled the ever-changing Brooklyn waterfront. A masterly colorist, he found beauty in unlikely places and could render the Gowanus Canal into something worthy of Renoir.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Tell Them That I Love Photography’', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gowanus (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gowanus Canal (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:49:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58edc687-42d9-5a2f-b0f6-ffd461887f83', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58edc687-42d9-5a2f-b0f6-ffd461887f83'}, {'abstract': 'After the mishandling of cases resurfaces, Democratic lawmakers call on the top law enforcement official in Maricopa County to step down.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/us/sheriff-joe-arpaio-criticized-over-handling-of-sex-crimes-cases.html', 'snippet': 'After the mishandling of cases resurfaces, Democratic lawmakers call on the top law enforcement official in Maricopa County to step down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GUADALUPE, Ariz. — Although Joe Arpaio calls himself “America’s Toughest Sheriff,” a growing chorus of local critics want another title for him: Retired.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/us/10sheriff-cnd/10sheriff-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 733, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/us/10sheriff-cnd/10sheriff-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/10/us/10sheriff-cnd/10sheriff-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Arpaio Is Criticized Over Handling of Sex-Crimes Cases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sex Crimes Were Ignored, Say Reports On Sheriff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARPAIO, JOSEPH M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maricopa County (Ariz)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phoenix (Ariz)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:51:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Lacey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lacey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42898a3a-091b-5d8a-a7d2-b249f78dd744', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42898a3a-091b-5d8a-a7d2-b249f78dd744'}, {'abstract': 'Just ask Herman Cain and Tiger Woods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/gloria-allred.html', 'snippet': 'Just ask Herman Cain and Tiger Woods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During a news conference with your client Sharon Bialek, you said that Herman Cain “decided to provide her with his idea of a stimulus package.” Are humorous phrases a hallmark of yours? In some instances? Yes. Is it in every? No. Is it appropriate in every one? No. Is it appropriate in some? Yes. Do feminists have a sense of humor? Yes.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11talk/mag-11Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11talk/mag-11Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11talk/mag-11Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Mess With Gloria Allred', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lawyer To The Brokenhearted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bialek, Sharon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allred, Gloria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/320bc20f-9a1e-5b75-82b9-bf650cc110c7', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/320bc20f-9a1e-5b75-82b9-bf650cc110c7'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/the-fracturing-of-pennsylvania.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I take issue with Eliza Griswold’s description (Nov. 20 )of the farmland in Washington County, Pa., as “hardscrabble.” As a young man, George Washington surveyed this land for Virginia, of which state Western Pennsylvania was then part. He purchased huge tracts for himself, because he knew it was valuable. Rich and prosperous farms developed, once he sold the land to settlers, and these continued to flourish well into the 20th century.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fracturing of Pennsylvania', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fracturing of Pennsylvania', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/e24df43b-02f4-5c64-870b-9299dad10407', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e24df43b-02f4-5c64-870b-9299dad10407'}, {'abstract': 'Twisted Dixie, a team of paranormal investigators in South Carolina, camp out at night in clients’ houses, barns, businesses or woods and “document paranormal activity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/the-dixie-ghostbusters.html', 'snippet': 'Twisted Dixie, a team of paranormal investigators in South Carolina, camp out at night in clients’ houses, barns, businesses or woods and “document paranormal activity.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Except for the eerie flickering of our flashlights, we batted away low branches and overgrown shrubs of the old cotton plantation in total darkness. There was no moonlight. It was, Grady Carter noted, “a perfect night for ghosts.” At that point his nephew Andy held up a hand and pointed into the woods. “I heard voices,” Andy said. We all stopped. Chris Carter, his cousin, whispered, “I see a red light.” “The spirits of dead slaves,” Grady confirmed. “A demonic orb.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11here1_span/11here_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11here1_span/11here_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11here1_span/mag-11Here-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11here1_span/11here_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11here1_span/11here_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dixie Ghostbusters', 'kicker': 'You Are Here: Antreville, S.C.', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Ya Gonna Call?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Occult Sciences', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ghosts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:53:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Jordan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9eb4885-68d8-5918-9b7b-15f698b512c0', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9eb4885-68d8-5918-9b7b-15f698b512c0'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/can-the-bulldog-be-saved.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benoit Denizet-Lewis highlights the lifetime of misery that bulldogs have to endure. In my eight years of clinical veterinary practice, 100 percent of the bulldogs I have seen suffer respiratory distress. Anybody persuaded by this article to place welfare first — and disregard what is deemed to be cute — when it comes to choosing a dog saves one more deliberately handicapped animal a lifetime of struggle. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can the Bulldog Be Saved?', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can The Bulldogbe Saved?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breeding of Animals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:53:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdeae893-897b-5b46-915f-09e97cb627aa', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdeae893-897b-5b46-915f-09e97cb627aa'}, {'abstract': 'How a 2-by-2-feet sheet of metal with black lettering on a white background evolved into a red octagonal icon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/stop-sign.html', 'snippet': 'How a 2-by-2-feet sheet of metal with black lettering on a white background evolved into a red octagonal icon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early automobile age, American streets existed in a Hobbesian, drive-or-be-plastered state of anarchy. “Not only were the streets in those days completely disgusting and filthy, but there were horses and bicycles, and it was just completely chaotic,” says Joshua Schank, C.E.O. of the Eno Transportation Foundation, whose namesake and founder, William Phelps Eno, is widely credited with conceiving the stop sign at the turn of the 20th century.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11wmt1/mag-11WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 805, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11wmt1/mag-11WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11wmt1/mag-11WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stop Sign Wasn’t Always Red', 'kicker': 'Who Made That?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '(Stop)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Signals and Signs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T16:57:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b0315a3-b4db-5a1c-bae3-2a052609d723', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b0315a3-b4db-5a1c-bae3-2a052609d723'}, {'abstract': 'Is technology killing indigenous languages or saving them? Well, you may soon be able to text in N’Ko.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/magazine/everyone-speaks-text-message.html', 'snippet': 'Is technology killing indigenous languages or saving them? Well, you may soon be able to text in N’Ko.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Ibrahima Traore takes his sons to a park in Montclair, N.J., he often sits on a bench and reads. He reads English, French and Arabic, but most of the time he reads N’Ko, a language few speakers of those languages would recognize. N’Ko is the standardized writing system for Mande languages, a family of closely related tongues — among them Traore’s language of Mandinka, but also Jula, Bamana, Koyaga, Marka — spoken, for the most part, in eight West African countries, by some 35 million people. N’Ko looks like a cross between Arabic and ancient Norse runes, written from right to left in a blocky script with the letters connected underneath. Traore types e-mail to his family on his laptop in N’Ko, works on his Web site in N’Ko, tweets in N’Ko on his iPhone and iPad and reads books and newspapers written in N’Ko to prepare for the N’Ko classes he teaches in the Bronx and for his appearances on an Internet radio program to discuss cultural issues around the use of N’Ko.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11languages1_span/11languages1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11languages1_span/11languages1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11languages1_span/mag-11languages-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 608, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11languages1_span/11languages1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/magazine/11languages1_span/11languages1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Everyone Speaks Text Message', 'kicker': 'Ventures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everyone Speaks Text Message', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indigenous People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guinea', 'rank': 5, 'major':