9/h-p-s-non-decision-decision-on-webos/', 'snippet': 'H.P.’s has decided to open its webOS operating system, which was the core of the Palm company, to the open-source programming community. It could have shut down or sold Palm, but this way it gains some relevance in the open-source world, and someo...', 'lead_paragraph': ' Hewlett-Packard is out of the operating system business – sort of.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'H.P.’s Nondecision Decision on WebOS', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Open-Source Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hewlett-Packard Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T20:59:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca2a8083-1279-52a7-90e3-fe55476f357f', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca2a8083-1279-52a7-90e3-fe55476f357f'}, {'abstract': 'The ‘Mary Poppins’ team will tackle another children’s classic.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/wind-in-the-willows-musical-in-the-works/', 'snippet': 'The ‘Mary Poppins’ team will tackle another children’s classic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two classic British children’s stories separated by several decades and a huge difference in sensibility – though both share an affection for rodents (a water rat in one, an army of trained squirrels in the other) – appear to be coming to the stage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Wind in the Willows’ Musical in the Works', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Roald', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fellowes, Julian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grahame, Kenneth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendes, Sam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiles, George', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/571f5984-62c4-5d8b-b123-1ccc57ebe36f', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/571f5984-62c4-5d8b-b123-1ccc57ebe36f'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen Adly Guirgis on the casting controversy', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/theater-talkback-diminishing-the-hat-about-that-casting-controversy/', 'snippet': 'Stephen Adly Guirgis on the casting controversy', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month the playwright Stephen Adly Guirgis went public on Facebook with his concerns about the casting of non-Hispanic actors in the roles of Puerto Rican characters in a production of his play “The __________ With the Hat” at TheaterWorks in Hartford, Conn. After attending a recent performance, Mr. Guirgis said that while the two actors were “talented and dedicated,” he found the production to be “dangerously close to a minstrel show.” ArtsBeat asked Mr. Guirgis to write about why he raised the issue publically.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: Diminishing The ‘Hat’? About That Casting Controversy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:03:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Adly Guirgis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'Adly', 'lastname': 'Guirgis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01951d78-c395-5643-ad7b-24f9a2b0f052', 'word_count': 1064, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01951d78-c395-5643-ad7b-24f9a2b0f052'}, {'abstract': 'The German director Wim Wenders has created “Pina,” a 3-D tribute to the choreographer Pina Bausch, who died in 2009.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/movies/wim-wenders-on-pina-his-film-on-pina-bauschs-dances.html', 'snippet': 'The German director Wim Wenders has created “Pina,” a 3-D tribute to the choreographer Pina Bausch, who died in 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': '“PINA” is a dance movie like no other. This documentary, by Wim Wenders, focuses on the German choreographer Pina Bausch and her company Tanztheater Wuppertal, but in a disarming twist it uses 3-D technology to take audiences inside the artist’s universe. It’s an immersive experience that at moments makes viewers feel as if they were dancing too. The film, one of 15 on the shortlist for the best documentary feature Oscar, was in the works when Bausch died unexpectedly at 68 in 2009. So Mr. Wenders, director of “Buena Vista Social Club” and “Wings of Desire,” decided to cancel it. Only after her dancers pressed him did he decide to finish the movie, which features excerpts from four major pieces and scenes of the performers in and around Wuppertal, Germany — on hilltops, in coal mines, in monorails and on medians — bringing to life a dreamscape only Bausch and Mr. Wenders could have conjured. Julie Bloom spoke with Mr. Wenders in New York about the film, which opens on Dec. 23. Here’s an edited version of their talk.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11WENDERS1_SPAN/11WENDERS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 234, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11WENDERS1_SPAN/11WENDERS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 234}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11WENDERS1_SPAN/11WENDERS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11WENDERS1_SPAN/11WENDERS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11WENDERS1_SPAN/11WENDERS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Vision of Dance, Preserved in 3-D', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Vision of Dance, Preserved in 3-D', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bausch, Pina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Three-Dimensional Devices and Effects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wenders, Wim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tanztheater Wuppertal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:05:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/f70a6d99-b101-5e54-af6c-a855ea53438d', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f70a6d99-b101-5e54-af6c-a855ea53438d'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Clinton presents his views on the country’s challenges and explains his plan for creating jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/back-to-work-why-we-need-smart-government-for-a-strong-economy-by-bill-clinton-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Bill Clinton presents his views on the country’s challenges and explains his plan for creating jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill Clinton’s new book, “Back to Work,” is less a bold plan to create jobs than it is a passionate rebuttal of “our 30-year antigovernment obsession.” That obsession, he insists, is public enemy No. 1. He also seems to be sending a barely disguised message to Barack Obama to join him in confronting the anti\xadgovernment chorus.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MADRICK/11MADRICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MADRICK/11MADRICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MADRICK/11MADRICK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MADRICK/11MADRICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MADRICK/11MADRICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Bill Clinton Would Do', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What He Would Do', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Madrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Madrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac798a09-3e68-5338-86f1-a47afef3eff3', 'word_count': 1337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac798a09-3e68-5338-86f1-a47afef3eff3'}, {'abstract': 'Condoleezza Rice offers her account of George W. Bush’s presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/no-higher-honor-a-memoir-of-my-years-in-washington-by-condoleezza-rice-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Condoleezza Rice offers her account of George W. Bush’s presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They were an unlikely pair: the cerebral A-student from a striving black family and the son of privilege who as a candidate for president couldn’t name Pakistan’s military ruler. But together they forged a vision of a muscular United States striking out on its own. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11CHIRA/11CHIRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11CHIRA/11CHIRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11CHIRA/11CHIRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11CHIRA/11CHIRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11CHIRA/11CHIRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Condoleezza Rice Looks Back', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'State Witness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cheney, Dick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Condoleezza', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rumsfeld, Donald H', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:09:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Chira', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chira', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8711ca57-c666-57b9-ac45-a4f1b0b63fef', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8711ca57-c666-57b9-ac45-a4f1b0b63fef'}, {'abstract': 'Carl T. Bogus traces the making of the man who remade conservatism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/buckley-william-f-buckley-jr-and-the-rise-of-american-conservatism-by-carl-t-bogus-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Carl T. Bogus traces the making of the man who remade conservatism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William F. Buckley Jr. was an immodest man with much to be immodest about. Not only was he the high priest of the modern American conservative movement and the founding editor in chief of its leading intellectual publication, National Review; he was also a gifted polemicist, best-selling novelist, sesquipedalian speaker, television star, political candidate, yachtsman, harpsichordist, wit and bon vivant. Small wonder that I once saw him nod approvingly when a tongue-tied freshman referred to his 1951 autobiographical best seller as “God as Man at Yale.” He performed his many roles with such panache, and such obvious enjoyment of being William F. Buckley Jr., that he captivated people who otherwise would have despised someone who did much to move the United States politically to the right from the early 1950s until his death in 2008. But even liberals had to laugh when Buckley, asked whether he slouched in his chair as host of the TV program “Firing Line” because he couldn’t think on his feet, drawled, “It is hard . . . to stand up . . . under the weight . . . of all that I know.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11KABASERVICE1/11KABASERVICE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11KABASERVICE1/11KABASERVICE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11KABASERVICE1/11KABASERVICE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11KABASERVICE1/11KABASERVICE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11KABASERVICE1/11KABASERVICE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'William F. Buckley Jr.: Right Man, Right Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Right Man, Right Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, William F Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:09:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoffrey Kabaservice', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kabaservice', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da916da-4220-50a5-b8c7-08e226a877be', 'word_count': 1234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da916da-4220-50a5-b8c7-08e226a877be'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in Anita Desai’s three novellas are in search of personal fulfillment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/the-artist-of-disappearance-three-novellas-by-anita-desai-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in Anita Desai’s three novellas are in search of personal fulfillment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes a mango is just a mango. This is rarely the case in Indian novels, where mangoes tend to be luminescent orbs dangling in steamy air, glistening with sweetness, sex and Being itself, waiting to be plucked, caressed, birthed. Either that or they’re muddy and rotten and piled high on a dirty road, surrounded by rancid garbage, rank cooking fires, beggar children and grinning, greasy swindlers. In other words, mangoes in India’s literary fiction are much like India in literary fiction: distinguished by pleasing aromas or permanent anarchy, if not some chutneyed combination. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11Boyagoda/11Boyagoda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11Boyagoda/11Boyagoda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11Boyagoda/11Boyagoda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anita Desai on Longing and Striving', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hidden in Plain Sight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Desai, Anita', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:09:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/88382956-f91e-5e43-a6e4-e32fe382c84c', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88382956-f91e-5e43-a6e4-e32fe382c84c'}, {'abstract': 'In Peter Orner’s novel, three generations of men share a fundamental lack of understanding of the women they love.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/love-and-shame-and-love-by-peter-orner-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Peter Orner’s novel, three generations of men share a fundamental lack of understanding of the women they love.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Love and Shame and Love”: It looks more like a line of free-form, possibly adolescent, poetry than the title of a novel, with the repeated “love” trying to buffer the fragile “shame”-filled center. Then again, that verbal image applies well to Alexander Popper, known to all simply as Popper, the melancholy, searching main character of Peter Orner’s third work of fiction. With its short and evocative chapters, its charged and playful language, its movement to and fro in time, the novel is indeed poetic. It’s epic too, encompassing Popper’s life from childhood into his early 30s as well as chunks of the life spans of his parents and two of his grandparents, with check-ins on various other relatives.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Russo/Russo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 908, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Russo/Russo-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Russo/Russo-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Searching for Love in the Windy City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sons of the Second City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orner, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:09:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1e0554e-31d8-5b19-9ce1-ee5e29704f3a', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1e0554e-31d8-5b19-9ce1-ee5e29704f3a'}, {'abstract': 'Percival Everett toys with the murder-mystery genre while pursuing larger themes of identity and truth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/assumption-by-percival-everett-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Percival Everett toys with the murder-mystery genre while pursuing larger themes of identity and truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Mexico is a place of great beauty and great melancholy, and, like all of the American West, a magnet for misfits. Willa Cather captured these aspects in her classic 1927 novel “Death Comes for the Archbishop.” Now Percival Everett, author of genre-bending novels like “I Am Not Sidney Poitier” and “Glyph,” vividly evokes the same elements in “Assumption,” his new trilogy of interwoven murder mysteries. Investigating all three cases is the enigmatic Ogden Walker, deputy sheriff in Plata, a fictional county in the northern part of the state, physically not far from the health farms and art galleries of trendy Taos, but in all other respects a world away. Plata is the opposite of trendy. It’s a blink-and-you-missed-it burg of shabby adobe dwellings and aging pickup trucks and trailers like the one Ogden lives in.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11BOYLAN/11BOYLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11BOYLAN/11BOYLAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11BOYLAN/11BOYLAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11BOYLAN/11BOYLAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11BOYLAN/11BOYLAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Deputy Sheriff as Misfit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hostile Territories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Everett, Percival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:10:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roger Boylan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boylan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/91ee0840-ef46-5bb5-bd88-c845220a238e', 'word_count': 1086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91ee0840-ef46-5bb5-bd88-c845220a238e'}, {'abstract': 'A young couple from Dresden go bed-hopping abroad and risk defecting just ahead of German reunification.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/adam-and-evelyn-by-ingo-schulzetranslated-by-john-e-woods-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A young couple from Dresden go bed-hopping abroad and risk defecting just ahead of German reunification.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The novelist Ingo Schulze was born in 1962 in Dresden. He was born, therefore, into a country called the German Democratic Republic — a country that no longer exists. The Berlin Wall fell on Nov. 9, 1989, and when Schulze was 27 East Germany disappeared.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Thirlwell/Thirlwell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1007, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Thirlwell/Thirlwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Thirlwell/Thirlwell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Before the Wall Fell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Before the Fall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulze, Ingo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, John E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:10:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ADAM THIRLWELL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'THIRLWELL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/23188a1e-1030-582b-be98-4fa5f3bf6043', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23188a1e-1030-582b-be98-4fa5f3bf6043'}, {'abstract': 'Like most difficult poems, Luis de Góngora’s “Solitudes,” recently published in a new translation, is often preoccupied with simplicity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/the-solitudes-by-luis-de-gongora-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Like most difficult poems, Luis de Góngora’s “Solitudes,” recently published in a new translation, is often preoccupied with simplicity.', 'lead_paragraph': '“But what is this poem about?” This dread question stalks almost every poetry classroom, and it’s vanquished only to return with a tenacity that would intimidate Michael Myers. Most recently, Ernie Lepore, a professor at Rutgers, took a swing at it in The New York Times’s philosophy blog, The Stone. Lepore is interested in what’s sometimes called “the heresy of paraphrase” — the idea that what a poem is really about is best represented by the poem itself. He concludes that while the usual reasoning behind the heresy claim is suspect, the idea itself is basically right. Poetry, Lepore argues, is not just about its paraphrasable data (and what we might make of that data), but also about the way that data is articulated. So Frost’s “Mending Wall” is about a not-so-bright farmer, a bunch of rocks and their various symbolic accouterments; yet it’s also about the break between the word “balance” in Line 18 and “Stay” in Line 19.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11ORR/11ORR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 663, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11ORR/11ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11ORR/11ORR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rediscovery of Luis de Góngora', 'kicker': 'On Poetry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ways of Saying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grossman, Edith', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:10:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Orr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcbb14c5-3a2a-5a24-93ee-dfd85e3cf6f4', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcbb14c5-3a2a-5a24-93ee-dfd85e3cf6f4'}, {'abstract': 'Bradford Morrow’s tales walk the line between clarity and chaos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/the-uninnocent-stories-by-bradford-morrow-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Bradford Morrow’s tales walk the line between clarity and chaos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bradford Morrow’s work has won him acclaim, but this inescapably grim collection of stories may not bolster that reputation. Here’s a taste of what his readers will encounter.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Myerson/Myerson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Myerson/Myerson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Myerson/Myerson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grim Tales of Clarity and Chaos', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trust No One', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morrow, Bradford', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Myerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Myerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a27806aa-4f48-51ed-996f-693c9e7cda0f', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a27806aa-4f48-51ed-996f-693c9e7cda0f'}, {'abstract': 'This book recounts the Victorian-era search for the river’s headwaters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/explorers-of-the-nile-the-triumph-and-tragedy-of-a-great-victorian-adventure-by-tim-jeal-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'This book recounts the Victorian-era search for the river’s headwaters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early 1870s in what is now Zambia, David Livingstone, the greatest of all Victorian explorers, led several expeditions in search of the source of the Nile. At times, he could barely walk, ulcers having chewed through the “muscle, tendon and bone” of his feet. Riddled with malaria, plagued with piles and weakened by pneumonia, he had pulled out several of his rotting teeth using strong thread and a heavy pistol. He had been attacked by roasting heat, pouring rain, tick fever, hostile slavers and leeches “as close as smallpox.” By 1873, he had an excruciatingly painful blood clot in his intestine the size of a fist. In this extremity of suffering, he found time to appreciate the “sweet” voice of a tree frog, but it is another entry in his diary — of truly towering understatement — that most perfectly sums up the wry fortitude of men like Livingstone: “It is not all pleasure this exploration.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Macintyre1/Macintyre1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Macintyre1/Macintyre1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Macintyre1/Macintyre1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Macintyre1/Macintyre1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/Macintyre1/Macintyre1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Risks and Rewards of Exploring the Nile', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upstream Costs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeal, Tim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nile River', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Macintyre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macintyre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6eb3bace-21f3-5f49-9a57-a133ddf76af8', 'word_count': 1286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6eb3bace-21f3-5f49-9a57-a133ddf76af8'}, {'abstract': 'The Bronx may have been burning, but downtown Manhattan’s mid-’70s music scene was even hotter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/love-goes-to-buildings-on-fire-five-years-in-new-york-that-changed-music-forever-by-will-hermes-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Bronx may have been burning, but downtown Manhattan’s mid-’70s music scene was even hotter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first record made by Talking Heads, a single released in February 1977, was called “Love → Building on Fire.” If you knew about Talking Heads then, you might have been the kind of person who thought the formula-like vector tucked into the title was inspired by the deadpan propositions that the conceptual artist Lawrence Weiner was stenciling at that time on the walls of SoHo galleries — which, in turn, might have made you think that the song itself was some sort of self-conscious concept art, sonically and lyrically examining the idea of a soulful pop song even as the insistent beat and David Byrne’s strained shouts and warbles made the song a real one, exuberant, funny in its way, and infectious. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MARZORATI/11MARZORATI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MARZORATI/11MARZORATI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MARZORATI/11MARZORATI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MARZORATI/11MARZORATI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/books/review/11MARZORATI/11MARZORATI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The CBGB Effect', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Real Live Wire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hermes, Will', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:11:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gerald Marzorati', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gerald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marzorati', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/856dae1f-0aa0-5c8b-91e6-fc3ae17efcdd', 'word_count': 1327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/856dae1f-0aa0-5c8b-91e6-fc3ae17efcdd'}, {'abstract': 'In this first novel, two sisters lose their relationship over a miscommunication.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/the-sisters-by-nancy-jensen-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this first novel, two sisters lose their relationship over a miscommunication.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ties that bind three generations of women also wrench them apart in “The Sisters,” Nancy Jensen’s first novel, which spans almost a century, from the 1920s to the current moment. Like so many painful family stories, this one begins with a miscommunication. The novel opens in 1927. Two sisters live in the hardscrabble town of Juniper, Ky. Bertie, about to receive her eighth-grade diploma, dreams of marrying her beau, Wallace, “just maybe, in another year.” Meanwhile her older sister, Mabel, secretively plans to leave home and escape their monstrous stepfather. Alas, things go wrong — disastrously and not quite plausibly. The stepfather is found dead. Mabel and Wallace vanish. Bertie assumes she has been betrayed. The sisters are lost to each other.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Novel of Sisters Unbound', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dead to Her', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:11:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Leavitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leavitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/884351a8-aaa1-5c85-a038-26ed83a8201d', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/884351a8-aaa1-5c85-a038-26ed83a8201d'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Gershom Gorenberg’s “The Unmaking of Israel.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/books/review/the-unmaking-of-israel.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Gershom Gorenberg’s “The Unmaking of Israel.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Unmaking of Israel’', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Unmaking of Israel’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T21:11:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://ar