oks', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d7adb91-42ef-5eff-96fc-7227d9d4140f', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d7adb91-42ef-5eff-96fc-7227d9d4140f'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Barnes’s novel “The Sense of an Ending” continues lurking on the best-seller list, one of several testaments to the influence of literary prizes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Julian Barnes’s novel “The Sense of an Ending” continues lurking on the best-seller list, one of several testaments to the influence of literary prizes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BIG DADDY BARNES: As literary prizes go, nothing beats the Pulitzer for securing a novel’s place on the best-seller list: 9 of the last 10 fiction winners have spent time here. (The exception is Geraldine Brooks’s “March,” which won in 2006 but never cracked the list.) What about the Man Booker Prize in Britain? It’s almost as powerful. Seven of the last 10 winners have gone on to become New York Times best sellers, including this year’s pick, “The Sense of an Ending,” by Julian Barnes, which has logged five weeks thus far and is still lurking on the hardcover extended list at No. 18.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18inside-pic/18inside-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18inside-pic/18inside-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value':