://audio/89513974-dba5-542d-9b27-c9d93be37f9b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/89513974-dba5-542d-9b27-c9d93be37f9b'}, {'abstract': 'A public-private transit project unveiled in 2002 was meant to be a centerpiece of Rick Perry’s governorship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/us/politics/perry-survived-failure-of-his-grand-transportation-plan.html', 'snippet': 'A public-private transit project unveiled in 2002 was meant to be a centerpiece of Rick Perry’s governorship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUSTIN, Tex. — In the bachelor-style apartment of his legislative years, where Rick Perry liked “to kick back and watch football with a cold one,” the future Texas governor forged a lasting bond with a hard-charging roommate who preferred smoking a pipe, studying flip charts and strategizing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/us/PERRY-1/PERRY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/10/us/PERRY-1/PERRY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/us/PERRY-1/PERRY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/us/PERRY-1/PERRY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/10/us/PERRY-1/PERRY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Perry Survived Even as His Big Plan for Texas Failed', 'kicker': 'The Long Run', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perry Survived Even as His Big Plan for Texas Failed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:24:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Sontag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sontag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70024cbf-87f0-5d6d-bc14-b95b62e92a5a', 'word_count': 2359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70024cbf-87f0-5d6d-bc14-b95b62e92a5a'}, {'abstract': 'At the chic Bowery Hotel, where a star of “Margin Call” was paying tribute to the Occupy Wall Street movement.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/scenesters-and-occupy-wall-streeters-together-at-last/', 'snippet': 'At the chic Bowery Hotel, where a star of “Margin Call” was paying tribute to the Occupy Wall Street movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was Radical Chic  redux. This week, the Bagger stopped by an event organized by a slew of New York scenesters and hot young things — including Alexa Chung, the MTV host turned designer; the photographer Aaron Stern; Zoe Kravitz, the young actress, and her boyfriend, Penn Badgley, a star of “Gossip Girl” and “Margin Call” — in support of the Occupy Wall Street movement. It was held at the swank Bowery Hotel, in a space normally reserved for Strokes afterparties and other downtown bacchanals. There were doormen and bowtied waiters (but no open bar).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scenesters and Occupy Wall Streeters, Together at Last', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:25:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20135719-c485-5913-94d5-66a0cfa2a5ee', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20135719-c485-5913-94d5-66a0cfa2a5ee'}, {'abstract': 'Enjoy.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/friday-night-music-suzanne-vega/', 'snippet': 'Enjoy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suzanne Vega was actually at the reception before Tuesday’s event — and I, of course, was completely inane on meeting her, with nothing to say except how much I love her music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Night Music: Suzanne Vega', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/191e443e-d0f6-5896-9e8c-76fce1af0a11', 'word_count': 34, 'uri': 'nyt://article/191e443e-d0f6-5896-9e8c-76fce1af0a11'}, {'abstract': 'Times editors and critics choose their favorite reads of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/editors-and-critics-pick-their-favorite-books-of-2011/', 'snippet': 'Times editors and critics choose their favorite reads of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To complement the Book Review’s 10 Best Books of 2011, we asked assorted editors and critics at The Times to name their own favorite book from the year nearly past. We received a variety of answers, including works by a Booker Prize winner, a confessional rapper and a global optimist.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors and Critics Pick Their Favorite Books of 2011', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harbach, Chad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kael, Pauline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:28:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a01dd76-4758-53e0-829e-8e78cf9862f2', 'word_count': 946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a01dd76-4758-53e0-829e-8e78cf9862f2'}, {'abstract': 'A growing number of sitcoms — “Parks and Recreation,” “How I Met Your Mother” — seem to be written with an Internet afterlife in mind.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/arts/television/parks-and-recreation-and-other-sitcoms-hashtags-and-memes.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of sitcoms — “Parks and Recreation,” “How I Met Your Mother” — seem to be written with an Internet afterlife in mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN one of the feistiest arcs on “Parks and Recreation” this season Tom returns to his old workplace at City Hall to pick up his former co-worker Donna for their yearly day of pampering and conspicuous consumption. Their motto: “Treat yo self.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11CARAMANICA1_span/11CARAMANICA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11CARAMANICA1_span/11CARAMANICA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11CARAMANICA1_span/11CARAMANICA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11CARAMANICA1_span/11CARAMANICA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11CARAMANICA1_span/11CARAMANICA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'So Long, Water Cooler; Hello, Hashtag', 'kicker': 'Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'So Long, Water Cooler; Hello, Hashtag', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:32:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc049ce9-5bdb-51c9-b22b-df5814b3a134', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc049ce9-5bdb-51c9-b22b-df5814b3a134'}, {'abstract': 'Criolo, the sensation from the São Paulo favela Grajau, played his first New York show on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/now-playing-mr-favela/', 'snippet': 'Criolo, the sensation from the São Paulo favela Grajau, played his first New York show on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I just hope that New York feels what I feel,” the Brazilian rapper Criolo said just before making his American debut on Wednesday night at Nublu in the East Village. “I hope they feel my heart and soul.” Now 36, Criolo was born Kleber Gomes in São Paulo and grew up (and still lives) in one of the city’s poorest neighborhoods, the infamous\xa0Grajaú favela. It’s a place where “one million people fight for life every day,” he said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Playing | Mr. Favela', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:33:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9dee431-ff22-5e79-8779-0bb78e9b2056', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9dee431-ff22-5e79-8779-0bb78e9b2056'}, {'abstract': 'What did the squirrel say to the hawk?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/caption-contest-showdown-in-madison-square-park/', 'snippet': 'What did the squirrel say to the hawk?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What did the squirrel say to the red-tailed hawk? And/or vice versa. Answers below please.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caption Contest: Showdown in Madison Square Park', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:34:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cec3b14-7f02-5683-8686-e8eaf043366e', 'word_count': 15, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cec3b14-7f02-5683-8686-e8eaf043366e'}, {'abstract': 'A small project that’s worth it.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/09/its-the-holidays-make-a-pate/', 'snippet': 'A small project that’s worth it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s City Kitchen, we’re not featuring a quick delicious meal. Or actually, we are, but it will require a 2 hour investment of time (though not necessarily consecutive hours). For cooks who want to have a little fun, here’s a recipe for a pork and chicken pâté — the rustic, old-fashioned kind a French grandmother might make. True, it’s a bit of a project, but the end result provides easy hors d’oeuvres for a holiday gathering, a great first course, or a fine sandwich. And once your pâté is made, it can wait in the refrigerator for a week before serving, all the while improving in flavor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s the Holidays — Make a Pâté', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-09T22:41:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'mi