al a decision that would make it difficult to recover potentially hundreds of millions of dollars from the owners of the Mets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ruling Expected on Whether Madoff Trustee Can Appeal', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blindness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Mario M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einhorn, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katz, Saul B', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picard, Irving H', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilpon, Fred', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T02:46:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d23626c-5226-59f1-8fd1-cdf8a3ffb6dc', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d23626c-5226-59f1-8fd1-cdf8a3ffb6dc'}, {'abstract': 'The maker of telephone equipment said it would not seek new customers in Iran and would limit business activities with existing customers because of the situation there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/business/global/chinese-manufacturer-restricts-business-with-iran.html', 'snippet': 'The maker of telephone equipment said it would not seek new customers in Iran and would limit business activities with existing customers because of the situation there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Huawei Technologies, a leading Chinese maker of telecommunications equipment, said it would voluntarily restrict business in Iran because of what it called the “increasingly complex situation” there.\xa0In an announcement on its Web site, Huawei said it would not seek new customers in Iran and would limit business activities with existing customers.\xa0Huawei, a privately owned company that has been seeking access to the American market, did not elaborate on its reasons for the pullback from Iran, which has become the target of increased sanctions by the United States and the European Union over its disputed nuclear program. Last year, some American lawmakers expressed concern over reports that Huawei had supplied communications technology to Iran and had sold equipment to Iraq during the regime of Saddam Hussein, in violation of United Nations sanctions. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Manufacturer Restricts Business With Iran', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Telecommunications', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese Manufacturer Restricts Business With Iran', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Huawei Technologies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T02:46:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3713b2d0-1e10-5055-a6f4-7cfffaf2f9f9', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3713b2d0-1e10-5055-a6f4-7cfffaf2f9f9'}, {'abstract': 'A 90-year-old woman can hear again after getting some money from The Times’s Neediest Cases Fund to pay for two hearing aids.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/opinion/still-active-with-a-little-help.html', 'snippet': 'A 90-year-old woman can hear again after getting some money from The Times’s Neediest Cases Fund to pay for two hearing aids.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Henrietta Edwards prays that she may live to 100. “After that, I want to find out what’s on the other side,” she said. Ms. Edwards will celebrate her 91st birthday on Christmas Day with her energy and wit intact. To reach her goal, she eats carefully, exercises, takes dance and yoga classes, and writes poetry at the Lincoln Square Neighborhood Center next door to her apartment.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Active, With a Little Help', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Active, With a Little Help', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hearing Aids', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T02:49:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6afacec-86da-58b5-9360-fc0d33bbb3a0', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6afacec-86da-58b5-9360-fc0d33bbb3a0'}, {'abstract': 'In a small community with its own public safety department, a number of burglaries made residents think twice about leaving their doors unlocked.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/nyregion/on-roosevelt-island-burglaries-bring-a-sense-of-unease.html', 'snippet': 'In a small community with its own public safety department, a number of burglaries made residents think twice about leaving their doors unlocked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You hear about places where people don’t bother to lock their front doors, and maybe you smile and think of home, or, if you’re a certain breed of New Yorker, you just shake your head. Rubes with nothing worth stealing. Give me three deadbolts and a chain in the big city any day. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/10/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 