rapy. Hemophilia B, which was carried by Queen Victoria and affected most of the royal houses of Europe, is the first well-known disease to appear treatable by gene therapy, a technique with a 20-year record of almost unbroken failure.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/world/hemophilia/hemophilia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 983, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/world/hemophilia/hemophilia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/world/hemophilia/hemophilia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Treatment for Blood Disease Is Gene Therapy Landmark', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Breakthrough for Gene Therapy, An Injection Works on Hemophilia B', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Journal of Medicine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hemophilia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immune System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T21:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Wade', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wade',