e': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T21:47:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/32232f58-d859-52d7-8cd4-db907d4b4b16', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32232f58-d859-52d7-8cd4-db907d4b4b16'}, {'abstract': 'Ana Dutra of Korn/Ferry says she has learned that consulting is all about helping people make dreams come true.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/jobs/ana-dutra-of-korn-ferry-on-helping-people-achieve.html', 'snippet': 'Ana Dutra of Korn/Ferry says she has learned that consulting is all about helping people make dreams come true.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GROWING up in Brazil, I developed a high tolerance for ambiguity. The country had a history of political instability and was volatile and complex. You never knew what you were going to find from day to day, from the price of gas to whether your parents would still have jobs. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/business/11-BOSS/11-BOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/business/11-BOSS/11-BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/business/11-BOSS/11-BOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sprinkles of Pixie Dust', 'kicker': 'The Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sprinkles of Pixie Dust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consultants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T21:51:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NAT', 'section_name': 'Job Market', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ana Dutra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dutra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd57e3a6-e9ff-5f2d-89cc-3f343f4d95d1', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd57e3a6-e9ff-5f2d-89cc-3f343f4d95d1'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column based on interviews with voters in Egypt who supported Muslim Brotherhood or Salafi candidates.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/10/readers-comment-on-sundays-egypt-column/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column based on interviews with voters in Egypt who supported Muslim Brotherhood or Salafi candidates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column is from Ismailia, Egypt, the birthplace of the Muslim Brotherhood. It tries to look at why so many Egyptians voted for Islamic parties, and my bottom-line feeling is that Americans are feeling a bit more panicky than is justified. It’s not that Egyptians seek a harsh Islamic regime; rather they think the Islamic candidates have a better record working in their neighborhoods and may be a little less likely to take bribes than other candidates. Plenty of the hand-wringing seems to be coming from Americans who’ve never actually met a Muslim Brotherhood member or a Salafi, and I’d suggest not overdoing the rhetoric. The picture is more complicated than some Americans seem to think (although I must acknowledge that plenty of Egyptian liberals are worried as well). Read the column and post your thoughts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Readers Comment on Sunday’s Egypt Column', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03421c3a-ba65-5251-ab60-a36379a631e3', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03421c3a-ba65-5251-ab60-a36379a631e3'}, {'abstract': 'A death penalty lawyer says the Supreme Court has undermined its own ruling against executing the mentally retarded.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/unfit-for-execution.html', 'snippet': 'A death penalty lawyer says the Supreme Court has undermined its own ruling against executing the mentally retarded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unfit for Execution', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unfit for Execution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad9affcd-5968-5867-8fa3-75e3d88e4355', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad9affcd-5968-5867-8fa3-75e3d88e4355'}, {'abstract': 'Newt Gingrich is playing to Republican revenge fantasies about outsmarting, and out-orating, Barack Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/douthat-professor-gingrich-vs-professor-obama.html', 'snippet': 'Newt Gingrich is playing to Republican revenge fantasies about outsmarting, and out-orating, Barack Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 2004, the Democrats were furious at what they considered the fraud to end all frauds: the selling of George W. Bush as a decisive military leader and all-American tough guy. So they nominated John Kerry for the presidency, hoping that having a real combat veteran as their standard-bearer — a bemedaled war hero, no less, who began his convention speech by announcing that he was “reporting for duty” — would finally expose Bush as the tinhorn chicken hawk that liberals believed him to be.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Professor vs. Professor', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Professor Vs. Professor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:04:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/db6c5b3a-1a4b-5132-9828-1a0547f76ae8', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db6c5b3a-1a4b-5132-9828-1a0547f76ae8'}, {'abstract': 'For better or worse, the ethos of the corporate troubleshooter hangs over Mitt Romney’s campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/sunday-review/consultant-nation.html', 'snippet': 'For better or worse, the ethos of the corporate troubleshooter hangs over Mitt Romney’s campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR decades before Mitt Romney arrived at Harvard Business School in the fall of 1972, the traditional path for Harvard M.B.A.’s had been a straight one: join a major company right out of school and climb the ranks.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/opinion/11leonhardt-img/11leonhardt-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 808, 'width': 395, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/opinion/11leonhardt-img/11leonhardt-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/opinion/11leonhardt-img/11leonhardt-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Consultant Nation', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Consultant Nation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bain & Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Consulting Group', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McKinsey & Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consultants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard Business School', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:04:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/e75d3159-8a61-5128-bab9-7306abadd265', 'word_count': 1897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e75d3159-8a61-5128-bab9-7306abadd265'}, {'abstract': 'The notion that black women are doomed to stay single is a myth, and a cruel one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/black-and-female-the-marriage-question.html', 'snippet': 'The notion that black women are doomed to stay single is a myth, and a cruel one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FEW things in life are more irritating than the unsolicited comments I get that black women, like me, are unlikely to marry. Family members ask, “Are you ever going to get married?” as if I am remaining single purposely to keep them from attending my wedding. Well-meaning married friends try to sell me on the idea that being single is liberating. And then there is my octogenarian aunt whom I love, but who also manages to unintentionally sucker punch me whenever I visit with the comment, “Maybe if you’d just straighten your hair you’d be able to find a man.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black, Female and Single', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Black, Female and Single', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Single Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANGELA STANLEY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Angela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STANLEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title':