//article/4d8e252b-fd15-5a7e-9829-7b08f2f16d80'}, {'abstract': 'With 11 acts over three and a half hours, there was little room for artistic expression at the radio station extravaganza known as Jingle Ball, unless you’re Lady Gaga.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/arts/music/music-review-z-100s-jingle-ball-at-madison-square-garden.html', 'snippet': 'With 11 acts over three and a half hours, there was little room for artistic expression at the radio station extravaganza known as Jingle Ball, unless you’re Lady Gaga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even early in Z-100’s Jingle Ball on Friday night, when the girl-screams at Madison Square Garden were not yet full-blooded, Elvis Duran, the Z-100 D.J. and the evening’s principal M.C., looked like a cat who had swallowed an eagle. He was sitting on an apotheosis, something that made Jingle Ball seem like much more than a promotional orgy for Top 40 radio and its advertisers, a place for pop acts to ram home their hits between screened advertisements for Splat hair color and Sweethearts Jewelry.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11jinglereview1_span/11jinglereview1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11jinglereview1_span/11jinglereview1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11jinglereview1_span/11jinglereview1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11jinglereview1_span/11jinglereview1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/arts/11jinglereview1_span/11jinglereview1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lady Gaga Rewrites the Script at Jingle Ball', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Holiday Extravaganza Rewritten by Lady Gaga', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gym Class Heroes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovato, Demi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LMFAO', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarkson, Kelly', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:09:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2a6aa90-b4d8-582f-a2d1-2fa7ef90beca', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2a6aa90-b4d8-582f-a2d1-2fa7ef90beca'}, {'abstract': 'When I read about Alec Baldwin and his Twitter tantrum over American Airlines, I realized how much my dad, a Hollywood guy, would have loved to tweet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/if-my-dad-could-tweet.html', 'snippet': 'When I read about Alec Baldwin and his Twitter tantrum over American Airlines, I realized how much my dad, a Hollywood guy, would have loved to tweet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My dad can’t tweet because he’s dead. He died in 1982, when telephones were about the only way to have a conversation with someone who was not in the same place you were.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If My Dad Could Tweet', 'kicker': 'Loose Ends', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If My Dad Could Tweet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:09:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Delia Ephron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Delia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ephron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e6bb752-35c3-5751-a753-a02ad44095c0', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e6bb752-35c3-5751-a753-a02ad44095c0'}, {'abstract': 'The United States — that epitome of the middle-class society, of the egalitarian dream that pulled millions of immigrants away from Latin America — has begun to go Latin American.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/on-the-middle-class-lessons-from-latin-america.html', 'snippet': 'The United States — that epitome of the middle-class society, of the egalitarian dream that pulled millions of immigrants away from Latin America — has begun to go Latin American.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN a Bertelsmann Foundation study on social justice released this fall, the United States came in dead last among the rich countries, with only Greece, Chile, Mexico and Turkey faring worse. Whether in poverty prevention, child poverty, income inequality or health ratings, the United States ranked below countries like Spain and South Korea, not to mention Japan, Germany or France. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Latin America Can Teach Us', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Latin America Can Teach Us', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bertelsmann Foundation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:10:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jorge G. Castañeda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jorge', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Castañeda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/43cdd71f-f22e-5f0b-8e27-3c0c8f67c971', 'word_count': 1581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43cdd71f-f22e-5f0b-8e27-3c0c8f67c971'}, {'abstract': 'Alaskan natural gas could provide an alternative to coal in electricity production and to oil in transportation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/the-pipeline-we-actually-need.html', 'snippet': 'Alaskan natural gas could provide an alternative to coal in electricity production and to oil in transportation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LISTENING to the debate over the proposed Keystone XL oil pipeline, you might think that all pipelines are bad. But thousands of miles of pipeline already crisscross the United States. Without them, there would not be gasoline at filling stations, or natural gas in restaurant kitchens. It is 100 percent certain that new pipelines must be built.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/sunday-review/EASTERBROOK/EASTERBROOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/11/sunday-review/EASTERBROOK/EASTERBROOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/sunday-review/EASTERBROOK/EASTERBROOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/sunday-review/EASTERBROOK/EASTERBROOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/sunday-review/EASTERBROOK/EASTERBROOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pipeline We Actually Need', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Pipeline We Actually Need', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Keystone Pipeline System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pickens, T Boone Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pipelines', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:11:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregg Easterbrook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Easterbrook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f902b657-4909-5dda-b5d0-3f008316a3e4', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f902b657-4909-5dda-b5d0-3f008316a3e4'}, {'abstract': 'Foreclosures and home abandonments have inspired creative thinkers, political protesters and civic leaders who envision new uses for homes besides nesting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/sunday-review/the-housing-busts-repurpose-driven-life.html', 'snippet': 'Foreclosures and home abandonments have inspired creative thinkers, political protesters and civic leaders who envision new uses for homes besides nesting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A MAN’S home is his castle — except when it becomes someone else’s marijuana plantation, crack den, movie set, homeless shelter, farm or public park.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goodbye House, Hello Pot Plantation', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Goodbye House, Hello Pot Plantation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Abandonment (Property)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-10T22:12:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ea4c098-e9fc-56ec-b915-5d089deb3637', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ea4c098-e9fc-56ec-b915-5d089deb3637'}, {'abstract': 'Newt Gingrich’s smear campaign on the justice system threatens the independence of judges and the Constitution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/opinion/sunday/mr-gingrichs-attack-on-the-courts.html', 'snippet': 'Newt Gingrich’s smear campaign on the justice system threatens the independence of judges and the Constitution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In any campaign season, voters are bound to hear Republican candidates talk about “activist judges” — jurists who rule in ways that the right wing does not like. But Newt Gingrich, who is leading in polls in Iowa, is taking the normal attack on the justice system to a deep new low.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Gingrich’s Attack on the Courts', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Gingrich’s Attack on the Courts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'va