 a shy daughter to a fabric dealer in Lucknow, suffered a similar fate with a bookish merchant, as did the hardheaded corporate climber Raina (Dipannita Sharma), with an art dealer in Mumbai. All the men, it seems, were one fellow, whom they call the Bloody Scoundrel. He has victimized 30 women — not with physical seduction but with charm, assumed names and swindles.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/ladies/ladies-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/ladies/ladies-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/ladies/ladies-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wooing to Reap Revenge', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Ladies vs Ricky Bahl'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wooing to Reap Revenge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T19:49:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86907344-0ecd-57b2-ae03-a75e4a9eb7fb', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86907344-0ecd-57b2-ae03-a75e4a9eb7fb'}, {'abstract': 'As many of the Continent’s banks face increased financing costs and limited access to much-needed capital, they are selling assets and raising interest rates on customers in an effort to bolster their balance sheets.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/european-banks-hunt-for-ways-to-increase-capital/', 'snippet': 'As many of the Continent’s banks face increased financing costs and limited access to much-needed capital, they are selling assets and raising interest rates on customers in an effort to bolster their balance sheets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — As Europe continues to grapple with its sovereign debt crisis, many of the Continent’s banks are facing increased financing costs and limited access to much-needed cash, according to the Bank for International Settlements, an association of the world’s central banks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Banks Hunt for Ways to Raise Cash', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BNP Paribas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commerzbank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Banking Authority', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lloyds Banking Group Plc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T20:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/333c4b5c-5bc5-5a0e-8bdd-bae2d8b73792', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/333c4b5c-5bc5-5a0e-8bdd-bae2d8b73792'}, {'abstract': 'In this edition: Newt Gingrich on the rise, fashionably late Republican candidates and brokered convention conversations.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/reads-reactions-15/', 'snippet': 'In this edition: Newt Gingrich on the rise, fashionably late Republican candidates and brokered convention conversations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this edition: Newt Gingrich on the rise, fashionably late G.O.P. candidates and brokered convention conversations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reads and Reactions', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T20:16:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d1ff9ba-4f30-55b6-862d-048aca4e8f32', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d1ff9ba-4f30-55b6-862d-048aca4e8f32'}, {'abstract': 'Victor Cruz, a recently obscure player who celebrates touchdowns with a few salsa steps, needs 268 receiving yards to break Amani Toomer’s single-season franchise record.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/sports/football/for-giants-cruz-a-victory-dance-is-already-in-order.html', 'snippet': 'Victor Cruz, a recently obscure player who celebrates touchdowns with a few salsa steps, needs 268 receiving yards to break Amani Toomer’s single-season franchise record.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — Victor Cruz found two large photographs of himself at his locker when he arrived at the Giants’ practice facility Friday. Someone in the organization was requesting Cruz’s autograph, perhaps for friends or family.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/sports/YCRUZ/YCRUZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 643, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/sports/YCRUZ/YCRUZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/sports/YCRUZ/YCRUZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giants’ Cruz Already Has Reason to Dance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giants&#8217; Cruz Already Has Reason To Dance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruz, Victor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T20:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60b67547-e84b-5777-8c6a-227a89942bb4', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60b67547-e84b-5777-8c6a-227a89942bb4'}, {'abstract': 'The government said it would spend $160 million to build an Egyptian border fence, expand detention centers and increase the policing of companies that hire immigrant workers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/world/middleeast/israel-steps-up-efforts-to-stop-illegal-immigration-from-africa.html', 'snippet': 'The government said it would spend $160 million to build an Egyptian border fence, expand detention centers and increase the policing of companies that hire immigrant workers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — The Israeli government on Sunday announced plans to spend $160 million on efforts to stem the growing number of Africans who enter the country illegally, seeking jobs and political asylum. The money will go toward work on an Egyptian border fence that is already under construction, an expansion of detention centers and increased policing of companies that hire undocumented workers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/world/ISRAEL/ISRAEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/world/ISRAEL/ISRAEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/world/ISRAEL/ISRAEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel Acts to Curb Illegal Immigration From Africa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israeli Plan Cracks Down On Migrants From Africa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Eritrea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fences', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T20:55:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f82cfe4a-d493-5650-adf7-3ac81ef6ca2d', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f82cfe4a-d493-5650-adf7-3ac81ef6ca2d'}, {'abstract': 'Aidan Burley, a Conservative member of the British Parliament, apologized for attending a party where one person was dressed in a Nazi uniform.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/british-leader-apologizes-after-attending-party-with-man-dressed-in-nazi-outfit/', 'snippet': 'Aidan Burley, a Conservative member of the British Parliament, apologized for attending a party where one person was dressed in a Nazi uniform.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The video captured the camaraderie at a bachelor party earlier this month. The celebrating men sat around a table at a restaurant in the French Alps, making toasts. But something was amiss. One jovial toast appeared to refer to the\xa0“Third Reich.” One man was dressed as a Nazi Waffen SS officer in full regalia. And next to him was a member of the British Parliament.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Conservative Apologizes for Attending Party With Man Dressed in Nazi Outfit', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T21:16:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d9fe80b-e8c8-5f81-89d2-37c1c84a53cd', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d9fe80b-e8c8-5f81-89d2-37c1c84a53cd'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Hollinghurst’s new novel, “The Stranger’s Child,” reflects English values and attitudes from 1913 until the cellphone age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/books/the-strangers-child-by-alan-hollinghurst-review.html', 'snippet': 'Alan Hollinghurst’s new novel, “The Stranger’s Child,” reflects English values and attitudes from 1913 until the cellphone age.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Stranger’s Child,” Alan Hollinghurst’s fifth novel, opens on a scene in Harrow and Wealdstone, a suburb north of London chosen by the author to represent the middle ground, that is the space between the upper and lower orders — or rather, this being England in 1913, between the orders of lower upper middle and upper upper middle. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 944, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In an England of Certainties, Even in Its Undercurrents', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In an England of Certainties, Even in Its Undercurrents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hollinghurst, Alan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T21:18:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Brockes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brockes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/14c92898-17a9-5e40-8552-35a08b4dfaec', 'word_count': 1078, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14c92898-17a9-5e40-8552-35a08b4dfaec'}, {'abstract': 'Reid Farrington’s version of “A Christmas Carol” is a technically complicated mash-up in which George C. Scott, Bill Murray and Donald Duck star along with live actors who double as stagehands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/theater/reviews/a-christmas-carol-at-abrons-arts-center-review.html', 'snippet': 'Reid Farrington’s version of “A Christmas Carol” is a technically complicated mash-up in which George C. Scott, Bill Murray and Donald Duck star along with live actors who double as stagehands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The director Reid Farrington is a man of the moment. A multimedia collage artist who transcends genres, he makes plays that blend theater and film, drama with art installation. His work is formally impressive and conceptually fascinating, but sometimes it’s hard to tell if his experiments are a step forward or back. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [