guage and submit your own one-liners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Viewers of the HBO fantasy series “Game of Thrones” could be forgiven for thinking that the jagged, vaguely malevolent dialect spoken by the show’s Dothraki tribe is just a random assortment of guttural noises. However, such assumptions would be ojil.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rosetta Throne: Learn to Speak Dothraki', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Game of Thrones (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:02:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2de926fa-3308-561e-b76d-f3f19f475da0', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2de926fa-3308-561e-b76d-f3f19f475da0'}, {'abstract': 'Bad moon on the rise.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/central-european-shadows/', 'snippet': 'Bad moon on the rise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It didn’t get much media attention, but last month’s Transition Report from the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development devoted a chapter to deteriorating belief in democracy and markets in the transition economies of Europe; there’s been a sharp decline in support for democracy, strongly correlated with the depth of economic crisis:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Central European Shadows', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:14:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/888e0b9b-ef11-5c4e-9d13-fb5d749a325f', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/888e0b9b-ef11-5c4e-9d13-fb5d749a325f'}, {'abstract': 'The important stuff.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/cheeseburgers/', 'snippet': 'The important stuff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OK, let’s talk about the important stuff. Via Brad DeLong, Waldo Jaquith explains that eating cheeseburgers would have been highly impractical before the coming of modern manufacturing, transportation,refrigeration, and more. Eat your heart out, pin factory!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cheeseburgers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:20:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8b8defd-8772-5ce1-a4fc-acaee6ad4e19', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8b8defd-8772-5ce1-a4fc-acaee6ad4e19'}, {'abstract': 'The soprano Karita Mattila focused on French and Finnish composers in her recital at Carnegie Hall on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/music/karita-mattila-