nd “Bored to Death.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/television/incest-as-plot-point-on-3-hbo-series-just-by-chance.html', 'snippet': 'Incest, that deeply ingrained taboo, by chance has been a topic in three current HBO series: “Boardwalk Empire,” “Game of Thrones,” and “Bored to Death.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'If it was not quite the “ick” heard ’round the world, there was a collective shudder that went up the spines of fans of serialized television at the moment it was revealed in the Dec. 4 episode of “Boardwalk Empire,” the Prohibition-era crime drama on HBO, that Jimmy Darmody, the aspiring young mobster played by Michael Pitt, had had an incestuous relationship with his mother (Gretchen Mol). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/incest-1/incest-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/incest-1/incest-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/incest-1/incest-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Family Affair as Old as Oedipus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Family Affair As Old As Oedipus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Incest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:29:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c73e81f-f4ac-568c-b60e-695685955cf5', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c73e81f-f4ac-568c-b60e-695685955cf5'}, {'abstract': 'Why is the idea that desire is the master of reason still causing fear and loathing in philosophy?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/of-hume-and-bondage/', 'snippet': 'Why is the idea that desire is the master of reason still causing fear and loathing in philosophy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone admiring David Hume as I do finds much to cheer, but much to lament in the state of academic philosophy, as this year, the 300th anniversary of his birth, comes to a close. Hume was an anatomist of the mind, charting the ways we think and feel — a psychologist or cognitive scientist before his time. The cheering feature of the contemporary scene is that plenty of people are following in those footsteps. The nature versus nurture battle has declared an uneasy draw, but the human nature industry is in fine fettle, fed by many disciplines and eagerly consumed by the public.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Of Hume and Bondage', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hume, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kant, Immanuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:30:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Blackburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blackburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': N