, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8a3b5be-99bb-56c3-b93a-0fb7a864468f'}, {'abstract': 'Zach Anner, a reality-show competition winner who has cerebral palsy, travels the country on “Rollin’ With Zach,” attempting things like water-skiing and stand-up comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/television/rollin-with-zach-starring-zac-anner-on-own-review.html', 'snippet': 'Zach Anner, a reality-show competition winner who has cerebral palsy, travels the country on “Rollin’ With Zach,” attempting things like water-skiing and stand-up comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zach Anner is the most engaging personality on a network that needs them badly. The host of the travel show “Rollin’ With Zach,” which has its premiere on Monday night on OWN, the floundering Oprah Winfrey Network, with two episodes, he’s witty and charming and mildly zany.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12zach/12zach-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12zach/12zach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12zach/12zach-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disabled Host, With the Emphasis on Able', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disabled Host, With the Emphasis on Able', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oprah Winfrey Network', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anner, Zach', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:34:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaf9faca-122c-5ddd-be13-314f08a9eb1b', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaf9faca-122c-5ddd-be13-314f08a9eb1b'}, {'abstract': 'The reality show “Fear Factor” returns to NBC on Monday night, and its producers have upped the ante for viewers who want “badder and grosser.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/television/fear-factor-returns-to-nbc-on-monday-night.html', 'snippet': 'The reality show “Fear Factor” returns to NBC on Monday night, and its producers have upped the ante for viewers who want “badder and grosser.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is probably one of the more unusual phone calls that a meat processing plant has ever received.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12FEAR/12FEAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 967, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12FEAR/12FEAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/12FEAR/12FEAR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Back, and Even More Disgusting', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Back, and Even More Disgusting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogan, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:41:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf650aad-3ed8-5270-b6d8-16462c66add7', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf650aad-3ed8-5270-b6d8-16462c66add7'}, {'abstract': 'Fans of the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater had a chance to strut their stuff on stage in the company premiere of “Minus 16,” a sampler based on works by the choreographer Ohad Naharin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/dance/alvin-aileys-ohad-naharin-premiere-at-city-center-review.html', 'snippet': 'Fans of the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater had a chance to strut their stuff on stage in the company premiere of “Minus 16,” a sampler based on works by the choreographer Ohad Naharin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a better group of people to finagle into intense audience participation than the Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater faithful? They are always primed for engagement: witness the anticipatory “Revelations” cheers as the lights dim or the ecstatic midperformance shout-outs. (Forget the path of righteousness; it is usually the “Sinner Man” section that draws the most raucous response, especially from women.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/sub-ailey/sub-ailey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'sub