c.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/arts/dance/merce-cunningham-troupes-last-us-repertory-night-review.html', 'snippet': 'Merce Cunningham Dance Company’s final American repertory evening featured Silas Riener in “Split Sides” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In its final week of repertory in America, the Merce Cunningham Dance Company, dancing six works in three programs at the Brooklyn Academy of Music from Wednesday to Saturday, kept hitting high after high. The highs kept extending our sense both of these dancers, an exceptionally attractive, diverse and skilled group, and of Cunningham himself.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/pond/pond-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/pond/pond-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/pond/pond-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/pond/pond-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/12/arts/pond/pond-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Visionary Offered Surprises Till the End', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Visionary Offered Surprises Till the End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riener, Silas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce, Dance Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T22:47:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd3d6c7c-15a2-5c56-93da-710fc249f9ef', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd3d6c7c-15a2-5c56-93da-710fc249f9ef'}, {'abstract': 'A theme by Gary Cee that highlights a place of great sentimental value to me: the room where I spent most of The Boy’s childhood.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/11/praiseworthy/', 'snippet': 'A theme by Gary Cee that highlights a place of great sentimental value to me: the room where I spent most of The Boy’s childhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — He’s not even completely grown, but my son has already joined the Emergency Room Frequent Fliers club. There was a time when we were on a first-name basis with many of the nurses at our local hospital’s E.R., all of whom thankfully had the good taste and training not to keep bringing up the fact that we spent most of our time there. Those of you who have raised children or been responsible for one for any length of time know what I’m talking about. Children are innocent creatures, filled with wonderment and the joy of experiencing the world for the first time, so it is completely natural for them to get themselves into predicaments that, had they happened to any normal adult, would have gotten that adult an immediate psychiatric evaluation (“Exactly HOW did the vacuum cleaner get stuck on his tongue, Ms. Amlen?”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Poem For The Praiseworthy', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-11T23:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0519753-a06a-57c2-ba76-2db43d3ba148', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0519753-a06a-57c2-ba76-2db43d3ba148'}, {'abstract': 'Ryan Braun faces a 50-game suspension and a stain on the legacy he was carefully starting to build.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/sports/baseball/bad-news-envelops-braun-thought-of-as-one-of-the-good-guys.html', 'snippet': 'Ryan Braun faces a 50-game suspension and a stain on the legacy he was carefully starting to build.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ryan Braun can still tell you the high school class in which he got a B: Advanced Placement Chemistry. Otherwise, Braun said this summer, on the bench before a game in Milwaukee, he got straight A’s. He attended the University of Miami on a partial academic scholarship and has tried to apply the lessons he learned in the classroom, branching out from baseball to business.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/sports/subYJPKEPNER/subYJPKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/12/sports/subYJPKEPNER/subYJPKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 