', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/227b1f90-5d0f-553e-967c-61416bd98889', 'word_count': 1451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/227b1f90-5d0f-553e-967c-61416bd98889'}, {'abstract': 'New York State must decide how to reduce the risks and ensure tight controls of hydraulic fracturing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/opinion/getting-gas-drilling-right.html', 'snippet': 'New York State must decide how to reduce the risks and ensure tight controls of hydraulic fracturing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After several crowded and often raucous hearings, Gov. Andrew Cuomo agreed to give the public until Jan. 11 to comment on 2,000 pages of environmental analysis and proposed regulations designed to govern natural gas drilling in deep shale formations in New York State. The extension makes sense. The drilling decision is a momentous one, for the environment and the economy, and it is vitally important to get it right.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Gas Drilling Right', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Getting Gas Drilling Right', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Environmental Conservation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T02:18:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3109f45-eb33-5d53-be23-7589439c31f2', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3109f45-eb33-5d53-be23-7589439c31f2'}, {'abstract': 'In Europe, much more than a shared currency hangs in the balance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/opinion/krugman-depression-and-democracy.html', 'snippet': 'In Europe, much more than a shared currency hangs in the balance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time to start calling the current situation what it is: a depression. True, it’s not a full replay of the Great Depression, but that’s cold comfort. Unemployment in both America and Europe remains disastrously high. Leaders and institutions are increasingly discredited. And democratic values are under siege.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-