, '_id': 'nyt://article/1b7c5faa-7f4b-5f1d-9c8b-d43ffb690f97', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b7c5faa-7f4b-5f1d-9c8b-d43ffb690f97'}, {'abstract': 'India Ink replies to “Devil’s Advocate.”', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/kapil-sibal-on-cnn-ibn-our-response/', 'snippet': 'India Ink replies to “Devil’s Advocate.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kapil Sibal, India’s minister of communications and information technology, appeared on the CNN-IBN program “Devil’s Advocate” with Karan Thapar over the weekend to discuss \xa0the government’s recent request \xa0that Internet companies like Facebook and Google screen user content. India Ink, which  broke the story on Dec. 5 and stands by it, would like to respond to a few points that were made.\xa0 Below are direct excerpts from the show, with India Ink’s responses in italics. \xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kapil Sibal on CNN-IBN: Our Response', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indian National Congress', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T09:24:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cd05f3a-69d2-5816-914c-1278aaff02c5', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cd05f3a-69d2-5816-914c-1278aaff02c5'}, {'abstract': 'Can you calculate the percentage of difference in market share between Beats and iPod headphones, if given Beats’ sales, the total revenue of the headphones market, the costs of both brands of headphones and the number of iPod headphones sold?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/test-yourself-math-dec-12-2011/', 'snippet': 'Can you calculate the percentage of difference in market share between Beats and iPod headphones, if given Beats’ sales, the total revenue of the headphones market, the costs of both brands of headphones and the number of iPod headphones sold?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by David Prince at Math for America from the article “Headphones With Swagger (and Lots of Bass).”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Dec. 12, 2011', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T09:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45227bb6-babb-5f6e-9cc4-4ccf68bb79fb', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45227bb6-babb-5f6e-9cc4-4ccf68bb79fb'}, {'abstract': 'The Financial Services Authority said it was partly responsible for the failure of Royal Bank of Scotland, and it recommended new preventive steps, including holding bank executives more personally accountable for bad decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/british-regulators-outline-tougher-banking-oversight-as-they-take-blame-for-r-b-s-failure/', 'snippet': 'The Financial Services Authority said it was partly responsible for the failure of Royal Bank of Scotland, and it recommended new preventive steps, including holding bank executives more personally accountable for bad decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:26 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Regulator Takes Partial Blame for R.B.S. Failure', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nationalization of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Services Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Bank of Scotland PLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T09:48:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d6ff6b6-1b90-5b9e-a9c7-4890e445d97b', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d6ff6b6-1b90-5b9e-a9c7-4890e445d97b'}, {'abstract': 'Before Photoshop allowed image makers to bend reality to their will with a single keystroke there was Jerry Uelsmann. His layered images came from seven enlargers, multiple negatives and his own hands.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/jerry-uelsmanns-analog-dreams/', 'snippet': 'Before Photoshop allowed image makers to bend reality to their will with a single keystroke there was Jerry Uelsmann. His layered images came from seven enlargers, multiple negatives and his own hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before Photoshop allowed image makers to bend reality to their will with a single keystroke there was Jerry Uelsmann. He created surreal images the hard way — not with digital shortcuts, but working in a darkroom with\xa0seven enlargers and multiple negatives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jerry Uelsmann’s Analog Dreams', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T10:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70f248f5-7b51-5a82-9061-cbf20a7f2fba', 'word_count': 1841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70f248f5-7b51-5a82-9061-cbf20a7f2fba'}, {'abstract': 'Why did tens of thousands of Russians take to the streets on Saturday?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/russian-protesters-cry-we-exist/', 'snippet': 'Why did tens of thousands of Russians take to the streets on Saturday?', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW many Russians took part in protests on Saturday in Moscow?HOW did the government respond?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russian Protesters Cry, ‘We Exist!’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T10:32:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e755efa-7278-5ce5-9b5c-3244c072fc95', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e755efa-7278-5ce5-9b5c-3244c072fc95'}, {'abstract': 'Maybe “60 Minutes” could do an investigation on what in the world happened to the N.F.L.’s pledge to keep replays under 90 seconds.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/packers-raiders-too-long-for-replay-too-long-to-play/', 'snippet': 'Maybe “60 Minutes” could do an investigation on what in the world happened to the N.F.L.’s pledge to keep replays under 90 seconds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who tuned in to see President Obama on “60 Minutes”\xa0on Sunday night had to wait essentially an entire television time slot while the Packers’ 46-16 victory over the Raiders wrapped up. The high volume of scoring prolonged the contest, but more time-consuming were the instant replays. Perhaps CBS’s N.F.L. and\xa0“60 Minutes” viewers could be appeased if Scott Pelley or Lesley Stahl did an investigation on what in the world happened to the league’s pledge to keep replays under 90 seconds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Packers-Raiders: Too Long for Replay, Too Long to Play', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T10:58:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d51544f2-f19b-5aa3-b93a-ca56038bb9ea', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d51544f2-f19b-5aa3-b93a-ca56038bb9ea'}, {'abstract': 'Changes in rules on welfare that are intended to encourage a strong work ethic among recipients have nowhere to go when little work is available, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/welfare-reform-revisited/', 'snippet': 'Changes in rules on welfare that are intended to encourage a strong work ethic among recipients have nowhere to go when little work is available, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welfare Reform Revisited', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Urban Institute', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T11:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/416f2888-7a31-5eca-909a-833e704fd222', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/416f2888-7a31-5eca-909a-833e704fd222'}, {'abstract': 'Art Rocks, a show at the Shizaru Gallery, features jewelry by established designers and new talent alike.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/jewelry-show-takes-over-london-gallery/', 'snippet': 'Art Rocks, a show at the Shizaru Gallery, features jewelry by established designers and new talent alike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Art and commerce collide at the jewelry exhibition Art Rocks at the Shizaru Gallery (112 Mount Street; 44-20-7499-2266; www.shizaru.com), on through Jan. 14. \xa0 Co-curated by the jewelry specialist Joanna Hardy, the show \xa0includes \xa0items from established designers like Kevin Coates, Leo De Vroomen and Sevan Biçakçi, as well as new talent such as Zoe Arnold and Sophia Mann.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jewelry Show Takes Over London Gallery', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sakhi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T11:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7981d02c-605f-5162-8e45-5bcea1b8a110', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7981d02c-605f-5162-8e45-5bcea1b8a110'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | How important is it to develop the skill of listening?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/are-we-losing-the-art-of-listening/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | How important is it to develop the skill of listening?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Listening, like other skills, can be developed through practice, or lost if not used regularly. Good listeners focus on what they are hearing. They pause to think about what they’ve heard before responding. They ask questions because they want to know the answers, not just to keep the conversation going. Do you often find yourself in the company of good listeners? Would you describe yourself as a good listener? Why or why not?In the op-ed piece “The Art of Listening,” Henning Mankell writes about what he has learned from living a “straddled existence, with one foot in African sand and the other in European snow”:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Are We Losing the Art of Listening?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T11:06:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a86c7d8a-8664-59f6-9ca1-3bbe29255521', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a86c7d8a-8664-59f6-9ca1-3bbe29255521'}, {'abstract': 'With mounds of European debt due to be refinanced early next year, the crisis is far from over, and there are risks even in getting the Brussels deal ratified.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/world/europe/european-debt-deal-may-not-be-a-cure.html', 'snippet': 'With mounds of European debt due to be refinanced early next year, the crisis is far from over, and there are risks even in getting the Brussels deal ratified.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIENNA — The deal on Friday in Brussels to reformulate the rules of the euro zone has probably saved the shared currency for now — but there may be less to it than meets the eye. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/world/EURO/EURO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/12/world/EURO/EURO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/world/EURO/EURO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/12/world/EURO/EURO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail':