e-for-matthew-hamilton-and-a-new-smith/', 'snippet': 'A sous chef takes over the Italian kitchen, an East Village chef moves to Fort Greene, a popular restaurant moves across from Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'P.J. Calapa, a chef from Brownsville, Tex., who began working with Michael White last year and was the sous-chef at Ai Fiori, has now been promoted to be chef de cuisine at the restaurant. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Chef for Ai Fiori, New Place for Matthew Hamilton and a New Smith', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T13:19:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60082a6e-659d-504b-bf1f-e8a78e49111c', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60082a6e-659d-504b-bf1f-e8a78e49111c'}, {'abstract': 'A German regulator has asked for changes to the proposed $9 billion merger between Deutsche Börse and NYSE Euronext after raising concerns about how the deal would affect their operations in Frankfurt.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/german-regulators-call-for-changes-to-deutsche-borse-deal-with-nyse/', 'snippet': 'A German regulator has asked for changes to the proposed $9 billion merger between Deutsche Börse and NYSE Euronext after raising concerns about how the deal would affect their operations in Frankfurt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A German regulator has called for changes to the proposed $9 billion merger between Deutsche Börse and NYSE Euronext after raising concerns about how the deal would affect their operations in Frankfurt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A German Regulator Seeks to Change NYSE-Deutsche Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T13:20:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b638651-919d-5af8-889a-aae61873f741', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b638651-919d-5af8-889a-aae61873f741'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese government claims censorship is necessary as a public health measure to fight “rumors,” but stricter controls only feed speculation.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/rumor-fever/', 'snippet': 'The Chinese government claims censorship is necessary as a public health measure to fight “rumors,” but stricter controls only feed speculation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — China hosts some 300 million microblog accounts (including my own), and officials say that domestic social media put out more than 200 million posts every day. In hopes of getting a handle on this potentially threatening surge of information, the government has started a campaign that aims to quash what it calls “rumors” — statements that it says threaten the public order but that it has not bothered to define. After a series of public opinion disasters this past year, the Communist Party has been pressuring social media providers to weed out allegations it finds threatening, and state media have tried to whip up fear over their malignant social effects.The party’s fever over rumors began in August, following the July 23 high-speed rail collision in Wenzhou. The government took a public opinion beating over the crash, in large part because social media harnessed anger over the bungled rescue effort, the safety of the high-speed rail network and corruption in the Railways Ministry. Once party leaders wrested back control of the story, they pushed all relevant facts into the darkness, leaving only rumors to sate the public’s appetite for the truth. And this summer two other embarrassing public opinion defeats for China’s leadership originally took off on the Internet: the Guo Meimei affair, which exposed irregularities at the government-run Red Cross Society of China, and the massive and well-organized public demonstrations against a chemical project in Dalian.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rumor Fever', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T13:23:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bandurski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bandurski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e46e0650-65f8-5ab1-8866-54899b838c2d', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e46e0650-65f8-5ab1-8866-54899b838c2d'}, {'abstract': 'Officials have created separate dorms for v