l steps.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/health/policy/the-personal-price-paid-for-shortages-of-doxil-and-other-drugs.html', 'snippet': 'Production problems have disrupted treatment plans. Experts advise patients to take a number of practical steps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jenny Morrill, who has been battling ovarian cancer since 2007, went to the hospital for her scheduled chemotherapy treatment in June, the nurse greeted her with both good news and bad.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/science/13JPCONS/13JPCONS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 859, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/science/13JPCONS/13JPCONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/13/science/13JPCONS/13JPCONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drug Scarcity’s Dire Cost, and Some Ways to Cope', 'kicker': 'The Consumer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drug Scarcity’s Dire Cost, And Some Ways to Cope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemotherapy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T21:36:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Money & Policy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56683350-4b0a-530c-ab2a-68b38d49c765', 'word_count': 1358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56683350-4b0a-530c-ab2a-68b38d49c765'}, {'abstract': 'Experts on child nutrition are debating whether some of the standard infant feeding practices and attitudes may be in need of revising, particularly the notion of getting infants to eat as much as possible.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/talking-to-parents-about-fat-babies/', 'snippet': 'Experts on child nutrition are debating whether some of the standard infant feeding practices and attitudes may be in need of revising, particularly the notion of getting infants to eat as much as possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Obesity in teenagers and adolescents is a major concern for pediatricians. But the discussion gets tricky when it turns to a weight crisis in infants. As Dr. Perri Klass explains in this week’s Science Times, experts on child nutrition are debating whether some of the standard infant feeding practices and attitudes may be in need of revising, particularly the notion of getting infants to eat as much as possible. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking to Parents About Fat Babies', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-12T21:40:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f8e5aff-9abc-5d21-a8ac-2acc7cc7d3a8', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f8e5aff-9abc-5d21-a8ac-2acc7cc7d3a8'}, {'abstract': 'Jes Gordon, an event producer and an operator of a design firm, discusses how she bounced back after an emergency airplane landing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/business/after-a-fainting-spell-learning-to-fly-again.html', 'snippet': 'Jes Gordon, an event producer and an operator of a design firm, discusses how she bounced back after an emergency airplane landing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A FEW years ago, I was working on a proposal for a wedding while I was traveling. My seatmate was a young woman who had this huge accordion folder on her lap. There must have been a million files in it, all related to her wedding. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/business/Flier/Flier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/business/Flier/Flier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/13/business/Flier/Flier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Fainting Spell, Learning to Fly Again', 'kicker': 'Frequent Flier', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After a Fainting Spell, Learning to Fly Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keyw