ng the decision, government officials indicated that the possibility of huge fines for Canada’s failure to meet greenhouse gas emissions targets had played a role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — Canada said on Monday that it would withdraw from the Kyoto Protocol, the 1997 treaty to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada Announces Exit From Kyoto Climate Treaty', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Canada Announces Exit From Kyoto Climate Treaty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservative Party (Canada)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Treaties', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kyoto Protocol', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T01:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75493bc4-c296-5024-88d2-5ada737b9312', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75493bc4-c296-5024-88d2-5ada737b9312'}, {'abstract': 'In New Hampshire, Newt Gingrich and Jon M. Huntsman Jr. participated in a two-man unmoderated debate devoted to foreign policy and national security.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/huntsman-and-gingrich-square-off-in-unmoderated-debate/', 'snippet': 'In New Hampshire, Newt Gingrich and Jon M. Huntsman Jr. participated in a two-man unmoderated debate devoted to foreign policy and national security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, N.H. — If Newt Gingrich is the Republican nominee and President Obama accepts his famous challenge to seven three-hour Lincoln-Douglas-style debates, viewers might find them only on C-SPAN and should plan to have plenty of black coffee on hand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Huntsman and Gingrich Square Off in Unmoderated Debate', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T01:07:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf22aa06-31ca-59b5-bf2d-0bf02fe2dba1', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf22aa06-31ca-59b5-bf2d-0bf02fe2dba1'}, {'abstract': 'The stakes were evident as the two leading Republican candidates campaigned in New Hampshire, engaging in their most direct attacks yet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/us/politics/romney-takes-direct-aim-at-gingrich-in-new-hampshire.html', 'snippet': 'The stakes were evident as the two leading Republican candidates campaigned in New Hampshire, engaging in their most direct attacks yet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WINDHAM, N.H. — For much of this presidential primary season, New Hampshire has been perceived as being in Mitt Romney’s back pocket. He has a house in Wolfeboro, N.H., and was governor of neighboring Massachusetts, where the news media made him a constant presence in New Hampshire living rooms.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/us/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/13/us/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/us/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/us/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/13/us/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2/ALT-CAMPAIGN-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Front of Republican Pack, Skirmishes Escalate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Front of G.O.P. Pack, Skirmishes Escalate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T01:11:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc163683-211e-5367-a75c-2d7b92cc5ae0', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc163683-211e-5367-a75c-2d7b92cc5ae0'}, {'abstract': 'Obama did.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/12/paying-for-health-reform/', 'snippet': 'Obama did.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve noticed an odd thing in comments whenever the subject of Obamacare comes up. Many commenters scoff when I say that the Obama health reform was fully paid for; not only that, but some of them confidently assert that the Congressional Budget Office says that the reform will increase the deficit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paying For Health Reform', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T01:27:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45713c89-0498-54a3-b559-165c2e86b1f6', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45713c89-0498-54a3-b559-165c2e86b1f6'}, {'abstract': 'That the Giants (7-6), with losses to free agency and injuries, would lead the N.F.C. East and control their playoff possibilities might have seemed improbable a few weeks ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/sports/football/giants-once-improbable-playoff-bid-looks-increasingly-more-certain.html', 'snippet': 'That the Giants (7-6), with losses to free agency and injuries, would lead the N.F.C. East and control their playoff possibilities might have seemed improbable a few weeks ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Giants’ season appeared doomed before it even started. The roster lost several familiar names to free agency in the off-season. Others disappeared because of injuries, and all the while, the Philadelphia Eagles were stockpiling talent.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/sports/subGIANTS/subGIANTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/13/sports/subGIANTS/subGIANTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/sports/subGIANTS/subGIANTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/13/sports/subGIANTS/subGIANTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/13/sports/subGIANTS/subGIANTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Win Tha