r bartenders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, bartenders in New York City won’t be required to wear a hair net.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Health Department Eases Some Rules', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Mental Hygiene Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:13:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41b47ea7-5af7-542a-ae0a-81270580eff2', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41b47ea7-5af7-542a-ae0a-81270580eff2'}, {'abstract': 'The news of a police officer’s death has particular resonance when your son plans to become a cop.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/when-your-child-picks-a-dangerous-career/', 'snippet': 'The news of a police officer’s death has particular resonance when your son plans to become a cop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The death of the New York City Police Officer Peter J. Figoski on Monday is about as incomprehensible as violence can be. Making it worse, of course, is that he was the father of four girls.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Your Child Picks a Dangerous Career', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:16:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84d7b0c2-e3e5-5030-82dc-a5c07f236445', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84d7b0c2-e3e5-5030-82dc-a5c07f236445'}, {'abstract': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration issued a mediocre three-star safety rating for the Fiat 500, but Chrysler said the agency didn’t take into account a safety upgrade made in July.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/wheelies-the-very-fine-print-edition/', 'snippet': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration issued a mediocre three-star safety rating for the Fiat 500, but Chrysler said the agency didn’t take into account a safety upgrade made in July.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The Very Fine Print Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACURA DIVISION OF HONDA MOTOR CO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audi Division of Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Highway Administration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Insurance Institute for Highway Safety', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LEXUS DIVISION OF TOYOTA MOTOR SALES USA', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mercedes-Benz', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:33:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8595b002-7bd2-5a9c-9850-ecf7b810ff6c', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8595b002-7bd2-5a9c-9850-ecf7b810ff6c'}, {'abstract': 'The richest of the city’s rich get about 34 percent of all income earned by New Yorkers. On the other hand, they pay more than 43 percent of the city’s personal income taxes.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/to-the-citys-top-1-a-third-of-the-income/', 'snippet': 'The richest of the city’s rich get about 34 percent of all income earned by New Yorkers. On the other hand, they pay more than 43 percent of the city’s personal income taxes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The very rich are, well, very rich. A new study says the 34,000 or so New Yorkers who constitute the top 1 percent of earners collected more than one-third of all the income earned in the city in 2009. Their average income was $2.2 million, and they reaped 58.3 percent of dividends and interest, 84.6 percent of business income, and 84.7 percent of capital gains. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To the City’s Top 1%, a Third of the Income', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oddo, James S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Independent Budget Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:35:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bbef7b71-76b1-5eb1-a41c-075befbb1b0b', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bbef7b71-76b1-5eb1-a41c-075befbb1b0b'}, {'abstract': 'How yesterday’s Huntsman-Gingrich debate undercut my case for less crowded debates.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/the-trouble-with-debates-ii/', 'snippet': 'How yesterday’s Huntsman-Gingrich debate undercut my case for less crowded debates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'O.K., I take it back. No sooner did I write a post suggesting that future primaries would benefit from more intimate debates — featuring smaller group of candidates rather than the whole field, longer answers rather than short ones, and so on — than yesterday’s Jon Huntsman-Newt Gingrich “Lincoln-Douglas” debate went and demonstrated why that could turn out to be an absolutely terrible idea. Trip Gabriel’s writeup for the Times does a good job of capturing the droning deadliness of the affair, the painful seminar-room quality, the absence any spark in the candidates’s answers to the exquisitely-polite questions. (“After an hour and a half, when the moderator offered the candidates a chance to hit the remaining five topics in a lightning round and they declined, the audience seemed to collectively sigh with relief.”) If Gingrich made good on his pledge to teach an online class while in the Oval Office, I imagine that it would turn out something like this. And if Huntsman’s supporters want to know why their man has struggled to inspire any excitement or strong support, despite an impressive record and solid conservative bona fides, the answer begins with the fact that he just spent 90 minutes on a stage with the candidate leading in the polls and never once said anything that might actually make some real news for his own struggling campaign. (And yes, I understand that it was all about tag-teaming Romney. But that’s no excuse for being lights-out boring.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With Debates (II)', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:36:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a554282a-e6b5-5482-a2d3-792e9c1ca562', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a554282a-e6b5-5482-a2d3-792e9c1ca562'}, {'abstract': 'An effort by the studios to let some of their biggest films play in living rooms just two months after their theatrical debuts was resisted by theater owners. And, ultimately, the studios blinked.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/the-year-in-media-hollywood-backs-down-on-video-on-demand/', 'snippet': 'An effort by the studios to let some of their biggest films play in living rooms just two months after their theatrical debuts was resisted by theater owners. And, ultimately, the studios blinked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Staff members of The Times’s media news department are highlighting the most significant developments this year in the industries they cover.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Media | Hollywood Backs Down on Video on Demand', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:37:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f67c1ce4-788f-5879-b9e3-516a87e70e6a', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f67c1ce4-788f-5879-b9e3-516a87e70e6a'}, {'abstract': 'The life reports are in. Meet James Opie.', 'web_url': 'https://brooks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/the-life-report-james-opie/', 'snippet': 'The life reports are in. Meet James Opie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The following Life Report was submitted in response to my column of Oct. 28, in which I asked readers over 70 to write autobiographical essays evaluating their own lives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Life Report: James Opie', 'kicker': 'David Brooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Opie, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:37:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17e4faf7-6326-5760-a00c-3b87c899ed2f', 'word_count': 1542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17e4faf7-6326-5760-a00c-3b87c899ed2f'}, {'abstract': 'Addressing readers’ objections to the word “illegals,” as I used it in my last column.', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/llegals/', 'snippet': 'Addressing readers’ objections to the word “illegals,” as I used it in my last column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A number of readers have written or tweeted their objection to my use of the word “illegals” as shorthand for “illegal immigrants.” A couple of samples from the in-box:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Illegals’', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-13T16:38:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c53d5a5-d61d-58e6-9dda-adc486b364df', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c53d5a5-d61d-58e6-9dda-adc486b364df'}, {'abstract': 'WowWee’s AppGear games feature objects that can be detected by an iPhone or iPod Touch’s display.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/13/new-ios-games-mix-the-physical-with-the-virtual/', 'snippet': 'WowWee’s AppGear games feature objects that can be detected by an iPhone or iPod Touch’s display.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you ever wanted to fly in sorties over the South Pacific, blow up aliens, or fight off angry homeowners after eating their garbage? WowWee‘s upcoming AppGear collection of toys will allo