ed the territory on the Golan Heights that it had seized from Syria in 1967. The New York Times called it an “abrupt move” that came as a surprise not only to international observers, but also to many Israelis who felt that it was hastily pushed through parliament by Prime Minister Menachem Begin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dec. 14, 1981 | Israel Annexes Former Syrian Territory of Golan Heights', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T09:08:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3e9927d-5071-51da-a383-359bbc20803b', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3e9927d-5071-51da-a383-359bbc20803b'}, {'abstract': 'With Hezbollah now feeling more vulnerable than it has in years, the Shiite group is turning up the volume on anti-Sunni rhetoric.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/nasrallahs-fighting-words/', 'snippet': 'With Hezbollah now feeling more vulnerable than it has in years, the Shiite group is turning up the volume on anti-Sunni rhetoric.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT — The Shiite holy day of Ashura, which commemorates the death of the Prophet Muhammad’s grandson Hussein at the Battle of Karbala in 680, marks the birth of the schism that still divides Sunnis and Shiites. This year, Ashura had a special echo in Lebanon: on Nov. 28, Hezbollah’s secretary general, Sayyed Hassan Nasrallah, gave a speech rife with sectarian overtones, casting Hussein’s ancient tragedy as a parable of contemporary Lebanese politics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nasrallah’s Fighting Words', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 