 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22fb690d-f1ad-51e7-931d-1bb477bccd7a'}, {'abstract': 'Sure, they can be infuriating, not to mention duplicitous, paranoid and self-pitying. But you try being a U.S. ally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/bill-keller-pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'Sure, they can be infuriating, not to mention duplicitous, paranoid and self-pitying. But you try being a U.S. ally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As an American visitor in the power precincts of Pakistan, from the gated enclaves of Islamabad to the manicured lawns of the military garrison in Peshawar, from the luxury fortress of the Serena Hotel to the exclusive apartments of the parliamentary housing blocks, you can expect three time-honored traditions: black tea with milk, obsequious servants and a profound sense of grievance.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/magazine/keller-mag/keller-mag-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/14/magazine/keller-mag/keller-mag-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pakistanis Have a Point', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Pakistanis Have a Point', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4042532-cce2-5eb6-8b42-778dbf1c78bc', 'word_count': 5997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4042532-cce2-5eb6-8b42-778dbf1c78bc'}, {'abstract': 'Shopping-based indicators are supposed to help us predict a recovery or a double dip. Women’s underwear sales look ominous, but cemetery plot sales offer hope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/adam-davidson-economic-indicators.html', 'snippet': 'Shopping-based indicators are supposed to help us predict a recovery or a double dip. Women’s underwear sales look ominous, but cemetery plot sales offer hope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Economics is all about consumption. People either spend money now or they use financial instruments — like bonds, stocks and savings accounts — so they can spend more later. A healthy economy is largely a result of a reasonable balance between consumption today and consumption deferred, and it’s pretty clear that balance has been ridiculously out of whack for a while.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18economy_span/18economy_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18economy_span/18economy_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18economy_span/mag-18Economy-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18economy_span/18economy_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18economy_span/18economy_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Nail Polish Sales Tell Us About the Economy', 'kicker': 'It’s the Economy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indicate This', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T10:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davidson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffbdf82f-7688-5f41-922d-272868f4b8cf', 'word_count': 1070, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffbdf82f-7688-5f41-922d-272868f4b8cf'}, {'abstract': 'Do you find yourself noticing new details about a place after having spent time away from it? What do you think you’ll miss about your current environment someday in the future?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/what-places-in-your-past-do-you-appreciate-more-now-from-a-distanc/', 'snippet': 'Do you find yourself noticing new details about a place after having spent time away from it? What do you think you’ll miss about your current environment someday in the future?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes, returning to places we knew in the past can surprise us: We might notice new details, or appreciate aspects we didn’t at the time. When have you ever seen a place you’ve lived or spent time in with “new eyes”? Do you think you are conscious of what’s special in your current environment, or does the tedium of daily life make you forget to notice these details? In his Op-Ed column “Time, Distance and Clarity,” Frank Bruni reflects on the phenomenon of experiencing a familiar place in a new and different way:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Places in Your Past Do You Appreciate More Now, From a Distance?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T10:50:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95849ade-2a53-5a78-89cc-4d75374f4068', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95849ade-2a53-5a78-89cc-4d75374f4068'}, {'abstract': 'A veteran college counselor shares tips on how applicants (and their families) can handle the combined stress of the admissions season and the holiday season.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/wasden/', 'snippet': 'A veteran college counselor shares tips on how applicants (and their families) can handle the combined stress of the admissions season and the holiday season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carol E. Wasden is director of college counseling at The Hockaday School in Dallas and a member of the Association of College Counselors in Independent Schools.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Holidays? An Application Checklist as Jan. 1 Looms', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T11:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Wasden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wasden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc3ede76-e322-5e1b-b226-3fdc45738077', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc3ede76-e322-5e1b-b226-3fdc45738077'}, {'abstract': 'Many New York voters do not like the way Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has performed on specific issues, like the Occupy Wall Street protests, but they still like his overall job performance.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/most-city-voters-approve-of-mayors-job-performance/', 'snippet': 'Many New York voters do not like the way Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has performed on specific issues, like the Occupy Wall Street protests, but they still like his overall job performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most voters in New York City disapprove of Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s handling of the Occupy Wall Street protests and continue to believe that he has lost focus in his third term, according to a survey released Wednesday morning by Quinnipiac University.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Voters Approve of Mayor’s Overall Job Performance', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'gloca