s Suspension of James Harrison the Right Call?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrison, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T12:47:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99cd0c24-4e12-5bf4-b8fc-66b0752f3c1a', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99cd0c24-4e12-5bf4-b8fc-66b0752f3c1a'}, {'abstract': 'Still absent is a clear commitment from Laos to halt all construction activity related to the proposed dam, which conservationists view as a major environmental threat.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/inevitable-or-in-limbo-a-dam-for-the-mekong/', 'snippet': 'Still absent is a clear commitment from Laos to halt all construction activity related to the proposed dam, which conservationists view as a major environmental threat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As The Times reported last week, environment ministers from Vietnam, Cambodia, Thailand and Laos have decided to await the results of further studies before making a decision on whether to proceed with construction of a dam in the Mekong River. Conservationists argue that damming the river, a vital and biologically diverse lifeline for people in all four countries, would be irresponsible. (Check out our slide show to get a sense of the river’s centrality to people in the basin.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inevitable, or in Limbo? A Dam for the Mekong', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydroelectric Power', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Bank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Laos', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T12:52:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91b89e0c-98fa-5aad-a576-a0047f6fc3ad', 'word_count': 897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91b89e0c-98fa-5aad-a576-a0047f6fc3ad'}, {'abstract': 'A look at some of the year’s top films, as selected by the co-chief film critics A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow.html', 'snippet': 'A look at some of the year’s top films, as selected by the co-chief film critics A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at some of the year’s top films, as selected by the co-chief film critics A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/14/movies/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB/critics-picks-slideshow-slide-MEMB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Critics’ Picks: Movies of 2011', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T13:01:29+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Movies', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d623c334-87c2-5bf8-a2e4-e255340879a2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d623c334-87c2-5bf8-a2e4-e255340879a2'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis discuss their favorite movies of 2011, from those that embrace old-fashioned cinema to more cosmic fare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/movies/awardsseason/film-favorites-of-a-o-scott-and-manohla-dargis-in-2011.html', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott and Manohla Dargis discuss their favorite movies of 2011, from those that embrace old-fashioned cinema to more cosmic fare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANOHLA DARGIS As we know, it’s become ritualistic for critics to whine about the end of the year being crowded with Oscar hopefuls. Oh, pity the poor movie critic forced to watch a new Martin Scorsese movie, a new David Fincher and two from Steven Spielberg in short succession! Yet while this period seems jammed with Important Movies From Harvey Weinstein, the rest of the year looks dire only if audiences count on the major studios and usual suspects for their film fixes. The problem is that the race to the Oscars now so profoundly consumes everyone’s attention that it has distorted the perception of what’s actually available.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18SUB3CRITICS1/18SUB3CRITICS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18SUB3CRITICS1/18SUB3CRITICS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18SUB3CRITICS1/18SUB3CRITICS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18SUB3CRITICS1/18SUB3CRITICS1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18SUB3CRITICS1/18SUB3CRITICS1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Old-Fashioned Glories in a Netflix Age', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Old-Fashioned Glories In a Netflix Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T13:01:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott and Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c9ccb7d-7c6b-5df4-861b-129e180de5f0', 'word_count': 2888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c9ccb7d-7c6b-5df4-861b-129e180de5f0'}, {'abstract': 'A move by the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries to increase its production target should have little lasting effect on oil prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/business/global/opec-sets-oil-production-target.html', 'snippet': 'A move by the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries to increase its production target should have little lasting effect on oil prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries agreed on Wednesday to increase its production target for the first time in three years, a move that appeared to signal that Saudi Arabia and Iran had put aside their recent differences on oil policy, at least temporarily.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/business/Opec/Opec-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 