b757d24d', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ae5ab7d-7b8f-5069-9127-b71ab757d24d'}, {'abstract': 'Benoit Coeuré, a French Treasury official who was confirmed Wednesday to the European Central Bank board, said the bank may have to step up bond purchases.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/business/global/new-ecb-official-may-be-open-to-bond-buying.html', 'snippet': 'Benoit Coeuré, a French Treasury official who was confirmed Wednesday to the European Central Bank board, said the bank may have to step up bond purchases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank might become more open to intervention in bond markets after European lawmakers on Wednesday approved Benoît Coeuré, a French economist and longtime Treasury official, for a seat on the policy making executive board.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/business/ECB/ECB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 698, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/business/ECB/ECB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/15/business/ECB/ECB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Frenchman to Join Board of Europe’s Bank', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Frenchman to Join Board of Europe’s Bank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:25:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fcb5f91-0bc4-53c2-a58a-222cec27eaea', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fcb5f91-0bc4-53c2-a58a-222cec27eaea'}, {'abstract': 'Start-ups can’t devote resources to building apps for Windows Phone 7 until more customers adopt the platform. But customers will not flock to Windows Phone 7 until their favorite apps exist. Some major applications are intrigued by its unique design, although they recognize the design may raise development costs.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/windows-phone-7-caught-in-mobile-app-catch-22/', 'snippet': 'Start-ups can’t devote resources to building apps for Windows Phone 7 until more customers adopt the platform. But customers will not flock to Windows Phone 7 until their favorite apps exist. Some major applications are intrigued by its unique des...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Designers and start-up tech firms seem to agree on two things when it comes to Microsoft’s Windows Phone 7 mobile platform. First, it’s gorgeous and uniquely designed. Second, they won’t be building an app for it in the near future.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Windows Phone 7 Caught in Mobile App Catch-22', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windows (Operating System)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foursquare', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TaskRabbit Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:35:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/665851cb-31c0-5d82-a21a-a061b0692b40', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/665851cb-31c0-5d82-a21a-a061b0692b40'}, {'abstract': 'Analyzing the nominations for the SAG Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/a-closer-look-at-the-actors-picks/', 'snippet': 'Analyzing the nominations for the SAG Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During all the awards hoopla, actors often talk about how meaningful it is to be recognized by their peers, and the Screen Actors Guild, which announced its nominees today, may be the highest expression of that sentiment, or at the least the ceremony where you hear it repeated in acceptance speeches the most.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Closer Look at the Actors’ Picks', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:39:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5781701b-fc5e-5dd8-9f3b-a6b1b0d67b0d', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5781701b-fc5e-5dd8-9f3b-a6b1b0d67b0d'}, {'abstract': 'Suzanne Hart, an advertising executive at Y&R, was entering an elevator at the company’s Madison Avenue headquarters when the car suddenly shot upward, pinning her between the elevator floor and the top of the entranceway, the authorities said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/three-hurt-in-midtown-elevator-plunge-one-critically/', 'snippet': 'Suzanne Hart, an advertising executive at Y&R, was entering an elevator at the company’s Madison Avenue headquarters when the car suddenly shot upward, pinning her between the elevator floor and the top of the entranceway, the authorities said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Read the updated version of this article that appears in the newspaper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Woman Fatally Crushed in Midtown Elevator Accident', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elevators and Escalators', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Suzanne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:42:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman, Meredith Hoffman and Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Meredith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba743411-6442-55ea-906a-2cb4a0be255f', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba743411-6442-55ea-906a-2cb4a0be255f'}, {'abstract': 'A federal recommendation to ban drivers from using cellphones is generating heated responses on both sides of the issue.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/outright-ban-on-cellphone-use-in-car-a-recommendation-too-far/', 'snippet': 'A federal recommendation to ban drivers from using cellphones is generating heated responses on both sides of the issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, the National Transportation Safety Board, an independent federal agency responsible for promoting traffic safety and investigating accidents, recommended that states ban drivers from using hands-free devices, including wireless headsets. As noted Tuesday by The Times, it was the first time that a federal agency urged an end to the practice completely, rather than endorsing a partial ban, as some state legislatures have adopted to permit hands-free talking.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outright Ban on Cellphone Use in Car: A Recommendation Too Far?', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Transportation Safety Board', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:42:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a3b1737-5dc1-59f2-9b88-080563e67e6e', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a3b1737-5dc1-59f2-9b88-080563e67e6e'}, {'abstract': 'Doom and gloom hovered over films in 2011, with prevalent themes of apocalypse (“Take Shelter,” “Melancholia”) and economic uncertainty (“Margin Call,” “The Descendants”).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/movies/awardsseason/2011-films-melancholia-tree-of-life-moneyball.html', 'snippet': 'Doom and gloom hovered over films in 2011, with prevalent themes of apocalypse (“Take Shelter,” “Melancholia”) and economic uncertainty (“Margin Call,” “The Descendants”).', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was the season of apocalypse. Whether your idea of the end is reconciliatory (a family reunion on a misty far shore in “The Tree of Life”) or punishing — the collision of Earth with another planet in “Melancholia”; extinction in a metaphysical tornado in “Take Shelter” — 2011 was the year of brooding cosmic consciousness in serious films. Even the mystical “Tree of Life” is shadowed with the notion of a fearsome, authoritarian God.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18HOLDEN/18HOLDEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18HOLDEN/18HOLDEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 257}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18HOLDEN/18HOLDEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18HOLDEN/18HOLDEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18HOLDEN/18HOLDEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Riding Off Into Civilization’s Sunset', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Riding Off Into Civilization’s Sunset', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:49:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/189c164d-6d80-5e1e-b20d-b1f398484a07', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/189c164d-6d80-5e1e-b20d-b1f398484a07'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Campaign Stops column.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/a-paul-gingrich-showdown/', 'snippet': 'This week’s Campaign Stops column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s column for Campaign Stops, I look at what could be the defining battle of the Iowa caucuses — Newt Gingrich versus Ron Paul:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Paul-Gingrich Showdown?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/885e9a9f-819f-51d8-9f23-a21e23e62fce', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/885e9a9f-819f-51d8-9f23-a21e23e62fce'}, {'abstract': 'It remained unclear when Asif Ali Zardari would return from the United Arab Emirates, adding to the speculation that his exit may have reflected pressure to leave from his country’s military.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/world/asia/hospitalized-pakistan-president-asif-ali-zardari-to-be-discharged-but-no-word-on-his-return.html', 'snippet': 'It remained unclear when Asif Ali Zardari would return from the United Arab Emirates, adding to the speculation that his exit may have reflected pressure to leave from his country’s military.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — President Asif Ali Zardari of Pakistan, who unexpectedly flew to the United Arab Emirates on Dec. 6 for what was officially described as medical treatment, will be discharged from a hospital there on Thursday, according to his aides and physician.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Word on President’s Return to Pakistan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Leader’s Return Is Uncertain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zardari, Asif Ali', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United Arab Emirates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T15:58:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5d8da60-bc3a-5ebd-8c48-95e106fac0c0', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5d8da60-bc3a-5ebd-8c48-95e106fac0c0'}, {'abstract': 'The only thing better than reading a play yourself is reading it with people you like, a fact I am reminded of by the opening tomorrow of Roman Planski’s new film, “Carnage,” based on Yasmina Reza’s play. What plays would you enjoy reading aloud with friends or family?', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/staging-your-own-theatrical-table-reading/', 'snippet': 'The only thing better than reading a play yourself is reading it with people you like, a fact I am reminded of by the opening tomorrow of Roman Planski’s new film, “Carnage,” based on Yasmina Reza’s play. What plays would you enjoy reading aloud w...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a fan of reading plays — new ones, old ones — as literature, and I have written before about how Americans are missing out by not keeping up, on the page, with the best theater in New York City and elsewhere. The only thing better than reading a play yourself is reading it with people you like, a fact I am reminded of by the opening tomorrow of Roman Polanski’s new film “Carnage.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Staging Your Own Theatrical Table Reading', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daniels, Jeff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Hope', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gandolfini, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harden, Marcia Gay', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polanski, Roman', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reza, Yasmina', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T16:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dwight Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf7e56d-ea77-5476-8311-20d691bf75b1', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf7e56d-ea77-5476-8311-20d691bf75b1'}, {'abstract': 'The famous pear-shaped “La Peregrina” pearl, with its ruby, diamond and pearl necklace by Cartier, sold for $11.8 million, a world record for a pearl jewel.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/elizabeth-taylors-collection-breaks-records-at-christies/', 'snippet': 'The famous pear-shaped “La Peregrina” pearl, with its ruby, diamond and pearl necklace by Cartier, sold for $11.8 million, a world record for a pearl jewel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elizabeth Taylor’s stardust lent a profitable glow to the first night of Christie’s auction of the movie star’s extensive collection of jewelry and clothing. The sale of Taylor’s “Legendary Jewels” grossed just under $116 million, more than doubling the previous record for a private jewel collection sold at auction. The jewels of the Duchess of Windsor garnered about $50 million in 1987.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Elizabeth Taylor’s Collection Breaks Records at Christie’s', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diamonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pearls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burton, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Elizabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T16:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d43c296-6f32-569c-aa49-3656b2bc28cb', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d43c296-6f32-569c-aa49-3656b2bc28cb'}, {'abstract': 'Nerve.com, a racy Web magazine, is unveiling an online dating site that is taking a page from social media.  Interactions are based around status updates and minireports about meals eaten, concerts watched and places visited.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/nerve-introduces-hip-dating-site-again/', 'snippet': 'Nerve.com, a racy Web magazine, is unveiling an online dating site that is taking a page from social media.  Interactions are based around status updates and minireports about meals eaten, concerts watched and places visited.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Before there were hip dating sites like OkCupid, Grindr and Grouper, there was Nerve Personals, the racy dating section of the online magazine Nerve.com, which billed itself as a home for “literate smut.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/technology/14bits-nerve/14bits-nerve-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nerve.com Introduces a Hip Dating Site — Again', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Dating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nerve.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T16:02:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f539197-a5ad-5ec3-9aa9-3b6cd6889d91', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f539197-a5ad-5ec3-9aa9-3b6cd6889d91'}, {'abstract': 'A panel investigating the 2010 gulf oil spill says that  safety had not been a focus of the oil industry or its regulators, a problem that became more grave over the years as the complexity of offshore drilling deepened.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/report-seeks-far-tighter-safety-system-for-oil-drilling/', 'snippet': 'A panel investigating the 2010 gulf oil spill says that  safety had not been a focus of the oil industry or its regulators, a problem that became more grave over the years as the complexity of offshore drilling deepened.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Companies involved in offshore drilling and the government agencies that regulate drilling should take a more robust and systematic approach to ensuring safety, a team of scientific experts advised on Wednesday in a report on last year’s Deepwater Horizon oil spill in the Gulf of Mexico. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Seeks Far Tighter Safety System for Oil Drilling', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico Oil Spill (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salazar, Ken', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Academies of the United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Academy of Engineering', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States National Research Council', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T16:04:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Campbell Robertson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Campbell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f94b209c-1008-5ee6-9f83-3eb12e8202c4', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f94b209c-1008-5ee6-9f83-3eb12e8202c4'}, {'abstract': 'The fight over the shale gas boom, which pits towns and cities against energy companies and states eager for growth, has raised a fundamental question about the role of local government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/us/towns-fighting-to-stand-ground-against-gas-drillers.html', 'snippet': 'The fight over the shale gas boom, which pits towns and cities against energy companies and states eager for growth, has raised a fundamental question about the role of local government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOUTH FAYETTE, Pa. — As energy companies move to drill in densely populated areas from Pennsylvania to Texas, battles are breaking out over who will have the final say in managing the shale gas boom. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/us/15natgas_cnd/15natgas_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/15/us/15natg