'main': "Comptroller Halts Custodians' Contract", 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T17:50:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By WINNIE HU', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HU', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a0f0ec6-18e2-5d91-8774-08e0759bb9a0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a0f0ec6-18e2-5d91-8774-08e0759bb9a0'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Holder is fighting back against efforts to suppress turnout at the polls.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/the-attorney-general-and-voting-rights/', 'snippet': 'Eric Holder is fighting back against efforts to suppress turnout at the polls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has been a coordinated attack this year on voting rights. More than a dozen states have enacted laws that are intended to make it more burdensome for Americans to cast a ballot, which President Lyndon Johnson called “the basic right, without which all others are meaningless.” New requirements – for special IDs, for example–will reduce turnout among minorities, the uneducated, the poor, the elderly, the newly arrived, students and other groups that traditionally vote for Democratic candidates. (For an explanation of why voter ID laws have a discriminatory effect, see my previous post on the subject.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Attorney General and Voting Rights', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T17:54:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecb5e08c-4f9a-52dd-b451-3fa3774442bd', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecb5e08c-4f9a-52dd-b451-3fa3774442bd'}, {'abstract': 'On Wednesday, Nokia announced the first step in its comeback in the United States with its first smartphone based on Microsoft’s Windows Phone operating system. Anyone hoping for Nokia to market its return with guns cocked and aimed at Apple and Google is likely to be disappointed, though.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/nokia-courts-smartphone-newbies-in-u-s-return/', 'snippet': 'On Wednesday, Nokia announced the first step in its comeback in the United States with its first smartphone based on Microsoft’s Windows Phone operating system. Anyone hoping for Nokia to market its return with guns cocked and aimed at Apple and G...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The past few years have not been kind to Nokia in the cellphone market, especially in the United States where homegrown iPhones and Android handsets crowded out the venerable Finnish handset maker. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nokia Courts Smartphone Newbies in U.S. Return', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windows (Operating System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nokia Oyj', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a68f49ae-bd68-5e96-be74-eec464396d55', 'word_count': 652, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a68f49ae-bd68-5e96-be74-eec464396d55'}, {'abstract': 'What if you created a floating, visa-free incubator 12 miles out to sea from Silicon Valley?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/the-start-up-boat/', 'snippet': 'What if you created a floating, visa-free incubator 12 miles out to sea from Silicon Valley?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Technology gurus have long lamented how hard it is for foreign talent to secure American visas and create start-ups here. As Congress spins its wheels with endless debate over immigration, an ambitious venture based in Sunnyvale, Calif., is trying to chart a more productive course aboard a 600-foot boat, or possibly a barge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Start-Up Incubator That Floats', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thiel, Peter A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T18:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81af3d08-046c-565a-9ab0-c83d1d1baae8', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81af3d08-046c-565a-9ab0-c83d1d1baae8'}, {'abstract': 'America’s neighbors to the north and south, which have rarely both made the finals for the foreign-language Oscar in the same year, look to have a good shot at repeating last year’s feat with two movies that couldn’t be less alike.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/canada-and-mexico-aim-for-an-oscar-nomination-repeat/', 'snippet': 'America’s neighbors to the north and south, which have rarely both made the finals for the foreign-language Oscar in the same year, look to have a good shot at repeating last year’s feat with two movies that couldn’t be less alike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the oddities of the most recent competition for the best foreign-language film Oscar was that the five finalists included both Canada and Mexico. That had happened only once before, in 2006, and precedent would seem to dictate that films from America’s neighbors to the north and south are unlikely to be nominated together in two consecutive years. Nevertheless, both countries have strong entries again in 2011, with films that could scarcely be more different from each other.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canada and Mexico Aim for an Oscar Nomination Repeat', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Falardeau, Philippe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Naranjo, Gerardo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T18:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78bc0499-ad3b-575d-8245-10a447d9c82d', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78bc0499-ad3b-575d-8245-10a447d9c82d'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney supporters escalate their attacks against New Gingrich.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/super-pac-backing-romney-up-with-anti-gingrich-ad/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney supporters escalate their attacks against New Gingrich.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How worried are Mitt Romney supporters?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'PAC Backing Romney Shows Anti-Gingrich Ad', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Will, George F', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Freddie Mac', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Restore Our Future', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T18:04:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8286f75-7005-55e5-8973-5b2b69df6180', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8286f75-7005-55e5-8973-5b2b69df6180'}, {'abstract': 'Physicians should parse the future more clearly for terminal patients and their families, says one experts.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/plain-speaking-at-the-end-of-life/', 'snippet': 'Physicians should parse the future more clearly for terminal patients and their families, says one experts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Stephen Workman, an internist at the Queen Elizabeth II Health Sciences Center in Halifax, Nova Scotia, takes exception to the language physicians use with patients near the end of life. In one of his recent publications, he called language the most important tool health professionals have to improve the care of dying patients.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Plain Speaking at the End of Life', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T18:06:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c620bd5-0c60-5489-a0d3-8504685c7586', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c620bd5-0c60-5489-a0d3-8504685c7586'}, {'abstract': 'The Austrian city abounds with tourists, most searching for the sound of music — be it Mozart or the von Trapps — yet some of its best offerings are its Christmas markets and taverns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/travel/36-hours-salzburg-austria.html', 'snippet': 'The Austrian city abounds with tourists, most searching for the sound of music — be it Mozart or the von Trapps — yet some of its best offerings are its Christmas markets and taverns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH its medieval citadel, Baroque palaces and yodel-ready Alpine vistas, Salzburg has never had trouble luring visitors. The Austrian city abounds with tourists, most searching for the sound of music — be it Mozart or the von Trapps. Yet some of its best offerings are its least historic: contemporary galleries, fair trade coffeehouses and the futuristic Hangar 7 complex. In winter, the city is at its most hospitable, as squares transform into bustling Christmas markets, and candlelit taverns fill with skiers refueling on Teutonic comfort food. Salzburg may no longer be frozen in the past, but its appeal is timeless.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/travel/18HOURS1_SPAN/18HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height'