t_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/255189f9-bc73-5611-87f3-fbd720022604', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/255189f9-bc73-5611-87f3-fbd720022604'}, {'abstract': 'Every working parent knows good child care is hard to find. When subsidies disappear for lower-income parents, it gets harder.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/when-child-care-subsidies-disappear-care-is-hard-to-find/', 'snippet': 'Every working parent knows good child care is hard to find. When subsidies disappear for lower-income parents, it gets harder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How can you be a working parent without child care? Day care, preschool, after-school programs: without them, or some alternative, a parent of younger school-age children can work only from 8 a.m. to 3 p.m., and a parent of even younger children can’t work at all.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Child Care Subsidies Disappear, Care is Hard to Find', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-14T22:48:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/836da831-6328-5328-9c91-1978c3a96f0d', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/836da831-6328-5328-9c91-1978c3a96f0d'}, {'abstract': 'Candy Spelling, the widow of the producer Aaron Spe