ivered of-the-moment jokes and lively dances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/theater/reviews/lysistrata-jones-at-walter-kerr-theater-review.html', 'snippet': '“Lysistrata Jones” is a throwback to the perishable good-time musicals in which peppy kids delivered of-the-moment jokes and lively dances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That blond babe we fell in love with in June is still looking hot in the cold light of December.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/arts/lysis/lysis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 386, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/15/arts/lysis/lysis-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/arts/lysis/lysis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/15/arts/lysis/lysis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/15/arts/lysis/lysis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yes, Even Sexting Is Off Limits', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Lysistrata Jones'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yes, Even Sexting Is Off Limits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transport Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beane, Douglas Carter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARISTOPHANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T03:05:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc9733c3-eab1-5efa-ace7-7108edeefafa', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc9733c3-eab1-5efa-ace7-7108edeefafa'}, {'abstract': 'Regulators have proposed a more objective system to determine the riskiness of the trading portfolios of the nation’s 30 biggest banks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/opinion/beyond-the-ratings-agencies.html', 'snippet': 'Regulators have proposed a more objective system to determine the riskiness of the trading portfolios of the nation’s 30 biggest banks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ratings agencies were one of the culprits of the financial crisis — bestowing top-notch, triple-A ratings on thousands of mortgage-backed securities that proved to be worth less than junk when the housing bubble burst. It has taken three years, but the nation’s banking regulators have now made a good proposal to remove the raters as the official arbiters of financial reliability and replace them with a set of objective, transparent criteria to determine the riskiness of assets in the trading portfolios of the nation’s 30 biggest banks. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond the Raters', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond the Raters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Ratings and Credit Rating Agencies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T03:05:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c557c557-0076-5970-b9cd-ba2c79c74daa', 'word_count': 440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c557c557-0076-5970-b9cd-ba2c79c74daa'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers are trying to impose their policy preferences on the president by exercising the power of the purse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/us/politics/spending-bill-held-up-as-congress-dickers-over-riders.html', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers are trying to impose their policy preferences on the president by exercising the power of the purse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Travel to Cuba. Abortions in the nation’s capital. Energy efficiency standards for light bulbs. Those are a few of the issues that have ensnarled members of Congress trying to finish an omnibus spending bill that was due to be completed more than two months ago.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Omnibus Spending Bill Held Up as Congress Dickers Over Riders and Restrictions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Omnibus Spending Bill Held Up as Congress Dickers Over Riders and Restrictions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T03:08:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2547bad-637f-5634-8eb9-1e4c7ead4869', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2547bad-637f-5634-8eb9-1e4c7ead4869'}, {'abstract': 'House Republican leaders sidetracked a sensible and overdue bill to ban members of Congress from using inside information in stock trades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/opinion/insider-trading-is-illegal-just-not-on-capitol-hill.html', 'snippet': 'House Republican leaders sidetracked a sensible and overdue bill to ban members of Congress from using inside information in stock trades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'House Republican leaders have foolishly sidetracked a bipartisan ethics bill in Congress to ban members from using inside information they gain in private hearings and discussions in stock trades. Insider trading is illegal generally, but Congress’s apparent exception from the ban was never a concern on Capitol Hill until a recent report on “60 Minutes” questioned whether various members were profiting from back-room knowledge.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Illegal, Just Not on Capitol Hill', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illegal, Just Not on Capitol Hill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value'