': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s “Masterpieces of Renaissance Portraiture” exhibition will feature free-standing sculptures in the round, the better to examine their details.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/arts/design/sculpture-as-portrait-at-the-metropolitan-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s “Masterpieces of Renaissance Portraiture” exhibition will feature free-standing sculptures in the round, the better to examine their details.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A surprising number of sculptures are included in “Masterpieces of Renaissance Portraiture,” which opens at the Metropolitan Museum of Art on Wednesday. But rather than display them against gallery walls the curators will show many of them free-standing and in the round. That’s because Keith Christiansen, chairman of European paintings at the Met, who has organized the exhibition with Stefan Weppelmann, curator of Italian and Spanish paintings at the Gemäldegalerie in Berlin, wants visitors to be able to examine some of these three-dimensional works from every angle.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16VOGEL/16VOGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 516, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16VOGEL/16VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16VOGEL/16VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sculpture as Portrait at the Metropolitan', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sculpture as Portrait at the Metropolitan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': 