ract': 'Families in search of rambling colonials, large backyards and low property taxes are invigorating home construction in Dutchess County.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/realestate/in-the-region-westchester-dutchess-county-joins-the-dance.html', 'snippet': 'Families in search of rambling colonials, large backyards and low property taxes are invigorating home construction in Dutchess County.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN sharp contrast to Westchester these days, Dutchess County is awash in new home construction — fueled mainly by young families in search of rambling colonials, large backyards and low property taxes. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18WEZONE_SPAN/18WEZONE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18WEZONE_SPAN/18WEZONE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18WEZONE_SPAN/we-zone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dutchess County Joins the Dance', 'kicker': 'In the Region | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dutchess Joins the Dance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dutchess County (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Property Taxes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T22:47:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elsa Brenner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elsa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brenner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4306262a-c99b-5f73-9f90-6afbb4e0ab90', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4306262a-c99b-5f73-9f90-6afbb4e0ab90'}, {'abstract': '“Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows” confects a smoky, overcast Victorian world, infuses it with an air of jocular, hairy laddishness and stages a lot of fights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/movies/sherlock-holmes-a-game-of-shadows-review.html', 'snippet': '“Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows” confects a smoky, overcast Victorian world, infuses it with an air of jocular, hairy laddishness and stages a lot of fights.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Downey Jr. is currently carrying two movie franchises — the Marvel “Iron Man” proto-“Avengers” thing for Paramount and the brawling steampunk “Sherlock Holmes” series for Warner Brothers — so it is perhaps understandable that he is showing a touch of fatigue. In the new Holmes adventure, “A Game of Shadows,” his imperiousness is hard to distinguish from boredom, and he seems to be in a hurry to spit out his lines, take his lumps, throw his punches and collect his paycheck.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16SHERLOCK_SPAN/SHERLOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16SHERLOCK_SPAN/SHERLOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16SHERLOCK_SPAN/SHERLOCK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16SHERLOCK_SPAN/SHERLOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16SHERLOCK_SPAN/SHERLOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Holmes and Watson, but Is There Mystery?', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Holmes and Watson, But Is There Mystery?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holmes, Sherlock (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritchie, Guy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Law, Jude', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McAdams, Rachel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rapace, Noomi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T22:47:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.&nbsp;O. SCOTT', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8293fb88-ce3d-5798-a5af-f8ec048bc23f', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8293fb88-ce3d-5798-a5af-f8ec048bc23f'}, {'abstract': '“Cathy Rigby Is Peter Pan,” starring Ms. Rigby, keeps young eyes wide in wonder with the antics of its title character and the sinister Captain Hook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/theater/reviews/cathy-rigby-is-peter-pan-at-theater-at-madison-square-garden-review.html', 'snippet': '“Cathy Rigby Is Peter Pan,” starring Ms. Rigby, keeps young eyes wide in wonder with the antics of its title character and the sinister Captain Hook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Curmudgeons (that is, theater critics) may scoff at the mere mention of its name, but young eyes will be wide in wonder throughout “Cathy Rigby Is Peter Pan,” the perennial favorite that is once again sprinkling handfuls of glitter — sigh, make that “fairy dust” — at the Theater at Madison Square Garden. Starring Ms. Rigby, 21 years after her Broadway debut in the title role, this likable, well-oiled musical only occasionally misses a beat, more often because of its script and staging than its cast. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/CATHY/CATHY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/CATHY/CATHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/CATHY/CATHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eternal Youth Still Defying Pull of Gravity', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Cathy Rigby Is Peter Pan'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eternal Youth Still Defying Pull of Gravity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rigby, Cathy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T22:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/edcad703-2da8-561b-9b41-ba4718156e30', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edcad703-2da8-561b-9b41-ba4718156e30'}, {'abstract': 'The inaugural exhibition at the Frick’s new Portico Gallery presents 65 pieces of tableware, vases and small, figurative sculptures, dating mainly from 1710 to 1730.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/arts/design/a-jewel-box-for-translucent-treasures.html', 'snippet': 'The inaugural exhibition at the Frick’s new Portico Gallery presents 65 pieces of tableware, vases and small, figurative sculptures, dating mainly from 1710 to 1730.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In light of the glass-box atrium plugged into the J. P. Morgan Library & Museum a few years ago, New York cultural custodians might have been understandably alarmed to learn of plans for architectural intervention at another great institution of Gilded Age ancestry. They need not have worried. The Frick Collection’s transformation of an outdoor colonnade into an indoor exhibition space, now called the Portico Gallery, is as subtly noninvasive as the Morgan’s addition is conspicuously anachronistic.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16FRICK1_SPAN/16FRICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16FRICK1_SPAN/16FRICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16FRICK1_SPAN/16FRICK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16FRICK1_SPAN/16FRICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16FRICK1_SPAN/16FRICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Jewel Box for Translucent Treasures', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Jewel Box for Translucent Treasures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T22:53:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bddbe276-b212-54f7-b2b5-e937bba20fd3', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bddbe276-b212-54f7-b2b5-e937bba20fd3'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly two-thirds of Americans said the United States should admit immigrants who were fleeing poverty, and more people said immigrants fill jobs than take them away.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/americans-still-mostly-welcome-immigrants-survey-finds/', 'snippet': 'Nearly two-thirds of Americans said the United States should admit immigrants who were fleeing poverty, and more people said immigrants fill jobs than take them away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Immigration may be a hot button issue but you would not know it from the latest public opinion survey, which shows Americans sharply critical of government policy but still welcoming of legal immigrants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans Still (Mostly) Welcome Immigrants, Survey Finds', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T22:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason DeParle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeParle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa052e1e-7598-586d-a5b1-5a1aba5127d0', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa052e1e-7598-586d-a5b1-5a1aba5127d0'}, {'abstract': 'Charles J. Hynes said he disagreed with the mayor’s criticism of the judge who released a man now accused in a police officer’s killing.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/brooklyn-district-attorney-defends-judge/', 'snippet': 'Charles J. Hynes said he disagreed with the mayor’s criticism of the judge who released a man now accused in a police officer’s killing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn District Attorney, Charles J. Hynes, on Thursday dismissed Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s criticism of Brooklyn Criminal Court Judge Evelyn Laporte, who released the suspected killer of a New York City policeman without bail on a drug possession charge in November.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklyn District Attorney Defends Judge', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hynes, Charles J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pride, Lamont', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T23:03:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d497b9d-4ce6-55fe-b95d-d0fbb47376f6', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d497b9d-4ce6-55fe-b95d-d0fbb47376f6'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Transit Museum in Brooklyn Heights is a museum of specimens, a natural history museum of the city’s public transportation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/arts/design/new-york-transit-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Transit Museum in Brooklyn Heights is a museum of specimens, a natural history museum of the city’s public transportation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of us, while visiting the New York Transit Museum, will not care too much that in the 1930s large coiled tubes containing liquid carbon tetrachloride were used as fuses to protect the city’s subway system from short circuits. Many of us approach this subject so lightly that we might think a label on an electrical contraption — “Edison Bipolar Dynamo” — is missing a colon after the name of the inventor, who may not have been bipolar but was certainly a dynamo. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16MUSEUM1_SPAN/16MUSEUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16MUSEUM1_SPAN/16MUSEUM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16MUSEUM1_SPAN/16MUSEUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16MUSEUM1_SPAN/16MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16MUSEUM1_SPAN/16MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stand Clear of the Ghosts', 'kicker': 'Museum Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stand Clear Of the Ghosts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Transit Museum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T23:03:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Rothstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f041094b-a46f-5ce2-8824-faae581ab65f', 'word_count': 1391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f041094b-a46f-5ce2-8824-faae581ab65f'}, {'abstract': 'The saxophonist Azar Lawrence, who played with Miles Davis, Freddie Hubbard and McCoy Tyner, among others, returned to the Jazz Standard to record a live album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/arts/music/azar-lawrence-quintet-at-jazz-standard-review.html', 'snippet': 'The saxophonist Azar Lawrence, who played with Miles Davis, Freddie Hubbard and McCoy Tyner, among others, returned to the Jazz Standard to record a live album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were heavy gales blowing from the stage at the Jazz Standard late Tuesday night: fast, roiling, tenacious. They embodied the focused effort of the tenor and soprano saxophonist Azar Lawrence, leading an assembled quintet. But “effort” didn’t feel like the impression Mr. Lawrence was trying to foster. Throughout his generously proportioned second set, he played as if plugging into a life force. He seemed committed to the idea of the self as a vessel; those winds were whipping through him. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16AZAR_SPAN/16AZAR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16AZAR_SPAN/16AZAR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16AZAR_SPAN/AZAR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16AZAR_SPAN/16AZAR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/arts/16AZAR_SPAN/16AZAR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bracing Wind, Propelling a Dynamic Comeback', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bracing Wind, Propelling a Dynamic Comeback', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz Standard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-15T23:04:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/46ba7293-8ab9-5664-8a6d-971afd14889d', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46ba7293-8ab9-5664-8a6d-971afd14889d'}, {'abstract': '“Shlemiel the First,” a musical drawn from the work of Isaac Bashevis Singer that played in New York briefly in 1994, is being revived at the Skirball Center for the Performing Arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/theater/reviews/shlemiel-the-first-at-skirball-center-review.html', 'snippet': '“Shlemiel the First,” a musical drawn from the work of Isaac Bashevis Singer that played in New York briefly in 1994, is being revived at the Skirball Center for the Performing Arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The easily disoriented title character of “Shlemiel the First” sets out on a multiyear sojourn, only to return to his town a few hours later. The musical bearing his name, Robert Brustein’s slight but pleasurable adaptation of Isaac Bashevis Singer’s folk tales, has seen a lot more of the world.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, '