rson': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7781e19-5899-5e0a-acb7-00dff74f58b4', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7781e19-5899-5e0a-acb7-00dff74f58b4'}, {'abstract': 'In Mississippi’s alligator hunt, which began in 2005, hunters travel the Pearl or the Pascagoula rivers using hooks, lines and the occasional shotgun.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/sports/in-alligator-hunt-look-for-glowing-eyes-then-watch-your-fingers.html', 'snippet': 'In Mississippi’s alligator hunt, which began in 2005, hunters travel the Pearl or the Pascagoula rivers using hooks, lines and the occasional shotgun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GAUTIER, Miss. — It was around midnight when Jeremy and JoAnna Mitchell’s spotlight caught the reflective eyes of the alligator. Judging from its head, Whac-a-Mole-like on the surface of the water, it was a large animal, maybe a 10-footer.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/sports/ALLIGATORfloater/ALLIGATORfloater-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 705, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/sports/ALLIGATORfloater/ALLIGATORfloater-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/sports/ALLIGATORfloater/ALLIGATORfloater-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Look for the Glowing Eyes, Then Watch Your Fingers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Look for the Glowing Eyes, Then Watch Your Fingers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALLIGATORS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hunting and Trapping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T02:23:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Card', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Card', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eceb9298-18c9-5fac-926f-0b033c27e039', 'word_count': 1318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eceb9298-18c9-5fac-926f-0b033c27e039'}, {'abstract': 'James Connolly, who has an arrhythmia and wears a defibrillator, moved to a senior living center after years in a five-story walk-up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/nyregion/battling-health-problems-bills-and-a-five-story-climb.html', 'snippet': 'James Connolly, who has an arrhythmia and wears a defibrillator, moved to a senior living center after years in a five-story walk-up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This May, when James Connolly, a slim New York City native with a slight stoop and a shock of white hair, started life anew, he took the downtown E train, carrying bags of his clothes and all the history books he could fit.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Stints in Hospital, a Struggle With Bills', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Stints in the Hospital, Facing a Fight With Bills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federation of Protestant Welfare Agencies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T02:23:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Higgins DeSmet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': 'Higgins', 'lastname': 'DeSmet', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2256a43-a948-52b0-b37f-e88aabbaaec6', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2256a43-a948-52b0-b37f-e88aabbaaec6'}, {'abstract': 'How a poem about a lonely Civil War picket guard caught fire across North and South.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/picket-lines/', 'snippet': 'How a poem about a lonely Civil War picket guard caught fire across North and South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Nov. 30, 1861, issue of Harper’s Weekly featured a poem destined to become one of the essential texts of the Civil War, Ethel Lynn Beers’s “The Picket-Guard.” Beers, a 34-year-old native of Goshen, N.Y., said later that she wrote her only famous work in a single morning, after a boardinghouse breakfast at which one of her fellow residents relayed a newspaper report of “all quiet along the Potomac, as usual.” Beers had answered by reading aloud the sub-headline, “except a poor picket shot.” Her versified elaboration on the dispatch, in which a lonely guard is killed by a sniper early one morning, posed moral questions about the culture of war-making that have proved far more durable than her sentimental literary style.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Picket Lines', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T02:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas J. Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e7ccf69-cd43-532b-890d-a2cf7aa30615', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e7ccf69-cd43-532b-890d-a2cf7aa30615'}, {'abstract': 'Ford Motor Company and WPP, the agency holding company, is seeking to reinvent its Lincoln luxury-car brand, which is lagging luxury-car competitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/business/media/wpp-to-start-boutique-agency-for-lincoln.html', 'snippet': 'Ford Motor Company and WPP, the agency holding company, is seeking to reinvent its Lincoln luxury-car brand, which is lagging luxury-car competitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS the Ford Motor Company seeks to reinvent its Lincoln luxury-car brand, WPP, the giant agency holding company that works for Ford Motor, is reinventing its model for creating campaigns for Lincoln.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/business/sub-ADCO1/sub-ADCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 256, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/16/business/sub-ADCO1/sub-ADCO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 256}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/business/sub-ADCO1/sub-ADCO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/16/business/sub-ADCO1/sub-ADCO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/16/business/sub-ADCO1/sub-ADCO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford’s Home Team Chooses New York as Site for Lincoln’s Boutique Agency', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ford’s Home Team Chooses New York As Site for Lincoln’s Boutique Agency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WPP Group Plc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Luxury Goods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Division of Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T02:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1617533-3d86-5358-a8f9-f94f21d45c23', 'word_count': 957, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1617533-3d86-5358-a8f9-f94f21d45c23'}, {'abstract': 'The “Frances” children’s books take us all the way to delight, using an easy-reader vocabulary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/opinion/russell-hoban-father-of-frances.html', 'snippet': 'The “Frances” children’s books take us all the way to delight, using an easy-reader vocabulary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to write a book. It’s harder to write one with living characters, clever scenes, warmth and wit. And it’s harder still when the people you’re writing for can’t read, or read only a little, when the words you choose must be simple, short and sweet. And if not always sweet, at least short.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russell Hoban, Father of ‘Frances’', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Appreciations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russell Hoban, Father of ‘Frances’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoban, Russell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T02:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3e73cdd-a42b-55c6-9039-c5ada16cdeaa', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3e73cdd-a42b-55c6-9039-c5ada16cdeaa'}, {'abstract': 'A look at several of the social programs featured here in 2011 that made progress in their mission to help others.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/15/news-flash-progress-happens/', 'snippet': 'A look at several of the social programs featured here in 2011 that made progress in their mission to help others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The benefit of writing a column about solutions is that it provides an alternative lens through which to view the world. The daily news tends to be dominated by daunting challenges (unemployment, climate change, the polarization of Congress) and flashpoint events (the killing of Osama bin Laden, the tsunami in Japan, the Penn State scandal). These stories are vital to cover. However, people often come away from the news with a lot more information about problems than about how society is dealing with them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Flash: Progress Happens', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'majo