ing an appreciative second look.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Weegee in Hollywood? You might as well imagine Atget in Cannes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weegee’s Other Naked City', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T10:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4389ca10-5922-51c7-967c-6ed00a23e880', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4389ca10-5922-51c7-967c-6ed00a23e880'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Is it inappropriate to buy food and resell it or contribute it to a party?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/is-it-wrong-to-sell-store-bought-pastries-at-a-bake-sale/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Is it inappropriate to buy food and resell it or contribute it to a party?', 'lead_paragraph': 'At bake sales and during the holiday season, at gatherings with family and friends, do you notice more homemade food or store-bought goods? Which do you prefer? Do you think it appropriate for people to bring store-bought pastries to a bake sale or potluck?In a Dining article, “‘Store Bought’ Spoils the Potluck Spirit‘,” Jennifer Steinhauer laments that some people, on the occasion of a bake sale or holiday potluck, might buy refreshments rather than make them themselves. She writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Wrong to Sell Store-Bought Pastries at a Bake Sale?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16