aterial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c9b1f22-625c-5194-83f5-9e613842bc24', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c9b1f22-625c-5194-83f5-9e613842bc24'}, {'abstract': 'Membership will end the anomaly of having a leading oil and gas exporter and permanent member of the United Nations Security Council outside the world trade system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/business/global/wto-accepts-russia-bid-to-join.html', 'snippet': 'Membership will end the anomaly of having a leading oil and gas exporter and permanent member of the United Nations Security Council outside the world trade system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GENEVA — Global trade ministers on Friday approved Russia’s bid to join the World Trade Organization, giving Prime Minister Vladimir V. Putin a victory on the international stage at a time of rising domestic opposition to his hold on power. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/business/WTO/WTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/17/business/WTO/WTO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/business/WTO/WTO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'W.T.O. Grants Russia Membership', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'W.T.O. Welcomes Russia As Its Newest Member', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Trade Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:15:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f3589db-8fd6-5b16-832c-e451ad5fe092', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f3589db-8fd6-5b16-832c-e451ad5fe092'}, {'abstract': 'If the drug charges against Bears receiver Sam Hurd end up being true, it will rank among the N.F.L.’s biggest scandals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/sports/the-shocking-arrest-of-sam-hurd-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'If the drug charges against Bears receiver Sam Hurd end up being true, it will rank among the N.F.L.’s biggest scandals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps it’s a good thing that the sports news still has the capacity to surprise and amaze us, even if the bad part is, it takes a whopper of a story to accomplish that. After all, once you’ve absorbed the child sexual abuse apocalypse at Penn State, the bar is raised a bit. But the story out of Chicago that Bears receiver Sam Hurd was charged Thursday with dealing a rather massive amount of cocaine and marijuana as an alleged sideline to his $2 million-a-year N.F.L. job, well, that still has the ability to drop a few jaws.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/sports/17leading2/17leading2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 583, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/sports/17leading2/17leading2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_na