'_id': 'nyt://article/10faee89-32f0-5c2e-9635-bee34eca403b', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10faee89-32f0-5c2e-9635-bee34eca403b'}, {'abstract': 'Today is free shipping day online, Facebook introduces its new Timeline format, the painful cycle of eczema and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/friday-reading-today-is-free-shipping-day-online/', 'snippet': 'Today is free shipping day online, Facebook introduces its new Timeline format, the painful cycle of eczema and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Today Is Free Shipping Day Online', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:20:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/805da4d2-f7eb-5300-80ba-a726f3cfc567', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/805da4d2-f7eb-5300-80ba-a726f3cfc567'}, {'abstract': 'Olga Bloom, who created Bargemusic, and Anthony Amato of Amato Opera are symbols of a world gone by, as the types of permanent performance spaces they championed become things of the past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/arts/music/deaths-of-olga-bloom-and-anthony-amato-represent-end-of-an-era.html', 'snippet': 'Olga Bloom, who created Bargemusic, and Anthony Amato of Amato Opera are symbols of a world gone by, as the types of permanent performance spaces they championed become things of the past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of them found a tiny old theater on a grungy stretch of the Bowery. The other mortgaged her home to buy a 19th-century coffee barge and haul it to the Fulton Ferry Landing in Brooklyn.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17AMATO1/17AMATO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 996, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17AMATO1/17AMATO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17AMATO1/17AMATO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Loss of 2 Arts Leaders, the End of an Era', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Loss of 2 Arts Leaders, the End of an Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bargemusic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloom, Olga', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amato, Anthony S', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMATO OPERA', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f55bf7b7-fbcf-51e4-9f67-8cb789f19fc7', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f55bf7b7-fbcf-51e4-9f67-8cb789f19fc7'}, {'abstract': 'In P. D. James’s sequel to “Pride and Prejudice,” Elizabeth Bennet’s impossible siblings are still making trouble and there’s a murder to be solved.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/death-comes-to-pemberley-by-p-d-james-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In P. D. James’s sequel to “Pride and Prejudice,” Elizabeth Bennet’s impossible siblings are still making trouble and there’s a murder to be solved.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aspiring writers take heart: one of the most beloved novels of all time was rejected when it was fluttered in front of publishers in 1797 — or so the historical record suggests. Called “First Impressions,” it was written by a country girl whose father was so impressed by his daughter’s sage and vivacious creation that he sent a letter of inquiry to a publisher, who refused even to look at the manuscript. Never mind. Sixteen years later, the book appeared under a different title. Perhaps you’ve heard of it: “Pride and Prejudice.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Schillinger/Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Schillinger/Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Schillinger/Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pride and Prejudice and Murder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Inferiority of Her Connections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, P D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:21:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/26cabbfb-4114-501e-b471-f0c09266df56', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26cabbfb-4114-501e-b471-f0c09266df56'}, {'abstract': 'In the second volume of his collected lyrics, Stephen Sondheim explicates his meticulous craft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/look-i-made-a-hat-by-stephen-sondheim-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In the second volume of his collected lyrics, Stephen Sondheim explicates his meticulous craft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephen Sondheim claims to dislike most opera, and I guess I believe it. He also says he avoids serious fiction, and I suppose I believe this, too, even if his new book, “Look, I Made a Hat,” which collects his lyrics for the musical theater written from 1981 to 2011, does conclude with a quotation from James Joyce’s “Ulysses.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18LEITHAUSER/18LEITHAUSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18LEITHAUSER/18LEITHAUSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18LEITHAUSER/18LEITHAUSER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18LEITHAUSER/18LEITHAUSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18LEITHAUSER/18LEITHAUSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sondheim on Sondheim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweet Clarity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sondheim, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:22:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Leithauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leithauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/953dec83-5b74-5c97-8051-ae66c78e130d', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/953dec83-5b74-5c97-8051-ae66c78e130d'}, {'abstract': 'In his later years, Philip K. Dick’s hallucinatory experiences overlapped with the concerns of his science fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/the-exegesis-of-philip-k-dick-edited-by-pamela-jackson-jonathan-lethem-and-erik-davis-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In his later years, Philip K. Dick’s hallucinatory experiences overlapped with the concerns of his science fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1979, I visited Philip K. Dick for a profile I was writing. In a modest apartment he shared with dusty stacks of books, deteriorating furniture, a vintage stereo system and a couple of cats, he took the opportunity to go public about a singular experience dominating his life. For the past five years, he told me earnestly, he had been receiving messages from a spiritual entity. “It invaded my mind and assumed control of my motor centers,” he said. “It set about healing me physically and my 4-year-old boy, who had an undiagnosed life-threatening birth defect that no one had been aware of. It had memories dating back over 2,000 years. . . . There wasn’t anything that it didn’t seem to know.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18platt-web/18platt-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 848, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18platt-web/18platt-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18platt-web/18platt-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Voices in Philip K. Dick’s Head', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dreaming of Androids', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dick, Philip K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lethem, Jonathan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:23:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Platt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Platt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d444ff28-343b-50f0-919e-68de7de2d39a', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d444ff28-343b-50f0-919e-68de7de2d39a'}, {'abstract': 'Novels by Helen Dunmore, Hillary Jordan, Alan S. Cowell, Joshua Mohr and Sonia Taitz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/fiction-chronicle.html', 'snippet': 'Novels by Helen Dunmore, Hillary Jordan, Alan S. Cowell, Joshua Mohr and Sonia Taitz.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE BETRAYAL  By Helen Dunmore.  Black Cat. Paper, $14.95.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/18STUART/18STUART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo