w. Forever, says the doctor, disappearing into cyberspace.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Callow/Callow-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Callow/Callow-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Callow/Callow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Callow/Callow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Callow/Callow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting to Know Charles Dickens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Great Expectations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hendrix, John (Illustrator)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickens, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Mick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Granstrom, Brita', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:28:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Callow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Callow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3bd9949-5333-582a-a8f4-dcf4037f40d8', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3bd9949-5333-582a-a8f4-dcf4037f40d8'}, {'abstract': 'Daniel Handler’s novel sifts through the wreckage of a high school romance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/why-we-broke-up-by-daniel-handler-illustrated-by-maira-kalman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Daniel Handler’s novel sifts through the wreckage of a high school romance.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s not you, it’s me — you’re great.” “I’m so sorry, but this just isn’t working out.” “I think we should see other people.” Whether it was the boy who dumped you in the sandbox for that kid with the bright red fire truck or the girl who abruptly stopped answering your notes during algebra, no one makes it through life without exposure to that miserable condition known as the breakup. And now we have Daniel Handler (who also writes as Lemony Snicket) giving us the lowdown on the rise and fall of one dissolution in particular in “Why We Broke Up.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Edinger/Edinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 745, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Edinger/Edinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/Edinger/Edinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daniel Handler’s Teenagers Out of Love', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Things We Did for Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalman, Maira', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Handler, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:29:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Edinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/921123fc-2f13-5b07-bbcc-2a66b1db9288', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/921123fc-2f13-5b07-bbcc-2a66b1db9288'}, {'abstract': 'While acknowledging that the legislation is an improvement over current regulations, some safety experts say that it does not go far enough.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/congress-approves-pipeline-safety-bill/', 'snippet': 'While acknowledging that the legislation is an improvement over current regulations, some safety experts say that it does not go far enough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A bill doubling the maximum fines that pipeline operators face for safety violations easily cleared the House and Senate this week along with other provisions intended to strengthen rules on oil and gas pipeline safety that critics have faulted as weak. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Congress Approves Pipeline Safety Bill', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pipelines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rockefeller, John D IV', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN PETROLEUM INSTITUTE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Transportation Safety Board', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Department (US)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:29:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Frosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a585dc75-52b0-5526-a736-47e95b7e7f51', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a585dc75-52b0-5526-a736-47e95b7e7f51'}, {'abstract': 'A true story of a girl who wins admission to an incredibly selective dance academy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/a-girl-named-faithful-plum-by-richard-bernstein-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A true story of a girl who wins admission to an incredibly selective dance academy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These days, reality TV shows like “American Idol” inundate us with the sad stories of talented hopefuls, most of them eventually let down. Featured in condensed clips, orchestrated with sound bites, their stories never inspire true reflection, but we watch anyway. We are enthralled by the idea of someone emerging from obscurity to achieve stardom. Making your dream come true — your own individual dream versus that of the collective — is the equivalent of the individual pursuit of happiness. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dancing Out of China', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dancing Out of China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernstein, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grace Lin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grace', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca563426-6dbd-51d1-9389-ec8eea0f90ef', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca563426-6dbd-51d1-9389-ec8eea0f90ef'}, {'abstract': 'The Giving Spree continues with more gifts to give and get from T’s friends and family.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/the-giving-spree-jeffrey-eugenides/', 'snippet': 'The Giving Spree continues with more gifts to give and get from T’s friends and family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who: Jeffrey Eugenides, the author, most recently, of “The Marriage Plot.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Giving Spree | Jeffrey Eugenides', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfb3b168-7132-592e-a4f7-7d87fa8f8689', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfb3b168-7132-592e-a4f7-7d87fa8f8689'}, {'abstract': 'What happens in a family when a girl’s imagination shades into psychosis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/without-tess-by-marcella-pixley-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'What happens in a family when a girl’s imagination shades into psychosis.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ll never let you go, Lizzie. No matter what happens to me, I’ll never ever let you go.” This refrain, which runs through “Without Tess,” contains a dark double message about the bonds and boundaries of sisterhood. Following up on themes raised in her well-received first novel, “Freak,” in which a teenager distances herself from a bullied younger sister, Marcella Pixley continues to explore the complexities of sibling loyalty; this time, a girl’s separation and loss as she comes to terms with her sister’s suicide.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Limits of Make-Believe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Limits of Make-Believe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mindy Lewis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mindy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/61f6838b-f9cd-5f95-a7b7-c64a122c7dc3', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61f6838b-f9cd-5f95-a7b7-c64a122c7dc3'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, two urban teenagers bond over a rescued pit bull.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/books/review/stay-with-me-by-paul-griffin-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, two urban teenagers bond over a rescued pit bull.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to legend, the Random House co-founder Bennett Cerf once said that to guarantee a best-selling book, you should title it “Lincoln’s Doctor’s Dog.” In these dog-crazed times, you could probably skip both the president and the doctor — especially if you throw an adoring retriever or a bug-eyed terrier on the cover. Think of “The Dogs of Babel,” “Marley & Me,” “You Had Me at Woof”; the list goes on.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pit Bull Plays Cupid', 'kicker': 'Young Adult', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘I Don’t Need Anybody Else’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griffin, Paul (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stay With Me (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T15:31:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathi Hanauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c5d2d0c-d69f-5e1b-919a-94a6dc3384f2', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c5d2d0c-d69f-5e1b-919a-94a6dc3384f2'}, {'abstract': 'The Jukes are being recalled for a defective weld that can cause a stalled engine, while the Sentra battery may suddenly lose voltage, leading to a stall-out.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/nissan-recalls-28000-jukes-and-34000-sentras-for-possible-stalling-proble