n finds that, alas, his mother knows best.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/grabbing-life-by-the-horns.html', 'snippet': 'Traveling abroad with a group of Orthodox friends, a son finds that, alas, his mother knows best.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a child, my mother told me that everyone in the world hated me. They hated me, she said, because I was a Jew.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18lives/mag-18Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18lives/mag-18Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18lives/mag-18Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grabbing Life by the Horns', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grabbing Life by the Horns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTI-SEMITISM', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:41:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shalom Auslander', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shalom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Auslander', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/406a87b0-fee9-5b58-8466-56299de1601c', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/406a87b0-fee9-5b58-8466-56299de1601c'}, {'abstract': 'The aggravating tics of new TV hits, like “Homeland” and “American Horror Story,” suggest that the current golden age of television is in peril.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/riff-homeland-american-horror-story.html', 'snippet': 'The aggravating tics of new TV hits, like “Homeland” and “American Horror Story,” suggest that the current golden age of television is in peril.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine that a laboratory rat has just been introduced to a maze. We know that rats will navigate mazes for a reward — a pellet of food, say, or a dose of cocaine — but what if the reward of this particular maze is only the promise of a mystery pellet of unknown origin?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18riff-horror/18riff-horror-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18riff-horror/18riff-horror-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18riff-horror/18riff-horror-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Clues That Lead to More Clues That Add Up to Nothing’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Clues That Lead to More Clues That Add Up to Nothing’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Havrilesky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Havrilesky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcb04e5f-f885-5679-ad7e-efbe6d449f59', 'word_count': 1741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcb04e5f-f885-5679-ad7e-efbe6d449f59'}, {'abstract': 'The frontman of the late R.E.M. loved being a pop star. Most of the time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/michael-stipe-is-not-grumpy.html', 'snippet': 'The frontman of the late R.E.M. loved being a pop star. Most of the time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A member of R.E.M. once said that in the early days you guys drank a lot and took a lot of speed. Listening to the older songs on your new retrospective, “Part Lies, Part Heart, Part Truth, Part Garbage,” I was unable to stop bouncing my leg. Is it the speed I’m hearing?  That certainly contributed. Peter Buck developed a style of guitar playing that was, at the time, very unpopular, picking rather than playing punk-rock power chords. Mike Mills’s favorite band was the Ramones, and with that you got that hyperanimated 16th note and 32nd note. And with speed and beer you get speed. I slowed down my vocal to try to counter the speed and inadvertently developed the vocal style that is still with me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18talk/mag-18talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 539, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18talk/mag-18talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18talk/mag-18talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Stipe Is Not Grumpy', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Michael Stipe Is Not Grumpy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'REM', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stipe, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/78cd43e0-a070-573e-bcd8-90a7d46969f3', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78cd43e0-a070-573e-bcd8-90a7d46969f3'}, {'abstract': 'In his obscure art exhibition, Shea Hembrey takes on the role of 100 artists. And not one of them is any less real than another.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/shea-hembrey.html', 'snippet': 'In his obscure art exhibition, Shea Hembrey takes on the role of 100 artists. And not one of them is any less real than another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could be forgiven for not knowing about the artist Mart Meyer, who lives in Dallas and photographs what he sees almost moment by moment. Or for not having heard of the Chinese artist Ju Hu, who created a large-scale work examining the “malleable flow of time” as he camped out in an autumnal forest and tried to tape every fallen leaf back onto its tree. Almost certainly, you’re not acquainted with the art made by Rosalie Starr, who died in 1969 but whose sculptures were recently discovered in the attic of a Georgia boardinghouse, or the guerrilla feces paintings — quite beautiful, really — that have been popping up on the walls of public restrooms in New York and four other states and attributed to an artist known only as the East Coast Swiper.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18hembrey_span/mag-18hembrey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18hembrey_span/mag-18hembrey-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 726}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18hembrey_span/mag-18hembrey-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18hembrey_span/mag-18hembrey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18hembrey_span/mag-18hembrey-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Hundred Artists Rolled Into One Man', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eat Your Heart Out, Andy Warhol', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corbett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74310fc2-eb55-5666-a5fe-1f84d1023712', 'word_count': 3624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74310fc2-eb55-5666-a5fe-1f84d1023712'}, {'abstract': 'The sand dunes of Glamis, Calif., are a majestic expanse of pristine sand and sky. Until the dune buggies arrive, that is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/18/magazine/look-glamis-dunes.html', 'snippet': 'The sand dunes of Glamis, Calif., are a majestic expanse of pristine sand and sky. Until the dune buggies arrive, that is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sand dunes of Glamis, Calif., are a majestic expanse of pristine sand and sky. Until the dune buggies arrive, that is.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sand Blast', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dunes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recreational Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALL-TERRAIN VEHICLES (ATV)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/447a272a-805e-5976-a422-ccde005eb3a2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/447a272a-805e-5976-a422-ccde005eb3a2'}, {'abstract': 'A Senegalese carpenter becomes a knife-wielding Ahab.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/an-old-fashioned-shark-hunt.html', 'snippet': 'A Senegalese carpenter becomes a knife-wielding Ahab.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While his children slept on the sand during the final hour before daybreak, Pathé Ndong packed a canister of diesel and an icebox full of knives for the day ahead. In Mbour, a shantytown on Senegal’s Atlantic coast, everyone is a fisherman in disguise, including this father of nine, his face obscured by a Western Union baseball cap and a pharaonic goatee. On mornings like this one, the carpenter becomes a knife-wielding Ahab. When I asked Ndong what he was out to catch, his 43-year-old eyes flared. “Gainde guedj,” he said in Wolof. The lion of the sea.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18here_span/mag-18Here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18here_span/mag-18Here-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18here_span/mag-18Here-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18here_span/mag-18Here-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18here_span/mag-18Here-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Old-Fashioned Shark Hunt', 'kicker': 'You Are Here: Mbour, Senegal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Great White Hope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Senegal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sharks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Drew Hinshaw', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Drew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hinshaw', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9933d34-b795-5651-8ef7-451db8a76f41', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9933d34-b795-5651-8ef7-451db8a76f41'}, {'abstract': 'How a shopping magnate in Oklahoma dreamed up the wheeling shopping cart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/who-made-that-shopping-cart.html', 'snippet': 'How a shopping magnate in Oklahoma dreamed up the wheeling shopping cart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Grocery shopping was once a relatively genteel pastime. Stores had pressed-tin ceilings and employees in white aprons. Patrons called in orders and had them delivered free. The notion that you might want to palpate your own cantaloupe was absurd.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18wmt/mag-18WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 870, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18wmt/mag-18WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/magazine/18wmt/mag-18WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grocery Shopping on Speed', 'kicker': 'Makers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Shopping Cart)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supermarkets and Grocery Stores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b580683-2e7f-5aad-a329-f81cfafc545d', 'word_count': 608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b580683-2e7f-5aad-a329-f81cfafc545d'}, {'abstract': 'Family ties; polling lies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/ethicist-bully-brother.html', 'snippet': 'Family ties; polling lies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 40-year-old brother is successful but has no friends or social skills. On his best days, he is monosyllabic. On his bad days, he’s belligerent, baiting our family into snapping at him or each other. He calls this “Zach Galifianakis humor.” My parents think, That’s just the way he is. I think he has Asperger’s syndrome. My question: What’s the ethical way to behave toward a close family member who has serious psychological issues and possibly a disability? I could ride it out for the few holidays we see him. Though I have had no success suggesting therapy, I feel I should be actively helping him. My husband wants to punch his lights out. This is also tempting. ANONYMOUS, AUSTIN, TEX.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Brother, My Bully', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Brother, My Bully', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Asperger's Syndrome", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bullies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1664fa5-f4cd-55b9-8d19-40a003911e28', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1664fa5-f4cd-55b9-8d19-40a003911e28'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/reply-all-waking-chris.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a graduate student in neuroscience who studies patients with disorders of consciousness, including some who respond to zolpidem, I commend the author for accurately capturing both the desperation and the hope associated with recovery from brain injury. But I strongly object to the article’s title as it appeared on The Times’s Web site: “A Drug That Wakes the Near Dead.” Patients with disorders of consciousness like the ones profiled in the article are not near death. Such a label conflates brain injury with brain degeneration. Further, it trivializes the often heartbreaking end-of-life decisions that families make. While the article made this point, the title you chose unfortunately reinforces a false stereotype of these very much alive patients.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Waking Chris', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waking Chris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:42:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa93d9be-a6dc-57b4-8a09-1cbdb90475b5', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa93d9be-a6dc-57b4-8a09-1cbdb90475b5'}, {'abstract': 'Just like normal people, rich and famous authors like Janet Evanovich often give books for the holidays. Sometimes they even give best sellers. Here’s the proof.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Just like normal people, rich and famous authors like Janet Evanovich often give books for the holidays. Sometimes they even give best sellers. Here’s the proof.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE GIFT LIST: Do bankers give money for the holidays? Do designers give clothes? I don’t know — but I do know that, just like you and me, rich and famous authors often give books. Sometimes they even give best sellers. Exhibit A is Janet Evanovich (at No. 6 on the hardcover fiction list this week with “Explosive Eighteen”), who will hand her husband a copy of Walter Isaacson’s biography of Steve Jobs (at No. 1 on the hardcover nonfiction list). Evanovich, inset, also plans to give Mario Batali’s new cookbook, “Molto Batali,” to her foodie son-in-law, and Dr. Seuss’ “How the Grinch Stole Christmas!” to her grandson. He’s named Max, just like the Grinch’s beleaguered dog.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/insidelistevanovich/insidelistevanovich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/books/review/insidelistevanovich/insidelistevanovich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:46:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9da99c51-0ff4-5e61-ba2f-a04b7a30ecb5', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9da99c51-0ff4-5e61-ba2f-a04b7a30ecb5'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/magazine/reply-all-mitt-romney-bot.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The problem with Mitt is that he is a dime-a-dozen Northeast manager. For the bottom line, he will do anything. Who gets taken care of: Wal-Mart, G.M., big pharma, foreign companies/donors, Chinese businesses, lobbyists. Who loses: seniors, I.T. workers, American taxpayers, honest and dedicated public servants and the military.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Building a Better Mitt Romney-Bot', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Building A Better Mitt Romney-Bot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T16:49:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f3dde72-acbc-5972-8ca3-492e5846aa75', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f3dde72-acbc-5972-8ca3-492e5846aa75'}, {'abstract': 'With so many financially stressed this year, the year-end expectations for gifts or donations require new calculations. Here’s what experts say about the ethics of giving (and refusing).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/your-money/in-a-time-of-giving-learning-when-to-say-no.html', 'snippet': 'With so many financially stressed this year, the year-end expectations for gifts or donations require new calculations. Here’s what experts say about the ethics of giving (and refusing).', 'lead_paragraph': 'THIS is the time of year when you are asked for all sorts of donations. I always find the process moderately stressful, but not because my wife and I are agonizing over how much to give.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/your-money/17wealth/17wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/17/your-money/17wealth/17wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth'