ar’s musical highlights I am struck by how many took me by surprise. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18TOMMASINI1/18TOMMASINI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18TOMMASINI1/18TOMMASINI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/arts/18TOMMASINI1/18TOMMASINI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Would Have Thought?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Would Have Thought?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mariinsky Theater Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Budapest Festival Orchestra', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:22:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5742f751-b4d3-5906-b04c-8aec7b9a6737', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5742f751-b4d3-5906-b04c-8aec7b9a6737'}, {'abstract': 'Developing a realistic and empirically valid notion of what polls tell us.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/how-our-primary-forecasts-work/', 'snippet': 'Developing a realistic and empirically valid notion of what polls tell us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today we’re officially launching the FiveThirtyEight polling-based forecasts of the upcoming presidential primaries and caucuses. This article will explain, in modestly but not excruciatingly technical detail, how they work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Our Primary Forecasts Work', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:26:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8ec8cc0-051e-5ae0-8ee2-bb2f838daaf3', 'word_count': 2050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8ec8cc0-051e-5ae0-8ee2-bb2f838daaf3'}, {'abstract': 'Most dog experts will tell you there are no bad apartment dogs, only bad owners. But there are ways to ensure a good fit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/realestate/dogs-living-in-new-york-city-city-as-chew-toy.html', 'snippet': 'Most dog experts will tell you there are no bad apartment dogs, only bad owners. But there are ways to ensure a good fit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CERTAIN charms of New York City living — the infinite brunch options, the 24-hour bodegas, the Museum of Modern Art — are no doubt lost on many pets. Even so, plenty of owners believe that the city is ideal for dogs, offering endless opportunities for socializing and sniffing at dog runs, hydrants and doggy day care centers. Working late? Dog walkers abound. Square footage is tight? More opportunity for canine-human bonding.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18COV_SPAN/18COV_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18COV_SPAN/18COV_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18COV_SPAN/jump-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18COV_SPAN/18COV_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/realestate/18COV_SPAN/18COV_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The City as Chew Toy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City as Chew Toy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elissa Gootman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gootman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43ce93ba-5192-591c-a216-04533464a505', 'word_count': 1987, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43ce93ba-5192-591c-a216-04533464a505'}, {'abstract': 'Renee Schoenfeld, the college adviser at Abraham Lincoln High School in Coney Island, is part wagon train driver, part Bobby Knight, part therapist, and all Jewish mother. She harasses, supports, nudges and guides the juniors and seniors at the 2,600-student school through the process of picking a college, writing essays, getting the applications in on time, pursuing financial aid and, over all, moving them on to the next step after high school. Sitting in her office for a day is like being on stage during a French farce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/16/at-lincoln-high-the-path-to-college-is-through-renee-schoenfeld/', 'snippet': 'Renee Schoenfeld, the college adviser at Abraham Lincoln High School in Coney Island, is part wagon train driver, part Bobby Knight, part therapist, and all Jewish mother. She harasses, supports, nudges and guides the juniors and seniors at the 2,...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Lincoln High, the Path to College Is Through Renee Schoenfeld', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By JESSICA SIEGEL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SIEGEL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f660dc6-f36f-530e-9b8a-9ffe5d16f3fb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f660dc6-f36f-530e-9b8a-9ffe5d16f3fb'}, {'abstract': 'A look at Japan’s new coffee generation.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/ristretto-tokyo-coffee/', 'snippet': 'A look at Japan’s new coffee generation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I tell people that I went to Tokyo to check out the coffee, I get two reactions. One is bewilderment — as if I went to Denver for the surfing. The other is fascination: those who pay attention to coffee know that Japan is the world’s third-largest importer (after the United States and Germany), with obsessive buyers who regularly land the winning bids at Cup of Excellence auctions, and that it produces the coffee gear everybody wants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ristretto | Tokyo Coffee', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:30:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dafae3a-7c27-5982-a322-f31b4abd8c09', 'word_count': 1717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dafae3a-7c27-5982-a322-f31b4abd8c09'}, {'abstract': 'The 112th Congress lurched toward the end of its tumultuous first session Friday as the House passed a $1 trillion spending bill and sent it to the Senate for approval.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/us/politics/boehner-ties-oil-pipeline-to-payroll-tax-bill.html', 'snippet': 'The 112th Congress lurched toward the end of its tumultuous first session Friday as the House passed a $1 trillion spending bill and sent it to the Senate for approval.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Senate leaders said on Friday night that they had reached a deal that would extend a payroll tax cut for two months — falling far short of the yearlong extension they had been seeking. The agreement would also speed the decision process for the\xa0construction of an oil pipeline from Canada to the Gulf Coast, a provision necessary to win over Republicans who opposed the tax break. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/us/17congress/17congress-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/us/17congress/17congress-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/17/us/17congress/17congress-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Leaders Agree on 2-Month Extension of Payroll Tax Cut', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Senate in Deal To Extend Cuts In Payroll Tax', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Keystone Pipeline System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T17:33:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'su