s/', 'snippet': 'Boy by Band of Outsiders showed a preppy-pretty prefall collection at the label’s Broome Street studio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This morning, Boy by Band of Outsiders showed a preppy-pretty prefall collection at the label’s Broome Street studio. There, collegiate plaids and sweet foulards on tie-neck blouses were tucked into pencil skirts and under tailored blazers and, in one instance, paired with a very Ivy League-looking velvet green suit. Here, a romantic red check peplum blouse and skirt that’s fit for eating clubs, social clubs and dance clubs alike.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Boy by Band of Outsiders', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Band of Outsiders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T21:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19c34330-d319-5e0b-8472-df7f82a44727', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19c34330-d319-5e0b-8472-df7f82a44727'}, {'abstract': 'The good news about roasting a whole beef tenderloin is that although it needs your attention, it doesn’t need it for very long.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/dining/roast-tenderloin-thats-worth-the-tending-a-good-appetite.html', 'snippet': 'The good news about roasting a whole beef tenderloin is that although it needs your attention, it doesn’t need it for very long.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROASTING a whole beef tenderloin can seem like the kind of thing that only a very accomplished — or mildly delusional — cook would undertake as the centerpiece to a holiday meal. Not only is it one of the priciest cuts, but it’s also one of the least forgiving. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21APPE_SPAN/21APPE_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tenderloin That’s Well Worth the Tending', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tenderloin That’s Well Worth the Tending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T21:33:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a473fb7-7d58-5365-963f-5399591a4e1d', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a473fb7-7d58-5365-963f-5399591a4e1d'}, {'abstract': 'In a video, Melissa Clark guides you and your twine.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/buying-and-tying-a-tenderloin/', 'snippet': 'In a video, Melissa Clark guides you and your twine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’re spending this kind of money on a hunk of meat, it really makes sense to find a butcher you can trust, or at least strike up conversation with the meat carvers in the back of your supermarket. You want to make sure the fillet you are buying is well trimmed of any silverskin, which is a thin layer of white connective tissue that is as unpleasant as gristle to chew (and all gristle should be expunged as well). Then have your butcher tie up the roast so it’s easier to handle and will cook more evenly. Failing that, you can watch my latest video, above, on how to tie up a roast. It’s not hard but does take more time than, say, having someone else do it for you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buying and Tying a Tenderloin', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 2, 'majo