': 'nyt://audio/8a56e517-1e1e-5766-b211-4355d496992b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/8a56e517-1e1e-5766-b211-4355d496992b'}, {'abstract': 'An underground iconoclast gets his reckoning.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/artifacts-robert-heineckens-copywork/', 'snippet': 'An underground iconoclast gets his reckoning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts | Robert Heinecken’s ‘Copywork’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T22:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Yablonsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yablonsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8324d8e4-c60a-580e-b7f3-1ad576b621a8', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8324d8e4-c60a-580e-b7f3-1ad576b621a8'}, {'abstract': 'Do more mothers than fathers really prefer part-time work, or are we not asking the right question?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/do-working-moms-really-prefer-part-time-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Do more mothers than fathers really prefer part-time work, or are we not asking the right question?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The National Marriage Project released an optimistic report this week on marital satisfaction and parenthood. Because “When Baby Makes Three” was edited by the director of the project, which aims to promote marriage by identifying “strategies to increase marital quality and stability,” and by the Director of the Center for Marriage and Families at the Institute for American Values, which seeks to increase “the proportion of children growing up with their two married parents,” this is not a report that focuses on numbers showing that marital happiness decreases among parents. It’s a report that looks at why that’s not true for a “substantial minority of husbands and wives.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Working Mothers Really Prefer Part-Time Jobs?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T22:03:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5e3e0fa-8cad-5b55-b2dd-8580b4438b04', 'word_count': 777, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5e3e0fa-8cad-5b55-b2dd-8580b4438b04'}, {'abstract': 'What Mitt Romney says he didn’t know about Medicaid leads to a campaign dust-up.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/what-romney-knew-about-medicaid/', 'snippet': 'What Mitt Romney says he didn’t know about Medicaid leads to a campaign dust-up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SIOUX CITY, Iowa — As Mitt Romney finished up an early morning town-hall-style meeting in Iowa, finishing up an answer on entitlement programs like Medicare and Medicaid, he offered a casual confession: “I have to admit, I didn’t know all the differences between these things before I got into government, and then got into it and understood that Medicaid is the health care program for the poor, by and large.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Romney Knew About Medicaid', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schultz, Debbie Wasserman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-16T22:04:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af902241-4e42-5806-9d07-330aecf3cdfe', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af902241-4e42-5806-9d07-330aecf3cdfe'}, {'abstract': 'A well-known Times Square hotel is told to pay a black employee $1.1 million for allowing a campaign of racial harassment.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/millennium-hotel-loses-racial-harassment-suit/', 'snippet': 'A well-known Times Square hotel is told to pay a black employee $1.1 million for allowing a campaign of racial harassment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On its Web site, the Millennium Broadway Hotel says “everyone receives star treatment.” But one longtime employee said he was treated just the opposite, claiming that he was the victim of a long campaign of racial harassment that the hotel did nothing to stop.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Millennium Hotel Loses Racial Harassment Suit', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': No