-theater-companies-enjoying-well-profits.html', 'snippet': 'Substantial runs for shows like “Venus in Fur,” “War Horse” and “Anything Goes” can mean fewer offerings for subscribers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By Sunday, when the critically acclaimed Broadway drama “Venus in Fur” closes at Manhattan Theater Club’s Samuel J. Friedman Theater after a two-month run, many of the club’s 15,000 subscribers will have seen the play. Normally, that would be that: Unlike commercial producers, who aim to enrich themselves with long-running hits, Manhattan Theater Club is a nonprofit that seeks to open (and close) several shows each year for subscribers and others.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17NONPROFIT1/17NONPROFIT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17NONPROFIT1/17NONPROFIT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17NONPROFIT1/17NONPROFIT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17NONPROFIT1/17NONPROFIT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/17/arts/17NONPROFIT1/17NONPROFIT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonprofit Companies Enjoying, Well, Profits', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonprofit Companies Enjoying, Well, Profits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Theater Club', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-17T01:47:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dc1db08-6639-52e6-b992-40636fb1ae1d', 'word_count': 1375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dc1db08-6639-52e6-b992-40636fb1ae1d'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s podcast: Sarah Wheaton and Jonathan Ellis of The New York Times discuss the new Election 2012 app for the iPhone and Pedro Rafael Rosado takes a look at AirPlay gear for wirelessly streaming media.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/tech-talk-podcast-election-apps/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s podcast: Sarah Wheaton and Jonathan Ellis of The New York Times discuss the new Election 2012 app for the iPhone and Pedro Rafael Rosado takes a look at AirPlay gear for wirelessly streaming media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The presidential campaign season will soon be in full swing. Sarah Wheaton, who updates the New York Times app’s Top News section, and Jonathan Ellis, who oversaw the new Election 2012 app development, stop by to talk about the election coverage and apps.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Talk Podcast: Election Apps', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-17T01:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c47cb37-73b5-50c8-ab18-123402009093', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c47cb37-73b5-50c8-ab18-123402009093'}, {'abstract': 'The cuts are significant, but appropriators did strip the 2012 spending bill of its worst ideology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/17/opinion/an-agreement-on-spending.html', 'snippet': 'The cuts are significant, but appropriators did strip the 2012 spending bill of its worst ideology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They are reviled by the right and were even kicked out of their lavish old offices on the House side by Republican leaders. But Congressional appropriators — known for handling the most basic task of the legislative branch in a centrist manner — did their jobs this week. The spending bill they wrote for the remainder of the 2012 fiscal year came together with a minimum of ideology and passed the House over the opposition of hard-liners. The Senate is expected to give its approval this weekend, averting a shutdown. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Agreement on Spending', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Agreement on Spending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-17T01:49:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2edbab31-0da2-5857-bd45-4e53847d2c4c', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2edbab31-0da2-5857-bd45-4e53847d2c4c'}, {'abstract': 'From 1955 to 1986, Irwin Hasen spent nearly every day drawing “Dondi.” Now 93, he still draws the cartoon almost daily, on the walls of an Upper East Side cafe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/nyregion/for-irwin-hasen-a-life-with-dondi.html', 'snippet': 'From 1955 to 1986, Irwin Hasen spent nearly every day drawing “Dondi.” Now 93, he still draws the cartoon almost daily, on the walls of an Upper East Side cafe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IRWIN HASEN, 93, says the happiest moment in his life came early one morning in September 1955, when he had just begun drawing the comic strip “Dondi.” ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/nyregion/18CHARACER1/18CHARACER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/nyregion/18CHARACER1/18CHARACER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStan