ory this week, Jonathan Mahler went there to investigate the city’s plan to revive itself with a publicly subsidized golf course and luxury-housing development in the very place where factories once provided thousands of blue-collar workers with a way of life.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/16/this-sunday-how-nice-can-benton-harbor-ever-be/', 'snippet': 'Detroit gets all the scary headlines, but there is a more desperate city in the state of Michigan, and it is called Benton Harbor. For the magazine’s cover story this week, Jonathan Mahler went there to investigate the city’s plan to revive itself...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Detroit gets all the scary headlines, but there is a more desperate city in the state of Michigan, and it is called Benton Harbor. For the magazine’s cover story this week, Jonathan Mahler went there to investigate the city’s plan to revive itself with a publicly subsidized golf course and luxury-housing development in the very place where factories once provided thousands of blue-collar workers with a way of life. Slowly, since the 1960s, Benton Harbor has lost all its factories and half its population; those who remain subsist on a per capita income of $10,000. The city’s finances are so wrecked that the state government sent in an emergency manager who answers to no one locally. Putting a golf course in the midst of all this is, as Jon observes, “almost a caricature of economic disparity.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: How Nice Can Benton Harbor Ever Be?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mahler, Jonathan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 4, 'major'