pe': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Spencer D. Turkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Spencer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ea4a443-b52f-516f-be2b-5fb724ba7cda', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ea4a443-b52f-516f-be2b-5fb724ba7cda'}, {'abstract': 'Vaclav Havel, the dissident playwright who wove theater into politics to peacefully bring down communism in Czechoslovakia, has died.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2011/12/18/world/europe/20111218_600_havel.html', 'snippet': 'Vaclav Havel, the dissident playwright who wove theater into politics to peacefully bring down communism in Czechoslovakia, has died.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vaclav Havel, the dissident playwright who wove theater into politics to peacefully bring down communism in Czechoslovakia, has died.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/world/europe/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/18/world/europe/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/18/world/europe/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M/20111218_havel-slide-MW8M-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vaclav Havel, Dead at 75', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Czech Republic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T18:03:55+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/02d0e04e-de13-5894-9fde-6e9d8a337a72', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/02d0e04e-de13-5894-9fde-6e9d8a337a72'}, {'abstract': 'Both of those films failed to meet ticket-selling expectations for opening weekend. But Tom Cruise found success in limited release with “Mission: Impossible — Ghost Protocol.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/sherlock-holmes-and-chipmunks-are-sluggish-leaders-at-box-office/', 'snippet': 'Both of those films failed to meet ticket-selling expectations for opening weekend. But Tom Cruise found success in limited release with “Mission: Impossible — Ghost Protocol.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bells are ringing in Hollywood, but they’re not the Christmas kind. Two big sequels, “Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows” and “Alvin and the Chipmunks: Chipwrecked,” delivered shaky results over the weekend, alarming studios about a continued downturn at the North American box office. “A Game of Shadows” (Warner Brothers) was No. 1 with about $40 million in ticket sales. Analysts had expected that film, which cost $125 million to make, to take in over $50 million; the first installment in this franchise opened with $62 million in 2009. “Chipwrecked” (20th Century Fox) was second, taking in about $23.5 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box office data. That result was 52 percent below the opening weekend total for the last Chipmunks movie in 2009. “Chip-Wrecked” cost an estimated $80 million to make. A variety of factors could have dented interest: sequel fatigue, family film overload, higher ticket prices, a lack of overall momentum at the box office. Elsewhere, Tom Cruise’s “Mission: Impossible — Ghost Protocol” (Paramount) was a powerful third, taking in about $13 million in limited release on 425 Imax screens. That result set an Imax record and positioned “Ghost Protocol” as a potential blockbuster and career rebound for Mr. Cruise as it expands nationwide on Wednesday. “New Year’s Eve” (Warner) was fourth with about $7.4 million in ticket sales, lifting its two-week total to $24.8 million. “The Sitter” (Fox) was fifth, taking in an estimated $4.4 million for a two-week total of $17.7 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sherlock Holmes’ and ‘Chipmunks’ Are Sluggish Leaders at Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cruise, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T18:07:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/750b6bc3-4cef-5457-9ac7-0f36013392f2', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/750b6bc3-4cef-5457-9ac7-0f36013392f2'}, {'abstract': 'Not one but two premature declarations of success.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/twice-told-irish-tales/', 'snippet': 'Not one but two premature declarations of success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One thing I meant to mention when discussing Ireland is something Kevin O’Rourke pointed out: this is the second time in two years that Ireland has been declared a success story for austerity based on a slight uptick, soon reversed. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twice-Told Irish Tales',