olence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt’s State and Independent News Media Clash Over Cause of Violence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ONTV Incorporated', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T21:05:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00845b0c-cd59-528f-8d3f-7084a6679536', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00845b0c-cd59-528f-8d3f-7084a6679536'}, {'abstract': 'Lynn Deas of Schenectady, N.Y., and Beth Palmer of Chevy Chase, Md., won the women’s teams and women’s pairs at the SportAccord World Mind Games in Beijing last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/crosswords/bridge/bridge-sportaccord-world-mind-games-in-beijing.html', 'snippet': 'Lynn Deas of Schenectady, N.Y., and Beth Palmer of Chevy Chase, Md., won the women’s teams and women’s pairs at the SportAccord World Mind Games in Beijing last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lynn Deas of Schenectady, N.Y., and Beth Palmer of Chevy Chase, Md., won the women’s teams and women’s pairs at the SportAccord World Mind Games in Beijing last week. They were particularly proud of their defense in the diagramed deal.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Defense Won the Mind Games in Beijing', 'kicker': 'Bridge', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Defense Won the Mind Games in Beijing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bridge (Card Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T21:39:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Bridge', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b40ef9c7-c67c-5b5e-b3b9-dc8290a5127e', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b40ef9c7-c67c-5b5e-b3b9-dc8290a5127e'}, {'abstract': 'If the Giants beat the Jets on Saturday and the Cowboys in the regular-season finale, they will advance to the postseason.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/playoffs-yes-giants-still-control-their-playoff-chances/', 'snippet': 'If the Giants beat the Jets on Saturday and the Cowboys in the regular-season finale, they will advance to the postseason.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Giants still control their playoff chances. If they beat the Jets on Saturday and the Cowboys in the regular-season finale, they will advance to the postseason.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playoffs? Yes, Giants Still Control Their Playoff Chances', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T21:57:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cd74013-be2d-5094-9e06-9877b2a1db9b', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cd74013-be2d-5094-9e06-9877b2a1db9b'}, {'abstract': 'Wendy White, the mezzo-soprano injured when she fell during a performance at the Metropolitan Opera on Saturday night, has been released from a hospital and is recuperating at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/fallen-opera-singer-said-to-be-recovering.html', 'snippet': 'Wendy White, the mezzo-soprano injured when she fell during a performance at the Metropolitan Opera on Saturday night, has been released from a hospital and is recuperating at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wendy White, the mezzo-soprano injured when she fell during a performance at the Metropolitan Opera on Saturday night, has been released from a hospital and is recuperating at home, a spokesman for the opera, Peter Clark, said Sunday. Ms. White sustained bruises in a fall from a platform eight feet above the stage, he said. It remains unclear when she will be well enough to perform again. The accident happened during Act III of a performance of Gounod’s “Faust.” Ms. White, in the role of Marthe, was walking onto a platform from a staircase behind René Pape, who was playing Méphistophélès, when a hinge securing a piece of plywood under her broke. After a half-hour break the show went on with an understudy. Ms. White is a Met stalwart who has made more than 500 appearances with the company since her 1989 debut as Flora in “La Traviata.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fallen Opera Singer Said to Be Recovering', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fallen Opera Singer Said to Be Recovering', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gounod, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Falls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:26:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e73d4e94-a9a6-5855-9cdd-a00eaaa99d85', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e73d4e94-a9a6-5855-9cdd-a00eaaa99d85'}, {'abstract': 'H. K. Gruber’s “Frankenstein!!,” a work inspired by children’s rhymes, is closer to Grimm than to Disney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/new-york-philharmonic-at-metropolitan-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'H. K. Gruber’s “Frankenstein!!,” a work inspired by children’s rhymes, is closer to Grimm than to Disney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t let the presence of toy instruments in H. K. Gruber’s “Frankenstein!!” fool you. Mr. Gruber calls the work “a pan-demonium for chansonnier and orchestra after children’s rhymes by H. C. Artmann,” but lurid and bloody, these are fairy tales for adults, closer to Grimm than to Disney.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/contact/contact-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/contact/contact-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 297}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/contact/contact-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 506, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gruesome and Gory, a Chaotic Fairy Tale Gone Terribly Awry', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gruesome and Gory, a Chaotic Fairy Tale Gone Terribly Awry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lindberg, Magnus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Alan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gruber, H K', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bae9dee-073d-5fd4-ad9b-711fa4608636', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bae9dee-073d-5fd4-ad9b-711fa4608636'}, {'abstract': 'In Five Points, a performance series presented by Take Dance and featuring original music by Pulse, five choreographers unveiled works that were created in tandem with the music, but the collaboration didn’t add up to much.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/dance/take-dance-and-pulse-at-merce-cunningham-dance-studios-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Five Points, a performance series presented by Take Dance and featuring original music by Pulse, five choreographers unveiled works that were created in tandem with the music, but the collaboration didn’t add up to much.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a rarity to come across live music in a contemporary dance setting, but when it happens, the result is usually something more than pleasant. Whether it’s made to fit the dance or not, the presence of musicians invariably deepens the texture of choreography, lending a richer hue. Then there are those instances when the relationship adds up to little more than a couple sharing a room but sleeping in separate beds. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Silent Screams and Fierce Walks, as Music Sets the Scene for Dance', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Silent Screams and Fierce Walks, as Music Sets the Scene for Dance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ueyama, Takehiro', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:33:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9711ec11-29b4-5189-8884-6c8a05ced8d5', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9711ec11-29b4-5189-8884-6c8a05ced8d5'}, {'abstract': 'In a visit to Japan, President Lee Myung-bak pressed the matter, saying that time was running out to resolve the issue during the victims’ lifetimes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/world/asia/south-korea-urges-japan-to-compensate-former-sex-slaves.html', 'snippet': 'In a visit to Japan, President Lee Myung-bak pressed the matter, saying that time was running out to resolve the issue during the victims’ lifetimes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — President Lee Myung-bak of South Korea used what was supposed to be a routine visit to Japan on Sunday to press for compensation for Korean women who were forced to work as prostitutes by the Japanese military during World War II. He called the issue a “burden” on relations between the two nations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea Urges Japan to Compensate Former Sex Slaves', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea Urges Japan to Compensate Former Sex Slaves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:37:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87158872-00e3-556e-a48f-1b6de8ffb9b5', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87158872-00e3-556e-a48f-1b6de8ffb9b5'}, {'abstract': 'Star Wars: The Old Republic, a sprawling multiplayer online adventure, is the first legitimate competition that World of Warcraft has faced for players’ hearts, minds, hours and dollars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/video-games/star-wars-the-old-republic-vs-world-of-warcraft-online.html', 'snippet': 'Star Wars: The Old Republic, a sprawling multiplayer online adventure, is the first legitimate competition that World of Warcraft has faced for players’ hearts, minds, hours and dollars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last third of a century no new fiction has engaged the popular imagination and become as thoroughly essential an element of mass culture as “Star Wars.” There can’t be many people anywhere who wouldn’t at least recognize a lightsaber or Darth Vader. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19WARCRAFT/19WARCRAFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19WARCRAFT/19WARCRAFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19WARCRAFT/19WARCRAFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19WARCRAFT/19WARCRAFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19WARCRAFT/19WARCRAFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lightsaber Rattling in the Online Galaxy', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lightsaber Rattling in the Online Galaxy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Electronic Arts Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:41:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Video Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Schiesel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schiesel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04f7b056-0dae-59d0-8fd7-382032df1e1e', 'word_count': 1190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04f7b056-0dae-59d0-8fd7-382032df1e1e'}, {'abstract': 'Despite its many flaws, “The X Factor” has somehow arrived at this week’s three-contestant finale with two singers who have real star potential and a third who isn’t terrible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/television/x-factor-despite-itself-has-finalists-with-real-star-potential.html', 'snippet': 'Despite its many flaws, “The X Factor” has somehow arrived at this week’s three-contestant finale with two singers who have real star potential and a third who isn’t terrible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a bloated, overwrought embarrassment. It has been full of laughably excessive production numbers and clumsily manufactured drama. It may have set records for clichéd sound bites, insipid banter and shameless product placement. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19XFACTOR/19XFACTOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19XFACTOR/19XFACTOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19XFACTOR/19XFACTOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 881, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19XFACTOR/19XFACTOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19XFACTOR/19XFACTOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Forced Drama, Product Placement and Star Power', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forced Drama, Product Placement and Star Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowell, Simon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:42:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/765975b0-7543-561e-b977-64dd7bf6deef', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/765975b0-7543-561e-b977-64dd7bf6deef'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Jones’s imaginative 2007 production of Engelbert Humperdinck’s “Hansel and Gretel” returns to the Metropolitan Opera, retaining its wit and charm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/hansel-and-gretel-at-metropolitan-opera-review.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Jones’s imaginative 2007 production of Engelbert Humperdinck’s “Hansel and Gretel” returns to the Metropolitan Opera, retaining its wit and charm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From my seat on the aisle in the orchestra section at the Metropolitan Opera House I saw only one family get up and leave during the third act of Engelbert Humperdinck’s “Hansel and Gretel” at its season premiere on Friday night. That seemed noteworthy because onstage, in a kitchen filled with lost children baked into gingerbread, a frumpy, gluttonous witch was compelling a little girl to help fatten her brother for the oven, between unsubtle hints that the girl was destined for the same fate.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19HANSEL/19HANSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19HANSEL/19HANSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19HANSEL/19HANSEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19HANSEL/19HANSEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/19HANSEL/19HANSEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Just Wait Until You See What’s on the Menu', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Just Wait Until You See What’s on the Menu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ticciati, Robin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Humperdinck, Engelbert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pountney, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:44:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e1d9779-ff1e-5a83-a472-a0eacd8ec00b', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e1d9779-ff1e-5a83-a472-a0eacd8ec00b'}, {'abstract': 'The trumpeter Wadada Leo Smith gave off an air of restless intent during a two-day celebration at Roulette in downtown Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/wadada-leo-smith-at-roulette-in-brooklyn-review.html', 'snippet': 'The trumpeter Wadada Leo Smith gave off an air of restless intent during a two-day celebration at Roulette in downtown Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wadada Leo Smith was a force in motion during the early stretch of his concert at Roulette in Brooklyn on Friday night. Stalking the stage, busily directing the output of his Golden Sextet, he gave off an air of restless intent. The exceptions mostly came when he played his trumpet, projecting in a hard, lustrous tone. In those moments he rooted himself: shoulders squared, the bell of his horn pointing either straight ahead or toward the floor. At every juncture he seemed to mean business.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/roulette/roulette-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/roulette/roulette-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/roulette/roulette-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/roulette/roulette-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/roulette/roulette-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Big Birthday, Leading Three Groups a Night', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Big Birthday, Leading Three Groups a Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Wadada Leo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:46:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ff71829-27a2-54b3-8328-f4e111da986c', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ff71829-27a2-54b3-8328-f4e111da986c'}, {'abstract': 'At Zankel Hall on Friday night the young conductor Pablo Heras-Casado and the mezzo-soprano Isabel Leonard, performing with Ensemble ACJW, drew the interest of music professionals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/ensemble-acjw-with-heras-casado-at-zankel-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'At Zankel Hall on Friday night the young conductor Pablo Heras-Casado and the mezzo-soprano Isabel Leonard, performing with Ensemble ACJW, drew the interest of music professionals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Through no doing of the Ensemble ACJW, its concert on Friday night at Zankel Hall took on a newsier aspect than might have been anticipated. Just a couple of weeks earlier the evening’s conductor, the young Spanish maestro Pablo Heras-Casado, still relatively unknown in New York, had been named principal conductor of the Orchestra of St. Luke’s, a major fixture in the city’s musical ecosystem.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/ensemble/ensemble-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scrutiny for a Maestro and a Mezzo', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scrutiny for a Maestro and a Mezzo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heras-Casado, Pablo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leonard, Isabel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ensemble ACJW', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:47:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd1c6219-6030-54b5-8c0a-058a7ad0fc49', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd1c6219-6030-54b5-8c0a-058a7ad0fc49'}, {'abstract': 'At its show at Madison Square Garden on Friday, the Swedish House Mafia leaned toward straightforward, four-on-the-floor mega-house, with little in the way of rhythmic variation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/swedish-house-mafia-at-madison-square-garden-review.html', 'snippet': 'At its show at Madison Square Garden on Friday, the Swedish House Mafia leaned toward straightforward, four-on-the-floor mega-house, with little in the way of rhythmic variation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late in its two-plus-hour set at Madison Square Garden on Friday night Swedish House Mafia was playing its second Coldplay remix in a row, “Every Teardrop Is a Waterfall” following “Fix You,” and most of the people in the sold-out arena were bopping, as they’d been all night. The seat next to me had been occupied by at least four different people, all of whom had danced in and then danced onward.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/mafia/mafia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 304, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/mafia/mafia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 304}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/mafia/mafia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/mafia/mafia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/mafia/mafia-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Relentless Waves of Sound, Reined by Drops of Thundering Bass', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Relentless Waves of Sound, Reined by Drops of Thundering Bass', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:49:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0f55ac5-2a5e-5f01-bcc2-c3bfba283eaf', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0f55ac5-2a5e-5f01-bcc2-c3bfba283eaf'}, {'abstract': 'Ballet Hispanico gave the premiere of its artistic director’s first piece for the company, “Asuka,” a homage to the Cuban-born salsa legend Celia Cruz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/dance/ballet-hispanico-at-the-apollo-theater-review.html', 'snippet': 'Ballet Hispanico gave the premiere of its artistic director’s first piece for the company, “Asuka,” a homage to the Cuban-born salsa legend Celia Cruz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Apollo Theater on Saturday afternoon Goya Foods provided samples, as if the lobby were a supermarket. There was free coffee and wafers with dulce de leche filling. One reliable purveyor of Latino products, promoting its brand, was sponsoring another: Ballet Hispanico. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/hispanico/hispanico-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/hispanico/hispanico-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/arts/hispanico/hispanico-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Confection Suite: Homage to the Queen of Salsa', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Confection Suite: Homage to the Queen of Salsa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vilaro, Eduardo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ballet Hispanico', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f09e0e5c-fb2b-53f3-9dcc-0ff7e7668d50', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f09e0e5c-fb2b-53f3-9dcc-0ff7e7668d50'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Public Library is negotiating to sell five floors at 188 Madison Avenue, the former B. Altman department store at 34th Street.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/new-york-public-library-is-in-talks-to-sell-space.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Public Library is negotiating to sell five floors at 188 Madison Avenue, the former B. Altman department store at 34th Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Public Library is negotiating to sell five floors at 188 Madison Avenue, the former B. Altman department store at 34th Street. The building is also home to the Science, Industry and Business Library, which is to remain there. The library would not disclose the potential price or buyer, pending a conclusion of the deal, which is expected early in the new year. Proceeds are expected to go toward a plan to transform the library’s main Fifth Avenue site into a combined research and circulating library. The space in the B. Altman building includes two floors that the library has leased out since it bought about 15 percent of the building in 1993. The other floors are used for administration; staff members there will move elsewhere. The B. Altman building also houses the City University of New York Graduate Center and the Oxford University Press.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Public Library Is in Talks to Sell Space', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Public Library Is in Talks to Sell Space', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buildings (Structures)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:52:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/761e8c5a-1116-543d-b097-d98ce3ffa6cf', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/761e8c5a-1116-543d-b097-d98ce3ffa6cf'}, {'abstract': 'Etta James, the seminal R&B singer, is terminally ill, according to her doctor, Elaine James (who is not related to Etta James).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/music/etta-james-is-terminally-ill.html', 'snippet': 'Etta James, the seminal R&B singer, is terminally ill, according to her doctor, Elaine James (who is not related to Etta James).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Etta James, the seminal R&B singer, is terminally ill, according to her doctor, Elaine James  (who is not related to Etta James). Dr. James told The Press-Enterprise in Riverside, Calif., that family members were told two weeks ago that Ms. James’s chronic leukemia could not be cured. “I am Southern and Christian and would just ask for the prayers of her fans and friends,” Dr. James said. “They know she’s been sick, but not how sick.” Etta James, 73, has been among the most celebrated American blues singers for decades, recording enduring hits like “At Last,” “Tell Mama” and “I’d Rather Go Blind.” She was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 1993. Her career was also influenced by drug addiction, troubled love relationships and health and legal problems. In 2010 she was hospitalized for a staph infection, and it became public that she was suffering from Alzheimer’s disease, and that she had been treated for dependence on painkillers. Dr. James has cared for Etta James, above, at her home in Riverside since March 2010, and she made the statements last week outside a hearing over control of Ms. James’s $1 million estate. Donato James, her son, has been fighting in court with her husband, Artis Mills, over who will be named the estate’s conservator.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Etta James Is Terminally Ill', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Etta James Is Terminally Ill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'James, Etta', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Leukemia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:52:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/96b5b85e-d17b-5d09-a158-f74dd8f18ce8', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96b5b85e-d17b-5d09-a158-f74dd8f18ce8'}, {'abstract': 'Jacob’s Pillow will put on a weeklong homage to the choreography of its founder, Ted Shawn, as part of an unusual and ambitious lineup for its 80th summer dance festival in Becket, Mass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/arts/dance/jacobs-pillow-announces-its-summer-slate.html', 'snippet': 'Jacob’s Pillow will put on a weeklong homage to the choreography of its founder, Ted Shawn, as part of an unusual and ambitious lineup for its 80th summer dance festival in Becket, Mass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jacob’s Pillow will put on a weeklong homage to the choreography of its founder, Ted Shawn, as part of an unusual and ambitious lineup for its 80th summer dance festival in Becket, Mass. The rotating cast for the series of performances in honor of Shawn starting on July 11 will include about 20 prominent male dancers, among them Arthur Mitchell of the Dance Theater of Harlem, the tap-dance specialist Jason Samuels Smith and the former New York City Ballet principal Jock Soto. The schedule for the festival, to run from June 16 to Aug. 26, was released on Sunday. Among the other highlights of this year’s festival are a rare United States performance by the Hong Kong Ballet of Kinsun Chan’s “Black on Black”; a world premiere by the Swedish choreographer and dancer Pontus Lidberg, performed by Morphoses; and a collaboration between Jonah Bokaer and David Hallberg. This year Ella Baff, the executive and artistic director of Jacob’s Pillow, decided to break with that institution’s tradition of offering mostly new works. She programmed repeat performances of three popular dances from past festivals: Tero Saarinen’s Shaker-inspired “Borrowed Light” from 2006, Crystal Pite’s “Dark Matters” from last summer and Doug Elkins’s “Fräulein Maria” from 2009. “I hope to encourage the public to consider more contemporary dance works as classics, to be revisited in the same way that we enjoy and deepen our appreciation for classic works of music, theater and the visual arts,” Ms. Baff said in a statement. (Below, Shawn as the biblical Jacob on Pillow Rock.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jacob’s Pillow Announces Its Summer Slate', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jacob’s Pillow Announces Its Summer Slate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Jacob's Pillow Dance Festival", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shawn, Ted', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T22:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e010884-80f9-5eb7-beb0-083207347e94', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e010884-80f9-5eb7-beb0-083207347e94'}, {'abstract': 'Atheists do not lose morality by giving up God. Instead, they must find it where it lives: in the natural world.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/good-minus-god/', 'snippet': 'Atheists do not lose morality by giving up God. Instead, they must find it where it lives: in the natural world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was heartened to learn recently that atheists are no longer the most reviled group in the United States: according to the political scientists Robert Putnam and David Campbell, we’ve been overtaken by the Tea Party.\xa0 But even as I was high-fiving my fellow apostates (“We’re number two!\xa0 We’re number two!”), I was wondering anew: why do so many people dislike atheists?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Minus God', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Atheism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T23:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louise M. Antony', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e8a450f-6f98-51e6-a081-b546947db656', 'word_count': 2329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e8a450f-6f98-51e6-a081-b546947db656'}, {'abstract': 'A Monday puzzle by Richard Chisholm. Whee!', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/hot-water/', 'snippet': 'A Monday puzzle by Richard Chisholm. Whee!', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — Today’s constructor, Richard Chisholm, has not been seen around these parts for almost a year, but he’s actually a fairly prolific puzzle maker. Mr. Chisholm has had crosswords published in The New York Times on all days of the week except for those most difficult days, Friday and Saturday. Come on, Mr. Chisholm; hit for the cycle already. Not that that makes a good constructor; hitting for the cycle is not really any more important than producing a puzzle that happens to be a pangram. It would just be fun to add another constructor to the list of those who have already done so.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday: Where Folks Get Into Hot Water', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T23:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2422317-0f27-53cf-b881-2cbb52f40335', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2422317-0f27-53cf-b881-2cbb52f40335'}, {'abstract': 'Another late-season sleep in a critical first half left the Giants in an unenviable playoff position as they head into Saturday’s game against the Jets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/sports/football/giants-fall-flat-in-crucial-home-loss-to-redskins.html', 'snippet': 'Another late-season sleep in a critical first half left the Giants in an unenviable playoff position as they head into Saturday’s game against the Jets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — A year ago, Justin Tuck roused the Giants from a first-half slumber against the Jacksonville Jaguars with a stirring halftime speech. Tuck, the team’s defensive captain, came into the locker room, ranted about how the Jaguars were disrespecting the Giants in their own home and challenged everyone to rise up. It was fiery and ferocious, teammates said, and the Giants responded, rallying from an 11-point deficit to win by 4.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/sports/ny-giants/ny-giants-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/sports/ny-giants/ny-giants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/19/sports/ny-giants/ny-giants-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back to Earth: Giants Go Flat, Imperiling Playoff Bid', 'kicker': 'Redskins 23, Giants 10', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back To Earth: Giants Go Flat, Imperiling Playoff Bid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amukamara, Prince', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Eli', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tuck, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Redskins', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-18T23:03:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '