], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cfd04af-8147-5b15-baf5-6149821d7eb8', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cfd04af-8147-5b15-baf5-6149821d7eb8'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Veterans Affairs is moving in the right direction, but it needs to expand the housing voucher program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/opinion/help-for-homeless-veterans.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Veterans Affairs is moving in the right direction, but it needs to expand the housing voucher program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Department of Veterans Affairs and Housing and Urban Development have announced modest but real progress in the effort to house homeless veterans. A count of people living in shelters and on the street in 400 communities on one night in January found 12 percent fewer veterans than at the same time last year — 67,495, compared with 76,329 in 2010. The percentage of homeless veterans living in shelters and transitional housing was also slightly better, up by 2 percent.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Help for Homeless Veterans', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Help for Homeless Veterans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vouchers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing and Urban Development Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T01:57:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9da69f2-a9d8-54f2-93b2-bb5973d66b4c', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9da69f2-a9d8-54f2-93b2-bb5973d66b4c'}, {'abstract': 'Is the prominence of Democratic lobbyists a problem for President Obama and his party as they try to set an election agenda focused on income inequality?', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/18/the-trouble-with-that-revolving-door/', 'snippet': 'Is the prominence of Democratic lobbyists a problem for President Obama and his party as they try to set an election agenda focused on income inequality?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, an inside-the-Beltway newsletter, First Street, published a unique top-ten list. It reveals which former members of Congress are among the most important Washington lobbyists.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With That Revolving Door', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Breaux, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Downey, Tom', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gephardt, Richard A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tauzin, Billy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trumka, Richard L', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FEDERATION OF LABOR-CONGRESS OF INDUSTRIAL ORGANIZATIONS', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/481945fd-b9f6-587d-8c3f-6665f98afb5e', 'word_count': 1466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/481945fd-b9f6-587d-8c3f-6665f98afb5e'}, {'abstract': 'Data tracking the conflicts in Iraq, Afghanistan and Pakistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/opinion/states-of-conflict-a-final-update.html', 'snippet': 'Data tracking the conflicts in Iraq, Afghanistan and Pakistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A VERSION of this chart has been appearing in the Op-Ed pages every few months for eight years. The first was published eight months after the invasion of Iraq, and data from the conflicts in Afghanistan and Pakistan were added in more recent years. Gains have been made in these three countries, but violence persists and peace is by no means assured. Nonetheless, with the end of the war in Iraq officially announced last week, this will be the last of the series.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/19/opinion/1219OPCHARTunikewicz/1219OPCHARTunikewicz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/19/opinion/1219OPCHARTunikewicz/1219OPCHARTunikewicz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}