ue': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T11:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39ae112e-85fd-5d87-8826-5f4646e888f7', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39ae112e-85fd-5d87-8826-5f4646e888f7'}, {'abstract': 'Google Music — which lets you upload tracks to an online server so you can play them from different mobile devices or computers — accepts songs in several file formats. Apple Lossless, unfortunately, is not one of them.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/qa-converting-music-file-formats/', 'snippet': 'Google Music — which lets you upload tracks to an online server so you can play them from different mobile devices or computers — accepts songs in several file formats. Apple Lossless, unfortunately, is not one of them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Converting Music File Formats', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audio Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iTunes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T11:12:53+0000', 'document_type'