e': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T13:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f694f01b-ba6c-5614-b620-426514345688', 'word_count': 2217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f694f01b-ba6c-5614-b620-426514345688'}, {'abstract': 'The biggest mystery surrounding the elevation of Kim Jong-il’s youngest son is whether he will be able to retain power in the world’s last bastion of hard-line Communism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/world/asia/an-enigmatic-young-son-steps-forward-or-seems-to.html', 'snippet': 'The biggest mystery surrounding the elevation of Kim Jong-il’s youngest son is whether he will be able to retain power in the world’s last bastion of hard-line Communism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — With the abrupt death of the North Korean leader, Kim Jong-il, the fate of his isolated, nuclear-armed regime has dropped into the hands of his youngest son, Kim Jong-un, who is such an unknown that the world did not even know for sure what he looked like until last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/20successor1_span/20korea1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/20/world/20successor1_span/20korea1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/20successor1_span/20korea1_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/20successor1_span/20korea1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/20/world/20successor1_span/20korea1_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Heir Faces Uncertain Transition in North Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Un', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T13:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e72f9a19-e22c-58b0-9768-4b51e10f1810', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e72f9a19-e22c-58b0-9768-4b51e10f1810'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Tebow has been criticized for his poor performances  in his victories.  In defeat on Sunday, he played one of his better games.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/despite-defeat-tebow-is-playing-better/', 'snippet': 'Tim Tebow has been criticized for his poor performances  in his victories.  In defeat on Sunday, he played one of his better games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he was winning in dramatic fashion, Tim Tebow was often criticized for his poor statistical performances.\xa0In defeat on Sunday, he played one of his better games, statistically and otherwise, with 2 touchdowns and 93 yards on the ground, and 194 passing yards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Defeat, Tebow Is Playing Better', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T13:37:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17147455-bce8-5c68-9425-e01a791c4f34', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17147455-bce8-5c68-9425-e01a791c4f34'}, {'abstract': "The Times reports that many educators, school boards and districts -- including the city's Department of Education -- are struggling to find a  balance to protect students from inappropriate behavior by the grown-ups in their lives using Facebook, Twitter and texting, while also allowing teachers enough freedom to take advantage of the social media tools that, after all, are how children communicate nowadays.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/19/communication-tools-that-can-lead-to-trouble/', 'snippet': "The Times reports that many educators, school boards and districts -- including the city's Department of Education -- are struggling to find a  balance to protect students from inappropriate behavior by the grown-ups in their lives using Facebook,...", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Communication Tools That Can Lead to Trouble', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': No