esk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. Claiborne Ray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/dabe830a-2756-5157-8ce0-ba1884a3c5c9', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dabe830a-2756-5157-8ce0-ba1884a3c5c9'}, {'abstract': 'The tremendous gravity of the hole at the center of the Milky Way, known as Sagittarius A*, should accelerate and compress the gas, causing it to emit X-rays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/science/forecast-for-sagittarius-a-black-hole-a-cold-gas-cloud.html', 'snippet': 'The tremendous gravity of the hole at the center of the Milky Way, known as Sagittarius A*, should accelerate and compress the gas, causing it to emit X-rays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the past 20 years, astrophysicists have been watching stars and other space matter swirling around\xa0Sagittarius\xa0A* \xa0(pronounced A-star),\xa0the black hole\xa0at the center of the Milky Way. But\xa0they have never seen anything sucked into it (or any other black hole, for that matter).', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBCLOUD2_SPAN/20OBCLOUD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBCLOUD2_SPAN/20OBCLOUD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBCLOUD2_SPAN/20OBOX2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 544, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credi