 at Bain Capital has gone viral.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/romneys-gordon-gekko-moment/', 'snippet': 'As Mitt Romney has become a front-runner for the Republican nomination, a photo from his early days at Bain Capital has gone viral.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our colleagues have an article in today’s New York Times on Mitt Romney and his lucrative retirement agreement from Bain Capital, the private equity fund that he helped start. Mr. Romney received a share of the firm’s profits on all of its funds raised through February 2009, earning him millions of dollars that have helped fuel his political career.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney’s Gordon Gekko Moment', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bain Capital', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T21:06:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b36f730-bea2-5506-929e-bce2db173888', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b36f730-bea2-5506-929e-bce2db173888'}, {'abstract': 'The improbable walking behavior of the African lungfish redraws the evolutionary route of life on earth from water to land.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/science/african-lungfish-study-indicates-that-walking-evolved-in-the-water.html', 'snippet': 'The improbable walking behavior of the African lungfish redraws the evolutionary route of life on earth from water to land.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roughly 400 million years ago, an ancient lobe-finned fish left its watery habitat to become the first four-limbed terrestrial creature. Its descendants — which are called tetrapods and include tree frogs, blue jays and human beings — typically get around by stepping, flying or jumping.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBLUNGFISH3/20OBOX3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBLUNGFISH3/20OBLUNGFISH3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/20/science/20OBLUNGFISH3/20OBLUNGFISH3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Walk Through Water Before Reaching Land', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Walk Through Water Before Reaching Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Chicago', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-19T21:07:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ritchie S. King', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ritchie', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'King', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b69911b-e023-5278-9e89-217b544aa860', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b69911b-e023-5278-9e89-217b544aa860'}, {'abstract': 'Newly mapped evolutionary relationships between plants may provide clues for improving agricultural practices.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/the-tree-of-life-gets-a-makeover/', 'snippet': 'Newly mapped evolutionary relationships between plants may provide clues for improving agricultural practices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Darwin called it the “abominable mystery”: the reason for a sudden explosion of diverse flowering plants in the fossil record. Biologists have pondered the issue for years but were hampered by the absence of full studies of some plants and a lack of sophisticated tools for analyzing large genetic data sets. But now, thanks to a method called “functional phylogenomics,” scientists have resolved some of the enigmatic relationships of plant families, potentially opening the way for advances in agriculture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tree of Life Gets a Makeover', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Proteins', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank':