eth Kormanik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kormanik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1af4ee-4424-5fc7-b9a8-ab83c00b0158', 'word_count': 650, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1af4ee-4424-5fc7-b9a8-ab83c00b0158'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Lim gets himself into hot water, sorry, soup with this puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/acrobat/', 'snippet': 'Julian Lim gets himself into hot water, sorry, soup with this puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — Today is Tuesday, which means we have an entertaining crossword puzzle to talk about, but before we discuss that I wanted to ask those of you who are reading this whether or not you like to do the Second Sunday or Variety puzzles. I’ve asked this before; however, commentary drops off markedly on those days (I don’t know how many people actually are doing the puzzles and just not commenting on the blog) and for the life of me, I don’t know why. I was not a Variety puzzle solver when I started this job, and now I’m absolutely hooked on them. They’re worth a try, and if you want to read a particularly good explanation on what it takes to construct these puzzles by my partners-in-noir-fiction, Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon, read Jim Horne’s new post here. Then come back and try one of these devilish masterpieces of construction. You might find yourself similarly hooked.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday: Like an Acrobat’s Limbs', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T03:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2648a3bc-61b1-5938-85ac-10be6139eef3', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2648a3bc-61b1-5938-85ac-10be6139eef3'}, {'abstract': 'A new advertisement by Gov. Rick Perry’s campaign casts the governor’s rivals Mitt Romney and Newt Gingrich as candidates loyal to Wall Street and K Street, not Main Street.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/19/perry-ad-casts-gingrich-as-a-k-street-man/', 'snippet': 'A new advertisement by Gov. Rick Perry’s campaign casts the governor’s rivals Mitt Romney and Newt Gingrich as candidates loyal to Wall Street and K Street, not Main Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The advertising assault on Newt Gingrich by his Republican opponents is getting louder, with the latest commercial from Gov. Rick Perry of Texas taking aim at the former House speaker’s career as a (don’t say lobbyist!) Washington power broker after he left public office.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perry Ad Casts Gingrich as a K Street Man', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T03:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 