knowledged serious challenges ahead in his first formal address.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — Mariano Rajoy, the new conservative prime minister of Spain, pledged on Monday to reduce the size of the country’s public sector, clean up its troubled banks and introduce corporate tax breaks to get Spain’s budget deficit down and revive investment. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/SPAIN/SPAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/20/world/SPAIN/SPAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/SPAIN/SPAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Prime Minister Vows to Fix Spain’s Economy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Prime Minister Vows to Fix Spain’s Economy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rajoy, Mariano', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T06:08:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23732715-5b08-5792-81eb-2888d1b1225f', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23732715-5b08-5792-81eb-2888d1b1225f'}, {'abstract': 'Pakistani military officers have begun returning to coordination centers in Afghanistan, NATO said on Monday, calling it a sign that tensions were easing after American airstrikes recently killed 24 Pakistani soldiers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/world/asia/afghanistan-pakistani-liaison-officers-returning-to-army-coordination-centers.html', 'snippet': 'Pakistani military officers have begun returning to coordination centers in Afghanistan, NATO said on Monday, calling it a sign that tensions were easing after American airstrikes recently killed 24 Pakistani soldiers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pakistani military officers have begun returning to coordination centers in Afghanistan, NATO said on Monday, calling it a sign that tensions were easing, nearly a month after American airstrikes killed 24 Pakistani soldiers. But Pakistan has not reopened the supply lines for NATO forces in Afghanistan that run through its territory. The supply lines were closed by Islamabad in the wake of the airstrikes, on Nov. 26, which infuriated Pakistani officials and put more strain on America’s troubled alliance with Pakistan. NATO forces generally get about two-thirds of their supplies in other ways, but the land route through Pakistan remains vital, and NATO asked again on Monday for the roads to be reopened to supply convoys. Pakistani officials did not immediately respond to the request. A Pakistani military spokesman, Maj. Gen. Athar Abbas, said that liaison officers were returning to the coordination centers, which are meant to ease communications among the Afghan and Pakistani armies and the NATO coalition, all of which operate near the border.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 485, 'width': 317, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan: Pakistani Liaison Officers Returning to Army Coordination Centers', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan: Pakistani Liaison Officers Returning to Army Coordination Centers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T06:22:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/16a1f764-d7c3-5cfc-8441-696aa1b5fa0b', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16a1f764-d7c3-5cfc-8441-696aa1b5fa0b'}, {'abstract': 'The decision by Japan to choose Lockheed Martin’s F-35 jet as its next main fighter reflected a desire to tighten its ties with the United States in the face of concerns over China and North Korea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/business/global/japan-picks-lockheed-fighter.html', 'snippet': 'The decision by Japan to choose Lockheed Martin’s F-35 jet as its next main fighter reflected a desire to tighten its ties with the United States in the face of concerns over China and North Korea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Japan said on Tuesday that it had chosen Lockheed Martin’s F-35 jet as its next main fighter, a lift for the American defense contractor and for Tokyo’s relations with the United States amid heightened fears of instability on the Korean peninsula after the death of Kim Jong-il.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/business/LOCKHEED/LOCKHEED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Picks Lockheed to Build Its Next Fighter Jet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Selects Lockheed To Supply New Fighters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lockheed Martin Corporation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'F-35 Airplane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Aircraft', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T06:32:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f686c2a6-997f-55ae-8cb0-f2c3479fba35', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f686c2a6-997f-55ae-8cb0-f2c3479fba35'}, {'abstract': 'The initial inadequate response by the police to last summer’s riots allowed the vandalism and looting to spread out of control, according to a parliamentary report released Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/world/europe/britain-report-faults-police-response-to-riots.html', 'snippet': 'The initial inadequate response by the police to last summer’s riots allowed the vandalism and looting to spread out of control, according to a parliamentary report released Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The initial inadequate response by the police to last summer’s riots in London, Birmingham, Manchester and other places allowed the vandalism and looting to spread out of control, according to a parliamentary report released Monday. The report, by the home affairs committee in the House of Commons, said that the police in many areas, particularly London, had failed to take charge of the situation quickly enough, creating an “absence of a police presence” and the “sight of police officers apparently standing by” and allowing criminal behavior to occur. A spokesman for the Metropolitan Police in London said that it had already acknowledged its failures and developed new plans for dealing with future riots. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Report Faults Police Response to Riots', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Report Faults Police Response to Riots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Birmingham (England)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vandalism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Looting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manchester (England)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T06:52:14+0000', 'docume