ph': 'Stocks surged on Tuesday, partly a result of positive economic data on the housing sector in the United States and brighter signals from the euro zone. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/business/MARKETS/MARKETS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/business/MARKETS/MARKETS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/business/MARKETS/MARKETS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Markets Jump in U.S. and Europe on Positive Economic Data', 'kicker': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Markets Jump in U.S. and Europe on Positive Economic Data', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Housing Starts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:13:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab2cab46-237b-54f4-97b5-fb2c62922b6d', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab2cab46-237b-54f4-97b5-fb2c62922b6d'}, {'abstract': 'Balance kills a referee’s credibility.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/politifact-r-i-p/', 'snippet': 'Balance kills a referee’s credibility.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is really awful. Politifact, which is supposed to police false claims in politics, has announced its Lie of the Year — and it’s a statement that happens to be true, the claim that Republicans have voted to end Medicare.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Politifact, R.I.P.', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab8f0002-ff91-53c3-9ae4-1af433c6cccf', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab8f0002-ff91-53c3-9ae4-1af433c6cccf'}, {'abstract': 'AT&T says the breakup fee for ending the T-Mobile USA takeover attempt is $4 billion. Deutsche Telekom values the fee at $6 billion. The difference, it seems, is a matter of accounting.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/att-and-t-mobile-whats-2-billion-among-friends/', 'snippet': 'AT&T says the breakup fee for ending the T-Mobile USA takeover attempt is $4 billion. Deutsche Telekom values the fee at $6 billion. The difference, it seems, is a matter of accounting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You say “po-TAY-to,” I say “po-TAH-to.” But in the case of the T-Mobile USA deal’s breakup fee, it’s a very big root vegetable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'T-Mobile and AT&T: What’s $2 Billion Among Friends?', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Telekom AG', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'T-Mobile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:22:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efe08bca-a2a1-5134-957e-ea694f2382ab', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efe08bca-a2a1-5134-957e-ea694f2382ab'}, {'abstract': 'The exchange of information used to be as easy as handing out a business card or phone number. Now it’s a bewildering exchange. We have to decide where to send people we meet online. Do they Friend us on Facebook, follow us on Twitter or just Google our name?', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/friend-me-follow-me-google-me/', 'snippet': 'The exchange of information used to be as easy as handing out a business card or phone number. Now it’s a bewildering exchange. We have to decide where to send people we meet online. Do they Friend us on Facebook, follow us on Twitter or just Goog...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It sometimes feels like it was easier to exchange contact information with someone before the Web came along. You really had two options: handing out a business card or scribbling down a phone number.\xa0And although the Web promised to make this transaction simpler, it feels more confusing than ever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friend Me, Follow Me or Google Me?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:26:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05c8a35e-15ab-5870-a36e-8978c9bc4045', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05c8a35e-15ab-5870-a36e-8978c9bc4045'}, {'abstract': 'John F. Haggerty Jr., found guilty of stealing campaign money from Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg, $750,000 worth, and sentenced to serve one and a third to four years in prison,  is being treated far more harshly than the despised Bernard L. Madoff.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/a-sentence-for-stealing-from-bloomberg-that-doesnt-compute/', 'snippet': 'John F. Haggerty Jr., found guilty of stealing campaign money from Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg, $750,000 worth, and sentenced to serve one and a third to four years in prison,  is being treated far more harshly than the despised Bernard L. Madoff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The wages of sin is death. It says so right there in Romans 6:23, questionable grammar and all. Death may be a tad harsh for most sins. But the concept prompts a question as to how to weigh a sin like stealing against the penalty meted out. \xa0A political consultant named John F. Haggerty Jr. brings it to mind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sentence for Stealing From Bloomberg That Doesn’t Compute', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:35:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0c6fc52-400a-5f26-a273-d5991bc6a21f', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0c6fc52-400a-5f26-a273-d5991bc6a21f'}, {'abstract': 'The maker of the iPhone won a battle against HTC on Monday in a struggle for global cellphone supremacy, with ruling that affects the underlying Android operating system created by Google. At the heart of the disputes are the kind of small but convenient features that would cause many people to complain if they were not in their smartphones.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/daily-report-apples-win-in-patent-ruling-hits-google/', 'snippet': 'The maker of the iPhone won a battle against HTC on Monday in a struggle for global cellphone supremacy, with ruling that affects the underlying Android operating system created by Google. At the heart of the disputes are the kind of small but con...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patent battles have engulfed the cellphone industry for years, reflecting the heated competition. An important ruling by a federal agency, United States International Trade Commission, on Monday is described as one of the most significant so far by Nick Wingfield of The New York Times. Apple won a partial victory over the manufacturer HTC that could could force changes in how Google’s Android phones function. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Apple’s Victory in Patent Ruling Hits Google', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:36:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9667fb4d-d1b0-5089-8c4c-d7280cc7a493', 'word_count': 316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9667fb4d-d1b0-5089-8c4c-d7280cc7a493'}, {'abstract': 'New Oscar rules, negative ads hurt Gingrich, Kim Jong-il’s death hits social media and Dalton Trumbo gets his credit back for “Roman Holiday.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/the-breakfast-meeting-dec-20-2/', 'snippet': 'New Oscar rules, negative ads hurt Gingrich, Kim Jong-il’s death hits social media and Dalton Trumbo gets his credit back for “Roman Holiday.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'New voting rules for the Academy Awards could help the movie “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” which will be released in Los Angeles and New York on Christmas and won’t play around the country until next year, Michael Cieply writes. The movie was shut out by Golden Globes voters, who were shown a screening at the beginning of the month; the voters said in interviews with Mr. Cieply that they were sharply divided on the movie, with some hating it, some loving it. That kind of polarity didn’t work at the Golden Globes, but it might now prove an advantage in the Oscar contest after changes in balloting for best picture have put a premium on first-place votes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting, Dec. 20', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "Lil' Kim", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trumbo, Dalton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weekly Standard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Writers Guild of America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:41:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fff66be-1f98-50dd-a036-8f9f4d6b102a', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fff66be-1f98-50dd-a036-8f9f4d6b102a'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn artist Kenneth E. Parris III is on tour with members of the Merce Cunningham Dance Company, documenting their two-year Legacy Tour.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/drawing-dance-the-merce-cunningham-dance-company-in-paris/', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn artist Kenneth E. Parris III is on tour with members of the Merce Cunningham Dance Company, documenting their two-year Legacy Tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn artist Kenneth E. Parris III is on tour with members of the Merce Cunningham Dance Company, documenting, through a series of drawings and paintings, their two-year legacy tour. After 58 years the company is scheduled to disband on New Year’s Day 2012. This is the 23rd in a series of graphite drawings that Mr. Parris plans to create from the road, providing a front-row seat to the final weeks of the company’s journey. This week’s entry is from Paris.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawing Dance: The Merce Cunningham Dance Company in Paris', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cunningham, Merce, Dance Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T14:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48bc349c-c225-576d-8016-67fe432aae79', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48bc349c-c225-576d-8016-67fe432aae79'}, {'abstract': 'The struggling women’s fashion retailer says it plans to explore strategic alternatives, essentially putting itself on the block.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/talbots-rebuffs-takeover-offer-from-sycamore/', 'snippet': 'The struggling women’s fashion retailer says it plans to explore strategic alternatives, essentially putting itself on the block.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talbots, the women’s fashion retailer, has rejected an unsolicited buyout offer from a private equity firm as too low. Instead, the company said on Tuesday that it would explore strategic alternatives, essentially putting itself on the block.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talbots Rebuffs Buyout Offer From Sycamore', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sycamore Partners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Talbots Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee5eb149-36ad-557e-b48f-5265445e51c1', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee5eb149-36ad-557e-b48f-5265445e51c1'}, {'abstract': 'Federal authorities are investigating the claims of Rabbi Yoshiyahu Yosef Pinto’s followers that a former aide to the rabbi had stolen the millions of dollars missing from their congregation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/nyregion/rabbi-pintos-followers-blame-aides-for-missing-millions.html', 'snippet': 'Federal authorities are investigating the claims of Rabbi Yoshiyahu Yosef Pinto’s followers that a former aide to the rabbi had stolen the millions of dollars missing from their congregation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six years ago, an Orthodox rabbi and mystic who traces his lineage to King David moved to New York from Israel and amassed a notable following. Real estate titans fetched him at the airport. Members of Congress attended his Hebrew classes. Even LeBron James, who is not Jewish, borrowed a friend’s yacht to consult the rabbi in private.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/21rabbi-pinto/21rabbi-pinto-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/21rabbi-pinto/21rabbi-pinto-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/21rabbi-pinto/21rabbi-pinto-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/21rabbi-pinto/21rabbi-pinto-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/21rabbi-pinto/21rabbi-pinto-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rabbi’s Followers Blame Aide for Missing Millions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Followers of Rabbi Blame an Ex-Aide for a Congregation’s Missing Millions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rabbis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Leigh Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ffcea27-68fd-5320-bb9b-10531b4a52b2', 'word_count': 1718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ffcea27-68fd-5320-bb9b-10531b4a52b2'}, {'abstract': 'A study published on Monday in “Pediatrics” indicates that nearly one in three Americans will be arrested by age 23.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/wanted-a-ron-paul-for-policing/', 'snippet': 'A study published on Monday in “Pediatrics” indicates that nearly one in three Americans will be arrested by age 23.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I don’t support Ron Paul and I can’t imagine how his libertarian philosophy could possibly work, especially at a time when the crippled economy is crying out for effective government. But I appreciate the novelty he has brought to the Republican primaries, and the way he sometimes breaks with right-wing orthodoxy. When the other presidential aspirants compete to out-tough each other on foreign policy, for example, I think it’s useful to have Mr. Paul jump in and say—as he did at the last debate—that the “greatest danger” is a president who “will overreact.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wanted: A Ron Paul for Policing', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime Rates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:03:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27861946-0b0b-56c5-a80b-72f4406f28f6', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27861946-0b0b-56c5-a80b-72f4406f28f6'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Berenson returned to the United States for the first time since serving more than 15 years in a Peruvian prison on a terrorism charge.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/american-jailed-in-peru-returns-to-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Berenson returned to the United States for the first time since serving more than 15 years in a Peruvian prison on a terrorism charge.', 'lead_paragraph': ' She has captivated New Yorkers for 16 years, the daughter of Manhattan academics convicted in Peru of aiding a leftist terrorist group, famous for a fist-waving rant to television cameras that cemented her reputation among Peruvians as a dangerous radical.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Jailed in Peru Returns to New York', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berenson, Lori Helene', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newark Liberty International Airport (NJ)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tupac Amaru', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/406d1c3b-ac87-5a7c-ada4-b7b56826f6ab', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/406d1c3b-ac87-5a7c-ada4-b7b56826f6ab'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/what-were-reading-335/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Economist: An ambitious analysis of how “a small, unremarkable country came to dominate the world of beermaking.” That would be Belgium. Crammed with statistics, including how many different beers (1,131), the most expensive ($800 per case for Westvleteren) and how much beer Belgians consume (84 liters per year). — Glenn Collins', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:15:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6d6c011-30f6-56bd-90a2-8ec3af732ff2', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6d6c011-30f6-56bd-90a2-8ec3af732ff2'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Sheen, who stars in “The Way,” talks about making a film with his family, changes in the Academy and making “Apocalypse Now.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/martin-sheen-family-filmmaking-man/', 'snippet': 'Martin Sheen, who stars in “The Way,” talks about making a film with his family, changes in the Academy and making “Apocalypse Now.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forget about the 10: Officially speaking, 265 features are in contention for the best picture Oscar, as the Academy announced Monday. One you may not have heard much about is “The Way,” a family drama written and directed by Emilio Estevez and starring his father, Martin Sheen. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Martin Sheen, Family (Filmmaking) Man', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Estevez, Emilio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheen, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:23:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7c060db-9b14-5777-b5d6-6ed26d9aa8e3', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7c060db-9b14-5777-b5d6-6ed26d9aa8e3'}, {'abstract': 'The media buzz about the president’s extended absence has embroiled Pakistanis in a wild guessing game.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/karachi-chatter-about-zardari-absence/', 'snippet': 'The media buzz about the president’s extended absence has embroiled Pakistanis in a wild guessing game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KARACHI, Pakistan – The phone calls and text messages began just after sunset on Sunday. “His royal highness will be returning to his palace by the sea, so you better get off the roads,” messaged one friend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Karachi Chatter', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zardari, Asif Ali', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:24:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Huma Yusuf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Huma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yusuf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed9fd754-dcb0-5bd1-9480-73459a733929', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed9fd754-dcb0-5bd1-9480-73459a733929'}, {'abstract': 'Downtown Cairo has been turned into a warzone. And while the military government has gone on the offensive, the protest movement has lost its way.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/the-unity-is-gone-in-cairo/', 'snippet': 'Downtown Cairo has been turned into a warzone. And while the military government has gone on the offensive, the protest movement has lost its way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — For the last several days in Egypt, there’s been only one question on my mind: what on earth is going on here?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unity Is Gone', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T15:26:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah A. Topol', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Topol', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d37fad5f-7384-55bc-80b4-9e43c2a0dd19', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d37fad5f-7384-55bc-80b4-9e43c2a0dd19'}, {'abstract': 'A voracious species with the potential to upend ecosystems is already in the Mississippi River. But how big is the risk to the Great Lakes?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/us/in-great-lakes-states-a-divide-over-the-asian-carp.html', 'snippet': 'A voracious species with the potential to upend ecosystems is already in the Mississippi River. But how big is the risk to the Great Lakes?', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — The leaders of the Great Lakes states had come to a moment of calm, glassy waters. It was 2008, and after years of negotiation, politicians in the eight states around the lakes had reached agreement on a compact that would protect their (and their Canadian counterparts’) precious fresh water from what they saw as one of the Midwest’s biggest threats: t