a-5185-9997-fa7b60defb67', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c549ba6a-708a-5185-9997-fa7b60defb67'}, {'abstract': 'Good healthful food and frozen yogurt parfaits are promised.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/stonyfield-farm-to-open-chelsea-piers-restaurant/', 'snippet': 'Good healthful food and frozen yogurt parfaits are promised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stonyfield Farm is about to make a leap from its well-established spot in the dairy aisle to a restaurant at Chelsea Piers. The prominent organic yogurt and milk company already has one cafe in Maine, and in April its new riverside location, at West 22nd Street, will open with mac and cheese, salads and flatbread sandwiches with ingredients from farms in New York State.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stonyfield Farm to Open Chelsea Piers Restaurant', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chelsea Piers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stonyfield Farm', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:06:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meredith Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meredith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cc680dc-c612-5951-b8bb-0356a01d9931', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cc680dc-c612-5951-b8bb-0356a01d9931'}, {'abstract': 'With global air traffic growing about 5 percent a year, industry executives and security experts say a fundamental rethinking of security checkpoints is inevitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/business/streamlining-airport-security.html', 'snippet': 'With global air traffic growing about 5 percent a year, industry executives and security experts say a fundamental rethinking of security checkpoints is inevitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travelers in the midst of another holiday season of shuffling shoeless through seemingly interminable airport security lines may find it difficult to imagine a future where screenings are not only speedy but thorough. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/technology/21security-span/21security-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/technology/21security-span/21security-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 358}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/technology/21security-span/21security-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/technology/21security-span/21security-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/technology/21security-span/21security-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking to Streamline Airport Security Screenings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clearing a Path at the Airport', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:09:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicola Clark and Jad Mouawad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicola', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mouawad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fbad5d3-e647-5894-a90c-1c5798bba969', 'word_count': 1418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fbad5d3-e647-5894-a90c-1c5798bba969'}, {'abstract': 'The discovery of Kepler 20e and Kepler 20f may be an encouraging sign that planet hunters would someday succeed in finding other Earth-like planets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/science/space/nasas-kepler-spacecraft-discovers-2-earth-size-planets.html', 'snippet': 'The discovery of Kepler 20e and Kepler 20f may be an encouraging sign that planet hunters would someday succeed in finding other Earth-like planets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what amounts to a kind of holiday gift to the cosmos, astronomers from NASA’s Kepler spacecraft announced Tuesday that they had discovered a pair of planets the size of Earth orbiting a distant star.\xa0The new planets, one about as big as Earth and the other slightly smaller than Venus, are the smallest yet found beyond the solar system.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/science/21planets_graphic/21planets_graphic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Earth-Size Planets Are Discovered', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Earth-Size Planets Are Discovered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kepler Space Telescope', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Planets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:16:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/038db8d8-52ea-5b3f-91eb-105e8e42bc2f', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/038db8d8-52ea-5b3f-91eb-105e8e42bc2f'}, {'abstract': 'Greenhill & Company said on Tuesday that two senior executives, Jeffrey F. Buckalew  and Rakesh Chawla, died in a plane crash in northern New Jersey.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/greenhill-says-2-executives-died-in-plane-crash/', 'snippet': 'Greenhill & Company said on Tuesday that two senior executives, Jeffrey F. Buckalew  and Rakesh Chawla, died in a plane crash in northern New Jersey.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:51 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greenhill Says 2 Executives Died in Plane Crash', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenhill & Company Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b2ad802-af51-5e0c-9e16-c065229d968a', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b2ad802-af51-5e0c-9e16-c065229d968a'}, {'abstract': 'Lowe’s pulled its advertising from TLC’s “All American Muslin” to quiet a controversy. And promptly found themselves in the midst of a far bigger one.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/tolerance-for-intolerance-bruises-lowes/', 'snippet': 'Lowe’s pulled its advertising from TLC’s “All American Muslin” to quiet a controversy. And promptly found themselves in the midst of a far bigger one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poor Lowe’s. The do-it-yourself chain made room in their budget for TLC’s “All-American Muslim,” a reality show about life in the Muslim American community in Dearborn, Mich., but the company ended up catching some focus for supporting a show that depicted Muslims as something besides terrorists. As it turned out, the protest was hardly broad based, with most of it coming from one guy. Still, the Florida Family Association, as David Caton’s Web site is called, claimed 65 companies agreed to stop advertising on “All-America Muslim.” That didn’t turn out to be precisely true.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tolerance for Intolerance Bruises Lowe’s', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Florida Family Assn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:16:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0b1bbda-1be0-57ba-865f-02d67bd077a9', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0b1bbda-1be0-57ba-865f-02d67bd077a9'}, {'abstract': 'The Slap Shot contributor Chris Botta talks New York area hockey in the SNY.tv Metro Hockey Podcast.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/listen-sny-tv-metro-hockey-podcast-2/', 'snippet': 'The Slap Shot contributor Chris Botta talks New York area hockey in the SNY.tv Metro Hockey Podcast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Slap Shot contributor Christopher Botta talks New York area hockey with Brian Compton of NHL.com and the agent Jay Grossman from The Puck Agency in the SNY.tv Metro Hockey Podcast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Listen: SNY.tv Metro Hockey Podcast', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:20:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fa58b81-8c91-5bfa-92a9-e734d83b83e2', 'word_count': 62, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fa58b81-8c91-5bfa-92a9-e734d83b83e2'}, {'abstract': 'Pop, country, R&B and classical stars all make appearances on the Academy’s shortlist of eligible best-song nominees. One notable exception: Madonna.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/lady-gaga-and-mary-j-blige-make-oscars-cut-but-not-madonna/', 'snippet': 'Pop, country, R&B and classical stars all make appearances on the Academy’s shortlist of eligible best-song nominees. One notable exception: Madonna.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not quite a musical year-end best-of, but the Academy’s shortlist of 39 songs that could go on to win the Oscar has enough indie, pop, country, R&B and classical stars to make critics from Pitchfork to Pazz and Jop happy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lady Gaga and Mary J. Blige Make Oscar’s Cut, but Not Madonna', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blige, Mary J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Close, Glenn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornell, Chris (1964- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'John, Elton', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lady Gaga', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenzie, Bret', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connor, Sinead", 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paisley, Brad', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pavarotti, Luciano', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f66a035-f35a-534c-a632-02e9312157db', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f66a035-f35a-534c-a632-02e9312157db'}, {'abstract': '“Enlightened,” which stars Laura Dern as a woman who undergoes a personal reawakening, will get another season on HBO, but “Bored to Death,” “Hung” and “How to Make It in America” will not.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/hbo-renews-enlightened-but-cancels-three-other-shows/', 'snippet': '“Enlightened,” which stars Laura Dern as a woman who undergoes a personal reawakening, will get another season on HBO, but “Bored to Death,” “Hung” and “How to Make It in America” will not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A pair of Golden Globe nominations for the HBO comedy “Enlightened” may have helped it earn a second season, but three other shows on that channel – “Hung,” “Bored to Death” and “How to Make It in America” – will not be ascending to another plane and have been canceled.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'HBO Renews ‘Enlightened’ but Cancels Three Other Shows', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Danson, Ted', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dern, Laura', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galifianakis, Zach', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jane, Thomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartzman, Jason', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorkin, Aaron', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'WHITE, MIKE', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:31:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/677870f1-db38-50f2-a2df-b29a19ba547a', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/677870f1-db38-50f2-a2df-b29a19ba547a'}, {'abstract': 'For three months, Jared Kushner has tried to persuade the Landmarks Preservation Commission that a penthouse atop the Puck Building would be appropriate. After he shrank his proposal enough, it agreed.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/landmarks-commission-approves-puck-building-penthouse/', 'snippet': 'For three months, Jared Kushner has tried to persuade the Landmarks Preservation Commission that a penthouse atop the Puck Building would be appropriate. After he shrank his proposal enough, it agreed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After three months of back-and-forth negotiating with the landlord Jared Kushner, the Landmarks Preservation Commission gave its unanimous approval on Tuesday morning to Mr. Kushner’s proposal for a residential penthouse atop the Puck Building, at Houston and Lafayette Streets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Landmarks Commission Approves Puck Building Penthouse', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berman, Andrew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kushner, Jared', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paulsen, Sherida E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Landmarks Preservation Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PKSB Architects', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:35:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6855e14-ef3c-5917-83cd-f01a4c282889', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6855e14-ef3c-5917-83cd-f01a4c282889'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Z. Klein, the N.H.L. reporter for The Times, and Christopher Botta, Slap Shot contributor, will answer questions from readers.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/have-n-h-l-questions-slap-shot-has-answers/', 'snippet': 'Jeff Z. Klein, the N.H.L. reporter for The Times, and Christopher Botta, Slap Shot contributor, will answer questions from readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are the Canucks as good as they were last season? Can the Flyers maintain their momentum? What do the Rangers need to remain competitive? Will Coach Randy Cunneyworth make a difference in Montreal? Jeff Z. Klein, the N.H.L. reporter for the The Times, and Slap Shot contributor Christopher Botta will answer questions from readers. Use the comment field for your questions, and answers will appear on Slap Shot later this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have N.H.L. Questions? Slap Shot Has Answers', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Jeff Z', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:36:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/580ebfa7-ec71-5b3b-a0ac-cb4ef0f92ca1', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/580ebfa7-ec71-5b3b-a0ac-cb4ef0f92ca1'}, {'abstract': 'This week, William Berloni, an animal trainer and the director of animal behavior for the Humane Society of New York, is visiting City Room to answer questions about choosing a dog as a companion or living with your current one.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/q-and-a-with-william-berloni/', 'snippet': 'This week, William Berloni, an animal trainer and the director of animal behavior for the Humane Society of New York, is visiting City Room to answer questions about choosing a dog as a companion or living with your current one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the weekend, readers of Elissa Gootman’s recent article offered up their suggestions for the best dogs for apartment living. People expressed great affection for their dogs’ breeds. There was even a mini-ode. Reader advice ranged from corgis to greyhounds to, of course, cats.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q. and A. With William Berloni: Which Doggy in the Window?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berloni, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:41:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/554db3bc-3343-5ff6-b7e2-659f1e66a15d', 'word_count': 120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/554db3bc-3343-5ff6-b7e2-659f1e66a15d'}, {'abstract': 'James Harvey Hylton, 77, drove 175 miles per hour at Daytona International Speedway on Sunday, in a test for the 2012 Automobile Racing Club of America race season.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/a-50-year-racing-career-with-no-checkered-flag-in-sight/', 'snippet': 'James Harvey Hylton, 77, drove 175 miles per hour at Daytona International Speedway on Sunday, in a test for the 2012 Automobile Racing Club of America race season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAYTONA BEACH, Fla. – The driving habits of senior citizens is a favorite conversation starter among Floridians. But those exchanges tend not to include the likes of James Harvey Hylton, 77, who borrowed a friend’s Ford on Sunday morning and drove it 175.634 miles per hour and later apologized for not going faster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A 50-Year Racing Career, With No Checkered Flag in Sight', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T18:41:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Cole Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Cole', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/225d34b3-8566-54c9-b63d-1d886909d7bf', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/225d34b3-8566-54c9-b63d-1d886909d7bf'}, {'abstract': "Images of a woman beaten by Egyptian soldiers sparks protests | A sectarian political rift in Iraq | Newt and Callista Gingrich's production company", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multim