: 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Samsung Electronics Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seagate Technology PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T20:30:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c55aee26-eca5-58d4-8620-ff5ef74169b1', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c55aee26-eca5-58d4-8620-ff5ef74169b1'}, {'abstract': 'McCartney’s Hawaii-inspired resort collection includes denim pieces subtly printed with tropical palm trees.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/the-get-stella-mccartney-hawaiian-print-jeans/', 'snippet': 'McCartney’s Hawaii-inspired resort collection includes denim pieces subtly printed with tropical palm trees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of her Hawaii-inspired resort collection, Stella McCartney is introducing a number of denim pieces subtly printed with tropical palm trees, the effect of which is similar to –– but much more innovative and exciting than –– acid wash or tie-dye. Available in early January when the designer is scheduled to open two new stores, one in Miami at the Bal Harbour Shops and the other on Greene Street in Soho, the jacket and blue jeans seen here are as ready for sunny South Beach as they are the chilly streets of the Northeast. “Denim is something that’s already ingrained in everyone’s wardrobe,” says McCartney, “so we played with it to give it a new twist.” Aloha!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Stella McCartney Hawaiian Print Jeans', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T20:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15a008cf-82ba-5486-8fea-5a7ebed10559', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15a008cf-82ba-5486-8fea-5a7ebed10559'}, {'abstract': 'The company, which was set up by Universal Music Group and Sony Music Entertainment, sees a future in being what MTV was in its earlier days:  a channel that will show music videos as well as other kinds of pop-culture entertainment.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/vevo-a-music-video-web-site-wants-to-get-into-television/', 'snippet': 'The company, which was set up by Universal Music Group and Sony Music Entertainment, sees a future in being what MTV was in its earlier days:  a channel that will show music videos as well as other kinds of pop-culture entertainment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In yet another example of the muddying of media categories, Vevo, the music video Web site set up by the two largest record companies is in talks with cable carriers to become a TV channel. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vevo, a Music Video Web Site, Wants to Get Into Television', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vevo.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T20:31:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97cee48a-47e7-51c2-8a76-b4816622e419', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97cee48a-47e7-51c2-8a76-b4816622e419'}, {'abstract': 'A missive from Michigan.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/benton-harbor-an-addendum-by-chris-savage/', 'snippet': 'A missive from Michigan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While I was in Benton Harbor, Mich., reporting this week’s cover article, I met a guy named Chris Savage and his wife one night for some pizza and beer. (We also went the beach and watched a pretty spectacular sunset on Lake Michigan.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Benton Harbor: An Addendum by Chris Savage', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benton Harbor (Mich)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T20:32:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By JONATHAN MAHLER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MAHLER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a50f29c4-7706-5e3c-9ce3-f0c7a5e6c39a', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://ar