icle', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88a37daa-3f73-513a-b657-69212afdcfcf', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88a37daa-3f73-513a-b657-69212afdcfcf'}, {'abstract': 'Fried and fired, the montanara pizza at Forcella has the naughty pleasure of street food.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/reviewing-forcella/', 'snippet': 'Fried and fired, the montanara pizza at Forcella has the naughty pleasure of street food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With only an oven, Giulio Adriani established his reputation as a pizzaiolo in Italy and at Olio Pizza e Più in Greenwich Village. Now, at Forcella, Mr. Adriani has a fryer, too. With it, he creates his montanara pizza, a margherita with a crust flash-fried before being finished in a wood-burning oven. The crust has a crisp airiness, not to mention a thin coat of oil that has the naughty pleasure of street food; the oven gives it color and adds a little smoke. Other pizzas on the menu aren’t up to the montanara’s caliber, but you’ll do well with the classics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing Forcella', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pizza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-20T22:19:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': 