ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e57b742-3b78-5bbf-92b0-4fac82381625', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e57b742-3b78-5bbf-92b0-4fac82381625'}, {'abstract': 'A Japanese hot dog place and a drip coffee bar highlight the restaurant openings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/dining/japadog-shake-shack-in-brooklyn-are-open-off-the-menu.html', 'snippet': 'A Japanese hot dog place and a drip coffee bar highlight the restaurant openings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opening', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Menu', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off the Menu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:31:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81089cc0-9be2-5239-a816-ec20b2031e6e', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81089cc0-9be2-5239-a816-ec20b2031e6e'}, {'abstract': 'A new butcher brings in carcasses from local farms and does all the butchering on the premises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/dining/harlem-shambles-a-butcher-shop-opens-uptown.html', 'snippet': 'A new butcher brings in carcasses from local farms and does all the butchering on the premises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wonder why a new uptown butcher is called Harlem Shambles? The noun shamble comes from an old word for butcher shop. Timothy Forrester, who owns this bright, spacious store with his brother, Mark, brings in carcasses from local farms and does all the butchering on the premises. Everything is grass-fed and antibiotic- and hormone-free. Dry-aging is evident in the look of the deeply crimson beef; a boneless strip steak was tender, with beefy flavor. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_BUTCHERSPAN/21STUFF2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_BUTCHERSPAN/21STUFF2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_BUTCHERSPAN/21STUFF2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_BUTCHERSPAN/21STUFF2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_BUTCHERSPAN/21STUFF2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harlem Shambles, a Butcher Shop, Opens Uptown', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Butcher in the Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butchers and Butchering', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:31:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bddedf2-0a23-5fac-9111-21150cef7973', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bddedf2-0a23-5fac-9111-21150cef7973'}, {'abstract': 'The Guilty Goose, a brasserie-style restaurant, will take care of the succulent, beautifully burnished free-range bird for you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/dining/a-roast-goose-ready-to-order-for-christmas.html', 'snippet': 'The Guilty Goose, a brasserie-style restaurant, will take care of the succulent, beautifully burnished free-range bird for you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Christmas brings to mind Charles Dickens and ye merry gentlemen, then dinner must be roast goose. You can let Christopher Parks, the executive chef at Guilty Goose, a brasserie-style restaurant, take care of the succulent, beautifully burnished free-range bird, with gravy and an earthy, winy dressing of brioche, goose sausage, bacon, raisins, apples and a whiff of sherry. Roasted brussels sprouts, squash purée, corn bread and, for dessert, apple crumble tart, are included in the dinner to go. Everything is easily reheated, unless, like the late Elizabeth David, you prefer your roast goose cold. Roast goose is also an Eastern European dish for Hanukkah. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF1_GOOSESPAN/21STUFF1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF1_GOOSESPAN/21STUFF1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF1_GOOSESPAN/21STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF1_GOOSESPAN/21STUFF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF1_GOOSESPAN/21STUFF1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Roast Goose Ready to Serve at Christmas', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Roast Goose Ready to Serve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:31:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/436780d6-f3a2-55ef-a8e7-279324646e19', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/436780d6-f3a2-55ef-a8e7-279324646e19'}, {'abstract': 'From Ohio, Jeni’s Splendid Ice Creams offers a selection, many themed to the holidays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/dining/jenis-ice-cream-in-holiday-flavors.html', 'snippet': 'From Ohio, Jeni’s Splendid Ice Creams offers a selection, many themed to the holidays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeni’s Splendid Ice Creams, a company whose particularly alluring products are worth ordering from Columbus, Ohio, has introduced new flavors, most with seasonal spicing and a touch of spirit, to embellish a holiday party. Boozy eggnog, goat cheese with Cognac figs, smoked tea and plum pudding, vanilla cedarwood and black currant lambic sorbet are some of the winners among 11 options. Dark chocolate peppermint is not so unusual, but Jeni’s is intense. Some flavors were less appealing: the marshmallow bits in the sweet potato ice cream were a tad rubbery, and the cake crumbs in the Black Forest made the texture grainy.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_ICESPAN/21STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_ICESPAN/21STUFF3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_ICESPAN/21STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_ICESPAN/21STUFF3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/dining/21STUFF_ICESPAN/21STUFF3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeni’s Ice Cream in Holiday Flavors', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ice Cream in Holiday Flavors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ICE CREAM', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:31:33+0000', 'document_typ