sed an upgrade for its iPad app, adding car reservation and trip-planning tools while introducing a brand-new design.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/kayak-ipad-app/', 'snippet': 'Kayak, a plane-booking service, has released an upgrade for its iPad app, adding car reservation and trip-planning tools while introducing a brand-new design.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kayak, the plane-booking service, released an upgrade on Tuesday for its iPad app, adding car reservation and trip-planning tools while introducing a brand-new design.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kayak iPad App Now Reserves Cars and Plans Trips', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Avis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kayak.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:44:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60b1b1aa-e5f4-5c15-b49b-7d8347ea9564', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60b1b1aa-e5f4-5c15-b49b-7d8347ea9564'}, {'abstract': 'Diane Kessler, 54, is unable to walk down the stairs from her second-floor apartment, though she manages the trip up, pulling herself along the railing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/nyregion/pulling-herself-up-the-stairs.html', 'snippet': 'Diane Kessler, 54, is unable to walk down the stairs from her second-floor apartment, though she manages the trip up, pulling herself along the railing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-one steps keep Diane Kessler a virtual prisoner in her home.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/NEEDIEST2/NEEDIEST2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/NEEDIEST2/NEEDIEST2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/NEEDIEST2/NEEDIEST2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/NEEDIEST2/NEEDIEST2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/NEEDIEST2/NEEDIEST2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Living With Multiple Sclerosis, and Separated From the World by a Stairway', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Living With Multiple Sclerosis, and Separated From the World by a Stairway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'UJA-Federation of New York', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Sclerosis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:48:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mathew R. Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mathew', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70ac9226-03b5-5476-acb0-94bcfeaf84c2', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70ac9226-03b5-5476-acb0-94bcfeaf84c2'}, {'abstract': 'The trainer, who will be approved by the players union, will have direct access to the team’s medical staff to help watch for players sustaining hits to the head.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/sports/football/nfl-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'The trainer, who will be approved by the players union, will have direct access to the team’s medical staff to help watch for players sustaining hits to the head.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.F.L., reacting to the Cleveland Browns’ treatment of quarterback Colt McCoy after he was hit on the head during a game Dec. 8, will pay for a certified trainer who is not affiliated with either team to be at each game to assist in spotting and checking for concussions. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'League to Add Independent Trainer at Each Game to Check for Concussions', 'kicker': 'N.F.L. Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'League to Add Independent Trainer at Each Game to Check for Concussions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCoy, Colt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Browns', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:50:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista and Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dee36abb-67fe-5a6a-ae54-cd04b37dfd87', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dee36abb-67fe-5a6a-ae54-cd04b37dfd87'}, {'abstract': 'One popular theory is that the president is benefiting from the battle over the payroll tax cut with Congress, but it seems more likely that an improving economy is making Americans less pessimistic.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/explaining-obamas-approval-rating-bounce/', 'snippet': 'One popular theory is that the president is benefiting from the battle over the payroll tax cut with Congress, but it seems more likely that an improving economy is making Americans less pessimistic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama has seen improved approval ratings in the past few weeks. His approval rating in the average of six polls tracked by Real Clear Politics now stands at 46.5 percent. That’s the highest the figure has been since July, and is up from a low of about 42 percent in early October.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Explaining Obama’s Approval Rating Bounce', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T00:52:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3d39ebf-cb0c-5306-a52b-e724d11c9472', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3d39ebf-cb0c-5306-a52b-e724d11c9472'}, {'abstract': 'Despite emerging as Latin America’s rising power, Brazil still trails its neighbors in prosecuting officials for crimes that include murder, disappearance and torture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/world/americas/brazil-uneasily-searches-for-truth-on-military-rule-abuses.html', 'snippet': 'Despite emerging as Latin America’s rising power, Brazil still trails its neighbors in prosecuting officials for crimes that include murder, disappearance and torture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRASÍLIA — After years of wrangling with the nation’s military hierarchy, the authorities here have created a truth commission to examine the abuses of Brazil’s long dictatorship, a move hailed as a sign that Brazil could be ready for a more active role against rights abuses, not just at home but globally as well. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/world/SUB-BRAZIL/SUB-BRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/world/SUB-BRAZIL/SUB-BRAZIL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/world/SUB-BRAZIL/SUB-BRAZIL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo'