s outside the busy parts of Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo struck a deal with the Bloomberg administration on Tuesday that would expand taxi service in New York by allowing livery cabs to be hailed on the street in parts of the city traditionally underserved by yellow cabs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/TAXI/TAXI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/TAXI/TAXI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/TAXI/TAXI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/TAXI/TAXI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/nyregion/TAXI/TAXI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deal Struck to Broaden Taxi Service in the City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deal Struck To Broaden Taxi Service In the City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taxi and Limousine Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T02:50:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6da737d-d48a-5254-92c8-c6e5d93804b2', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6da737d-d48a-5254-92c8-c6e5d93804b2'}, {'abstract': 'Iraq’s Sunni vice president, Tariq al-Hashimi, on Tuesday strongly rebutted accusations by the Shiite-led central government that he had ordered the assassinations of government officials.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/iraqs-sunni-vice-president-denies-ordering-killings/', 'snippet': 'Iraq’s Sunni vice president, Tariq al-Hashimi, on Tuesday strongly rebutted accusations by the Shiite-led central government that he had ordered the assassinations of government officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tuesday’s TimesCast video looks at developments in the deepening political crisis in Iraq. Stephen Farrell reports that the country’s Sunni vice president, Tariq al-Hashimi, strongly rebutted accusations by the Shiite-led central government that he had ordered the assassinations of government officials. Mr. Hashimi then accused the government of Prime Minister Nuri Kamal al-Maliki of using security forces to persecute political opponents. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq’s Sunni Vice President Denies Ordering Killings', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'S