/www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/business/norman-krim-who-championed-the-transistor-dies-at-98.html', 'snippet': 'Working for Raytheon, Mr. Krim first put transistors into hearing aids. He later had a hand in the early growth of the RadioShack chain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Norman Krim, an electronics visionary who played a pivotal role in the industry’s transition from the bulky electron vacuum tube, which once lined the innards of radios and televisions, to the tiny, far more powerful transistor, died on Dec. 14 in a retirement home in Newton, Mass. He was 98.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/business/KRIM-obit/KRIM-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/business/KRIM-obit/KRIM-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/21/business/KRIM-obit/KRIM-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Norman Krim, Who Championed the Transistor, Dies at 98', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Norman Krim, 98, Dies; Championed the Transistor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'RadioShack Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Raytheon Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T03:24:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Hevesi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hevesi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/c42bca4b-9bef-5ff2-9693-cbff43fecc8b', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c42bca4b-9bef-5ff2-9693-cbff43fecc8b'}, {'abstract': 'A long-awaited contingent of soldiers from Djibouti arrived in Mogadishu, Somalia’s capital, on Tuesday to join the African Union peacekeeping force.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/world/africa/somalia-soldiers-from-djibouti-arrive.html', 'snippet': 'A long-awaited contingent of soldiers from Djibouti arrived in Mogadishu, Somalia’s capital, on Tuesday to join the African Union peacekeeping force.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A long-awaited contingent of soldiers from Djibouti arrived in Mogadishu, Somalia’s capital, on Tuesday to join the African Union peacekeeping force. After a few hours of confusion about where the Djiboutians would be based, about 200 soldiers wearing helmets and flak jackets marched off the plane and headed to a camp along the ocean. Djibouti had promised for years to send peacekeepers to Somalia. It is the third country to contribute combat units, along with Uganda and Burundi. Djiboutian officials say they expect to send several hundred more troops in the coming months. The total African Union force is now about 10,000 soldiers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somalia: Soldiers From Djibouti Arrive', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somalia: Soldiers From Djibouti Arrive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Djibouti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN UNION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T03:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Gettleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gettleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/79a59751-3e07-5715-bc5f-3fab8e7ed8c1', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79a59751-3e07-5715-bc5f-3fab8e7ed8c1'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney cast President Obama as a president turning away from American exceptionalism, and giving up on the mission of making the country better.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/20/romney-reworks-stumps-speech-but-keeps-focus-on-obama/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney cast President Obama as a president turning away from American exceptionalism, and giving up on the mission of making the country better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEDFORD, N.H. — Mitt Romney drew some of the sharpest and harshest distinctions yet between himself and President Obama on Tuesday night, painting the upcoming election as a choice between “two destinies” — his vision of a capitalist society based on merit and enterprise, versus what he called the president’s embrace of entitlement programs and an intent “to use the invisible boot of government to bring us all down.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Reworks Stump Speech, but Keeps Focus on Obama', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama