r.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/word-of-the-day-partiality/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in eight New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'partiality •\\ˌpär-she-ˈa-lə-te, pär-ˈsha-lə-te\\• noun', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | partiality', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T05:09:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46679045-25cc-5b7f-a193-e8504d8e7bc3', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46679045-25cc-5b7f-a193-e8504d8e7bc3'}, {'abstract': 'How Mississippi’s abandoned antebellum mansions give insight into the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/what-remains/', 'snippet': 'How Mississippi’s abandoned antebellum mansions give insight into the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps the most famous house of 1861 was, by July of that year, no longer standing. The home, called Spring Hill, belonged to Judith Henry, an invalid widow, and stood on a hill overlooking Bull Run. In the battle that engulfed the fields around Spring Hill on July 21, Henry was killed and her home destroyed, save for a few remnant beams and a section of chimney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Remains', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mansions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foote, Shelby', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T05:11:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nell Dickerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/729585b4-812f-595c-aa13-043fe7b6b0c4', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/729585b4-812f-595c-aa13-043fe7b6b0c4'}, {'abstract': 'Seif al-Islam el-Qaddafi is alive and being held by a rebel militia outside Tripoli, according to an American organization that was granted access to him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/world/africa/qaddafi-son-seif-al-islam-is-alive-and-held-by-rebels-rights-group-says.html', 'snippet': 'Seif al-Islam el-Qaddafi is alive and being held by a rebel militia outside Tripoli, according to an American organization that was granted access to him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya — Seif al-Islam el-Qaddafi, the son and presumed heir of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi, is alive and well and being held by a rebel militia outside the Libyan capital, according to an American human rights organization that was granted rare access to him. But the group said that Mr. Qaddafi had been denied access to legal counsel as he awaited trial on charges of crimes against humanity.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/21/world/LIBYA/LIBYA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 377, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None