ors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94b0c822-053d-5a1d-978d-4a2c92ef125f', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94b0c822-053d-5a1d-978d-4a2c92ef125f'}, {'abstract': 'infants whose parents fed them starchy table foods like crackers and breakfast cereals, which typically contain added salt, were more likely to gravitate to salty foods by the time they reached preschool.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/taste-for-salt-begins-early-in-life/', 'snippet': 'infants whose parents fed them starchy table foods like crackers and breakfast cereals, which typically contain added salt, were more likely to gravitate to salty foods by the time they reached preschool.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is no question that Americans have an unhealthy appetite for salt, but one big reason may be overexposure to salty foods in infancy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taste for Salt Is Shaped Early in Life', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Foods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T12:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/caea20bd-36a7-5ee7-9823-1b181b1e26ad', 'word_count': 807, '