'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gumbel, Bryant', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Logan, Lara', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Al Jazeera English', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T16:29:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dda70c4-dc06-5d35-abb2-165e9df3fc38', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dda70c4-dc06-5d35-abb2-165e9df3fc38'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese director Zhang Yimou discuses the making of “The Flowers of War” and Chinese government control.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/zhang-yimou-and-the-challenges-of-filming-in-china/', 'snippet': 'The Chinese director Zhang Yimou discuses the making of “The Flowers of War” and Chinese government control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As someone whose life was upended by the Cultural Revolution, the Chinese director Zhang Yimou, 60, has every reason to be wary of politics. But his new film, “The Flowers of War,” which opens Wednesday, is China’s submission for the Academy Award for best foreign-language film and has already picked up a Golden Globe nomination. (It’s the subject of an article in the Arts & Leisure section here.) It deals with one of the most horrific episodes in modern Chinese history: the Nanjing Massacre of December 1937, in which Japanese troops overran China’s capital, killing more than 200,000 people and raping as many as 80,000 women.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zhang Yimou and the Challenges of Filming in China', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-21T16:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31aebe50-2911-558e-ae39-ed24ed14a7c9', 'word_count': 1686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31aebe50-2911-558e-ae39-ed24ed14a7c9'}, {'abstract': 'Mayors around the country are asking: Why has Washington cut one of the main federal programs for cities by a quarter in the last couple of years?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/us/cities-struggle-as-us-slashes-block-grants-program.html', 'snippet': 'Mayors around the country are asking: Why has Washington cut one of the main federal programs for cities by a quarter in the last couple of years?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALLENTOWN, Pa. — It is no secret that these are hard times for cities, with tax collections down, state aid dwindling, unemployment high and foreclosures pitting many blocks. So, as he sat in his office here, Mayor Ed Pawlowski of Allentown echoed the question mayors around the country are asking: Why has Washington cut one of the main federal programs for cities by a quarter in the last couple of years? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20