adelphia Daily News', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T01:50:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e4c4ebf-fa3a-5805-963f-e015d4304cd2', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e4c4ebf-fa3a-5805-963f-e015d4304cd2'}, {'abstract': 'A ruling that the state’s process to revise the rules for lethal injection was invalid is further proof that the death penalty system is badly broken and in need of being shut down.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/opinion/californias-lethal-injections.html', 'snippet': 'A ruling that the state’s process to revise the rules for lethal injection was invalid is further proof that the death penalty system is badly broken and in need of being shut down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2006, a federal district court ordered California to stop executing people because the state’s three-drug protocol for lethal injection lacked “reliability.” Six of the 11 men executed by lethal injection in the state since 1978 may have suffered cruel and unusual punishment in violation of the Constitution because the injection inflicted “excessive pain,” Judge Jeremy Fogel found. He said the state could cure the constitutional problem by being transparent in devising a new protocol and using a one-drug, anesthetic-only method (as Ohio and Washington do). ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California’s Lethal Injections', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California’s Lethal Injections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Death Penalty Information Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T01:58:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fccbd07-63dd-5d1b-a09b-c5e884bb68de', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fccbd07-63dd-5d1b-a09b-c5e884bb68de'}, {'abstract': 'What’s the quickest way to hit every county in Iowa?', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/21/the-problem-of-the-traveling-politician/', 'snippet': 'What’s the quickest way to hit every county in Iowa?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Politicians have always been heavy travelers, but recent news out of Iowa takes the notion of hitting the campaign trail to new extremes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Problem of the Traveling Politician', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2008', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachmann, Michele M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T02:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Cook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77b212e3-ca0b-5bc0-9e0b-4157a3bb5271', 'word_count': 1056, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77b212e3-ca0b-5bc0-9e0b-4157a3bb5271'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Chamberlain almost singlehandedly gave automotive metal a place in the history of sculpture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/arts/design/john-chamberlain-artist-of-auto-metal-dies-at-84.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Chamberlain almost singlehandedly gave automotive metal a place in the history of sculpture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Chamberlain, who almost singlehandedly gave automotive metal a place in the history of sculpture, smashing and twisting together a poetic fusion of Abstract Expressionism and Pop from fenders, fins, bumpers and hoods, died on Wednesday in Manhattan. He was 84.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/22/arts/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/22/arts/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/22/arts/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit/CHAMBERLAIN1-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'