 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T15:34:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9eced1b-1c3d-5dc2-a04a-6c662546f655', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9eced1b-1c3d-5dc2-a04a-6c662546f655'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Opera’s “Enchanted Island,” which opens on New Year’s Eve, is a pastiche in which “The Tempest” meets “A Midsummer Night’s Dream.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/arts/music/the-enchanted-island-a-baroque-mash-up-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Opera’s “Enchanted Island,” which opens on New Year’s Eve, is a pastiche in which “The Tempest” meets “A Midsummer Night’s Dream.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Oxford English Dictionary defines a pastiche as “a medley of various ingredients; a hotchpotch, farrago, jumble.” The word is derived from the 13th-century Latin “pasticium,” a savory Mediterranean pie filled with whatever is left over in the kitchen. A modern analogue, pastitsio, can be found at every Greek diner in the land.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25ENCHANTED1_SPAN/25ENCHANTED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25ENCHANTED1_SPAN/25ENCHANTED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur