ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:11:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35a79e0a-cef7-5bd5-956e-07abc62a400d', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35a79e0a-cef7-5bd5-956e-07abc62a400d'}, {'abstract': 'For its final Facebook live chat of the week (and the year), The Choice blog invites juniors, in particular, to submit their questions about the admissions process, and to tune in tonight at 8 p.m. Eastern time for answers.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/chat-recap/', 'snippet': 'For its final Facebook live chat of the week (and the year), The Choice blog invites juniors, in particular, to submit their questions about the admissions process, and to tune in tonight at 8 p.m. Eastern time for answers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thursday night on The Choice blog’s Facebook page — facebook.com/nytimesthechoice — we’ll host our final, hourlong admissions Q. and A. of the week (and the year). And we’d like the main (but by no means only) theme to be this one: What college admissions homework might high-school juniors (and their parents) assign themselves over the holiday break, and what should their priorities be right after New Year’s?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Choice Q. and A. on Facebook: An Invitation to Juniors, and a Recap', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:14:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg and Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b747a661-00f6-563f-a69c-a7977f03eba5', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b747a661-00f6-563f-a69c-a7977f03eba5'}, {'abstract': 'The Concord Monitor endorsed Jon M. Huntsman Jr. as the best candidate to take on President Obama, giving him a bit of a boost as he makes his case in New Hampshire.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/concord-monitor-endorses-huntsman/', 'snippet': 'The Concord Monitor endorsed Jon M. Huntsman Jr. as the best candidate to take on President Obama, giving him a bit of a boost as he makes his case in New Hampshire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even as Mitt Romney and Newt Gingrich grab the headlines with a nasty slug-fest, Jon M. Huntsman Jr. is quietly working New Hampshire in the hope of pulling a come-from-nowhere upset in the nation’s first primary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Concord Monitor Endorses Huntsman', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:19:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c776c85-ede1-50d6-b932-0d81a462c30c', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c776c85-ede1-50d6-b932-0d81a462c30c'}, {'abstract': 'Doing what it says it won’t, through the back door?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/the-subtle-ecb/', 'snippet': 'Doing what it says it won’t, through the back door?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A number of people have asked me for my reaction to Floyd Norris’s piece today on Europe. It’s a good summary of the argument many people are making about why the European situation looks less dire right now than it seemed a few weeks ago. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Subtle ECB', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:29:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a715bc9e-ae25-54cd-b962-0c92ecaa86fe', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a715bc9e-ae25-54cd-b962-0c92ecaa86fe'}, {'abstract': 'An honor for the director of “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/stephen-daldry-directors-director/', 'snippet': 'An honor for the director of “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not all of the awards buzz has faded for “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” Stephen Daldry’s 9/11 Oscar bait. Mr. Daldry, whose three previous features (“The Hours,” “The Reader” and “Billy Elliot”) were all Oscar nominees or winners, will receive the director of the year award from the Palm Springs International Film Festival, it announced today. In a statement, Harold Matzner, the festival chairman, said that Mr. Daldry would be honored “for this haunting film and for all of his achievements as a director’s director.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stephen Daldry, Director’s Director', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daldry, Stephen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3640d29d-98cb-5097-897d-e079a41459b9', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3640d29d-98cb-5097-897d-e079a41459b9'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times’s pop music critics discuss their favorite songs of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/popcast-the-best-songs-of-2011/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times’s pop music critics discuss their favorite songs of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': '// ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Popcast: The Best Songs of 2011', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:33:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f38c41b-6807-5cd5-a68e-2c10604c064f', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f38c41b-6807-5cd5-a68e-2c10604c064f'}, {'abstract': 'The decision in Los Angeles to swap out corn dogs and chocolate milk for quinoa salads and vegetarian curries was seen as a move that might inspire other districts across the country to do the same, but students aren’t biting.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/students-say-no-to-healthy-school-fare/', 'snippet': 'The decision in Los Angeles to swap out corn dogs and chocolate milk for quinoa salads and vegetarian curries was seen as a move that might inspire other districts across the country to do the same, but students aren’t biting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Los Angeles Unified School District broke new ground earlier this year when it started phasing out junk food and offering students healthful new fare. The decision by L.A. Unified, one of the largest districts in the nation, to swap out corn dogs and chocolate milk for quinoa salads and vegetarian curries was seen as a move that might inspire other districts across the country to do the same.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Students Say No to Healthful School Fare', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T17:36:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f186cd7-de37-5d85-9d9c-6fe30ebfa917', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f186cd7-de37-5d85-9d9c-6fe30ebfa917'}, {'abstract': 'The military hearing against Pfc. Bradley Manning closed with military lawyers portraying him as a premeditated traitor, and the defense calling him an emotionally troubled whistle-blower.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/us/hearing-in-private-mannings-wikileaks-case-ends.html', 'snippet': 'The military hearing against Pfc. Bradley Manning closed with military lawyers portraying him as a premeditated traitor, and the defense calling him an emotionally troubled whistle-blower.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT MEADE, Md. — The military hearing against Pfc. Bradley Manning closed on Thursday, with lawyers and onlookers alternately portraying the young soldier as a traitor who acted with premeditation and as an emotionally troubled whistle-blower.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/us/23manning_cnd/23manning_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 958, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/us/23manning_cnd/23manning_cnd-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/23/us/23manning_cnd/23manning_cnd-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hearing in Soldier’s WikiLeaks Case Ends', 