der and Heat will meet in the finals. Beck sits down with reporter Lynn Zinser to discuss the 2011-12 season. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: N.B.A. Season Preview', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Mavericks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:37:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d2d1611-46eb-5121-a8c4-cfddf412806b', 'word_count': 42, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d2d1611-46eb-5121-a8c4-cfddf412806b'}, {'abstract': '“Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” a film with Sept. 11 as a backdrop, stars Tom Hanks and the young newcomer Thomas Horn, and is based on the novel by Jonathan Safran Foer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/movies/extremely-loud-incredibly-close-with-tom-hanks-review.html', 'snippet': '“Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” a film with Sept. 11 as a backdrop, stars Tom Hanks and the young newcomer Thomas Horn, and is based on the novel by Jonathan Safran Foer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the years since Sept. 11, that day has been revisited infrequently on American screens and with a circumspection that can feel like reluctance. Some moviemakers have probably shied away from the subject for fear of offending viewers; others, like Michael Moore, who directed “Fahrenheit 9/11,” haven’t worried about alienating a general audience because they’ve played to a specific constituency, giving their viewers what they wanted to see and hear. “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” a new Sept. 11 movie, takes a different tack from most of its predecessors by treating that day not as an occasion for personal sacrifice, for national mourning or reflection, but as kitsch. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/23EXTREME_SPAN/23EXTREME_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/23EXTREME_SPAN/23EXTREME_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/23EXTREME_SPAN/23EXTREME_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/23EXTREME_SPAN/23EXTREME_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Youngster With a Key, a Word and a Quest', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Youngster With a Key, A Word and a Quest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daldry, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'von Sydow, Max', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Viola', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bullock, Sandra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hanks, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foer, Jonathan Safran', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:40:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/70d37c60-f4a7-5f6e-b003-5519400d42b3', 'word_count': 1260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70d37c60-f4a7-5f6e-b003-5519400d42b3'}, {'abstract': 'The emerging restaurant scene is making the Biblical city more than just a quick stop on religious tours.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/travel/nazareth-as-a-culinary-destination.html', 'snippet': 'The emerging restaurant scene is making the Biblical city more than just a quick stop on religious tours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE bartender at Misk, a new restaurant and concert space in Nazareth, Israel, hurriedly scrolled through YouTube videos. His search was projected on a large screen, a new addition to the Ottoman-era white stone building. At last, he found a dance remix of an Arabic birthday song, and as the frenetic opening chords filled the room, a waitress strode out holding a cake fizzing with 18 sparkler candles.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25NEXT1_SPAN/25NEXT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25NEXT1_SPAN/25NEXT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25NEXT1_SPAN/25NEXT1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25NEXT1_SPAN/25NEXT1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25NEXT1_SPAN/25NEXT1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nazareth as an Eating Destination', 'kicker': '\u2002Next Stop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sleepy Nazareth Develops A Dining Scene', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel B. Doyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ce09b57-f106-5772-bec7-5cb76d83da77', 'word_count': 1403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ce09b57-f106-5772-bec7-5cb76d83da77'}, {'abstract': 'The payroll tax debate and conservative policy dysfunction.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/the-republican-cul-de-sac/', 'snippet': 'The payroll tax debate and conservative policy dysfunction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are any number of things I’d rather be doing 72 hours before Christmas than writing about the payroll tax debate, but it’s an important window into the continuing policy dysfunction afflicting the Republican Party. The problem, to be clear, doesn’t lie in the particular stance that the House Republicans have taken over the last week, insisting on a full-year extension of the payroll tax cut rather than two-month extension agreed to in the Senate. (On the merits, the House is right to want a full-year extension, the Senate’s punt was an embarrassment, and the compromise that may be in the offing looks like better policy than a purely short-term deal.) Rather, it lies in the fact that we’ve been having drawn-out negotiations about the issue at all. Republicans should have just taken yes for answer, embraced a bipartisan tax cut, declared victory and gone home for Christmas. Instead, a Republican Party that blithely voted to extend the Bush tax cuts without any offsets suddenly became very, very concerned about “paying for” a far more modest measure, and a G.O.P. that’s supposedly committed to the lowest possible middle class tax burden decided that it needed to extract concessions from a Democratic president in exchange for supporting … a middle class tax cut.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Republican Cul-De-Sac', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:45:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07da5374-43c0-53f7-bac2-142f506ee432', 'word_count': 541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07da5374-43c0-53f7-bac2-142f506ee432'}, {'abstract': 'One of the most popular restaurants in Navigli is an American-style steakhouse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/travel/joe-cipolla-milan-italy-restaurant-review.html', 'snippet': 'One of the most popular restaurants in Navigli is an American-style steakhouse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not quite a pairing to rival the spaghetti western, but it’s not far off: In Navigli, a trendy neighborhood in Milan, one of the most popular restaurants is an American-style steakhouse. With red-and-white-checkered tablecloths, walls crowded with black-and-white photos and a butcher counter up front, the five-year-old Joe Cipolla approximates a vintage meat-and-potatoes joint.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25BITES_SPAN/25BITES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25BITES_SPAN/25BITES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25BITES_SPAN/25BITES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25BITES_SPAN/25BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/travel/25BITES_SPAN/25BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joe Cipolla, Milan, Italy', 'kicker': 'Bites ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'MILAN: Joe Cipolla', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milan (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABIZAID, JOHN P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:46:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7723af36-7a1f-576e-8c2e-1bc5cca11ee8', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7723af36-7a1f-576e-8c2e-1bc5cca11ee8'}, {'abstract': 'The Justice Department required the sale of the German exchange’s stake in Direct Edge Holdings within two years.  Still, the biggest obstacle left is winning approval from the European Union.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/22/justice-approves-merger-of-nyse-euronext-and-deutsche-borse/', 'snippet': 'The Justice Department required the sale of the German exchange’s stake in Direct Edge Holdings within two years.  Still, the biggest obstacle left is winning approval from the European Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Justice Department approved on Thursday the proposed merger of NYSE Euronext and Deutsche Börse, but required the sale of the German exchange’s stake in Direct Edge Holdings within two years. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Dept. Approves Merger of NYSE Euronext and Deutsche Borse', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Borse AG', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Direct Edge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NYSE Euronext', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-22T19:47:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced