lue': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T00:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ba39074-a0d5-5325-a22a-0ef2719d1fdd', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ba39074-a0d5-5325-a22a-0ef2719d1fdd'}, {'abstract': 'The street-level freight line going through the tenement districts on 10th and 11th Avenues from 1846 to 1941 killed so many people its route became known as Death Avenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/realestate/the-railroad-tracks-that-turned-a-street-into-death-avenue.html', 'snippet': 'The street-level freight line going through the tenement districts on 10th and 11th Avenues from 1846 to 1941 killed so many people its route became known as Death Avenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOUSANDS of kids are going to find model train sets under the tree on Christmas morning — freight trains, circus trains, Wild West trains, military trains, express trains, commuter trains. ... But one train setup you won’t see is a replica of the street-level freight line that plied the West Side from 1846 to 1941. The line killed and mutilated hundreds of people, and its path well earned the name Death Avenue. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/realestate/25STREETS1_SPAN/25STREETS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/realestate/25STREETS1_SPAN/25STREETS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jp