ce,” about a bookish beauty (Audrey Hepburn) reluctantly transformed into a high-fashion model at the hands of a visionary photographer (Fred Astaire) — died on Sunday in Los Angeles. She was 86. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/AVEDON-obit/AVEDON-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1011, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/AVEDON-obit/AVEDON-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/23/arts/AVEDON-obit/AVEDON-obit-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doe Avedon, Fashion Model and Actress, Dies at 86', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doe Avedon, 86, Fashion Model and Actress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T05:23:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margalit Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margalit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c1a196-3879-58a7-9e48-599fa6a5aea1', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c1a196-3879-58a7-9e48-599fa6a5aea1'}, {'abstract': 'Pyongyang calls the South’s decision to express sympathy for the North Korean people but not to send a delegation to Mr. Kim’s funeral “an unbearable insult and mockery of our dignity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/world/asia/north-korea-warns-south-to-show-respect-for-kim-jong-il.html', 'snippet': 'Pyongyang calls the South’s decision to express sympathy for the North Korean people but not to send a delegation to Mr. Kim’s funeral “an unbearable insult and mockery of our dignity.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — North Korea urged South Korea on Friday to “show proper respect” over the death of its leader, Kim Jong-il, calling the South’s decision to express sympathy for the North Korean people but not to send a government delegation to Mr. Kim’s funeral next week “an unbearable insult and mockery of our dignity.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea Warns South to Show ‘Respect’ for Kim Jong-il', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea Seeks Signs Of ‘Respect’ From Seoul', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Un', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T05:46:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecdbb25d-d3a4-55fd-b680-c0e4a2ffac21', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecdbb25d-d3a4-55fd-b680-c0e4a2ffac21'}, {'abstract': 'The light from the Arab Spring rose from the ground up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/arab-spring.html', 'snippet': 'The light from the Arab Spring rose from the ground up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BREATH AND HUM of democracy seemed almost a libidinous thing in parts of the Middle East, but, in truth, the body heat had been simmering for years. The protests took hold in Tunisia in late 2010 after the street vendor Mohamed Bouazizi set himself on fire. The kindle caught and the spirit of his self-immolation lit a fuse across the region. A wave of protests struck Algeria, Egypt, Yemen, Jordan. Almost as soon as protesters in Cairo were being herded away from Tahrir Square, they were dancing at the news of Mubarak’s resignation. Ruling families began to fall. Power-transfer treaties were brokered. Parliaments were dissolved. Monarchs locked their gates and boarded their windows. In Libya, Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi was ousted and killed. NATO planes streaked a sky so blue it seemed it to speak of other Septembers. Faces, too, were lit by a new blue light. It was the era of the revolution down through the wires: time was collapsed and geography shrunk by the use of social networking. The whole world was indeed watching, listening, e-mailing. Some in the media lost their lives for the art of the images that emerged — the photographs didn’t just capture singular moments, but they looked backward and forward at the same time, at what has happened, and what is yet to come. The\xa0light from the Arab Spring rose from the ground up; the hope\xa0is now that the darkness doesn’t fall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arab Spring', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f94d3ccf-2852-5fb6-acb5-775425ec2b9e', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f94d3ccf-2852-5fb6-acb5-775425ec2b9e'}, {'abstract': 'The bankers stood back, the corporations hushed a moment, and the cops took out their pepper spray.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/occupy-wall-street.html', 'snippet': 'The bankers stood back, the corporations hushed a moment, and the cops took out their pepper spray.', 'lead_paragraph': '“WE ARE THE 99 PERCENT.” It was so much beyond a local slogan: the louder it was spoken, the more it began to say. My job, my streets, my economy, my riot, my skin. Cities shook their souls out. The yearning went global. Some protesters poured gasoline into bottles and lighted the sky with fire. Others unfurled hammocks under light poles. The idea was to be seen, to be heard, to be tweeted. The year 2011 was an evanescent spectacle of anger. Every corner was a world, and every world had its corners. The bankers stood back, the corporations hushed a moment, the politicians held their breath, and the cops took out their pepper spray. It might have begun in Wisconsin, had its adolescence in Greece, and shattered the night in London, but it lost and won its innocence in Zuccotti Park. Suddenly, a tent had as much meaning as a tower. Kids from all over the country boiled collard greens in the courtyard of commerce. The city’s bureaucrats tried frantically to barricade them in, or out — nobody seemed sure which. The anger rolled all the way down to Wall Street and the markets shivered a moment. When cold weather kicked in, the tents began to disappear and the anger diffused, but the images remained, another sort of fire in flight. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/018ae3e1-46dc-5747-bade-72fdba9d3b91', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/018ae3e1-46dc-5747-bade-72fdba9d3b91'}, {'abstract': 'We were all, in some way, looking for a brand-new roof.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/the-nation.html', 'snippet': 'We were all, in some way, looking for a brand-new roof.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOMETIMES there is a necessity for anniversaries, even for things that cannot be forgotten. The memorial services for 9/11 were remarkable for their grace and dignity. The falling water, beside the reflecting pools, spoke volumes. While the Republicans fumbled for their own sort of memory — trying somehow to bring back the apparent ease of the Reagan years — the rest of the country drove down a street of foreclosed homes. We were all, in some way, looking for a brand-new roof. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Nation', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/210b3d5f-2f7f-53bf-a96f-9616b6481f6c', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/210b3d5f-2f7f-53bf-a96f-9616b6481f6c'}, {'abstract': 'The surprise of the world is that it can still surprise us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/the-world.html', 'snippet': 'The surprise of the world is that it can still surprise us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOSE WEREN’T JUST tank lights leaving the desert behind. That was the darkness of a nearly nine-year war made visible. The soldiers moved through the sandstorms, and landed back home in the United States to deal with the continuing legacy of American foreign policies. In 2011, Barack Obama’s promises were finally beginning to find their way onto the front pages. A spectacular nighttime raid found Osama bin Laden in Abbottabad, Pakistan. The helicopters landed. The weight of the past was kicked up in the dust. Author of a decade of killing, Bin Laden had endpages that were quick and sharp. He was shot dead, buried at sea, and the White House held its breath: it sounded so much like a sigh of joy, exhausted and necessary. The soldiers continued their journeys: it was a big world they were returning to. The seven billionth baby was born, starting global discussions on issues of environment, health and technology. Europe teetered on the edge of ruin, stunned by even more economic philandering. In the fall, lights were lit for Kim Il-sung and a regime that has hidden its face. In Norway the island of Utoya heard gunshots among the trees. Parts of Africa were dealing, yet again, with an almost impossible hunger and thirst. But stories and images are always new, no matter how old the theme. The surprise of the world is that it can still surprise us. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The World', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/47606c16-9c93-5a35-85b5-43fc49f9eb25', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47606c16-9c93-5a35-85b5-43fc49f9eb25'}, {'abstract': 'We look for calm, but there is always an inevitability of storm.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/nature.html', 'snippet': 'We look for calm, but there is always an inevitability of storm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE WATER ROSE. And rose. Soon there were traffic lights flipping, doors tumbling, tatami mats spinning in the current. Cars began to float alongside washing machines, computers, people. The incredible could occur. And then the tsunami seemed to wash away the last vain dream of the 20th century: the nuclear answer. The Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant went into meltdown and watched its poisons filter out to sea, and what remained is what we all must live with: the democratic rage of nature. No matter what we do, we cannot regulate how we are shaped by weather. We look for calm, but there is always an inevitability of storm. The images from Joplin, Mo., were pure with grief. A snowstorm paralyzed Tulsa, Okla. The Souris River shed more than a tear. The streets of Bangkok were almost apocalyptic during floods. There were droughts, earthquakes, wildfires scattering animals like dice. So, the question, then, is ancient: How with such rage shall beauty hold a plea? Rescuers in Turkey put their fingers on part of the pulse when they managed to pull 2-week-old Azra Karaduman from the rubble. Her mother and grandmother also emerged safely. She was tiny and voiceless, yet she said so much. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nature', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earthquakes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/99ef6d77-346c-5373-89ca-73acce638414', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99ef6d77-346c-5373-89ca-73acce638414'}, {'abstract': 'While the news might dwell on what we’ve lost, we occasionally still witness the most remarkable music: marriages, journeys, longings, births and returns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/opinion/sunday/milestones.html', 'snippet': 'While the news might dwell on what we’ve lost, we occasionally still witness the most remarkable music: marriages, journeys, longings, births and returns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A PHOTOGRAPH is sometimes a perfect testament to the notion that we can’t afford to lose our delight. While the news, and its makers, might dwell on what we’ve lost, we occasionally still witness the most remarkable music: marriages, journeys, longings, births and returns. Every now and then, the ground just won’t show its shadows. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Milestones', 'kicker': 'Year in Pictures', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:15:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colum McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colum', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/403c8d90-3397-560b-b9e0-c8d546ae059b', 'word_count': 56, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403c8d90-3397-560b-b9e0-c8d546ae059b'}, {'abstract': 'A Siberian court is scheduled to hear arguments for and against banning the Bhagavad Gita at the end of the month.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/in-russia-the-bhagavad-gita-has-a-date-in-court/', 'snippet': 'A Siberian court is scheduled to hear arguments for and against banning the Bhagavad Gita at the end of the month.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Russia, the Bhagavad Gita Has a Date in Court', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9862490f-8340-593d-9d00-25d6f875d92b', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9862490f-8340-593d-9d00-25d6f875d92b'}, {'abstract': 'President Dilma Rousseff of Brazil rightly warns that without limits on what the powerful may do, the emerging ideology of humanitarian intervention could become a tool of manipulation.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/the-arab-spring-seen-from-brazil/', 'snippet': 'President Dilma Rousseff of Brazil rightly warns that without limits on what the powerful may do, the emerging ideology of humanitarian intervention could become a tool of manipulation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO — Last week, a photograph of Dilma Rousseff’s terrorism trial in 1970 circulated on the Internet, sending shockwaves across Brazil. She was 22 and an active member of a guerrilla cell fighting the dictatorial regime of the day. In the shot, young Dilma looks pretty and clean-faced, like a freedom-fighter girl taking on a gerontocracy of mighty generals.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Arab Spring, Seen From Brazil', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rousseff, Dilma', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T07:55:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matias Spektor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spektor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5b3bf8e-c585-5255-b31e-5e48f30b868f', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5b3bf8e-c585-5255-b31e-5e48f30b868f'}, {'abstract': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/news-quiz-dec-23-2011/', 'snippet': 'See what you know about the news of the day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'See what you know about the news below. To prepare, you might scan the articles or summaries on today’s paper.', 'source': 