eelings of envy — ‘not because he got into Stanford, but because he knew what he wanted to do and is now able to do it.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where to begin? Should I simply tell you my name? Or maybe where I’m from? Perhaps I should just talk, and see where that takes us. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Undecided, Without One College Choice ‘Ranked Above All Others’', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T10:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Solis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e861e37-377c-55d1-b618-7dda72824b6d', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e861e37-377c-55d1-b618-7dda72824b6d'}, {'abstract': 'Coffee culture has slowly worked its way to the German capital, and the selection of specialty roasts offered in Berlin is more varied than ever, thanks to an influx of expat-run cafes. Here’s a survey of three of them.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/berlin-imports-coffee-and-its-culture/', 'snippet': 'Coffee culture has slowly worked its way to the German capital, and the selection of specialty roasts offered in Berlin is more varied than ever, thanks to an influx of expat-run cafes. Here’s a survey of three of them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s second largest importer of coffee, Germany has embraced more than just beans. Coffee culture has slowly worked its way to the German capital, and the selection of specialty roasts offered in Berlin is more varied than ever, thanks to an influx of expat-run cafes. Following are three coffee entrepreneurs who give the traditional afternoon kaffee\xa0and\xa0kuchen\xa0new verve.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Berlin Imports Coffee, and Its Culture', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Hannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a01a938b-33d2-567a-abe2-23c29b464cc6', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a01a938b-33d2-567a-abe2-23c29b464cc6'}, {'abstract': 'House Republicans agreed to a temporary payroll tax cut extension on Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/the-early-word-concession/', 'snippet': 'House Republicans agreed to a temporary payroll tax cut extension on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Concession', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f21a221d-eda3-5045-9cc5-d31d3acfac44', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f21a221d-eda3-5045-9cc5-d31d3acfac44'}, {'abstract': 'The roaster, reportedly one of the first three Corvettes ever sold, is expected to attract a price from $450,000 to $650,000.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/corvette-chassis-no-5-heads-to-auction-in-arizona/', 'snippet': 'The roaster, reportedly one of the first three Corvettes ever sold, is expected to attract a price from $450,000 to $650,000.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Crude” might be a charitable word to characterize the early Chevrolet Corvette. The first few were actually hand-built in a garage in Flint, Mich., and the fit and finish of those early 1953 models, as one Chevrolet engineer remarked shortly after its construction, was “not commercially acceptable.” Yet the fiberglass-body cars were pushed onto dealers, who in some cases apologetically offered them at discounted prices.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corvette Chassis No. 5 Heads to Auction in Arizona', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phoenix (Ariz)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8dfd2099-c3d9-50d0-a395-94571d8d60a2', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8dfd2099-c3d9-50d0-a395-94571d8d60a2'}, {'abstract': 'Eli Manning and Mark Sanchez are down in the dumps as Christmas Eve approaches. Fans are a little fed up with both. But can you imagine a world where neither of them had even been born?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/week-15-matchups-its-a-wonderful-game/', 'snippet': 'Eli Manning and Mark Sanchez are down in the dumps as Christmas Eve approaches. Fans are a little fed up with both. But can you imagine a world where neither of them had even been born?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Giants (7-7) at Jets (8-6)Saturday, 1 p.m.Line: Jets by 3 ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 16 Matchups: It’s a Wonderful Game', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Tanier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb682f3d-23b0-5e47-a177-2f5dc2837abd', 'word_count': 2363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb682f3d-23b0-5e47-a177-2f5dc2837abd'}, {'abstract': 'For Clare Tiarsmith of Atlanta, a wish list of colleges is tempered by the concern that her parents might have to work “until they’re 90″ to make her dreams come true. “Which isn’t as appealing as retirement in Florida,” she adds.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/clare-tiarsmith/', 'snippet': 'For Clare Tiarsmith of Atlanta, a wish list of colleges is tempered by the concern that her parents might have to work “until they’re 90″ to make her dreams come true. “Which isn’t as appealing as retirement in Florida,” she adds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My name is Clare Tiarsmith, and I am a high school senior from Atlanta.\xa0 For the past several months, my life has been filled with college visits, college applications and college meetings; in other words, my life along with every other senior’s has been filled with college, college, college.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Budding Psychology Major Seeks to Help Veterans with Post-Traumatic Stress', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clare Tiarsmith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clare', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tiarsmith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff3520dd-da1b-5583-8020-37189131566e', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff3520dd-da1b-5583-8020-37189131566e'}, {'abstract': 'The much-discussed Wyden-Ryan plan offers no new vision for containing health-care costs and relies on unproven hyptheses, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/the-wyden-ryan-plan-deja-vu-all-over-again/', 'snippet': 'The much-discussed Wyden-Ryan plan offers no new vision for containing health-care costs and relies on unproven hyptheses, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uwe E. Reinhardt is an economics professor at Princeton. He has some financial interests in the health care field.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wyden-Ryan Plan: Déjà Vu All Over Again', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wyden, Ron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uwe E. Reinhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uwe', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Reinhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/654b70e8-f775-52ca-9401-1bc4c51c0b57', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/654b70e8-f775-52ca-9401-1bc4c51c0b57'}, {'abstract': 'The life of Dennis Ritchie, who developed the C programming language, is briefly recounted in The Lives They Lived edition of the Sunday Times Magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/hello-world-the-inventor-of-c/', 'snippet': 'The life of Dennis Ritchie, who developed the C programming language, is briefly recounted in The Lives They Lived edition of the Sunday Times Magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Hello, world.” Those were the words that appeared on the screen once you had programmed and run the iconic first example in the book “The C Programming Language,” which Dennis Ritchie, the creator of C, co-wrote with Brian Kernighan. In The Lives They Lived edition of the Times Sunday Magazine, the life of Mr. Ritchie is highlighted. Read the entire item in the Magazine. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hello World: The Inventor of C', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T11:47:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe38b31-e2d4-5d13-a651-c4ad1b29d446', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe38b31-e2d4-5d13-a651-c4ad1b29d446'}, {'abstract': 'Turkey halted diplomatic consultations and military dealings with France on Thursday after the lower house of the French Parliament approved legislation about the Armenian genocide in the early 20th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/world/europe/turkey-lashes-out-over-french-bill-about-genocide.html', 'snippet': 'Turkey halted diplomatic consultations and military dealings with France on Thursday after the lower house of the French Parliament approved legislation about the Armenian genocide in the early 20th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — In a deepening diplomatic rupture, Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey accused France on Friday of genocide against Algerians in the period of French colonial rule, one day after France moved ahead with a measure that would make it a crime to deny the Armenian genocide by the Ottoman Turks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/turkey/turkey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/24/world/turkey/turkey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/turkey/turkey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/turkey/turkey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'heigh