oward their shores. Officials report that it is the worst offshore spill there since 1998. [The Guardian]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Nigerian Coast Braces for Oil’s Advance', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T12:47:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69c8a666-3fb5-5704-a60a-a8641fc39caa', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69c8a666-3fb5-5704-a60a-a8641fc39caa'}, {'abstract': 'In 2011, the singer became one of the busiest stars in pop, logging thousands of miles while taping television shows and music videos, recording an album and making personal appearances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/26/business/media/ceelo-timeline.html', 'snippet': 'In 2011, the singer became one of the busiest stars in pop, logging thousands of miles while taping television shows and music videos, recording an album and making personal appearances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2011, the singer became one of the busiest stars in pop, logging thousands of miles while taping television shows and music videos, recording an album and making personal appearances.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Cee Lo Green, a Busy Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T12:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/dbb9002c-1f15-52fe-a885-16c6ba57da23', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/dbb9002c-1f15-52fe-a885-16c6ba57da23'}, {'abstract': 'The city\'s deputy chancellor for portfolio planning explains the decision to recommend the closing of Washington Irving High School. Marc Sternberg wrote: "Our obligation is to our students, and we are not going to set them up for failure by pretending a school will suddenly make progress after years of failing its kids."', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/23/why-washington-irving-high-must-close/', 'snippet': 'The city\'s deputy chancellor for portfolio planning explains the decision to recommend the closing of Washington Irving High School. Marc Sternberg wrote: "Our obligation is to our students, and we are not going to set them up for failure by prete...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Washington Irving High Must Close', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARY ANN GIORDANO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Ann', 'lastname': 'GIORDANO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05de5f21-8a97-5b89-a4eb-1bc66eb16295', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05de5f21-8a97-5b89-a4eb-1bc66eb16295'}, {'abstract': 'Play our crossword to learn more about Hispanic people and culture in the United States, past and present.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/student-crossword-hispanic-heritage/', 'snippet': 'Play our crossword to learn more about Hispanic people and culture in the United States, past and present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Play our crossword (PDF) to learn more about Hispanic people and culture in the United States. You can find the answers here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Crossword | Hispanic Heritage', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:11:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Longo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d12e01f-af2a-57b5-b49e-8aad425080d9', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d12e01f-af2a-57b5-b49e-8aad425080d9'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/morning-take-out-393/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Turkey, Western Companies Find Stability and Growth \xa0|\xa0 Turkey’s split personality has often left it caught between two worlds. Some European nations have vocally opposed the country’s attempts to build closer ties with the West. And many of its Middle Eastern neighbors have been wary of the avidly secular state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:12:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/588653ce-bef2-53bf-9ee5-be35d4a6bfbf', 'word_count': 2059, 'uri': 'nyt://article/588653ce-bef2-53bf-9ee5-be35d4a6bfbf'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/opinion-report-the-payroll-tax-cut-deal/', 'snippet': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the editorial page', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: The Payroll Tax Cut Deal', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:18:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d031be3e-30e3-5e3b-adca-3dca9758ea1f', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d031be3e-30e3-5e3b-adca-3dca9758ea1f'}, {'abstract': 'French chef Daniel Boulud is mixing Indian flavors into French fare.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/indian-flavors-daniel-bouluds-quail-with-garam-masala/', 'snippet': 'French chef Daniel Boulud is mixing Indian flavors into French fare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Daniel Boulud might be famous for his French cooking, but he seems equally taken by Indian flavors. The chef and owner of 13 restaurants including Daniel, his eponymous Michelin three-starred eatery, spent several hours before a recent interview with India Ink creating a green curry using the distinctly Indian black salt and pomegranate powder. His plan is to use the saucy base to enliven rabbit, which is a staple in his native France and can be bland on its own, he said. “I am a huge fan of Indian food and incorporate spices from India into my cooking whenever I can,” he said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indian Flavors: Daniel Boulud’s Quail with Garam Masala', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boulud, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michelin Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:24:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c27c3b81-d6a8-5e46-8a2a-9a1c1eb32290', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c27c3b81-d6a8-5e46-8a2a-9a1c1eb32290'}, {'abstract': 'People connected with books related to new films have a few issues: they’re not happy with “Dragon Tattoo” or “Iron Lady.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/what-do-dragon-tattoo-and-iron-lady-have-in-common-ask-the-grousers/', 'snippet': 'People connected with books related to new films have a few issues: they’re not happy with “Dragon Tattoo” or “Iron Lady.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s time for the holidays and, as ever, it’s time for eggnog recipes and disgruntlement over holiday movies. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do ‘Dragon Tattoo’ and ‘Iron Lady’ Have in Common? Ask the Grousers', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fincher, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gabrielsson, Eva', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larsson, Stieg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mara, Rooney', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thatcher, Margaret H', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdc05c87-1575-5b8d-85ac-562b3335ccd2', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdc05c87-1575-5b8d-85ac-562b3335ccd2'}, {'abstract': 'Debate is still dominated by ideas that have been proved wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/survival-of-the-wrongest/', 'snippet': 'Debate is still dominated by ideas that have been proved wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeff Madrick has a good post on the 10 worst economic ideas of 2011. What continues to fascinate me — in a bad way — is how economic debate continues to be dominated by ideas that (1) are in conflict with textbook macroeconomics and (2) have led to bad predictions every step of the way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Survival of the Wrongest', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T13:40:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': N