he Brighton slopes and a visit to a whiskey distillery in Park City, Utah.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/great-escape-snowed-in/', 'snippet': 'The stylist Marina Muñoz on her holiday vacation plans — skiing on the Brighton slopes and a visit to a whiskey distillery in Park City, Utah.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Great Escape | Snowed In', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b6079ec-c9da-59bb-ad8c-a5abcc87fd39', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b6079ec-c9da-59bb-ad8c-a5abcc87fd39'}, {'abstract': 'Iggy Azalea, Kreayshawn and K.Flay are among a handful of white female rappers who want to be taken seriously in a musical genre that has always been about the ethos of black men.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/arts/music/white-female-rappers-challenging-hip-hops-masculine-ideal.html', 'snippet': 'Iggy Azalea, Kreayshawn and K.Flay are among a handful of white female rappers who want to be taken seriously in a musical genre that has always been about the ethos of black men.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HIP-HOP is primarily a celebration of black masculinity. Sure, there have long been significant black female and white male figures, but the majority of the conversation in hip-hop is and has always been about the actions, thoughts, feelings and ethos of black men. But this hegemony cannot last forever. Eventually the throne will have to be shared. The world of hip-hop has some diversity: Eminem, Mac Miller and Nicki Minaj now; the Beastie Boys, Lauryn Hill and Missy Elliott in the past. We have respected rappers of South Asian descent: M.I.A. and Heems from Das Racist. But what about the American white woman? Could she ever rock the mic for real? ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25GIRL2_SPAN/25GIRL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25GIRL2_SPAN/25GIRL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25GIRL2_SPAN/25GIRL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25GIRL2_SPAN/25GIRL2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25GIRL2_SPAN/25GIRL2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenging Hip-Hop’s Masculine Ideal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Challenging Hip-Hop’s Masculine Ideal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kreayshawn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:30:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By TOURÉ', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Touré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a26c13f-436b-5eb7-9057-a12146f78b0e', 'word_count': 1678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a26c13f-436b-5eb7-9057-a12146f78b0e'}, {'abstract': 'A new season of “The Donna Reed Show” is being released on DVD, giving viewers a look back at what seemed like a simpler, more prosperous epoch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/arts/television/a-new-season-of-the-donna-reed-show-on-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'A new season of “The Donna Reed Show” is being released on DVD, giving viewers a look back at what seemed like a simpler, more prosperous epoch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO baby boomers of a certain age Donna Reed was a sunny-bright television fixture for eight seasons, the perfect mom — at least according to the prevailing values of the day — radiantly gliding down the staircase every week to answer the beckoning phone, its ringing augmented by an impossibly perky musical theme fittingly titled “Happy Days.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25DONNA2_SPAN/25DONNA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/arts/25DONNA2_SPAN/25DONNA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 