919-c4de-5eda-8a50-a55545fcb317', 'word_count': 1784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16888919-c4de-5eda-8a50-a55545fcb317'}, {'abstract': 'Both Zynga and Groupon have faced a number of bumps in the road after their initial public offerings. So why is the deals company faring better in the marketplace than the virtual farm maker?', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/groupons-real-deals-trump-zyngas-virtual-sheep/', 'snippet': 'Both Zynga and Groupon have faced a number of bumps in the road after their initial public offerings. So why is the deals company faring better in the marketplace than the virtual farm maker?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This holiday season, investors seem to prefer real deals to virtual sheep.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Groupon’s Real Deals Trump Zynga’s Virtual Sheep', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Groupon Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zynga Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfa6fec7-0b2e-5711-b554-7de2365d1615', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfa6fec7-0b2e-5711-b554-7de2365d1615'}, {'abstract': 'Students say no to healthier school fare, House Republicans back temporary payroll tax extension, retailers slash prices ahead of the holiday and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/friday-reading-students-say-no-to-healthier-school-fare/', 'snippet': 'Students say no to healthier school fare, House Republicans back temporary payroll tax extension, retailers slash prices ahead of the holiday and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Students Say No to Healthier School Fare', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:40:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/231fc86c-ed22-5dd8-af59-be52c465e6fd', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/231fc86c-ed22-5dd8-af59-be52c465e6fd'}, {'abstract': 'Like early medieval monks, we too are prone to the ills that come with solitary, sedentary, cerebral work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/their-noonday-demons-and-ours.html', 'snippet': 'Like early medieval monks, we too are prone to the ills that come with solitary, sedentary, cerebral work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By some miracle, you set aside a day to tackle that project you can’t seem to finish in the office. You close the door, boot up your laptop, open the right file and . . . five minutes later catch yourself thinking about dinner. By 10 a.m., you’re staring at the wall, even squinting at it between your fingertips. Is this day 50 hours long? Soon, you fall into a light, unsatisfying sleep and awake dizzy or with a pounding headache; all your limbs feel weighed down. At which point, most likely around noon, you commit a fatal error: leaving the room. I’ll just garden for a bit, you tell yourself, or do a little charity work. Hmmm, I wonder if my friend Gregory is around?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Plutz/Plutz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Plutz/Plutz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Plutz/Plutz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Their Noonday Demons, and Ours', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Their Noonday Demons, and Ours', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monasteries and Monks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Plotz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plotz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81dc1da7-c96b-5802-ad87-5bdda87a883b', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81dc1da7-c96b-5802-ad87-5bdda87a883b'}, {'abstract': 'A German vacationer and a South American burn victim engage in a war strategy game in Roberto Bolaño’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/the-third-reich-by-roberto-bolano-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A German vacationer and a South American burn victim engage in a war strategy game in Roberto Bolaño’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roberto\xa0Bolaño is one of the most prolific of dead writers: some half a dozen books in Spanish and at least twice that in English since his death (at the age of 50) in 2003. Of course he didn’t write these works in the afterlife, even if such a feat is no doubt every author’s fantasy; the bulk of the posthumous material is drawn from the beginning and middle of his career, before the Spanish publication of “The Savage Detectives” in 1998 turned him into an international phenomenon. The amount of unpublished work tells a story. We read these previously missing early texts differently because we have the later ones. It is also possible that the early work will help us with the later work. (This is likely to be true of the still untranslated “Sinsabores del Verdadero Policía,” published in Spanish this year, which Bolaño was working on from the 1980s to his death. Literally, a sinsabor is something that lacks savor, but idiomatically the word means a minor unpleasantness — a fluent literary version of the title might be “The Troubles of the True Policeman.”)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 733}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 839, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1637, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Wood/Wood-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roberto Bolaño Plays With History', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Playing With History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bolano, Roberto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:48:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/372122da-53ee-55b3-bb32-e2a7e61fe810', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/372122da-53ee-55b3-bb32-e2a7e61fe810'}, {'abstract': 'In search of enlightenment, Eric Weiner explores eight faiths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/man-seeks-god-by-eric-weiner-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In search of enlightenment, Eric Weiner explores eight faiths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Books about God tend to fall into two categories: objective inquiries into the nature of belief and personal tales of spiritual awakening. One type explores history, creation myths and religious ritual. In the other, the author typically undergoes a crisis — a terminal illness, the death of a loved one, an onset of existential dread — that causes him to confront his life’s emptiness, coming to realize that there is something out there greater than himself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Hammer/Hammer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 269, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Hammer/Hammer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Hammer/Hammer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding My Religion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding My Religion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiner, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:49:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/71d68c0d-367a-588e-b35d-a516a6705eb4', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71d68c0d-367a-588e-b35d-a516a6705eb4'}, {'abstract': 'An evolutionary biologist investigates why lying and self-deception play such prominent roles in our lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/the-folly-of-fools-by-robert-trivers-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'An evolutionary biologist investigates why lying and self-deception play such prominent roles in our lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1995 I traveled to the University of California, Santa Barbara, for the annual meeting of the Human Behavior and Evolution Society, which turned out to be a pep rally for psychologists, anthropologists and others who view humanity through the lens of evolutionary theory. Attendees heard Darwinian takes on lust, love, infidelity, status-seeking, mental illness, violence, patriotism, politics, economics and religion, as well as keynote addresses from such luminaries as Richard Dawkins and Steven Pinker. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBHORGAN/25SUBHORGAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBHORGAN/25SUBHORGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBHORGAN/25SUBHORGAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why We Lie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Duped', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Folly of Fools (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trivers, Robert L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:51:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Horgan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horgan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86956d71-2a59-5cbf-8b2e-4d01a5a0fc7e', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86956d71-2a59-5cbf-8b2e-4d01a5a0fc7e'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir and meditation on the experience of insomnia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/nothing-a-portrait-of-insomnia-by-blake-butler-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir and meditation on the experience of insomnia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to ignore the force of the sentences in Blake Butler’s new book, “Nothing: a Portrait of Insomnia.” Best known for his fiction, Butler is obsessed with the possibilities of syntax, and the most obvious feature of “Nothing” is a lyric and intellectual buffer overflow that results in long, often interestingly ungrammatical sentences, sometimes stretching over six pages. The most ornate of these is adorned with footnotes, a nod to David Foster Wallace, to whose memory the book is dedicated. As such the book draws attention to its own linguistic surfaces in ways that most memoirs never attempt.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Monson/Monson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 801, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Monson/Monson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Monson/Monson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Insomnia Feels', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up All Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butler, Blake', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insomnia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:53:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ander Monson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96bd50e3-615b-59cd-9667-ab16f1cdb93e', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96bd50e3-615b-59cd-9667-ab16f1cdb93e'}, {'abstract': 'Ashley Parker reports from New Hampshire on the week with Mitt Romney as the campaign shows its organizational might.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/politics/100000001242847/the-caucus--romney-campaign-ramps-up.html', 'snippet': 'Ashley Parker reports from New Hampshire on the week with Mitt Romney as the campaign shows its organizational might.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ashley Parker reports from New Hampshire on the week with Mitt Romney as the campaign shows its organizational might.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/us/politics/video-caucus-111223-romney/video-caucus-111223-romney-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/23/us/politics/video-caucus-111223-romney/video-caucus-111223-romney-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/us/politics/video-caucus-111223-romney/video-caucus-111223-romney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/23/us/politics/video-caucus-111223-romney/video-caucus-111223-romney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caucus | Romney Campaign Ramps Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:53:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/7b45b04e-f382-5cb4-b68c-13bd4b0a449d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/7b45b04e-f382-5cb4-b68c-13bd4b0a449d'}, {'abstract': 'Never mind Voltaire. The Enlightenment’s more radical thinkers set the world on its modern course, Jonathan I. Israel argues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/democratic-enlightenment-by-jonathan-i-israel-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Never mind Voltaire. The Enlightenment’s more radical thinkers set the world on its modern course, Jonathan I. Israel argues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent decades, Jonathan I. Israel writes, the Enlightenment has emerged as “the single most important topic, internationally, in modern historical studies, and one of crucial significance also in our politics, cultural studies and philosophy.” That is a large claim for a movement of 18th-century thought, and many will find it exaggerated, if not self-serving, seeing that its author, a professor of history at the Institute for Advanced Study in Prince\xadton, N.J., has devoted the last decade of his life to exploring that very subject.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25MCMAHON1/25MCMAHON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 576, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25MCMAHON1/25MCMAHON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25MCMAHON1/25MCMAHON1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Enlightenment’s True Radicals', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Artillery of Words', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Israel, Jonathan I', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Democratic Enlightenment (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Darrin M. McMahon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Darrin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0812123-43ec-5d9a-9f21-e8c7b47054c3', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0812123-43ec-5d9a-9f21-e8c7b47054c3'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Nelson, an early arbiter of rock, championed the myth of the macho outsider.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/everything-is-an-afterthought-the-life-and-writings-of-paul-nelson-by-kevin-avery-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Nelson, an early arbiter of rock, championed the myth of the macho outsider.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Nelson, a propagandist committed to some dubious values, had a gift for imbuing disreputable, even dangerous ideas with discomforting grace. You might almost say he was the Leni Riefenstahl of rock criticism. One of the first writers of the post-Elvis era to take the popular music of his time seriously, he never liked black music and thought the blues were overrated, negligible in comparison with the work of overtly cerebral and conspicuously poetic artists of the ’60s and early ’70s like Bob Dylan and Jackson Browne. Nelson revered Browne. He subscribed, both in his writing and in his life, to the macho outcast myths of noir movies and pulp fiction, and he seemed blind to the importance of the great female artists nearly absent in his writing, like Joni Mitchell and Aretha Franklin. Nelson loathed the music of Patti Smith.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25HAJDU/25HAJDU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25HAJDU/25HAJDU-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25HAJDU/25HAJDU-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paul Nelson: Bad Boy Rock Critic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Boy as Critic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:57:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Hajdu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hajdu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f140d1ce-e687-5c90-a32e-3bd0daab495a', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f140d1ce-e687-5c90-a32e-3bd0daab495a'}, {'abstract': 'The producers of “Stromberg,” a television comedy that is Germany’s answer to “The Office,” say they have raised about $1.3 million in an online fundraising campaign for a movie adaptation of the show.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/five-seasons-and-a-movie-fans-raise-money-for-german-office-film/', 'snippet': 'The producers of “Stromberg,” a television comedy that is Germany’s answer to “The Office,” say they have raised about $1.3 million in an online fundraising campaign for a movie adaptation of the show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While devotees of the struggling sitcom “Community” gather in Rockefeller Center and don fake beards in hopes of convincing NBC to keep that show on the air, fans of Germany’s equivalent of “The Office” are actually reaching into their pockets to see the series continue: They have raised about $1.3 million in an online campaign to produce a movie based on the German show, which is currently in its fifth and final season, The Hollywood Reporter said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Seasons and a Movie: Fans Raise Money for German ‘Office’ Film', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gervais, Ricky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merchant, Stephen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:57:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f75da2f5-229c-5417-90b2-e9ac9d99a15c', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f75da2f5-229c-5417-90b2-e9ac9d99a15c'}, {'abstract': 'Ashley Parker reports from Mitt Romney’s New Hampshire bus tour about the campaign showing its organizational might.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/caucus-video-the-romney-campaign-ramps-up/', 'snippet': 'Ashley Parker reports from Mitt Romney’s New Hampshire bus tour about the campaign showing its organizational might.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ashley Parker reports from Mitt Romney’s New Hampshire bus tour about the campaign showing its organizational might.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Caucus Video: The Romney Campaign Ramps Up', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:57:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03b7473e-bdda-5947-b498-9dfa930081c0', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03b7473e-bdda-5947-b498-9dfa930081c0'}, {'abstract': 'The diaries of an acute observer of turn-of-the-century Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/journey-to-the-abyss-the-diaries-of-count-harry-kessler-1880-1918-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The diaries of an acute observer of turn-of-the-century Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Count Harry Kessler (1868-1937), whose diaries provide an unusual guided tour of belle époque and early-20th-century artistic and high life in Berlin, Paris and London, was born in Paris to great wealth. A lifetime of extravagant spending and the Depression took their toll. After a long and painful disease, he died impoverished and solitary in Pontanevaux, a village in the Beaujolais region of France.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25BEGLEY/25BEGLEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25BEGLEY/25BEGLEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25BEGLEY/25BEGLEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harry Kessler’s Fin de Siècle Diaries', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harry Kessler’s Fin de Siècle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kessler, Harry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T14:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louis Begley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Begley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33e2b8a1-4afb-5f64-bc98-f8faa597cfad', 'word_count': 1198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33e2b8a1-4afb-5f64-bc98-f8faa597cfad'}, {'abstract': 'A number of factors can affect a browser’s ability to display a PDF (Portable Document Format) file within its window.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/qa-trouble-shooting-browser-problems-with-pdf-files/', 'snippet': 'A number of facto