at emphasize its musical roots, including performances by Drake and Guns N’ Roses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Celebrating New Year’s Eve in Las Vegas isn’t a novel concept, but the Hard Rock Hotel & Casino Las Vegas is offering a fresh way to ring in 2012. The hotel has scheduled three days of events, beginning on Dec. 30, that emphasize its musical roots. The hip-hop star Drake, the alternative singer Corey Taylor, the heavy metal band Guns N’ Roses and a popular local band, Sin City Sinners, are all scheduled to perform.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hard Rock in Las Vegas Plans a Three-Day New Year’s Celebration', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drake', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Guns N' Roses", 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hard Rock Hotel and Casino', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bad55696-963d-56d1-9d8e-230456babf56', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bad55696-963d-56d1-9d8e-230456babf56'}, {'abstract': 'In this sequel to “The Hummingbird’s Daughter,” a Mexican saint wanders the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/queen-of-america-by-luis-alberto-urrea-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this sequel to “The Hummingbird’s Daughter,” a Mexican saint wanders the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Luis Alberto Urrea spent nearly 20 years researching his family history for his enchanting 2005 novel “The Hummingbird’s Daughter.” Out of old letters, historical documents and oral histories emerged the fantastical story of the author’s great-aunt Teresa, a Mexican saint and revolutionary who was the illegitimate daughter of Tomás Urrea, a wealthy landowner, and a Yaqui Indian woman known simply as Cayetana, or the Hummingbird. By the final wrenching pages of that novel, Teresita, as she’s called, has by presidential decree been declared the Most Dangerous Girl in Mexico. Banished from the country, she and her father are put on a train headed north to the United States.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBRAO/25SUBRAO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 911, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBRAO/25SUBRAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25SUBRAO/25SUBRAO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Most Dangerous Girl in Mexico Goes to America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North of the Border', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Urrea, Luis Alberto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mythili G. Rao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mythili', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Rao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dec5e7c-887c-5e88-86ba-69920432467e', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dec5e7c-887c-5e88-86ba-69920432467e'}, {'abstract': 'By pairing stimulus and spending cuts, lawmakers give with one hand and take away with another.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/paying-for-the-payroll-tax-cut/', 'snippet': 'By pairing stimulus and spending cuts, lawmakers give with one hand and take away with another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So House Republicans have decided to go along with a two-month extension of the payroll tax cut.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paying for the Payroll Tax Cut', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:01:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce52fa7e-dc8e-595f-83a8-8cea54d3375f', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce52fa7e-dc8e-595f-83a8-8cea54d3375f'}, {'abstract': 'The ex-cop Moe Prager confronts cancer and the murder of his ex-wife’s sister in Reed Farrel Coleman’s “Hurt Machine.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/last-exits-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'The ex-cop Moe Prager confronts cancer and the murder of his ex-wife’s sister in Reed Farrel Coleman’s “Hurt Machine.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nobody knows a man better than his ex-wife. So Moe Prager’s ex-wife, Carmella, is wise to this veteran private eye, accepting the fact that no matter how many times he marries, his first love will always be Brooklyn. “When you die, they should just bury you right here, under the boardwalk,” she tells him in HURT MACHINE (Tyrus, $24.95; paper, $15.95), Reed Farrel Coleman’s latest book in a series heavily saturated with local color. Since Prager has recently been told he has stomach cancer, that day may come sooner than Carmella thinks. But this stubborn old shamus is determined to do two things before his ashes are consigned to the sands of Coney Island: Attend his daughter’s wedding, and find the person who murdered Carmella’s older sister, Alta.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 721, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/14/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/14/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Exits in Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Exits in Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holt, Anne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Hurt Machine (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finch, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coleman, Reed Farrel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haas, Derek', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:02:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b811cac7-8ef3-5148-85de-f0f833d9a21a', 'word_count': 928, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b811cac7-8ef3-5148-85de-f0f833d9a21a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Greil Marcus’s “The Doors.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/the-doors.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Greil Marcus’s “The Doors.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Doors’', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Doors’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:02:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3beeb125-57e0-58f9-8ff3-8af5e84595f8', 'word_count'