dline': 'What It All Means', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:04:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dfc81ea-eeaa-587e-b272-a485c9c10ee2', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dfc81ea-eeaa-587e-b272-a485c9c10ee2'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HEDY’S FOLLY: The Life and Breakthrough Inventions of Hedy Lamarr, the Most Beautiful Woman in the World, by Richard Rhodes (Doubleday, $26.95.) A glamorous movie star and a Hollywood composer designed a sophisticated weapons system. Really.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:06:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/361560dc-3dc3-545a-abcf-36b727d47603', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/361560dc-3dc3-545a-abcf-36b727d47603'}, {'abstract': 'Tom Clancy, whose new novel hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 2 this week, is often described as prescient. But is he really?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Tom Clancy, whose new novel hits the hardcover fiction list at No. 2 this week, is often described as prescient. But is he really?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE USUAL SUSPECTS: Tom Clancy’s fans like to describe his macho geek-boy thrillers as prescient, citing among other things his 1994 novel “Debt of Honor,” in which a deranged pilot crashes a 747 into the Capitol. On Sept. 11, 2001, Clancy himself played up the overlap on CNN, without mentioning that the book is a ludicrous alarmist fantasy about a corporate-controlled Japan making war on America. In other words, it was less prescient than derivative: “Rising Sun,” Michael Crichton’s version of “The Japanese are coming!” had been a best seller two years before. In a telephone interview on “Charlie Rose” later on 9/11, Clancy again seemed out of the loop. As Rose’s panelists talked about Osama bin Laden and the possibility of military retaliation, Clancy jumped in petulantly. “Saying ‘Osama bin Laden,’ ” he insisted, “is like Claude Rains in ‘Casablanca’ saying, ‘Round up the usual suspects.’ He’s simply the usual suspect. But there has been no definitive information linking him to this event. Until there is, talking about him is just a waste of air.” Later on the program, as the former National Security Adviser Samuel Berger tried to answer a question, Clancy’s disembodied voice floated over the set. “Why is there a car in our driveway?” he asked, apparently forgetting he was wired for sound.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25clancy/25clancy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 328, 'width': 273, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25clancy/25clancy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/25clancy/25clancy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside The List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/077beb6c-5a0b-5bc9-ab31-70d03ec9ab60', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/077beb6c-5a0b-5bc9-ab31-70d03ec9ab60'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Wood’s restless imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Wood’s restless imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Wood’s first three books about literature concerned a Frenchman (Stendhal), a Latin American (García Márquez) and a Russian emigrant to America (Nabokov). If this sounds like the start of a bar joke, it also confirms Wood’s commitment to cultural diversity — as does the fact that between Stendhal and García Márquez, he also found time for a book about film, “America in the Movies.” In a recent e-mail, Wood recalled that when Wilfrid Sheed reviewed that book in 1976, he “said I had a grasshopper mind. It wasn’t a compliment but it’s not untrue, and one can make some sort of virtue even out of bad habits.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/25/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a8a128-15e9-563f-8ac1-37e506ccaeaf', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a8a128-15e9-563f-8ac1-37e506ccaeaf'}, {'abstract': 'The trustee liquidating MF Global’s trading operations said that his office was seeking the return of more than $600 million from the failed firm’s British arm, potentially setting up a cross-border fight over customer money.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/mf-global-brokerage-trustee-seeks-funds-from-british-arm/', 'snippet': 'The trustee liquidating MF Global’s trading operations said that his office was seeking the return of more than $600 million from the failed firm’s British arm, potentially setting up a cross-border fight over customer money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The trustee liquidating MF Global’s trading operations said on Friday that his office was seeking the return of more than $600 million from the failed firm’s British arm, potentially setting up a cross-border fight over customer money.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MF Global Trustee Seeks Money From British Arm', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giddens, James W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KPMG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MF Global Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1cd19ef-19b6-563b-84fe-d5c933d18fdf', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1cd19ef-19b6-563b-84fe-d5c933d18fdf'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/25/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GATHERING EVIDENCE: A Memoir, by Thomas Bernhard. Translated by David McLintock (Vintage International, $16.95.) Bernhard, the Austrian playwright and novelist who died in 1989, describes his harrowing childhood in Nazi-dominated schools and the bouts of pneumonia and tuberculosis that shaped his lifelong obsession with death. This volume includes “My Prizes,” translated by Carol Brown Janeway, a series of acerbic essays that recall Bernhard’s literary awards and muster moral outrage against the idea of state-sanctioned art.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c1b0d44-2cf9-5e81-bde8-6ced9b5a4363', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c1b0d44-2cf9-5e81-bde8-6ced9b5a4363'}, {'abstract': 'A full transcript from the third and final live admissions chat on Facebook put on this week by the Choice blog, in collaboration with the Association for College Counselors in Independent Schools.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/choice-facebook-3/', 'snippet': 'A full transcript from the third and final live admissions chat on Facebook put on this week by the Choice blog, in collaboration with the Association for College Counselors in Independent Schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday night, Jennifer Wong Christensen, the co-director of college counseling at Marin Academy in San Rafael, Calif., and the former director of admission at Claremont McKenna College, concluded the third installment in The Choice blog’s series of live chats on our Facebook page. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Recap From the Third (and Final) Night of the Choice Blog’s Live Chat on Facebook', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:14:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b718330-5d8f-5a90-908a-929267ff32a9', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b718330-5d8f-5a90-908a-929267ff32a9'}, {'abstract': 'This week, a conversation with John Horgan about “The Folly of Fools” by Robert Trivers; Christopher Beha on a number of new books about our relationship to animals; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2011/12/23/books/23bookreview_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, a conversation with John Horgan about “The Folly of Fools” by Robert Trivers; Christopher Beha on a number of new books about our relationship to animals; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. S...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, a conversation with John Horgan about “The Folly of Fools” by Robert Trivers; Christopher Beha on a number of new books about our relationship to animals; Julie Bosman has notes from the field; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John Horgan on Robert Trivers’s “The Folly of Fools” and best-seller news.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:18:43+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/dec1eb9d-fc98-5c92-8246-8ea0ad708f56', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/dec1eb9d-fc98-5c92-8246-8ea0ad708f56'}, {'abstract': 'The final installment of a series following a Halloween tradition in Cobble Hill, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/farewell-pumpkins-of-kane-street-they-did-not/', 'snippet': 'The final installment of a series following a Halloween tradition in Cobble Hill, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We bid adieu to the pumpkins of Kane Street, an array of  100 carved by Jane Greengold of Cobble Hill, Brooklyn that have become a local Halloween tradition. The horrific decay of the pumpkins has reached a tipping point on the scales of good taste so rigorously upheld by City Room. This will be our final installment of the series.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Farewell, Pumpkins of Kane Street? They Did Not.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Halloween', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pumpkins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cobble Hill (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:24:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kolomatsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kolomatsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91968f04-ad66-531c-b9fd-47c3ab9f22dc', 'word_count': 62, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91968f04-ad66-531c-b9fd-47c3ab9f22dc'}, {'abstract': 'After spending most of this year sticking to his message on the economy, Mitt Romney enters a new phase of his presidential campaign during which he can no longer be the remote front-runner.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/for-romney-a-new-phase-of-the-campaign/', 'snippet': 'After spending most of this year sticking to his message on the economy, Mitt Romney enters a new phase of his presidential campaign during which he can no longer be the remote front-runner.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’re going to call this ‘Hair Force One,’” Ann Romney said, eyeing her husband’s impeccably coiffed black hair and teasing: “In honor of you.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Romney, a New Phase of the Campaign', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:24:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b948b66-36da-51a4-9803-90203c168da9', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b948b66-36da-51a4-9803-90203c168da9'}, {'abstract': 'Owl Creek Asset Management, the hedge fund founded by Jeffrey Altman that survived the financial crisis relatively unscathed, is now suffering, with the flagship portfolio off 13 percent through November.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/once-lofty-owl-creek-falls-13/', 'snippet': 'Owl Creek Asset Management, the hedge fund founded by Jeffrey Altman that survived the financial crisis relatively unscathed, is now suffering, with the flagship portfolio off 13 percent through November.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeffrey Altman, the wealthy founder of Owl Creek Asset Management, belongs to an elite circle of hedge fund managers who survived the financial crisis relatively unscathed. As he told investors in 2008, “We feel very fortunate to have dodged a significant number of bullets, landmines and other incendiary devices during this dramatic year.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Once Lofty Owl Creek Falls 13%', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Owl Creek Asset Management', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T15:39:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cecfdf49-3468-5326-a936-c38edc02f90a', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cecfdf49-3468-5326-a936-c38edc02f90a'}, {'abstract': 'Behind the unsung Dan Orlovsky, the Colts win another game — and could lose the rights to Andrew Luck in the process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/sports/for-the-colts-and-maybe-andrew-luck-a-rewrite.html', 'snippet': 'Behind the unsung Dan Orlovsky, the Colts win another game — and could lose the rights to Andrew Luck in the process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just when you thought you had the script for this N.F.L. season all figured out, things started going awry. The first taste of this was when the Packers, on a mesmerizing march to a perfect season, tripped over the dreadful Chiefs. And now, at the bottom of the standings, the actors are no longer dutifully playing their parts. Somewhere late in the fourth quarter of Thursday night’s game against the Texans, the Colts went full ad-lib on us with a game-winning touchdown drive in the final minute, led by quarterback Dan Orlovsky, who almost no one realized was even a member of the Colts’ cast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/sports/24leadingoff/24leadingoff-jumbo.j