tha Rose Shulman tweaks the taco to feed a holiday crowd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tacos are a favorite quick dish in many homes, but this week Martha Rose Shulman tweaks the taco to feed a holiday crowd. She writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Festive Holiday Tacos', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T16:41:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c35d8f3-ca1f-5a1a-addb-a29563eee214', 'word_count': 337, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c35d8f3-ca1f-5a1a-addb-a29563eee214'}, {'abstract': 'Should a stepmother and her husband fight to see his children on the holidays, or should they let it go?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/parental-quandary-after-a-divorce-who-gets-the-children-for-the-holidays/', 'snippet': 'Should a stepmother and her husband fight to see his children on the holidays, or should they let it go?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all the sticky quandaries in the parenting world, divorce, especially when it comes with questions of custody, must be the stickiest, and it only gets stickier when a new spouse enters the picture. This week’s quandary comes from one such “new spouse” and stepparent. Four years (and two children) after her marriage, she’s not sure if she and her husband have done the right thing by letting her stepchildren spend every holiday with their mother.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Quandary: After a Divorce, Who Gets the Children for the Holidays?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'prin