be chief designer at the Hyundai Design Center in nearby Irvine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christopher Chapman, Designer of BMW X5, to Head Hyundai Studios in California', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ART CENTER COLLEGE OF DESIGN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audi Division of Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bay Area Rapid Transit', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DesignworksUSA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyundai Motor Co', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kia Motors Corp', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mercedes-Benz', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:19:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/137c8e72-292b-5df8-abaf-c07a8d5cf5ea', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/137c8e72-292b-5df8-abaf-c07a8d5cf5ea'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/weekend-fare-73/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Saturday, Christmas Eve, Woks and Lox will celebrate the modern Jewish tradition of eating Chinese food on Christmas. A dinner party, $59 per person, for up to 20 people, will be held from 5 to 7 p.m. at Queens Kickshaw, 40-17 Broadway, in Astoria, Queens. A nosh party will follow, $28 per person, from 7 to 9 p.m., with snacks like Sichuan peppercorn latkes and ginger Sriracha wok-fried bagel strips. Tickets will be on sale until midnight Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b156c23-cfe5-55a4-b147-c8ffceb37b6c', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b156c23-cfe5-55a4-b147-c8ffceb37b6c'}, {'abstract': 'The Giants and Jets play Saturday in a game that could determine playoff chances for both teams.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/even-on-christmas-eve-jets-giants-is-a-hot-ticket/', 'snippet': 'The Giants and Jets play Saturday in a game that could determine playoff chances for both teams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The war of words leading up to the Battle of East Rutherford on Saturday isn’t just hype. The prices of tickets being resold for the Jets-Giants game are near record highs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Even on Christmas Eve, Jets-Giants Is a Hot Ticket', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huff, Sam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:26:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4eca168d-f9a7-5a52-b59c-ad57c196dacf', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4eca168d-f9a7-5a52-b59c-ad57c196dacf'}, {'abstract': 'The consolidation of the airline industry has decreased the variety of airplane designs at the nation’s airports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/24/business/airplane-livery.html', 'snippet': 'The consolidation of the airline industry has decreased the variety of airplane designs at the nation’s airports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The consolidation of the airline industry has decreased the variety of airplane designs at the nation’s airports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing Airline Livery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN AIRLINES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Airlines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Delta Air Lines, Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'US Airways Group Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:28:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3096c849-0a2b-5ae2-9e87-036cd7011287', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3096c849-0a2b-5ae2-9e87-036cd7011287'}, {'abstract': 'Carolina Herrera Baez, who has had a quiet influence in her family’s atelier for most of her life, is finally taking on the role of designer by making a Goya leather bucket tote called the Mini Matryoshka.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/business-is-in-the-bag/', 'snippet': 'Carolina Herrera Baez, who has had a quiet influence in her family’s atelier for most of her life, is finally taking on the role of designer by making a Goya leather bucket tote called the Mini Matryoshka.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carolina Herrera Baez, not to be confused with her mother, the designer Carolina Herrera, has been a quiet influence in her family’s atelier for most of her life. But it isn’t until now that she could call herself a designer. Two years into her post as a consultant for her mother’s CH Carolina Herrera collection, Herrera Baez is introducing her own accessory: the Mini Matryoshka bag — a bucket tote made of Goya leather that comes in seven different Bauhaus-inspired colorblocks, like canary yellow and grass green, or Piet Mondrian-esque red and black. Though there’s much in the capsule that can be credited to her mother — “She has a small purse philosophy,” says Herrera Baez by way of explaining why, for example, the bag is the compact size that it is. “You carry only what is essential” — the bag’s graphic look and feel are entirely original. “Sometimes, great ideas on paper are just that,” says Herrera Baez. “The ability to fine tune an aesthetic in tandem with intuition is a good trait for any designer to have.” Tuck that one in your tote.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Business Is in the Bag', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bf06961-676b-583f-be84-be3b3eb2b700', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bf06961-676b-583f-be84-be3b3eb2b700'}, {'abstract': 'It is one of the most famous images in American history — the painting depicting Washington crossing the Delaware River — but it’s not exactly attached to facts.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/a-famous-painting-meets-its-more-factual-match/', 'snippet': 'It is one of the most famous images in American history — the painting depicting Washington crossing the Delaware River — but it’s not exactly attached to facts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are few images as enduring in American history as the one of General George Washington standing tall, next to the Stars and Stripes, in a rowboat gliding past mini-icebergs as he leads his troops across the Delaware River on Christmas Day 1776 to start a surprise attack on Hessian forces during the Battle of Trenton. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crossing the Delaware, More Accurately', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kunstler, Mort', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leutze, Emanuel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suozzi, Thomas R', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New-York Historical Society', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stars and Stripes', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware River', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da323d18-5006-5f3c-9bc2-5fa886c004a6', 'word_count': 1166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da323d18-5006-5f3c-9bc2-5fa886c004a6'}, {'abstract': 'The television cameras will be there again, but for both the Rangers, Friday night’s game is the focus.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/rangers-flyers-ready-for-more-hbo-close-ups-at-the-garden/', 'snippet': 'The television cameras will be there again, but for both the Rangers, Friday night’s game is the focus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rangers have defeated their local rivals, the Devils and Islanders, this week, but won’t consider the stretch a success unless they beat the Philadelphia Flyers Friday night at Madison Square Garden.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rangers, Flyers Ready for More HBO Close-Ups at the Garden', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biron, Martin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryzgalov, Ilya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dubinsky, Brandon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laviolette, Peter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lundqvist, Henrik', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richards, Brad', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Stars', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Islanders', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Flyers', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:42:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Botta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Botta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1375f444-a377-5249-9067-9c29386bba86', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1375f444-a377-5249-9067-9c29386bba86'}, {'abstract': 'Please submit your favorite really, really short reads by Dec. 29, or mark them with the Twitter handle @reallyreallyshortreads. We’ll announce the winner before the year is up.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/really-really-short-reads-contest/', 'snippet': 'Please submit your favorite really, really short reads by Dec. 29, or mark them with the Twitter handle @reallyreallyshortreads. We’ll announce the winner before the year is up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve officially entered that part of the year when people feel the need to rank everything. And that includes really long magazine articles. So those of us at the One-Page Magazine (you know, that brand-spanking- new, extremely cool, funny first page of The Times Magazine) decided to inaugurate our own list of the year’s best. We’re calling it really, really short reads.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Really, Really Short Reads Contest', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outdoor Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:44:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Kelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba38618b-af0e-5080-b99c-da1cc5617ca0', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba38618b-af0e-5080-b99c-da1cc5617ca0'}, {'abstract': 'The colorful paint jobs on airplanes have gone the way of free meals and pillows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/business/on-the-exterior-of-jetliners-a-parade-of-plain-vanilla.html', 'snippet': 'The colorful paint jobs on airplanes have gone the way of free meals and pillows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Airplanes have become just as bland on the outside as they are on the inside.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/business/paint-span/paint-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 192, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/20/business/paint-span/paint-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 192}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/business/paint-span/paint-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 327, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/20/business/paint-span/paint-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/20/business/paint-span/paint-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Jet Exteriors, a Parade of Vanilla', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Jet Exteriors, A Parade Of Vanilla', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paint', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Color', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:44:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jad Mouawad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mouawad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47ddcce9-5cdc-5c1f-898a-142b0ff7b5ff', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47ddcce9-5cdc-5c1f-898a-142b0ff7b5ff'}, {'abstract': 'Marilynne Robinson on the Bible’s influence on Western literature and more.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/book-review-podcast-what-literature-owes-the-bible/', 'snippet': 'Marilynne Robinson on the Bible’s influence on Western literature and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this Sunday’s Book Review, the Pulitzer Prize-winning novelist Marilynne Robinson considers the debt that Western literature owes to the Bible. Ms. Robinson writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: What Literature Owes the Bible', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bible', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Marilynne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:45:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e33bc2ba-9e1d-5f41-923e-6c8299c3d1ae', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e33bc2ba-9e1d-5f41-923e-6c8299c3d1ae'}, {'abstract': 'The owner of JoMart Chocolates proudly calls himself “the son of a son of a candy maker,” and the shop’s employees are family, or treated like it.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/neighborhood-joint-jomart-chocolates/', 'snippet': 'The owner of JoMart Chocolates proudly calls himself “the son of a son of a candy maker,” and the shop’s employees are family, or treated like it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With peanut butter fudge truffles, raspberry caramels and peppermint bark vying for her attention, Lorna Fontana, 12, had a difficult decision to make as she pressed her face to the glass counter at JoMart Chocolates like a kid, well, in a candy store.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Family Tree Just Covered in Chocolate', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marine Park (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T17:54:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Devan Sipher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Devan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sipher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee3a9edb-1a4e-5ced-9fae-3be1c64c46de', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee3a9edb-1a4e-5ced-9fae-3be1c64c46de'}, {'abstract': 'Pakistan lurched between crises, with its military chief, Gen. Ashfaq Parvez Kayani, dismissing talk of an attempt to seize power and canceling a visit from a top American general.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/world/asia/pakistan-military-seeking-to-quash-rumors-denies-conspiracy-to-seize-power.html', 'snippet': 'Pakistan lurched between crises, with its military chief, Gen. Ashfaq Parvez Kayani, dismissing talk of an attempt to seize power and canceling a visit from a top American general.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — Pakistan lurched between crises on Friday with its military chief, Gen. Ashfaq Parvez Kayani, dismissing talk of a coup and canceling a visit from a top American general, a sign that Pakistani fury at the United States over airstrikes that killed 26 soldiers is far from abating.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/asia/24pakistanALT/24pakistanALT-jumbo.jp