d it all culminated a few weeks ago, in Professional Day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2011/12/23/on-a-college-path-and-traveling-together/', 'snippet': 'With final deadlines for many college applications approaching, many students are scrambling to finalize their packages. Students at the <a href="http://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/school/976-high-school-of-telecommunication-arts-and-technology">Hi...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On a College Path, and Traveling Together', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T20:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By PHIL WEINBERG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'WEINBERG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74edbebf-00e5-529c-b20f-08451a697a5a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74edbebf-00e5-529c-b20f-08451a697a5a'}, {'abstract': 'A few words about The Choice blog’s school vacation.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/a-short-winter-break-on-the-choice/', 'snippet': 'A few words about The Choice blog’s school vacation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many of you, The Choice blog is going to take a few days off to spend time with family during the holidays. We’ll still publish any big admissions news should it break — and we’ll keep updating our early-admission acceptances chart, in the event any more data flows in. We’ve also got at least one essay scheduled for the week after Christmas, intended to provide some holiday food-for-thought for seniors who might be contemplating a gap year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Short Winter Break on The Choice', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T20:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacques Steinberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacques', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d61d93fe-cb1f-5ae2-98f3-57c0298cab86', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d61d93fe-cb1f-5ae2-98f3-57c0298cab86'}, {'abstract': 'The company will close Jan. 1 due to financial problems.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/opera-boston-to-shut-down/', 'snippet': 'The company will close Jan. 1 due to financial problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opera Boston, a small company known for innovative works, is shutting down because of financial problems, the board said in a statement on Friday, according to news media in Boston. The company this season had scheduled three productions, Berlioz’s “Be’atrice and Be’ne’dict” in October and Tippett’s “The Midsummer Marriage” and Bellini’s “I Capuleti e I Montecchi” in the winter and spring. The company commissioned the opera “Madame White Snake” from the composer Zhou Long, who was given the 2011 Pulitzer Prize in music for the work. The company faced an “insurmountable budget deficit,” the board said. “We find ourselves in a financially untenable situation, and the responsible thing is to work with our creditors and cease operations.” The company will close Jan. 1. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opera Boston to Shut Down', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T20:11:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/239bbe9a-e52d-5ee7-9b82-4612ff070e01', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/239bbe9a-e52d-5ee7-9b82-4612ff070e01'}, {'abstract': 'The show’s ratings may have been disappointing after all the hype, but recent corporate filings detail a deal with Sony that generated a large payment for its creator and star.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/x-factor-leads-to-42-million-payday-for-simon-cowell/', 'snippet': 'The show’s ratings may have been disappointing after all the hype, but recent corporate filings detail a deal with Sony that generated a large payment for its creator and star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For one of the most hyped television shows of the year, “The X Factor” may have had disappointing ratings, but the show still turned out to be a big payday for Simon Cowell, its creator.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘X Factor’ Leads to $42 Million Payday for Simon Cowell', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowell, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-23T20:11:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'origin