eak of demonstrations outside the capital since the current wave of protests against the ruling military council began Nov. 18. Several thousand protesters gathered in Tahrir Square in Cairo, and significant protests were reported in Alexandria, Suez, Luxor and elsewhere. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/egypt/egypt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/world/egypt/egypt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/24/world/egypt/egypt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Protests Against Military Spread Beyond Cairo to New Cities', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Protests Against Military Spread Beyond Cairo to New Cities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-24T01:37:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4510588-907e-5fc6-b050-cd708c3a3dd1', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4510588-907e-5fc6-b050-cd708c3a3dd1'}, {'abstract': 'By competing to be the “most conservative,” the Republican candidates diminish their appeal to a broad electorate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/opinion/the-republican-candidates-race-to-the-right.html', 'snippet': 'By competing to be the “most conservative,” the Republican candidates diminish their appeal to a broad electorate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The toxic effects of right-wing extremism in Washington were vividly on display during the payroll-tax fiasco — even to the right wing. On the campaign trail, though, those lessons are being ignored. The leading Republican presidential candidates are overtly competing for the title of Most Conservative, distorting their own records and advocating increasingly radical positions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Race to the Right', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Race to the Right', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-24T01:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec898d84-8ba1-5f4d-9da5-c934391680db', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec898d84-8ba1-5f4d-9da5-c934391680db'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s supreme governing body agreed to release the prisoners but said that those convicted of serious crimes would not be part of the amnesty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/world/americas/cuba-2900-prisoners-pardoned.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s supreme governing body agreed to release the prisoners but said that those convicted of serious crimes would not be part of the amnesty.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Cuba has pardoned 2,900 prisoners and may release dozens of foreigners serving jail sentences as a “humanitarian gesture,” President Raúl Castro said Friday.\xa0The amnesty will not include Alan Gross, an American contractor whose arrest two years ago has strained relations between Cuba and the United States.\xa0 Cuba’s vice foreign minister, Josefina Vidal, told The Associated Press that Mr. Gross was “not on the list.” The prisoners, who were pardoned by the Council of State, Cuba’s highest governing body, include women, the ailing and people over 60, Mr. Castro said in a speech to Parliament. Those released would also include some convicted of crimes against the state who had served a large part of their prison sentence with good behavior. He said the government would also release 86\xa0foreign prisoners from 25 countries, provided that their governments agreed to repatriate them. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuba: 2,900 Prisoners Pardoned', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuba: 2,900 Prisoners Pardoned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Raul', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-24T01:47:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Burnett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burnett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9848ef7-6b23-5b36-8d4d-644019eedc7e', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9848ef7-6b23-5b36-8d4d-644019eedc7e'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s top priority now should be a complete reform of New York’s campaign finance fund-raising system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/opinion/gov-cuomos-next-big-task.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s top priority now should be a complete reform of New York’s campaign finance fund-raising system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s success on big issues like same-sex marriage shows that he can get things done. His top priority now should be to clean up Albany’s pay-to-play culture, beginning with a thorough overhaul of the campaign fund-raising system. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gov. Cuomo’s Next Big Task', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gov. Cuomo’s Next Big Task', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', '