ction_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46b90af0-afab-5fe5-a09d-2225c079c07d', 'word_count': 1234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46b90af0-afab-5fe5-a09d-2225c079c07d'}, {'abstract': 'It’s that 1 Across entry that will wake you up in this Saturday offering from Ned White.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/23/show/', 'snippet': 'It’s that 1 Across entry that will wake you up in this Saturday offering from Ned White.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SATURDAY’S PUZZLE — If you are skilled enough to start a Saturday puzzle at 1 Across (as opposed to just looking for any toehold), you might have been surprised by the entry there today. We’ve seen a clue like “X-ed” or “X-ed out” many times before, but always clued in some way that refers to something being crossed out, or perhaps as a rudimentary signature. We’ve never seen it clued as SPREADEAGLE. What a way to wake up on a Saturday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saturday: Where the Show Must Go On', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-24T03:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/310e6b95-caac-5882-8bce-cd10d86a37ba', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/310e6b95-caac-5882-8bce-cd10d86a37ba'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Ames, the creator of the recently canceled HBO series “Bored to Death,” invited local fans to join him at a Boerum Hill bar Wednesday in its memory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/nyregion/bored-to-death-canceled-after-3-seasons-and-fans-mourn.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Ames, the creator of the recently canceled HBO series “Bored to Death,” invited local fans to join him at a Boerum Hill bar Wednesday in its memory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The show did not pander, fans said. It did not condescend. It showed Brooklyn, they insisted, for what it was — a place, apparently, with unlicensed private detective work, armies of baby strollers and a bearded superhero whose most salient superpower, acquired after a below-the-belt run-in with the third rail of a subway, cannot be described in a family newspaper.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/nyregion/BORED/BORED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/24/nyregion/BORED/BORED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/nyregion/BORED/BORED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/24/nyregion/BORED/BORED-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/24/nyregion/BORED/BORED-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gathering to Mourn the End of a Show That Celebrated Brooklyn', 'kicker': 