eak-for-themselves-to-their-detriment.html', 'snippet': 'The prevalence of in-their-own-words advertising campaigns is helping to make the Republican primary campaign a ferociously negative contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seemed innocuous enough at the time. The Democratic speaker of the House and one of her Republican predecessors cozy up on a love seat arranged for them at the bottom of Capitol Hill. They look into the camera, then at each other, and declare that they are really not all that different when it comes to caring about global warming.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/26/us/ADS/ADS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/26/us/ADS/ADS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/26/us/ADS/ADS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Modernizing Attack Ads by Using Old Videos', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Modernizing Attack Ads By Using Old Videos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T05:55:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f92a7358-af8b-5dec-85ed-b8cf5fd7a680', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f92a7358-af8b-5dec-85ed-b8cf5fd7a680'}, {'abstract': 'Since Putin took power, I have felt like my views are shared by only a handful of people and the world outside our small circle is hostile. Until last week.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/from-russia-with-lightness/', 'snippet': 'Since Putin took power, I have felt like my views are shared by only a handful of people and the world outside our small circle is hostile. Until last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Every night last week, writers, artists, actors, ad men, office managers and assorted others climbed the stairs to the fifth floor of a converted factory building in Moscow to make a statement. Pavel Bardin, a well-known young film director, had set up a camera in a conference room there. Everyone who came in — some by invitation, some having found out about the filming from friends or Facebook — wrote his or her name and vocation on a length of masking tape, and named his or her reason for planning to attend what would be a giant protest on Saturday, the 24th. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Russia With Lightness', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T08:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/009db62b-089c-5819-8c48-551527dba339', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/009db62b-089c-5819-8c48-551527dba339'}, {'abstract': 'Australian Test Cricket has fascinated and frustrated Indian fans for years.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/pain-and-glory-from-down-under/', 'snippet': 'Australian Test Cricket has fascinated and frustrated Indian fans for years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For an Indian fan, test cricket in Australia is about waking up at 5 a.m. in order to watch the first session of play with the television on mute so as to not wake the rest of the family. It is about watching the Boxing Day test match in Melbourne and soaking in the tradition even if it is from thousands of miles away. It is about wondering how something as ubiquitous as the wind can be called “the fremantle doctor” during test matches in Perth. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pain and Glory From Down Under', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tendulkar, Sachin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nokia Oyj', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'