ction capacity to its South Korean partner, Samsung.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/technology/sony-sells-stake-in-lcd-panel-joint-venture.html', 'snippet': 'In a bid to streamline its money-losing television business, the Japanese technology giant is turning over production capacity to its South Korean partner, Samsung.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — In a bid to streamline its unprofitable television business, Sony said Monday that it would sell its stake in its flat-panel screen venture with Samsung Electronics, cutting its production capacity at a time when outsourcing has become the norm for manufacturers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sony to Cease Its Flat-Screen Partnership With Samsung', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sony to Cease Its Flat-Screen Partnership With Samsung', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Samsung Electronics Co', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television Sets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Samsung Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T11:22:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d018e2dd-b555-52d8-bdc9-407718771e40', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d018e2dd-b555-52d8-bdc9-407718771e40'}, {'abstract': 'More than half of the players interviewed in an Associated Press article said they would try to conceal a possible concussion rather than exit the game.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/ex-players-sue-over-concussions-current-players-hide-them/', 'snippet': 'More than half of the players interviewed in an Associated Press article said they would try to conceal a possible concussion rather than exit the game.', 'lead_paragraph': '“In a series of interviews about head injuries with The Associated Press over the last two weeks, 23 of 44 NFL players — slightly more than half — said they would try to conceal a possible concussion rather than pull themselves out of a game.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Players Sue Over Concussions; Current Players Hide Them', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concussions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T11:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c05d198-216d-5ac8-b28c-1a96d77fb134', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c05d198-216d-5ac8-b28c-1a96d77fb134'}, {'abstract': 'African countries should abandon rituals that violate fundamental values like equality and dignity.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/when-modernity-trumps-tradition/', 'snippet': 'African countries should abandon rituals that violate fundamental values like equality and dignity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHANNESBURG — The Southern Africa Report recently ran a story alleging that as part of an annual ceremony called Incwala King Mswati III of Swaziland has sex with a bull beaten into semi-consciousness. I have no way of confirming the report. But it did serve to raise what is a very real issue in this part of the world: the clash between tradition and modernity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Modernity Trumps Tradition', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T12:24:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eusebius McKaiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eusebius', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKaiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d4bb88-a0d6-5cf1-9b99-88a63a6e7717', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d4bb88-a0d6-5cf1-9b99-88a63a6e7717'}, {'abstract': 'Rex Ryan gave Mark Sanchez a pass, focusing more on the offensive line’s problems and on the play-calling. That suggested that he wasn’t happy with the offensive coordinator Brian Schottenheimer’s pass-heavy play-calling.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/what-rex-ryan-said-after-the-jets-giants-game/', 'snippet': 'Rex Ryan gave Mark Sanchez a pass, focusing more on the offensive line’s problems and on the play-calling. That suggested that he wasn’t happy with the offensive coordinator Brian Schottenheimer’s pass-heavy play-calling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reviewing Rex Ryan’s comments to reporters after the Jets lost to the Giants on Saturday. \xa0The full transcript is below, via Jets.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Rex Ryan Said After the Jets-Giants Game', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Rex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T12:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d998e4-6ec9-593a-a1bc-f1151c863076', 'word_count': 1365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d998e4-6ec9-593a-a1bc-f1151c863076'}, {'abstract': 'Crystal Bridges, a museum of American art built by a Walmart heir, has opened in Arkansas.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/arts/design/crystal-bridges-the-art-museum-walmart-money-built-review.html', 'snippet': 'Crystal Bridges, a museum of American art built by a Walmart heir, has opened in Arkansas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BENTONVILLE, Ark. — By just about any measure, the Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art, which opened last month in this small town in northwest Arkansas, is off to a running start. The dream-come-true of Alice Walton, an heir to the Walmart fortune, it is characterized by people both inside and outside the museum as a work in progress, with plenty of room for improvement. But there it stands, a big, serious, confident, new institution with more than 50,000 square feet of gallery space and a collection worth hundreds of millions of dollars in a region almost devoid of art museums.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'captio