 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seeds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T19:31:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. Claiborne Ray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5ac1403-d2e4-5e60-a243-a880cd3ba4cd', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5ac1403-d2e4-5e60-a243-a880cd3ba4cd'}, {'abstract': '“The London Merchant,” about the downfall of a young man led astray by a prostitute, focused on the British working classes and was popular in the 1700s', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/eighteenth-century-play-to-get-first-north-american-performance/', 'snippet': '“The London Merchant,” about the downfall of a young man led astray by a prostitute, focused on the British working classes and was popular in the 1700s', 'lead_paragraph': '“The London Merchant,” a tragedy from 1731 that was one of the first British dramas to focus on the middle and working classes – and that has apparently never received a professional production in North America – will be performed Jan. 6-28 at the Theater at the Church of Notre Dame in Morningside Heights in Upper Manhattan. Written by George Lillo, “The London Merchant” is about the downfall of a young apprentice who is led astray by a prostitute. It was highly popular in the 18th century. The Storm Theater Company and Blackfriars Repertory Theater will jointly produce the work, directed by Peter Dobbins, Storm’s artistic director. A spokesman for the production, Pete Sanders, said on Monday that the producers had done research on the history of the play and could not find evidence that it had been performed professionally in the United States or Canada.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eighteenth-Century Play to Get First North American Performance', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blackfriars Repertory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Storm Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morningside Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T19:40:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ac23b67-093e-5c50-b2a8-f1eb7d663053', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ac23b67-093e-5c50-b2a8-f1eb7d663053'}, {'abstract': 'A man was stabbed to death on a famous shopping street in London on Monday, during the traditional Boxing Day sales.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/killing-on-oxford-street-mars-boxing-day-in-london/', 'snippet': 'A man was stabbed to death on a famous shopping street in London on Monday, during the traditional Boxing Day sales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The annual British day of bargain-hunting was cut short in London on Monday, as a young man in London was stabbed to death just after noon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killing on Oxford Street Mars Boxing Day in London', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boxing Day', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discount Selling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T19:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26f2b89e-e562-5861-b8d2-5557ddf0df28', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26f2b89e-e562-5861-b8d2-5557ddf0df28'}, {'abstract': 'That classic movie and some well-timed sporting events proved to be ratings winners for broadcast networks over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/wonderful-life-lifts-nbc-in-holiday-ratings/', 'snippet': 'That classic movie and some well-timed sporting events proved to be ratings winners for broadcast networks over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Television programming has its own holiday traditions, and they were reflected in the Nielsen ratings over the weekend. An encore telecast on NBC of “It’s a Wonderful Life,” the 65-year-old Christmas stalwart, was the No. 1 program in all ratings categories on Saturday, with 4.6 million total viewers. It was the most-watched film on any of the broadcast networks since April.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Wonderful Life’ Lifts NBC in Holiday Ratings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T1