eality for teams on the cusp. Thanks to a helter-skelter schedule, depth is more important than ever, and teams like the Lakers, Clippers, Celtics and Knicks will have to find ways to balance their strong starting lineups with unremarkable reserves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Preliminary Returns and Concerns for Contending Teams', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Celtics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Clippers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Lakers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/003042ed-a49b-5a6f-936c-f8aa5ebedb60', 'word_count': 1047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/003042ed-a49b-5a6f-936c-f8aa5ebedb60'}, {'abstract': 'Imagine being unable to recognize your children or your closest friend. For those with a condition called faceblindness, recognizing anyone is a challenge.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/a-world-without-faces/', 'snippet': 'Imagine being unable to recognize your children or your closest friend. For those with a condition called faceblindness, recognizing anyone is a challenge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine being unable to recognize your children or your closest friend. You can see their faces perfectly fine, but if you passed them on the street you wouldn’t be able to place their unique eyes, nose and ears.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A World Without Faces', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Face', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prosopagnosia (Brain Disorder)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:09:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Barrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edb7bdec-3343-592b-8214-7b1b81e5e38f', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edb7bdec-3343-592b-8214-7b1b81e5e38f'}, {'abstract': 'What is it like to be told you have a disease that may rob you of your sight and ability to walk? Eight men and women speak about life after a diagnosis of MS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2008/12/03/health/healthguide/TE_MULTIPLESCLEROSIS.html', 'snippet': 'What is it like to be told you have a disease that may rob you of your sight and ability to walk? Eight men and women speak about life after a diagnosis of MS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is it like to be told you have a disease that may rob you of your sight and ability to walk? Eight men and women speak about life after a diagnosis of MS.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Patient Voices: Multiple Sclerosis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Sclerosis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:18:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Health', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Health Guide', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/42425571-8597-53b1-aa63-682554f3d386', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/42425571-8597-53b1-aa63-682554f3d386'}, {'abstract': 'After decades of research, a rapid rollout of sophisticated treatments is changing how the disease is managed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/health/new-drugs-raise-hope-for-patients-with-ms.html', 'snippet': 'After decades of research, a rapid rollout of sophisticated treatments is changing how the disease is managed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three years ago, Kristie Salerno Kent, a singer-songwriter, was standing in a security line at the airport on her way home from a gig when her legs went numb. “From the waist down, it felt as though I was trying to walk through a bowl of oatmeal,” said the 38-year-old musician, who has multiple sclerosis. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Drugs Raise Hope for Patients With M.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Drugs Raise Hope For Patients With M.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tysabri (Drug)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Preventive Medicine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Multiple Sclerosis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:18:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Tarkan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tarkan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebf263dd-caca-54b3-8160-efa5b225824b', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebf263dd-caca-54b3-8160-efa5b225824b'}, {'abstract': 'Poisoning is now the leading cause of injury death, and 90 percent of poisonings were caused by drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/health/more-americans-died-of-poisoning-than-in-car-crashes-in-2008.html', 'snippet': 'Poisoning is now the leading cause of injury death, and 90 percent of poisonings were caused by drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008, for the first time in nearly 30 years, more people died of poisoning than in car crashes. Poisoning is now the leading cause of injury death, and 90 percent of poisonings were caused by drugs. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poisoning Kills More Than Car Crashes', 'kicker': 'Vital Statistics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poisoning Kills More Than Car Crashes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poisoning and Poisons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pain-Relieving Drugs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Center for Health Statistics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:22:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7a5e259-93cc-5a87-bf35-6e512557f840', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7a5e259-93cc-5a87-bf35-6e512557f840'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Darkest Hour,” four young adults partying in Moscow fight for their lives when aliens invade and glowing fireballs transform most of humanity into cremains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/movies/the-darkest-hour-directed-by-chris-gorak-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Darkest Hour,” four young adults partying in Moscow fight for their lives when aliens invade and glowing fireballs transform most of humanity into cremains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Based on the admittedly flimsy evidence of films like “The Darkest Hour,” the best defense against invading aliens is to be as attractive as possible. Being one-dimensional doesn’t hurt, either, even if that particular character flaw can be safely blamed on whoever is responsible for the screenplay — in this case, Jon Spaihts. Given that he is also one of two writers credited on Ridley Scott’s much anticipated 2012 film (and possible “Alien” prequel) “Prometheus,” it is to be hoped that his lazy ideas were not equally apportioned between the two projects.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/dark/dark-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1001, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/dark/dark-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/dark/dark-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poof to Life as You Knew It', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'The Darkest Hour'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poof to Life as You Knew It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorak, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T20:57:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ba9302c-0a3e-5da3-adb0-24309f848133', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ba9302c-0a3e-5da3-adb0-24309f848133'}, {'abstract': 'Other researchers were unable to confirm the findings of the 2010 study, which had lent support to an idea put forth in a 2009 paper that was retracted last week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/health/research/scholars-retract-another-study-linking-xmrv-to-chronic-fatigue-syndrome.html', 'snippet': 'Other researchers were unable to confirm the findings of the 2010 study, which had lent support to an idea put forth in a 2009 paper that was retracted last week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the second time in a week, a key study suggesting a link between a retroviral infection and chronic fatigue syndrome has been retracted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scholars Retract Another Study Linking Virus to Fatigue Syndrome', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scholars Retract Another Study Linking Virus to Fatigue Syndrome', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chronic Fatigue Syndrome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-26T21:03:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49a10397-a12e-5bd3-8428-dff80148406f', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49a10397-a12e-5bd3-8428-dff80148406f'}, {'abstract': 'Hanukkah is what keeps many Jews from feeling alienated in this dazzling red and green season. But how do we keep it from becoming just a Jewish Christmas?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/26/tough-choices-this-hanukkah-holiday-season/', 'snippet': 'Hanukkah is what keeps many Jews from feeling alienated in this dazzling red and green season. But how do we keep it from becoming just a Jewish Christmas?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Tuesday was the first night of Hanukkah, and my 14-year-old son sang in his all-male high school’s holiday concert. When my children were enrolled full-time in a Jewish day school, they would head to the gold-domed State House in Boston and perform an annual Hanukkah concert. Every year the same tin-eared politician would lurch forward with a boxer’s stance and proclaim, “When you shake my hand, wish me a Merry Christmas instead of a happy holiday!” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tough Choices This Hanukkah (Holiday) Season', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, '