 was able to give up his illustration career and paint full time. His most famous works captured the lives of the men and women who worked near the seaside, as well as children at play.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday: Start of a Cry by Juliet', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T03:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c57a6be1-d17b-51ab-9b61-7da266446042', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c57a6be1-d17b-51ab-9b61-7da266446042'}, {'abstract': 'The renminbi is not fully convertible and therefore cannot yet compete with the dollar as a global reserve currency, though the move could be a “baby step” in that direction, one expert said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/business/global/china-and-japan-in-currency-agreement.html', 'snippet': 'The renminbi is not fully convertible and therefore cannot yet compete with the dollar as a global reserve currency, though the move could be a “baby step” in that direction, one expert said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — China and Japan have agreed to start direct trading of their currencies, officials announced during a visit here on Monday by Japan’s prime minister, Yoshihiko Noda. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Currency Agreement for Japan and China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Currency Agreement For Japan And China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Curren