The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T11:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Clagett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clagett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/468c8358-2043-572d-8a8e-71c4fa31ec00', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/468c8358-2043-572d-8a8e-71c4fa31ec00'}, {'abstract': 'Reading about Ndamukong Suh’s stomping incident or the Jim Schwartz-Jim Harbaugh postgame scuffle is entertaining, but it won’t make anyone a smarter football fan.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/top-10-n-f-l-trends-on-the-field/', 'snippet': 'Reading about Ndamukong Suh’s stomping incident or the Jim Schwartz-Jim Harbaugh postgame scuffle is entertaining, but it won’t make anyone a smarter football fan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The problem with many \xa0“Top 10 Stories of the Year” articles is that they focus on drama and hype. Reading about Ndamukong Suh’s stomping incident or the Jim Schwartz-Jim Harbaugh postgame scuffle or the public’s reaction to Tim Tebow is entertaining, but it won’t make anyone a smarter football fan. For those who prefer analysis, here are the top 10 trends and developments that took place between the sidelines in the N.F.L. this season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top 10 N.F.L. Trends on the Field', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T11:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byli