out the market for television sets, where prices have continued to fall, to the delight of consumers and the chagrin of manufacturers. There is little money to be made in televisions, in part because companies like Sony and Samsung ramped up production capacity for flat-screen sets just before the economic downturn, causing a glut. Online retailers and low-cost set makers have also put pressure on profit margins.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Trouble for TV Makers Is Good for Consumers', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T14:17:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3b78fe7-0b5a-53c5-bc9d-95d327bebe21', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3b78fe7-0b5a-53c5-bc9d-95d327bebe21'}, {'abstract': 'If you root for a team whose name ends in “ets,” you’re swimming in a sea of troubles.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/can-the-ill-fortunes-of-a-sports-team-be-told-in-its-name/', 'snippet': 'If you root for a team whose name ends in “ets,” you’re swimming in a sea of troubles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Developments in the New York sports world over the holiday weekend lead to a conclusion that has been bubbling for a while, and now seems inescapable: If you root for a team whose name ends in “ets,” you’re swimming in a sea of troubles. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Sports Team’s Ill Fortunes Be Foretold in Its Name?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Organizational', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goetz, Bernard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Madoff, Bernard L', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prok