'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T21:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0ebe51ee-6820-5c4a-a0e9-5d0a732a048c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0ebe51ee-6820-5c4a-a0e9-5d0a732a048c'}, {'abstract': 'Two of the biggest deals of 2011, the buyouts of J.Crew and Del Monte Foods, were reopened to bidders after shareholders sued, but the conduct in both cases was relatively unpenalized.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/for-wall-street-deal-makers-sometimes-it-pays-to-be-bad/', 'snippet': 'Two of the biggest deals of 2011, the buyouts of J.Crew and Del Monte Foods, were reopened to bidders after shareholders sued, but the conduct in both cases was relatively unpenalized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Instead of awarding F’s for the worst deals and deal-making of the year, I am going to focus on two of the year’s biggest buyouts. The first: J.Crew’s $3 billion buyout by its chief executive, Millard S. Drexler, and the private equity firms TPG Capital and Leonard Green & Partners. The second: Del Monte Foods’ $5.3 billion acquisition by Kohlberg Kravis Roberts & Company, Vestar Capital Partners and Centerview Capital.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Wall Street Deal Makers, Sometimes It Pays to Be Bad', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drexler, Millard S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Del Monte Foods', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T21:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97085221-9b09-519d-aff4-b763254114bc', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97085221-9b09-519d-aff4-b763254114bc'}, {'abstract': 'It was not unlike a political caucus. The candidates — in this case, nearly 100 photography books published this year — took over every inch of available counter space in the photo department, where they were carefully scrutinized by a group of opinionated voters, each of whom was given just 10 Post-it notes as ballots.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/our-top-ten-photo-books-of-2011/', 'snippet': 'It was not unlike a political caucus. The candidates — in this case, nearly 100 photography books published this year — took over every inch of available counter space in the photo department, where they were carefully scrutinized by a group of op...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was not unlike a political caucus. The candidates — in this case, nearly 100 photography books published this year — took over every inch of available counter space in the photo department, where they were carefully scrutinized by a group of opinionated voters, each of whom was given just 10 Post-it notes as ballots. Impassioned speeches were made. Votes were cast: a few books grew polychromatic bouquets of Post-its — each voter had his or her own individual color — while others sprouted single lonely Post-its. Votes were changed: worthy candidates, including the likes of Diana Vreeland and Gerhard Richter, were abandoned. Finally, after weeks, a group of winners emerged. Like all campaigns, it was brutal. Here are the 10 victors, in no particular order.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Top Ten Photo Books of 2011', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedlander, Lee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuss, Adam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maier, Vivian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGinley, Ryan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sante, Luc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Cindy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vreeland, Diana', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APERTURE FOUNDATION', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Magnum Photos', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T21:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The photo department', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The photo department'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd0ec7fb-b2b5-551c-8eaa-a7c180e6518c', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd0ec7fb-b2b5-551c-8eaa-a7c180e6518c'}, {'abstract': 'An offshoot of Occupy Wall Street plans to protest at candidate events this week, but not at the actual caucuses.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/occupy-the-caucus-is-ready-to-go-in-iowa/', 'snippet': 'An offshoot of Occupy Wall Street plans to protest at candidate events this week, but not at the actual caucuses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES — As the Republican presidential candidates make their final pleas to voters across Iowa, a local offshoot of the Occupy Wall Street protests here has traded in tents for its own campaign office. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy the Caucus Is Ready to Go in Iowa', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T21:15:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Yaccino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8528eb22-7e3f-5bce-b050-d087c5e1bdea', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8528eb22-7e3f-5bce-b050-d087c5e1bdea'}, {'abstract': 'As part of a busy start to the N.B.A. season, the Knicks claimed point guard Jeremy Lin, a Harvard product, off waivers.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/knicks-claim-former-harvard-guard-off-waivers/', 'snippet': 'As part of a busy start to the N.B.A. season, the Knicks claimed point guard Jeremy Lin, a Harvard product, off waivers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREENBURGH, N.Y. — Jeremy Lin, a Harvard-educated point guard and N.B.A. summer-league sensation, is joining the Knicks. His stay may be short.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knicks Claim Former Harvard Guard Off Waivers', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lin, Jeremy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-27T21:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f6f2afa-8e23-524a-a713-8a22fb05337f', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f6f2afa-8e23-524a-a713-8a22fb05337f'}, {'abstract': 'Studies tease apart the intricacies of bush meat consumption in Central and West Africa, which may help guide conservation efforts in those regions.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/27/could-chicken-be-the-new-monkey-someday/', 'snippet': 'Studies tease apart the intricacies of bush meat consumption in Central and West Africa, which may help guide conservation efforts in those regions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Wildlife Conservation Society set out to study bush meat consumption in Gabon, researchers reluctantly had to rule out some sites. Especially in the rainy season, it became hard to gain access to some rural communities. Washed-out roads grounded trucks in the muck, and precarious mountain passes were in some cases too risky to navigate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Could Chicken Be the New Monkey Someday?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject',