n art. Both emerged in the 1950s and provided crucial links between art styles, specifically helping to forge the transition from Abstract Expressionism to what lay beyond.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/frank/frank-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/frank/frank-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/27/arts/frank/frank-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Artists Who Embraced Freedom', 'kicker': "Critic's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Artists Who Embraced Freedom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frankenthaler, Helen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chamberlain, John (1927-2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T00:43:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Appraisal', '_id': 'nyt://article/41f2c6f4-d544-5d20-95b8-1cfca8475372', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41f2c6f4-d544-5d20-95b8-1cfca8475372'}, {'abstract': 'Known as Mighty Mite, Danoff was a top amateur golfer before turning pro in 1949 and starting the Ladies Professional Golf Association with 12 others in 1950.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/sports/golf/bettye-danoff-founding-member-of-lpga-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'Known as Mighty Mite, Danoff was a top amateur golfer before turning pro in 1949 and starting the Ladies Professional Golf Association with 12 others in 1950.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bettye Danoff, one of 13 founding members of the Ladies Professional Golf Association, which began as a hardy, poorly paid band of women who traveled the country for the chance to play the game, died on Thursday in McKinney, Tex. She was 88.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/sports/golf/28danoff/28danoff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/sports/golf/28danoff/28danoff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/28/sports/golf/28danoff/28danoff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bettye Danoff, Founding Member of L.P.G.A., Dies at 88', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bettye Danoff, 88, Star Amateur And Co-Founder of L.P.G.A.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berg, Patty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zaharias, Babe Didrikson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ladies Professional Golf Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T00:49:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margalit Fox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margalit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fox', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/08a8289e-1b6c-5885-9444-cef23d2bf70b', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08a8289e-1b6c-5885-9444-cef23d2bf70b'}, {'abstract': 'Andrea Bruce, a New York Times photographer, peered into the lives of Iraqis over the last months of the war and captured quietly powerful portraits that show optimism and resilience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/world/middleeast/portraits-of-iraqis-making-themselves-heard-in-a-changed-country.html', 'snippet': 'Andrea Bruce, a New York Times photographer, peered into the lives of Iraqis over the last months of the war and captured quietly powerful portraits that show optimism and resilience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — The day after a lumbering line of whooping, high-fiving American soldiers drove out of Iraq and into history, the 30 million Iraqis they left behind woke to a bright cold day filled with echoes of nearly nine years of war. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/world/PORTRAITS-1/PORTRAITS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/28/world/PORTRAITS-1/PORTRAITS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/world/PORTRAITS-1/PORTRAITS-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail'