, President Hamid Karzai dropped his opposition to allowing the Taliban to set up an office in the Persian Gulf state of Qatar, saying on Tuesday that he could abide by such a step if it was what the United States wanted. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Karzai Agrees to Let Taliban Set Up Office in Qatar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Karzai Agrees To Let Taliban Set Up Office In Qatar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Qatar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T01:05:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Rosenberg and Sharifullah Sahak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sharifullah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sahak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c175db7f-225c-5aae-896d-5674299c09d5', 'word_count': 651, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c175db7f-225c-5aae-896d-5674299c09d5'}, {'abstract': 'Despite Supreme Court rulings in 1962 and ’63, open prayer and Christian symbols have never really disappeared from some schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/us/battling-anew-over-the-place-of-religion-in-public-schools.html', 'snippet': 'Despite Supreme Court rulings in 1962 and ’63, open prayer and Christian symbols have never really disappeared from some schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JEFFERSON, S.C. — It has been nearly 50 years since the Supreme Court ruled that officially sponsored prayer in public schools violated the separation of church and state. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/us/PRAYER/PRAYER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/us/PRAYER/PRAYER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/28/us/PRAYER/PRAYER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Battling Anew Over the Place of Religion in Public Schools', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Battling Anew Over the Place Of Religion in Public Schools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prayers and Prayer Books', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T01:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fef32996-5cc8-5ea6-9f8b-d5cf34a08f49', 'word_count': 1328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fef32996-5cc8-5ea6-9f8b-d5cf34a08f49'}, {'abstract': 'A decision to retire by Ben Nelson, the embattled two-term Democrat from Nebraska, helps Republicans in an effort to bring the Senate under their control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/us/politics/ben-nelson-nebraska-senator-will-not-seek-re-election.html', 'snippet': 'A decision to retire by Ben Nelson, the embattled two-term Democrat from Nebraska, helps Republicans in an effort to bring the Senate under their control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Senator Ben Nelson, the embattled two-term Democrat from Nebraska, said Tuesday that he would not seek re-election in 2012, giving Republicans a decided advantage in their drive to win his Senate seat and a leg up in their effort to bring the Senate under Republican control. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/us/NELSON/NELSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/us/NELSON/NELSON-thumbStandar