ers into the new Libyan state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRIPOLI, Libya — Sometimes the young fighters gather under a date palm next to the empty swimming pool to joke and strum the guitar. When it rains, they retreat indoors to play foosball in an improvised game room inside their favorite hangout, a ransacked house where one of Col. Muammar el-Qaddafi’s sons once lived and entertained.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/world/SUB-TRIPOLI/SUB-TRIPOLI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/28/world/SUB-TRIPOLI/SUB-TRIPOLI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/world/SUB-TRIPOLI/SUB-TRIPOLI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/28/world/SUB-TRIPOLI/SUB-TRIPOLI-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/28/world/SUB-TRIPOLI/SUB-TRIPOLI-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where a Qaddafi Son Once Played, Ex-Rebels Wait and Wonder', 'kicker': 'Tripoli Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where a Qaddafi Son Once Played, Ex-Rebels Now Wait and Wonder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muatassim el-', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transitional National Council (Libya)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qaddafi, Muammar el-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T03:32:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af71004e-fb7d-53a4-acf3-7a29518046f5', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af71004e-fb7d-53a4-acf3-7a29518046f5'}, {'abstract': 'Who wrote the Ron Paul newsletters, and why won’t he disavow his racist supporters?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/opinion/mr-pauls-discredited-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'Who wrote the Ron Paul newsletters, and why won’t he disavow his racist supporters?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ron Paul long ago disqualified himself for the presidency by peddling claptrap proposals like abolishing the Federal Reserve, returning to the gold standard, cutting a third of the federal budget and all foreign aid and opposing the Civil Rights Act of 1964. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Paul’s Discredited Campaign', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Paul’s Discredited Campaign', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republ