[], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1630f0e4-5aa4-569c-a70f-9e832cc30546', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1630f0e4-5aa4-569c-a70f-9e832cc30546'}, {'abstract': 'As most of us prepare our exercise resolutions for 2012, now seems the ideal time to review the past year in fitness science and the lessons it contained, both encouraging and cautionary.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/the-year-in-fitness/', 'snippet': 'As most of us prepare our exercise resolutions for 2012, now seems the ideal time to review the past year in fitness science and the lessons it contained, both encouraging and cautionary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If all the Phys Ed columns published this year have a single message, it is that now is a fine time to own a body. The diverse exercise-related experiments published in 2011 and covered in this space each week suggest that it’s possible to retain your cognitive powers, muscle mass, running speed and waistline, even as you age, and that a little exercise can go a long way in terms of physiological benefit. Recent, important science even tells us that coffee, chocolate and beer enhance exercise performance, which is fortunate, since I have no plans to give up any of those. As most of us prepare our exercise resolutions for 2012, now seems an ideal time to review the past year in fitness science and the lessons it contained, both encouraging and cautionary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Fitness', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muscles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value