': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T15:01:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e79d415-43c6-5eae-9456-f0ad662b7d54', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e79d415-43c6-5eae-9456-f0ad662b7d54'}, {'abstract': 'In New York City, it’s not the 1 percent who people might want to be upset with; it’s actually the 6 percent who rake in 51 percent of the income.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/the-1-percent-thats-so-2011-its-the-6-percent-you-should-be-worried-about/', 'snippet': 'In New York City, it’s not the 1 percent who people might want to be upset with; it’s actually the 6 percent who rake in 51 percent of the income.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here at City Room, we’ve had enough of people picking on the 1 percent. Sure, some might brand them greedy, but even in New York City, where, thanks to the financial-service industry, they are particularly well compensated, they receive only 33.8 percent of the total income, kindly leaving two-thirds for the rest of us.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 1 Percent? That’s So 2011. It’s the 6 Percent You Should Be Worried About.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Independent Budget Office', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T15:11:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88ebea95-7138-5231-ac0c-1bff309e15da', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88ebea95-7138-5231-ac0c-1bff309e15da'}, {'abstract': 'Clearly, weight loss is an intense struggle, one in which we are not fighting simply hunger or cravings for sweets, but our own bodies.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/why-lost-pounds-come-back/', 'snippet': 'Clearly, weight loss is an intense struggle, one in which we are not fighting simply hunger or cravings for sweets, but our own bodies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s New York Times Magazine, I explore new research that helps explain why most dieters who lose weight end up gaining it all back. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Lost Pounds Come Back', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T15:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2958386-b414-5e40-bdd4-568c91023a15', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2958386-b414-5e40-bdd4-568c91023a15'}, {'abstract': 'As part of this week’s Q&A, David Naylor, an architectural historian and author of “Railroad Stations: The Buildings That Linked the Nation” (Norton), took readers’ questions on railroad stations.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/ask-a-railroad-station-expert-2/', 'snippet': 'As part of this week’s Q&A, David Naylor, an architectural historian and author of “Railroad Stations: The Buildings That Linked the Nation” (Norton), took readers’ questions on railroad stations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of this week’s Q&A, David Naylor, an architectural historian and author of “Railroad Stations: The Buildings That Linked the Nation” (Norton), took readers’ questions on railroad stations. He is no longer taking questions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ask a Railroad Station Expert', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-28T15:43:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0fdff6c-e905-5a20-ad51-f65a4472a36d', 'word_count': 2619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0fdff6c-e905-5a20-ad51-f65a4472a36d'}, {'abstract': 'Deutsche Borse and NYSE Euronext have extended the deadline to complete their $9 billion proposed merger to March 31, amid resistance from European regulators.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/nyse-and-deutsche-boerse-extend-merger-deadline/', 'snippet': 'Deutsche Borse and NYSE Euronext have extended the deadline to complete their $9 billion proposed merger to March 31, amid resistance from European regulators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deutsche Börse and NYSE Euronext have extended the deadline to complete their $9 billion proposed merger to March 31, amid resistance from Euro