'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T01:50:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Yaccino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d95b449d-ae22-5ac8-bfc8-b9cab069c5da', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d95b449d-ae22-5ac8-bfc8-b9cab069c5da'}, {'abstract': 'Many young women are leaving the labor force to upgrade their skills, while their male counterparts are more likely to take whatever job they can find.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/business/young-women-go-back-to-school-instead-of-work.html', 'snippet': 'Many young women are leaving the labor force to upgrade their skills, while their male counterparts are more likely to take whatever job they can find.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Workers are dropping out of the labor force in droves, and they are mostly women. In fact, many are young women. But they are not dropping out forever; instead, these young women seem to be postponing their working lives to get more education. There are now — for the first time in three decades — more young women in school than in the work force.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/business/jp-WOMEN/jp-WOMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/29/business/jp-WOMEN/jp-WOMEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo'