ns-the-corner.html', 'snippet': 'After a 5-26 record two seasons ago, Wagner is now 8-3 under coach Dan Hurley, including an impressive victory over then-No. 15 Pittsburgh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dan Hurley did not give his team much time to savor its first victory over a ranked opponent in 33 years. By 11:30 a.m. Tuesday, Wagner’s holiday was over and another relentless two-hour practice was under way.\xa0', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/sports/WAGNER/WAGNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 749, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/sports/WAGNER/WAGNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/29/sports/WAGNER/WAGNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Staten Island, a Team Resurfaces as Relevant', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Staten Island, a Team Resurfaces as Relevant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurley, Bobby', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Staten Island (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Pittsburgh', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurley, Dan (1973- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T01:52:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zach Schonbrun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zach', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schonbrun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f6876dc-72b9-5120-8101-620d82ce5cad', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f6876dc-72b9-5120-8101-620d82ce5cad'}, {'abstract': 'About 8,000 workers at an electronics factory in Nanjing are on strike over what they call low wages from their South Korean employers, according to an advocacy group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/world/asia/workers-at-korean-factory-strike-over-low-wages.html', 'snippet': 'About 8,000 workers at an electronics factory in Nanjing are on strike over what they call low wages from their South Korean employers, according to an advocacy group.', 'lead_paragraph': ' About 8,000 workers at an electronics factory in the southern city of Nanjing have been on strike this week against what they call low wages from their South Korean employers, according to a statement on Tuesday by China Labor Watch, an advocacy group based in the United States. Caijing, a Chinese newsmagazine, reported some details of the strike on Wednesday. The workers are angry that their year-end bonus equals only a month’s pay, while Korean workers are getting a year-end bonus equal to six months’ salary, according to the statement from the labor group. The strike began Monday. On Wednesday afternoon, according to Reuters, there were indications that workers might accept an offer by employers of two months’ salary as a bonus. The factory makes electronics for a unit of LG Group, a South Korean company. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Strike at a Korean Factory', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Strike at a Korean Factory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nanjing (China)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T01:56:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/08881f89-60ee-52ea-b8df-c1a6c9faf3ad', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08881f89-60ee-52ea-b8df-c1a6c9faf3ad'}, {'abstract': 'For most justices, most of the time, the relationship between ideology and outcome is oblique.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/28/what-we-think-about-when-we-think-about-the-court/', 'snippet': 'For most justices, most of the time, the relationship between ideology and outcome is oblique.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wherever I go these days, someone is bound to ask me how I think the Supreme Court will rule in the challenge to the Affordable Care Act, set for argument over three days during the last week in March. My response, that I expect the court to uphold the law, predictably evokes an expression of disbelief verging on pity for my display of such naïveté. “Won’t the justices just vote their politics?” my questioner invariably asks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline'