to publish another article in a few days about private education in India, and one or two others early next year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Dharavi, Another View of India', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T07:05:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77464f96-5007-50a3-b625-d16ad69b581a', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77464f96-5007-50a3-b625-d16ad69b581a'}, {'abstract': 'A quiz to test your memory of the memes like the stories that you find in the pages of The Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/opinion/the-year-in-questions.html', 'snippet': 'A quiz to test your memory of the memes like the stories that you find in the pages of The Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like a circle in a spiral, like a wheel within a wheel, never-ending news is spooling off a never-ending reel ... Now a quiz is set to test you and your memory of the memes ...', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/opinion/29schott-img/29schott-img-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/29/opinion/29schott-img/29schott-img-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/opinion/29schott-img/29schott-img-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 772, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Questions', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor | Op-Chart ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Year in Questions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T07:38:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Schott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/380a2d26-82d1-5e71-9162-963cbf9272b3', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/380a2d26-82d1-5e71-9162-963cbf9272b3'}, {'abstract': 'When Israelis raise their voices discussing politics these days, it’s while talking about social justice, health care and the status of women — not the peace process.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/peace-process/', 'snippet': 'When Israelis raise their voices discussing politics these days, it’s while talking about social justice, health care and the status of women — not the peace process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOUNT GILBOA, Israel — Having spent Hanukkah seeing more friends this year than usual, I was able to canvass our conversations for a fair number of topics beyond kids, health, real estate and other standard catching-up fare. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Peace? Process?', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T09:29:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f254937a-2ed8-5700-a455-fbdd5ec7ef34', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f254937a-2ed8-5700-a455-fbdd5ec7ef34'}, {'abstract': 'Once you have finished your book, perhaps one of the easiest ways to get it out there for sale is to use publishing tools from the major online bookstores like Amazon and Barnes and Noble.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/qa-publishing-your-own-e-book/', 'snippet': 'Once you have finished your book, perhaps one of the easiest ways to get it out there for sale is to use publishing tools from the major online bookstores like Amazon and Barnes and Noble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Publishing Your Own E-Book', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barnes & Noble Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T10:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/155fd64b-ff9f-55d1-ac53-1bdfa6f7c522', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/155fd64b-ff9f-55d1-ac53-1bdfa6f7c522'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time in nearly 200 years, 65 works that had been at the Louvre will return to the Galleria Borghese in Rome for the exhibition “The Borghese Family and Antiquity,” which runs through April 9, 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/borghese-treasures-return-to-rome/', 'snippet': 'For the first time in nearly 200 years, 65 works that had been at the Louvre will return to the Galleria Borghese in Rome for the exhibition “The Borghese Family and Antiquity,” which runs through April 9, 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Napoleon acquired 695 ancient statues from the Borghese family collection in 1807, flabbergasted contemporaries called the act “an indelible shame.” The collection was born two centuries earlier when Cardinal Scipione Borghese, nephew of Pope Paul V, made it his life’s work to bring together the masterpieces available to him. He assembled more than 2,000 pieces, including antique statues, sculptures by Bernini and canvases by Raphael, Caravaggio and Titian.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Borghese Treasures Return to Rome', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caravaggio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Parla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ecb9e96-af2d-5790-a6bc-198f523c9fd7', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ecb9e96-af2d-5790-a6bc-198f523c9fd7'}, {'abstract': 'The former overseer of N.F.L. officials says the ESPN analyst Jon Gruden “was a loudmouth as a coach”  and “is a blowhard in the broadcast booth” who doesn’t know the rules.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/mike-pereira-is-not-a-fan-of-jon-gruden/', 'snippet': 'The former overseer of N.F.L. officials says the ESPN analyst Jon Gruden “was a loudmouth as a coach”  and “is a blowhard in the broadcast booth” who doesn’t know the rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he was the overseer of officials for the N.F.L., Mike Pereira frequently fielded calls from angry coaches who wanted explanations for calls that went against them. Bill Belichick once saw him and sympathetically told him he looked run-down. “You never win, do you?” Belichick said, according to Pereira.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mike Pereira Is ‘Not a Fan’ of Jon Gruden', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gruden, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pereira, Mike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f69d4895-e5be-5555-a8a9-59b0b7f9b6fa', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f69d4895-e5be-5555-a8a9-59b0b7f9b6fa'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times, and a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/the-early-word-seesaw/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times, and a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Today’s Times:Hundreds of Ron Paul’s college-age volunteers descended on the Hawkeye State on Wednesday with barely a week left until the Iowa caucuses. Richard A. Oppel Jr. writes that members of Mr. Paul’s “army” arrived under a cloak of secrecy and with strict rules for comportment as they prepare to coax likely caucus-goers into voting for Mr. Paul, a 76-year-old veteran Texas congressman who is drawing supporters for his libertarian and antiwar views.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Seesaw', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3f9fae-e004-564a-8fe0-baab357c6dee', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3f9fae-e004-564a-8fe0-baab357c6dee'}, {'abstract': 'Seeding is crucial, especially for the top two teams in each conference. The first-round bye is an automatic win that essentially doubles a team’s chances of making it to the Super Bowl.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/week-17-n-f-l-probabilities-importance-of-seeding/', 'snippet': 'Seeding is crucial, especially for the top two teams in each conference. The first-round bye is an automatic win that essentially doubles a team’s chances of making it to the Super Bowl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The path into the playoffs for the Giants is clear enough. Win and they’re in as the N.F.C. East champion. The Jets’ path to an A.F.C. wild card is much more complicated, and more dependent on the fortunes of other teams, including the Bengals, the Titans, the Broncos and the Raiders. The only other unsettled playoff berth is the A.F.C. West championship, which will go to \xa0the Broncos or Raiders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 17 N.F.L. Probabilities: Importance of Seeding', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9823a7d9-d7bf-5f19-937b-208eee7860f0', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9823a7d9-d7bf-5f19-937b-208eee7860f0'}, {'abstract': 'Many vegan dishes, like fruit salad and peanut butter and jelly, are already beloved. However, the problem faced by many of us is in imagining less-traditional vegan dishes that are interesting and not challenging. Here are some more creative options to try.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/01/magazine/eat-vegan-recipes.html', 'snippet': 'Many vegan dishes, like fruit salad and peanut butter and jelly, are already beloved. However, the problem faced by many of us is in imagining less-traditional vegan dishes that are interesting and not challenging. Here are some more creative opti...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many vegan dishes, like fruit salad and peanut butter and jelly, are already beloved. However, the problem faced by many of us is in imagining less-traditional vegan dishes that are interesting and not challenging. Here are some more creative options to try.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Recipes for the Semi-Vegan', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:59:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7c279060-5bc6-5367-b6e7-bc000c6ab09d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7c279060-5bc6-5367-b6e7-bc000c6ab09d'}, {'abstract': 'A recipe for the classic Depression-era cocktail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/electric-current-fizz-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'A recipe for the classic Depression-era cocktail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Vigorously shake over ice:', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Electric Current Fizz', 'kicker': 'Recipe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T11:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e680372-e8c0-58ca-9123-758974f5f0b0', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e680372-e8c0-58ca-9123-758974f5f0b0'}, {'abstract': 'The ideal next-day drink mixes fizz with protein. (And yes, that means a raw egg.)', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/hangover-drink.html', 'snippet': 'The ideal next-day drink mixes fizz with protein. (And yes, that means a raw egg.)', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is sometimes the case that on the morning of Jan. 1, I arise bleary-eyed from a brief sleep with those famous final words of Beckett’s “Unnamable” on my mind: “You must go on, I can’t go on, I’ll go on.” Fortunately, I’m not reflecting on the whole messy enterprise of life itself. I’m concerned with drinking. Can I, should I, really keep going after a season of festive drinking that ended a little too festively?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01drink_span/01drink_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01drink_span/01drink_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01drink_span/01drink_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01drink_span/01drink_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01drink_span/01drink_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Perfect Hair of the Dog', 'kicker': 'Drink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drink It Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f9a667c-e50e-5c1c-bcfa-f9a99c3d1b47', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f9a667c-e50e-5c1c-bcfa-f9a99c3d1b47'}, {'abstract': 'Going semi-vegan: a resolution with no downside.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/mark-bittman-going-semi-vegan.html', 'snippet': 'Going semi-vegan: a resolution with no downside.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among your other resolutions — do more good? make more money? — you’ve probably made the annual pledge to eat better, although this concept may be more often reduced simply to “lose some weight.” The weight-loss obsession is both a national need and a neurotic urge (those last five pounds really don’t matter, either cosmetically or medically). But most of us do need to eat “better.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01eat_span/mag-01Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01eat_span/mag-01Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01eat_span/mag-01Eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01eat_span/mag-01Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01eat_span/mag-01Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Meat, No Dairy, No Problem', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Meat, No Dairy, No Problem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d795602-75aa-5cdb-afd3-9aa5e30e5f62', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d795602-75aa-5cdb-afd3-9aa5e30e5f62'}, {'abstract': 'This year may well be remembered as the most numbing ever for mainstream rock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/arts/music/rock-in-2011-hot-chelle-rae-foster-the-people-chevelle.html', 'snippet': 'This year may well be remembered as the most numbing ever for mainstream rock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every week since the end of May the band Sublime With Rome has had at least one song, sometimes two, on Billboard’s Rock Songs chart or its Hot Modern Rock Tracks chart, often both. The most successful have been “Panic” and “Take It or Leave It,” both true to the band’s mission to reincarnate the band Sublime, whose singer, Brad Nowell, died in 1996, and whose ska-lite Sublime With Rome both covers and, in its originals, shamelessly apes.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01ROCK4/01ROCK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01ROCK4/01ROCK4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01ROCK4/01ROCK4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01ROCK4/01ROCK4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01ROCK4/01ROCK4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year When Rock Just Spun Its Wheels', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Year When Rock Just Spun Its Wheels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Two Thousand Eleven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/230b0c09-b955-5105-bd1f-1358fca9fa65', 'word_count': 1478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/230b0c09-b955-5105-bd1f-1358fca9fa65'}, {'abstract': 'The former riot-grrrl rocker and “Portlandia” co-star Carrie Brownstein refuses to settle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/carrie-brownstein.html', 'snippet': 'The former riot-grrrl rocker and “Portlandia” co-star Carrie Brownstein refuses to settle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carrie Brownstein, the exuberant pixie rocker, says that if she had to live as one of the characters on her cult-hit sketch comedy series, “Portlandia,”\xad she’d be Toni, the depressed and passively sadistic feminist bookstore owner. This is not an obvious choice. Best known for her guitar heroics in Sleater-Kinney and, more recently, in her third band, Wild Flag, Brownstein, 37, is often found wearing bright red lipstick, a fashionable shag and skinny jeans. Toni, by contrast, favors burlap sacks and never smiles.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01brownstein/01brownstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01brownstein/01brownstein-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01brownstein/01brownstein-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 988, 'width': 810, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01brownstein/mag-01Brownstein-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01brownstein/mag-01Brownstein-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carrie Brownstein, Riot Grrrnup', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Riot Grrrnup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brownstein, Carrie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sleater-Kinney', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portland (Ore)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wild Flag (Music Group)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Weil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0442eecf-3742-5725-bdf6-906a8a54e46f', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0442eecf-3742-5725-bdf6-906a8a54e46f'}, {'abstract': 'The hackers who said they broke into the Web site of a security research firm now claim to have breached SpecialForces.com, a veterans-owned Web site that sells military-inspired merchandise and gives away a portion of profits to charity.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/stratfor-hackers-claim-another-attack/', 'snippet': 'The hackers who said they broke into the Web site of a security research firm now claim to have breached SpecialForces.com, a veterans-owned Web site that sells military-inspired merchandise and gives away a portion of profits to charity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hackers who said they attacked Stratfor Global Intelligence service, a security research group based in Austin, Tex., over the weekend have claimed a second target: Special Forces, a veterans-owned Web site that sells military-inspired merchandise and gives away a portion of profits to charity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stratfor Hackers Claim Another Attack', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Special Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anonymous (Internet Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:23:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/238bb7d8-ae37-5f65-aa55-3a0ed4642b89', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/238bb7d8-ae37-5f65-aa55-3a0ed4642b89'}, {'abstract': 'Methane is already at two and a half times the level that prevailed in the atmosphere before the Industrial Revolution. After a hiatus, it has been rising again in the last few years for reasons that researchers do not fully understand.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/the-puzzle-of-rising-methane/', 'snippet': 'Methane is already at two and a half times the level that prevailed in the atmosphere before the Industrial Revolution. After a hiatus, it has been rising again in the last few years for reasons that researchers do not fully understand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an article the other day about thawing permafrost, I mentioned that no signal had emerged in the atmosphere to suggest a sharp rise in methane emissions from the Arctic. But that does not mean all is stable with methane in the air — quite the contrary, in fact.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Puzzle of Rising Methane', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Methane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb333f56-efe9-541e-8490-5579631a9f6a', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb333f56-efe9-541e-8490-5579631a9f6a'}, {'abstract': 'Using a clever statistical method, a developer of social network applications says the population of Google’s social network increased by one-quarter in the past month. By the end of 2012, he said, it will have 400 million members.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/google-gains-traction-researcher-says/', 'snippet': 'Using a clever statistical method, a developer of social network applications says the population of Google’s social network increased by one-quarter in the past month. By the end of 2012, he said, it will have 400 million members.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google’s mission to compete with Facebook in social networking may be gaining speed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Google+ Gains Traction, Researcher Says', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEN, PAUL', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google+', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T12:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'by