her favorite clips.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carrie Brownstein Starter Kit', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brownstein, Carrie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Patti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'B-52s (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kids in the Hall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sleater-Kinney', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wild Flag (Music Group)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T16:31:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Weil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/433e0ae1-8844-520a-82ec-401fe5686642', 'word_count': 437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/433e0ae1-8844-520a-82ec-401fe5686642'}, {'abstract': 'When did doctors become “providers”? The term has a deliberate sterility to it that wrings out any sense of humanity, and connotes a widgetlike framework for that which is being “provided.”', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/the-provider-will-see-you-now/', 'snippet': 'When did doctors become “providers”? The term has a deliberate sterility to it that wrings out any sense of humanity, and connotes a widgetlike framework for that which is being “provided.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I can’t quite remember when the term “provider” slipped into the hospital lexicon. It was perhaps 10 years ago, when our hospital started hiring physician assistants and nurse practitioners to share the clinical load. In contrast to the regular staff nurses, who cared for the patients in conjunction with the doctors, physician assistants and nurse practitioners would see patients independently, the way the rest of the doctors did. So there needed to be a term that would include all three groups – physician assistants, nurse practitioners and doctors — who could have primary responsibility for patients.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Provider Will See You Now', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T16:35:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Ofri, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ofri', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7073ad18-c1f3-52a2-961f-0b871102ea42', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7073ad18-c1f3-52a2-961f-0b871102ea42'}, {'abstract': 'Hotel restaurants often reinvent themselves to keep up with food trends. At a time when new is the norm, though, some long-standing restaurants are sticking to tried and true.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/hotel-restaurant-menus-that-dont-grow-stale/', 'snippet': 'Hotel restaurants often reinvent themselves to keep up with food trends. At a time when new is the norm, though, some long-standing restaurants are sticking to tried and true.', 'lead_parag