b7-5112-a814-6f8f460635b8'}, {'abstract': 'The best commercial of 2011? It shouldn’t take too much trouble to guess what one Web site chose. Other top commercials may not be quite as memorable.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/wheelies-the-tv-commercial-edition/', 'snippet': 'The best commercial of 2011? It shouldn’t take too much trouble to guess what one Web site chose. Other top commercials may not be quite as memorable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The TV Commercial Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MSNBC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Porsche AG', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Jose Mercury News', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:17:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/011e2dbe-c038-5aa3-84cd-d7c303ff3748', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/011e2dbe-c038-5aa3-84cd-d7c303ff3748'}, {'abstract': 'With a new movie about the character due in February, a judge rules against a freelance writer.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/marvel-wins-court-battle-over-rights-to-ghost-rider/', 'snippet': 'With a new movie about the character due in February, a judge rules against a freelance writer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge in New York has ruled that Marvel Entertainment owns the rights to Ghost Rider, a supernatural character possessed by a spirit of vengeance who first appeared in 1972. The character was the subject of a lawsuit by a writer seeking to cash in on lucrative movie rights, according to a report by The Associated Press. United States District Judge Katherine Forrest threw out on Wednesday the four-year-old claims by ruling that Gary Friedrich, a freelance writer, gave up all ownership rights when he signed checks including language-relinquishing rights to the predecessor companies of Marvel Entertainment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Marvel Wins Court Battle Over Rights to Ghost Rider', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cage, Nicholas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirby, Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegel, Jerry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC Comics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Comics', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Entertainment', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'X-Men', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:19:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5d73b30-8fe6-5019-8417-040cfa742bb0', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5d73b30-8fe6-5019-8417-040cfa742bb0'}, {'abstract': 'Amid talks with Yahoo, the fast-growing Chinese Internet company has hired the Duberstein Group, led by Kenneth M. Duberstein, who was President Reagan’s White House chief of staff.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/alibaba-taps-lobbying-firm/', 'snippet': 'Amid talks with Yahoo, the fast-growing Chinese Internet company has hired the Duberstein Group, led by Kenneth M. Duberstein, who was President Reagan’s White House chief of staff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Alibaba Group, the fast-growing Chinese Internet company, has hired an influential lobbying firm in Washington as it navigates a potential takeover bid for Yahoo. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alibaba Taps Lobbying Firm', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ma, Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALIBABA.COM', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yahoo! Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bcf8737-d0c1-5d62-a1e4-527ad6a07e1a', 'word_count': 350, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bcf8737-d0c1-5d62-a1e4-527ad6a07e1a'}, {'abstract': 'In Chicago’s north side, three theaters have recently opened, seemingly defying economic logic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/travel/in-chicago-three-newcomers-to-the-theater-scene.html', 'snippet': 'In Chicago’s north side, three theaters have recently opened, seemingly defying economic logic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN times are tough, the arts suffer more than most. Pinched pocketbooks — both corporate and individual — force institutions to give up ambitious plans to focus on simply staying afloat. But in Chicago, three theaters have recently opened on the north side of the city, seemingly defying economic logic. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/travel/01HEADSUP_SPAN/01HEADSUP_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/travel/01HEADSUP_SPAN/01HEADSUP_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/travel/01HEADSUP_SPAN/01HEADSUP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/travel/01HEADSUP_SPAN/01HEADSUP_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/travel/01HEADSUP_SPAN/01HEADSUP_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Curtains Up for Chicago Stages', 'kicker': 'Heads Up', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curtains Up for Chicago Stages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:25:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a75b8f90-6463-5692-b17f-97c0fc0084bd', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a75b8f90-6463-5692-b17f-97c0fc0084bd'}, {'abstract': 'Architecture magazines and fanzines are having a moment, and London is very much a part of the scene.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/london-underground-architecture-in-print/', 'snippet': 'Architecture magazines and fanzines are having a moment, and London is very much a part of the scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last few years have seen a new generation of alternative publications and editorial talent emerge, and London is very much a part of the scene. This month saw the release of the fourth issue of P.E.A.R. (Paper for Emerging Architectural Research). Printed on newspaper stock, this zine (included in “Archizines,” a show I curated at the Architectural Association School of Architecture) was started by the editorial collective of Rashid Ali, Matthew Butcher, Julian Krueger and Megan O’Shea, with the designer Avni Patel, in 2009. A few issues in, they have really hit their stride. “The paper aims to re-establish the fanzine as a primary medium for the dissemination of architectural ideas, musings, research and works, and to present the complexity and variety of architectural practice,” O’Shea explains. “We actively seek to publish thinking that wouldn’t ordinarily be seen in the mainstream architectural press, and we are interested in showing that the architectural is concerned with more than just architecture.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Underground | Architecture in Print', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elias Redstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Redstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6e2a39c-8cbc-5cc0-8d49-1bbbeacdf4f6', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6e2a39c-8cbc-5cc0-8d49-1bbbeacdf4f6'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times submitted a set of questions to each of the major presidential candidates about their views on the scope and limits of the powers they would wield if elected. Here are their answers in full.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2011/12/29/us/election-news/candidates-on-executive-power.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Times submitted a set of questions to each of the major presidential candidates about their views on the scope and limits of the powers they would wield if elected. Here are their answers in full.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times submitted a set of questions to each of the major presidential candidates about their views on the scope and limits of the powers they would wield if elected. Here are their answers in full.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Candidates on Executive Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Privilege, Doctrine of', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:31:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/af57b297-6c56-504d-9bbb-509f26ab54f7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/af57b297-6c56-504d-9bbb-509f26ab54f7'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Bailey, if healthy, is a great addition to the Red Sox. But the Boston bullpen now rests on the closer’s fragile right elbow.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/red-sox-add-a-closer-and-some-anxiety/', 'snippet': 'Andrew Bailey, if healthy, is a great addition to the Red Sox. But the Boston bullpen now rests on the closer’s fragile right elbow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the first real move of the Ben Cherington administration, the Red Sox appear to have secured a top closer, Andrew Bailey, for a reasonable price. But for Boston fans, famous for their worries and complaints, the new object of their obsession will be Bailey’s right elbow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Red Sox Add a Closer and Some Anxiety', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cherington, Ben', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Red Sox', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oakland Athletics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wagner College', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:32:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bba8020-566b-5ee2-9e14-40344aa2b6d3', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bba8020-566b-5ee2-9e14-40344aa2b6d3'}, {'abstract': 'A city lawmaker raises concerns about a police sting operation; and a promotion for a precinct commander following the killing of one of his officers.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/criticism-of-undercover-operation-aimed-at-smartphone-thefts/', 'snippet': 'A city lawmaker raises concerns about a police sting operation; and a promotion for a precinct commander following the killing of one of his officers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To combat a surge in the theft of handheld electronic devices, which contributed to a high rate of grand larcenies in the city this year, the Police Department devised Operation Take Back, aimed at anyone seeking a stolen iPhone or iPad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Criticism of Undercover Operation Aimed at Smartphone Thefts', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gentile, Vincent J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:40:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a5e38f7-67fa-504c-8ad6-7f4acb40436f', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a5e38f7-67fa-504c-8ad6-7f4acb40436f'}, {'abstract': '“Ghost Light” is a new play inspired by the life of George Moscone, the mayor of San Francisco who was assassinated alongside Harvey Milk in 1978.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/theater/a-play-inspired-by-slain-san-francisco-mayor-moscone.html', 'snippet': '“Ghost Light” is a new play inspired by the life of George Moscone, the mayor of San Francisco who was assassinated alongside Harvey Milk in 1978.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERKELEY, Calif. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01MOSCONE1/01MOSCONE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01MOSCONE1/01MOSCONE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01MOSCONE1/01MOSCONE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 619, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01MOSCONE1/01MOSCONE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01MOSCONE1/01MOSCONE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Illuminating the Legacy of a Fallen Father', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illuminating the Legacy of a Fallen Father', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Milk, Harvey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berkeley Repertory Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taccone, Tony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:48:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/852299ee-c5b4-559f-ba26-74e548c07010', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852299ee-c5b4-559f-ba26-74e548c07010'}, {'abstract': 'Angeli Caffe, a beloved and influential Los Angeles restaurant, will close after Jan. 8.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/an-l-a-landmark-angeli-caffe-is-closing/', 'snippet': 'Angeli Caffe, a beloved and influential Los Angeles restaurant, will close after Jan. 8.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was just a neighborhood restaurant. But starting in the mid-1980s, from its small perch in Los Angeles, Angeli Caffe helped popularize a new kind of California-Mediterranean cuisine, spawned a cookbook and launched the reputation of its owner and chef, Evan Kleiman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An L.A. Landmark, Angeli Caffe Is Closing', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LA Weekly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:50:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0281a2a3-ef88-54c8-8970-15f7a35a9602', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0281a2a3-ef88-54c8-8970-15f7a35a9602'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney is playing increasingly hard in Iowa in the waning days before the caucuses.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/romney-campaigns-hard-in-iowa-as-caucuses-near/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney is playing increasingly hard in Iowa in the waning days before the caucuses.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:42 p.m. | Updated  DES MOINES — After all the hemming and hawing about whether Iowa would be a critical element of his bid to be the Republican nominee, the question for Mitt Romney is now moot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Campaigns Hard in Iowa as Caucuses Near', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T17:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear and Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a677ad90-9de6-5dc9-845b-9e279f7519e0', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a677ad90-9de6-5dc9-845b-9e279f7519e0'}, {'abstract': 'Classical music, like other performing arts, has long depended on the 1 percent while also struggling to fight the perception — an unfair perception — that it is elitist and inaccessible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/arts/music/balcony-seats-can-help-economic-inequalities-in-arts.html', 'snippet': 'Classical music, like other performing arts, has long depended on the 1 percent while also struggling to fight the perception — an unfair perception — that it is elitist and inaccessible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Juilliard School’s inviting 275-seat Paul Hall was nearly packed recently for an early-evening concert, a free 60-minute Liederabend program on which four gifted student singers, each paired with a comparably gifted pianist, sang songs by Schumann. Classical music has struggled for a long time to fight the perception — an unfair perception — that it is elitist and inaccessible. But here was a program that didn’t even require tickets. People just showed up. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01OCCUPY1_SPAN/01OCCUPY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'