nts for, by and about children, teenagers and families in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/arts/spare-times-for-children-for-dec-30-jan-5.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to events for, by and about children, teenagers and families in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prospect Park Audubon Center', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spare Times: For Children, for Dec. 30-Jan. 5', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spare Times: For Children', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T22:23:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurel Graeber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graeber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0356356-92ba-53ea-b869-47a6fa9dedf2', 'word_count': 3916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0356356-92ba-53ea-b869-47a6fa9dedf2'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from North Korea, Turkey, Bahrain and Iran.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/29/pictures-of-the-day-north-korea-and-elsewhere-3/', 'snippet': 'Photographs from North Korea, Turkey, Bahrain and Iran.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photographs from North Korea, Turkey, Bahrain and Iran.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: North Korea and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahrain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T22:24:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9db60f64-9b5f-5de8-b5ae-ffee543f8142', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9db60f64-9b5f-5de8-b5ae-ffee543f8142'}, {'abstract': 'Classical music fans can look forward to ample chances to hear works by John Cage in 2012, his centennial year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/arts/music/juilliards-focus-festival-celebrates-john-cages-centenary.html', 'snippet': 'Classical music fans can look forward to ample chances to hear works by John Cage in 2012, his centennial year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Cage is an anomaly among contemporary composers for a great many reasons, not the least of which is that his name and some of his conceptual notions are more widely known than his music. His most famous score is silent — or at least people think it is. Actually, that work — “4’33”\xa0” — was composed for Maverick Concerts, a summer festival near Woodstock, N.Y., held in an open shed, and Cage’s hope was that listeners would focus on the natural sounds that filtered into the concert space as the piece was performed. Obviously, the piece works in indoor halls as well, once you understand how to approach it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30CLASSICALYr/30CLASSICALYr-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30CLASSICALYr/30CLASSICALYr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30CLASSICALYr/30CLASSICALYr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Cage at His Briefest to Wagner at His Longest', 'kicker': 'Classical Music | The Year Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Cage at His Briefest to Wagner at His Longest: Classical Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cage, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T22:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a9de319-3edd-59a9-9ccb-e800a3eb36be', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a9de319-3edd-59a9-9ccb-e800a3eb36be'}, {'abstract': 'More than 70 television shows are making their debuts or having their season premieres in January, among them “30 Rock” on NBC and the new show “Luck,” with Dustin Hoffman, on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/arts/television/on-tv-new-shows-and-season-premieres-set-for-january.html', 'snippet': 'More than 70 television shows are making their debuts or having their season premieres in January, among them “30 Rock” on NBC and the new show “Luck,” with Dustin Hoffman, on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New TV shows are liable to pop up any time these days, but over the last few years January has solidified its position as the start of television’s second season. There are probably more series making their debuts or having their season premieres next month than there were during the official start of the 2011-12 broadcast network season in September. A quick scan turns up more than 70.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30MIDSEASON_SPAN/30MIDSEASON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30MIDSEASON_SPAN/30MIDSEASON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30MIDSEASON_SPAN/30MIDSEASON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30MIDSEASON_SPAN/30MIDSEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30MIDSEASON_SPAN/30MIDSEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Second Chance for TV’s Season', 'kicker': 'Television | The Year Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Second Chance For TV’s Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-29T22:34:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5388c554-dceb-52a2-8469-6576868fc6b1', 'word_count': 1481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5388c554-dceb-52a2-8469-6576868fc6b1'}, {'abstract': 'Among the musical and operatic offerings in 2012 will be Robert Lepage’s new production of Wagner’s “Ring” cycle in April and May.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/arts/music/met-to-present-wagners-entire-ring-cycle.html', 'snippet': 'Among the musical and operatic offerings in 2012 will be Robert Lepage’s new production of Wagner’s “Ring” cycle in April and May.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You cannot fairly assess a production of Wagner’s “Ring” until you see the entire cycle of four operas. Wagner buffs will finally get that chance this spring when the Metropolitan Opera presents the director Robert Lepage’s new production of the “Ring” in three complete cycles in April and May. This ambitious venture is rightly the most anticipated event of the Met season. First, “Götterdämmerung,” the last installment, will be introduced on Jan. 27, with Fabio Luisi, the Met’s principal conductor, on the podium. The pros and cons of Mr. Lepage’s work in the first three operas have been heatedly discussed, and the debate will surely continue.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30OPERAYr/30OPERAYr-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30OPERAYr/30OPERAYr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/30/arts/30OPERAYr/30OPERAYr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Met to Present Entire ‘Ring’ Cycle, and More', 'kicker': 'Opera | The Year Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_h