 Juan Carlos as a suspect in a fraud scandal that involves millions of dollars in public money given to his private foundation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/world/europe/spain-kings-son-in-law-summoned-to-court-over-fraud-allegations.html', 'snippet': 'A high court in Spain issued a summons to the son-in-law of King Juan Carlos as a suspect in a fraud scandal that involves millions of dollars in public money given to his private foundation.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A high court in Spain issued a summons Thursday to the son-in-law of King Juan Carlos as a suspect in a spreading fraud scandal that involves millions of dollars in public money given to his private foundation. A judge in Palma, Majorca, called on the son-in-law, Iñaki Urdangarin, 43, to face questioning on Feb. 6 about allegations that he used his royal credentials to secure multimillion-dollar contracts for his foundation, the Noós Institute, to organize sports and tourism events. After he was named as a formal suspect on Thursday, the Spanish palace and a number of government institutions issued careful comments offering “respect” for the decision of judges. The king has taken a number of steps to keep a distance from his son-in-law, whose family did not spend Christmas Eve with the royal family for the first time in years. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spain: King’s Son-in-Law Summoned to Court Over Fraud Allegations', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spain: King’s Son-in-Law Summoned To Court Over Fraud Allegations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Juan Carlos I', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T00:49:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doreen Carvajal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doreen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carvajal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d48fdf7-76b5-510a-bc58-a5cf2196ec2e', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d48fdf7-76b5-510a-bc58-a5cf2196ec2e'}, {'abstract': 'Even as they advocate for limited government, many of the Republican candidates hold expansive views about the scope of the executive authority they would wield if elected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/us/politics/gop-field-has-broad-views-on-executive-power.html', 'snippet': 'Even as they advocate for limited government, many of the Republican candidates hold expansive views about the scope of the executive authority they would wield if elected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Even as they advocate for limited government, many of the Republican presidential candidates hold expansive views about the scope of the executive powers they would wield if elected — including the ability to authorize the targeted killing of United States citizens they deem threats and to launch military attacks without Congressional permission.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/us/SUB-POWER/SUB-POWER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/30/us/SUB-POWER/SUB-POWER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/us/SUB-POWER/SU