s raised against the Kremlin this month after parliamentary elections widely dismissed as fraudulent, perhaps the most surprising was that of Patriarch Kirill I, the leader of the Russian Orthodox Church, who defended popular protests as a “lawful negative reaction” to corruption.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/world/orthodox1/orthodox1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/30/world/orthodox1/orthodox1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/world/orthodox1/orthodox1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/world/orthodox1/orthodox1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/30/world/orthodox1/orthodox1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disputed Voting Turns Church, a Kremlin Ally, Into Its Critic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disputed Voting Turns Church, A Kremlin Ally, Into Its Critic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirill I', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Orthodox Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T01:58:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Kishkovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kishkovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b7ae29d-ec98-56c8-a648-0b6ae30b7c1b', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b7ae29d-ec98-56c8-a648-0b6ae30b7c1b'}, {'abstract': 'The emerging question is to what extent Ron Paul’s more dovish stances are so far out of the Republican orthodoxy that they will limit his support in many places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/us/politics/pauls-foreign-policy-stance-divides-many-gop-voters.html', 'snippet': 'The emerging question is to what extent Ron Paul’s more dovish stances are so far out of the Republican orthodoxy that they will limit his support in many places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES — This month, a wealthy Republican called up two officers who served as platoon leaders in Iraq and Afghanistan with a proposition: He would lend them his private jet, and they would fly around Iowa to Representative Ron Paul’s campaign events in the weeks before the Jan. 3 caucuses, telling reporters at each location that the congressman’s foreign policy and military positions were dangerous and naïve.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/30/us/PAUL/PAUL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url