as sought to define 2012 as a clash of competing visions for ensuring the future for an American middle class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES – The 2012 presidential election, Mitt Romney said on Thursday, “could be our last chance” to save the United States from Greece-like bankruptcy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and Republicans Seek to Define 2012', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T12:33:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard W. Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f9543da-7167-5a3a-a9e7-269cf5739b88', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f9543da-7167-5a3a-a9e7-269cf5739b88'}, {'abstract': 'All year, Democrats have been focused like a laser on Mitt Romney, even as Republicans flirted with other hopefuls.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/democrats-keep-focus-on-romney/', 'snippet': 'All year, Democrats have been focused like a laser on Mitt Romney, even as Republicans flirted with other hopefuls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES — If Mitt Romney wins the Iowa caucuses and goes on to eventually snag the Republican nomination, the prize for the best crystal ball may go to none other than the Democratic Party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Democrats Keep Focus on Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T12:33:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1b0fd35-be10-550e-af90-45754f7f904e', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1b0fd35-be10-550e-af90-45754f7f904e'}, {'abstract': 'A day after two of its foreign workers were gunned down, the aid group, Doctors Without Borders, vowed on Friday to continue working in Somalia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/world/africa/two-foreign-aid-workers-killed-in-somali-capital.html', 'snippet': 'A day after two of its foreign workers were gunned down, the aid group, Doctors Without Borders, vowed on Friday to continue working in Somalia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOGADISHU, Somalia — A day after two of its foreign workers were gunned down by a former colleague, a medical aid group vowed on Friday to continue working in Somalia. The attack was the latest in a series on humanitarian workers and journalists, underscoring the dangers of operating in this war-torn country.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/world/30somalia-image/30s