, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b5f1e63-da8e-54ba-a9d0-462fe76c0c82', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b5f1e63-da8e-54ba-a9d0-462fe76c0c82'}, {'abstract': 'Clutching the side of a submersible while wearing a “rebreather” device, a scientist descends to an biodiverse undersea habitat and sees far more than he had expected.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/high-tech-tools-primeval-wonders/', 'snippet': 'Clutching the side of a submersible while wearing a “rebreather” device, a scientist descends to an biodiverse undersea habitat and sees far more than he had expected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some days ago here, in a rich biodiverse ecosystem off the Costa Rican coast, we had planned to take another step closer to my lifelong dream of fully integrating advanced closed-circuit rebreather technology with a sophisticated submersible to leverage the strengths of each for exploring the coral reef “twilight zone” — that is, coral-reef habitat at depths of about 200 to 500 feet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'High-Tech Tools, Primeval Wonders', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T14:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard L. Pyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Pyle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/096fb953-9128-5042-9dae-01c94ab456a9', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/096fb953-9128-5042-9dae-01c94ab456a9'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/what-were-reading-342/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chicago Reader: It seems like the perfect cooking ingredient for the close of a year: ash. — Jeff Gordinier', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T14:56:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b355b196-e714-5ae0-8a57-490888054fea', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b355b196-e714-5ae0-8a57-490888054fea'}, {'abstract': 'Working to keep the Latin sound alive in a small pocket of Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/at-a-brooklyn-music-store-resolving-to-adapt/', 'snippet': 'Working to keep the Latin sound alive in a small pocket of Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A cheery holiday card from Tati and Gerry at Ansonia Records rests on the counter of Manny Rivera’s Brooklyn music store, hidden behind a pile of compact discs, domino sets and assorted tropical knick-knacks. It’s a nice, personal touch in a business that has long since lost it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At a Brooklyn Music Store, Resolving to Adapt', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, MARC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T14:58:17+0000', 'document_typ