aph': 'THERE are 16 shots in Lars von Trier’s hauntingly beautiful overture to “Melancholia,” a movie about love, family and the apocalypse. The movie, among Mr. von Trier’s greatest, stars Kirsten Dunst as Justine, a young advertising copywriter who, shortly after she gets married endures two separate yet related catastrophes: A wedding party at an oceanside golf resort owned by her brother-in-law ends with her new husband leaving. This in turn brings on the depression that overtakes her and seems to inaugurate the end of the world or her dream of the same. Many of the movie’s themes are introduced in the first eight minutes, a masterpiece in miniature that is a palimpsest of literary, artistic and cinematic allusions.', 'print_section': 'MT', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01DARGIS_SPAN/01DARGIS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 256, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01DARGIS_SPAN/01DARGIS_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 256}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01DARGIS_SPAN/01DARGIS_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01DARGIS_SPAN/01DARGIS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01DARGIS_SPAN/01DARGIS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Is How the End Begins', 'kicker': 'The Oscars', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'This Is How the End Begins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'von Trier, Lars', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunst, Kirsten', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gainsbourg, Charlotte', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be485ebb-1efd-507e-9768-2fd8b416e184', 'word_count': 1617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be485ebb-1efd-507e-9768-2fd8b416e184'}, {'abstract': 'In essays and poems, the imprisoned Chinese poet demonstrates a considerable amount of anger while retaining his Gandhian nonviolent spirit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/liu-xiaobos-plea-for-the-human-spirit.html', 'snippet': 'In essays and poems, the imprisoned Chinese poet demonstrates a considerable amount of anger while retaining his Gandhian nonviolent spirit.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I have no enemies, and no hatred.” Liu Xiaobo, the 2010 Nobel Peace Prize winner, spoke those words on Dec. 23, 2009, just before he was sentenced to 11 years in prison for “incitement of subversion of state power.” It was his fourth jail term since the Tiananmen Square massacre of 1989. Liu is the only Chinese citizen to win any Nobel while living in China and, as Perry Link notes in introducing a new collection of Liu’s writings, one of only five Nobel Peace Prize winners unable to appear in Oslo to receive the gold medal: “In 1935, Carl von Ossietzky was held in a Nazi prison; in 1975, Andrei Sakharov was not allowed to leave the Soviet Union; in 1983, Lech Walesa feared he would be barred from re-entering Poland if he went to Oslo; and in 1991, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi was under house arrest in Burma.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01MIRSKY/01MIRSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01MIRSKY/01MIRSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01MIRSKY/01MIRSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01MIRSKY/01MIRSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01MIRSKY/01MIRSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Liu Xiaobo’s Plea for the Human Spirit', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exiled at Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu Xiaobo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:03:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mirsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mirsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e02c322-7cc1-5045-9aeb-157e4ab3adb0', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e02c322-7cc1-5045-9aeb-157e4ab3adb0'}, {'abstract': 'Joyce E. Chaplin’s path to Benjamin Franklin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Joyce E. Chaplin’s path to Benjamin Franklin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It might have seemed inevitable that Joyce E. Chaplin’s interests in science, technology and early American history would lead her to that grand figure of early American science — Benjamin Franklin. But her path to him wasn’t inevitable at all. Years ago, when a colleague suggested Franklin as a subject, her reaction, as she describes it in an e-mail message, was: “Boring, too obvious.” Only after she had written books on agricultural innovation in the 18th and early 19th centuries and on frontier science and technology did she turn to Franklin, who, she is quick to point out, “of course isn’t boring at all.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 634, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:04:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f168e07a-5aa4-515e-aac5-8b43c01034cb', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f168e07a-5aa4-515e-aac5-8b43c01034cb'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent essay about rereading books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/read-it-again-sam.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent essay about rereading books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Read It Again, Sam’', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Read It Again, Sam’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:05:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e9efd55-f071-5ec3-bdc1-56efbf812d5e', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e9efd55-f071-5ec3-bdc1-56efbf812d5e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Carl T. Bogus’s “Buckley: William F. Buckley Jr. and the Rise of American Conservatism.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/buckley.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Carl T. Bogus’s “Buckley: William F. Buckley Jr. and the Rise of American Conservatism.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Buckley’', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Buckley’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:05:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae41de03-a401-52d1-93cf-4313dadf6f55', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae41de03-a401-52d1-93cf-4313dadf6f55'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of “Life Upon These Shores” by Henry Louis Gates Jr.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/life-upon-these-shores.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of “Life Upon These Shores” by Henry Louis Gates Jr.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Life Upon These Shores’', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Life Upon These Shores’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:05:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e313b6a-0961-5975-9e42-b825775a1945', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e313b6a-0961-5975-9e42-b825775a1945'}, {'abstract': 'Robert K. Massie is at No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list this week with his biography of Catherine the Great, who “always kept a book in her room and carried another in her pocket.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Robert K. Massie is at No. 8 on the hardcover nonfiction list this week with his biography of Catherine the Great, who “always kept a book in her room and carried another in her pocket.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE READER: One of the things Robert K. Massie reveals in his magisterial biography of Catherine the Great, at No. 8 in its sixth week on the hardcover nonfiction list, is that the empress had a voracious appetite for books — especially as an escape from her prosaic husband, Czar Peter III, whose conversation was “of a dullness,” Massie quotes her saying, “that I have never seen equaled. When he left me, the most boring book seemed delightful.” Catherine read plenty in Russian, Massie reports, “but French was the language she preferred, and she read French books indiscriminately, picking up whatever her ladies-in-waiting happened to be reading. She always kept a book in her room and carried another in her pocket.” The empress eventually bought Diderot’s library for more than £50,000, thus sealing her reputation as a patron of the arts and making the rest of Europe finally take notice of Russia as a cultural center.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/books/review/08catherine/08catherine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 301, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/29/books/review/08catherine/08catherine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/29/books/review/08catherine/08catherine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:06:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4bf3f72-33bb-543c-86b5-78aa03a5e116', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4bf3f72-33bb-543c-86b5-78aa03a5e116'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE THIRD REICH, by Roberto Bolaño. Translated by Natasha Wimmer (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $25.) A German vacationing in Spain plays a World War II strategy game with a mysterious burn victim in a novel found among Bolaño’s papers after his death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors Choice: Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:06:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac59956e-9ac3-57a0-80d9-2cc6bea41c72', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac59956e-9ac3-57a0-80d9-2cc6bea41c72'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MASTER SWITCH: The Rise and Fall of Information Empires, by Tim Wu (Vintage, $15.95.) This history of modern communications — covering phones, radio, television, movies and, finally, the Internet — is organized around what Wu calls “the Cycle”: “from somebody’s hobby to somebody’s industry; from jury-rigged contraption to slick production marvel; from a freely accessible channel to one strictly controlled by a single corporation or cartel — from open to closed system.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:06:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/440d3faa-3890-5897-a89f-38f0afc304ad', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/440d3faa-3890-5897-a89f-38f0afc304ad'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph Epstein argues that gossip serves a number of worthwhile purposes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/gossip-the-untrivial-pursuit-by-joseph-epstein-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph Epstein argues that gossip serves a number of worthwhile purposes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of us scriveners feel obliged to call our books something catchy in the hope of impressing potential readers with how clever we are. Not so Joseph Epstein. “What’s your book about?” “Gossip.” “What’s it called?” “Gossip.” Epstein has repeatedly opted for titles with the generic ring of labels on the files he must have kept for his research: “Ambition,” “Friendship,” “Snobbery,” “Envy.” On the part of any other writer, this might be taken as a sign of laziness or exhaustion. But in Epstein’s case, it seems a bold grab to own a great big subject, even — or especially — one on which the Almighty has had a tendency to dominate the conversation. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01BRUBACH/01BRUBACH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01BRUBACH/01BRUBACH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01BRUBACH/01BRUBACH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dirt About Gossip', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tell-All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gossip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Epstein, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:08:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Brubach', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brubach', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6611d6dd-2dec-58de-9e4f-6d9cecb10175', 'word_count': 1403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6611d6dd-2dec-58de-9e4f-6d9cecb10175'}, {'abstract': 'A mugging changes the lives of several characters in Penelope Lively’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/how-it-all-began-by-penelope-lively-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A mugging changes the lives of several characters in Penelope Lively’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How it begins: “The pavement \xadrises up and hits her.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Cokal/Cokal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 357, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Cokal/Cokal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 357}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Cokal/Cokal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 654, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Cokal/Cokal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Cokal/Cokal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Penelope Lively’s Anatomy of a Mugging', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unintended Consequences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lively, Penelope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:09:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susann Cokal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cokal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcd98ce1-6544-5481-81d5-5e528b6f9eda', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcd98ce1-6544-5481-81d5-5e528b6f9eda'}, {'abstract': 'A pregnant 15-year-old and her family await the arrival of Hurricane Katrina in this National Book Award-winning novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/salvage-the-bones-by-jesmyn-ward-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A pregnant 15-year-old and her family await the arrival of Hurricane Katrina in this National Book Award-winning novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Job has nothing on 15-year-old Esch. She’s poor and pregnant and plain unlucky. Mama’s dead, Daddy’s a drunk and dinner is Top Ramen every night. Sex is the only thing that has ever come easily to her. When the boys used to take her down in the dirt or in the back seats of stripped cars in her front yard, she could escape briefly, pretend to be Psyche, Eurydice, Daphne, her favorite nymphs and goddesses from the Greek myths. But Manny, the boy who put the baby inside her, won’t look at her anymore. Esch can’t lie down in the dirt and pretend to be someone else or anywhere else. She’s stuck in shabby Bois Sauvage, a predominantly black Mississippi bayou town in the direct path of a hurricane they’re calling Katrina.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surviving Katrina', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Wind and the Rain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HURRICANE KATRINA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:09:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63e18e31-3959-53bb-b25c-cda22027ca4c', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63e18e31-3959-53bb-b25c-cda22027ca4c'}, {'abstract': 'Pico Iyer writes about the kinship he feels with Graham Greene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/the-man-within-my-head-by-pico-iyer-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Pico Iyer writes about the kinship he feels with Graham Greene.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wilson sat on the balcony of the Bedford Hotel with his bald pink knees thrust against the ironwork.” Those who love Graham Greene — and their numbers are legion — will recognize this sentence, the first line of his quietly devastating novel “The Heart of the Matter,” published in 1948.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01SCHILLINGER/01SCHILLINGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 786, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01SCHILLINGER/01SCHILLINGER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01SCHILLINGER/01SCHILLINGER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pico Iyer’s Kinship With Graham Greene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fellow Traveler', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iyer, Pico', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greene, Graham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:12:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccc618fd-fad7-5dca-8cd6-43a59afe9b4b', 'word_count': 1178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccc618fd-fad7-5dca-8cd6-43a59afe9b4b'}, {'abstract': 'A fixture in the worlds of art and fashion reflects on a splendid career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/some-of-my-lives-by-rosamond-bernier-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A fixture in the worlds of art and fashion reflects on a splendid career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late summer of 1926, when Rosamond Bernier was not quite 10 years old, her father put her on a ship and sent her off to her English boarding school, all by herself. Every evening she changed into a party dress, ate cold smoked tongue and then retired to the smoking room for gambling. “I had spectacular luck,” Bernier writes in her bonbon of a book, “Some of My Lives: A Scrapbook Memoir.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01Thomas1/01Thomas1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 534, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01Thomas1/01Thomas1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01Thomas1/01Thomas1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rosamond Bernier and the Art of Living', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art of Living', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernier, Rosamond', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:13:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louisa Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1496481e-b452-542c-af12-a32ae03007ca', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1496481e-b452-542c-af12-a32ae03007ca'}, {'abstract': 'In 1958, Richard and Mildred Loving were arrested in a nighttime raid in their bedroom. Their crime: being married to each other.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/01/magazine/look-loving-versus-bigotry.html', 'snippet': 'In 1958, Richard and Mildred Loving were arrested in a nighttime raid in their bedroom. Their crime: being married to each other.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1958, Richard and Mildred Loving were arrested in a nighttime raid in their bedroom. Their crime: being married to each other.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case of Loving v. Bigotry', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Center of Photography', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loving, Richard', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loving, Mildred', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:12+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f14a670c-2dad-56a1-8fa4-1f50c18e8f11', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f14a670c-2dad-56a1-8fa4-1f50c18e8f11'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/reply-all-benton-harbor.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to the quote from the unelected Mr. Harris about the city finance director he fired: “He was smart and articulate, but he just wasn’t doing anything that I couldn’t do.” Is that the new criterion for dissolving democracy? I find this situation very troubling. If a corporation has laudable reasons for establishing a company town, it should do the things that uplift the residents and help them participate in their own stability. It is obvious that what Whirlpool and the current state government were really after was a takeover of the shorefront property for a resort that benefits the wealthy outsiders and edges out the undesirable community who has been gifted the shorefront land. Being poor does not negate protection under the law and does not eliminate any American’s constitutional rights. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Strange Second Life of a Michigan Factory Town', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Strange Second Life of a Michigan Factory Town', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benton Harbor (Mich)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/30840b09-0236-536b-8f95-7872684c1d3c', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30840b09-0236-536b-8f95-7872684c1d3c'}, {'abstract': 'The first, worst gig of a future radical-poet-musician.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/pieces-of-a-very-young-man.html', 'snippet': 'The first, worst gig of a future radical-poet-musician.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was not motivated by “things” that dipped into my mother’s finances. I was not on her for this or that; I had what I needed to get by. I managed to hold myself together by hustling. I made deliveries up the hill to old ladies on my bike. In the evening I met the newspaper trucks for the night editions and rolled them back to a corner candy store on Fordham Road and Sedgwick Avenue. Between what I covered with the wagon and peddled up that hill, I kept pocket money that I often doubled by betting on stickball games at the park in the afternoons.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01lives/mag-01Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01lives/01lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01lives/01lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pieces of a (Very Young) Man', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pieces of a (Very Young) Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gil Scott-Heron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott-Heron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7eb4bbc2-e738-5c22-8b70-45062f51196f', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7eb4bbc2-e738-5c22-8b70-45062f51196f'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/reply-all-pakistanis-have-a-point.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The article by Bill Keller is an incisive and balanced analysis of what has brought the Pakistan-United States relationship to its low point. Both sides have no clear understanding of the vital interests of the other. For Pakistan, it is not in its interest to antagonize the Taliban, who are sure to come back to power in Afghanistan once the Americans leave, but at the very least, it can prevent the Taliban from attacking U.S. and NATO forces from its soil and not give any support in the planning and execution of such attacks. On the other hand, the U.S. fails to understand how alarming it is for Pakistan, and rightfully so, to see the growing strengthening of the India-Afghanistan axis, both its bitter enemies. Successive Afghan governments since the day that Pakistan was created have maintained a very hostile attitude except for the few years under Taliban rule. Therefore, in order not to have two unfriendly neighbors, Pakistan has chosen to bet on the return of the Taliban.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pakistanis Have a Point', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Pakistanis Have a Point', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5f6a0e8-eaa0-5147-b2f5-0701f5d5a6f8', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5f6a0e8-eaa0-5147-b2f5-0701f5d5a6f8'}, {'abstract': 'Separation of policy and philanthropy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/politics-en-pointe.html', 'snippet': 'Separation of policy and philanthropy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was excited to take my granddaughter, Rachel, to see a local production of “The Nutcracker.” But this season, the production was being underwritten in large part by David Koch, a billionaire who supports numerous political causes that I think harm our nation. He also supports many worthy medical, educational and arts organizations, but I think those good works buy the complicity of the institutions in question. I’m sure my granddaughter would have liked to see the show, but rather than validate this patron’s actions and beliefs, I boycotted it. Should those who feel as I do have joined me? EDITH PALMER, BROOKLYN', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Politics en Pointe', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Politics en Pointe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boycotts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, David H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/df3b7caa-a852-526c-8188-112894a43365', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df3b7caa-a852-526c-8188-112894a43365'}, {'abstract': 'Like the symphony orchestra, psychoanalysis and much of Western civilization, the snow globe traces its heritage to Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/the-self-contained-snowstorm.html', 'snippet': 'Like the symphony orchestra, psychoanalysis and much of Western civilization, the snow globe traces its heritage to Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like the symphony orchestra, psychoanalysis and much of Western civilization, the snow globe traces its heritage to Europe. The first known record of the venerable paperweight-turned-kitsch-souvenir, writes Nancy McMichael in “Snowdomes,” the definitive account of the tchotchke, was entered in the 1878 “Reports of the United States Commissioners to the Paris Universal Exposition.” It tells of seven French contraptions featuring heavy glass paperweights filled with water and “white powder, which, when turned upside down, falls in imitation of a snowstorm.” Eleven years later, the next exposition showcased both the 986-foot-tall Eiffel Tower and an inches-tall ceramic replica in a water-filled dome.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01wmt/01wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 638}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01wmt/mag-01WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1012, 'width': 874, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01wmt/mag-01WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01wmt/mag-01WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Self-Contained Snowstorm', 'kicker': 'Who Made That?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Snow Globe)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memorabilia and Souvenirs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernsen, Corbin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11442562-b563-5625-8e3f-88b743ec6bac', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11442562-b563-5625-8e3f-88b743ec6bac'}, {'abstract': 'But the billionaire founder of Go Daddy says he knows his audience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/magazine/bob-parsons.html', 'snippet': 'But the billionaire founder of Go Daddy says he knows his audience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You just sold 65 percent of GoDaddy.com, your Web site domain registry, to a private equity group led by Kohlberg Kravis Roberts for $2.25 billion. Do you think they’ll want to tone down your ads featuring extremely busty models? Our advertising isn’t edgy because I’m an edgy guy. I am, all right, but our advertising is edgy because we stumbled on something that absolutely worked right out of the gate. The guys at K.K.R., Silver Lake and Technology Crossover Ventures are savvy businessmen. They like to do stuff that works. So if our advertising could be softened and still work, they’d be all for it. But none of our tests have ever proved that to be true.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 427, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': "Bob Parsons Doesn't Do Subtle", 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Bob Parsons Doesn't Do Subtle", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Go Daddy Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:14:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b624967-4106-5987-ba5e-ce7bd906aa70', 'word_count': 722, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b624967-4106-5987-ba5e-ce7bd906aa70'}, {'abstract': 'The author of best-selling fantasy no