None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a795fd50-87e3-564c-b77d-79aa4bb4d199', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a795fd50-87e3-564c-b77d-79aa4bb4d199'}, {'abstract': 'John M. Barry explores the personal trials that shaped a Puritan dissident’s advocacy for the separation of church and state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/roger-williams-and-the-creation-of-the-american-soul-church-state-and-the-birth-of-liberty-by-john-m-barry-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'John M. Barry explores the personal trials that shaped a Puritan dissident’s advocacy for the separation of church and state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Should you find yourself in front of the Rhode Island Statehouse in Providence, look up and east, and tip your hat — real or imagined — to Roger Williams. A 35-foot statue of the Protestant theologian (1603?-1683) stands high in Prospect Terrace Park, with right hand extended, as if blessing the city he founded. The beatific image does not quite resemble its cantankerous model, for reasons that John M. Barry explores, if only partly, in his new biography, “Roger Williams and the Creation of the American Soul.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01CHAPLIN/01CHAPLIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01CHAPLIN/01CHAPLIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01CHAPLIN/01CHAPLIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 996, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01CHAPLIN/01CHAPLIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01CHAPLIN/01CHAPLIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Roger Williams: The Great Separationist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Errand in the Wilderness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barry, John M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:20:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce E. Chaplin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Chaplin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9551e0c3-8b6a-5d9a-80a2-ada958eb2821', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9551e0c3-8b6a-5d9a-80a2-ada958eb2821'}, {'abstract': 'Essays about the foreign-policy stalwart Richard Holbrooke, and some writings of his own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/the-unquiet-american-richard-holbrooke-in-the-world-edited-by-derek-chollet-and-samantha-power-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Essays about the foreign-policy stalwart Richard Holbrooke, and some writings of his own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1991 Strobe Talbott, who would become deputy secretary of state in the Clinton administration, visited Richard Holbrooke at his weekend home in Connecticut. After playing tennis and going for a swim, they had brunch at a neighbor’s house. But when Holbrooke saw a trampoline on the far side of a manicured lawn, he excused himself, hopped onto the canvas and insisted that Talbott join him. “The result,” Talbott recalls, “was an exceedingly amateurish blend of gymnastic duet and duel — sometimes semicoordinated, sometimes dangerously competitive and constantly accompanied by talk, most of it coming from Richard. The subject, naturally, was world affairs.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01HEILBRUN/01HEILBRUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 334, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01HEILBRUN/01HEILBRUN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 334}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01HEILBRUN/01HEILBRUN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 994, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01HEILBRUN/01HEILBRUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/01HEILBRUN/01HEILBRUN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering Richard Holbrooke', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man of the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Power, Samantha', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chollet, Derek', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holbrooke, Richard C', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Heilbrunn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heilbrunn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63d57c66-5cb2-5373-b11b-b7be5efe8a07', 'word_count': 1231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63d57c66-5cb2-5373-b11b-b7be5efe8a07'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist examines the unlikely campaign of five cold war veterans to eliminate our nuclear arsenal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/the-partnership-five-cold-warriors-and-their-quest-to-ban-the-bomb-by-philip-taubman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist examines the unlikely campaign of five cold war veterans to eliminate our nuclear arsenal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the day after a nuclear bomb annihilates Washington, New Delhi, Islamabad, Seoul, Tel Aviv or Moscow, vaporizing and burning to death hundreds of thousands of people, our present complacency about nuclear proliferation will look like daylight madness. Even the chilliest of realists have shuddered at our capability for radioactive massacre. In 1977, the strategist George F. Kennan declared, “No one is good enough, wise enough, steady enough, to have control over the volume of explosives that now rest in the hands of this country.” Nuclear arms, he concluded, “shouldn’t exist at all.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/BASS/BASS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 341, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/BASS/BASS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/BASS/BASS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How They Learned to Hate the Bomb', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Farewell to Arms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shultz, George P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Drell, Sidney D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kissinger, Henry A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, William J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taubman, Philip', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nunn, Sam', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:22:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary J. Bass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Bass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/214fde34-c472-563c-b1e1-a8bcb981c80e', 'word_count': 1215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/214fde34-c472-563c-b1e1-a8bcb981c80e'}, {'abstract': 'These brief tales first appeared on Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/books/review/420-characters-stories-written-and-illustrated-by-lou-beach-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'These brief tales first appeared on Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Short stories are mightily resilient little creatures — like those bacteria they keep discovering in the hearts of volcanoes or at the bottoms of oceans. You can bury them in the dense, lifeless-looking pages of college literary magazines, and they keep flourishing. Or you can fill them with pulpish aliens and gun-toting molls, and they happily motor on, outlasting all those snobbish Pulitzer winners who once sneered at them (not to mention their rarefied panels of judges). That’s because stories don’t need much to survive, just a situation worth pursuing, a character worthy of pursuing it and three magical Aristotelian ingredients: beginning, middle and end. Suddenly, you’ve got a perfectly formed, self-\xaddetermining little beast on your hands. It breathes. It breeds.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Bradfield/Bradfield-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Bradfield/Bradfield-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/books/review/Bradfield/Bradfield-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Facebook Auteur', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Social Network Stories', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:23:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Bradfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1586870-37d2-53de-b5b2-613da3f9298f', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1586870-37d2-53de-b5b2-613da3f9298f'}, {'abstract': 'OTE, which is controlled by Deutsche Telekom, plans to use the proceeds from the $512 million deal to reduce its debt load.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/greek-telephone-operator-sells-serbian-stake/', 'snippet': 'OTE, which is controlled by Deutsche Telekom, plans to use the proceeds from the $512 million deal to reduce its debt load.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — OTE, the Greek telephone operator controlled by Deutsche Telekom, agreed on Friday to sell its 20 percent stake in Telekom Serbia back to company, a state-owned former monopoly, for 397 million euros, or about $512 million, in cash and dividends.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greek Telephone Operator Sells Serbian Stake', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Telekom AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Telekom Serbia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:38:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': "By Kevin J. O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c9e75eb-eaab-5021-ac62-4a7404acf04e', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c9e75eb-eaab-5021-ac62-4a7404acf04e'}, {'abstract': 'Dick Clark’s 40th “New Year’s Rockin’ Eve” countdown show this year will be preceded by an anniversary-and-tribute program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/arts/television/dick-clark-and-rockin-eve-reach-milestone.html', 'snippet': 'Dick Clark’s 40th “New Year’s Rockin’ Eve” countdown show this year will be preceded by an anniversary-and-tribute program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dick Clark’s countdown to the New Year used to begin before dawn on New Year’s Eve, when he would appear on “Good Morning America” and then, by satellite, on dozens of local newscasts and talk shows. He’d head home, eat something and hurry back to Times Square for “Dick Clark’s New Year’s Rockin’ Eve.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/arts/31rockin-span/31rockin-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/31/arts/31rockin-span/31rockin-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/arts/31rockin-span/31rockin-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/arts/31rockin-span/31rockin-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/31/arts/31rockin-span/31rockin-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Like Clockwork, Dick Clark Is to Ring in Another Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '4 Decades Later, He Still Counts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Dick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Seacrest, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e99ae496-7531-5958-9911-9c5ffcf8ce47', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e99ae496-7531-5958-9911-9c5ffcf8ce47'}, {'abstract': 'On the one hand, it’s nice to know that the Meh List is not just a haphazard list randomly thrown together, that it actually warrants discussion and decision-making. On the other hand, maybe it’s a little distressing that the Meh List is not just a haphazard list randomly thrown together, that it actually warrants discussion and decision-making.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/talking-bout-meh-generation/', 'snippet': 'On the one hand, it’s nice to know that the Meh List is not just a haphazard list randomly thrown together, that it actually warrants discussion and decision-making. On the other hand, maybe it’s a little distressing that the Meh List is not just ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As copy editors, we see the magazine’s articles undergo hundreds of changes as they mature from raw text to publication. Some changes clear up the facts a little, some clear up the language — some are so subtle as to hardly be noticeable; others so drastic that, frankly, they are confounding (“We have to change ‘dozens’ to ‘millions’ ” or “Let’s change ‘Rasputin’ to ‘the Captain and Tennille.’ ”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking ‘Bout Meh Generation', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:45:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7094f9ff-05ae-5d70-a4c6-0cd8cfc49578', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7094f9ff-05ae-5d70-a4c6-0cd8cfc49578'}, {'abstract': 'Forget egocentric New Year’s resolutions. It’s time for New Rules for how the world should work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/opinion/sunday/bill-mahers-new-rules-for-the-new-year.html', 'snippet': 'Forget egocentric New Year’s resolutions. It’s time for New Rules for how the world should work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW YEAR’S resolutions are the original New Rules. Except that resolutions are usually self-oriented: I am going to lose weight this year. My New Year’s resolution, by the way, is to do the ones from ’75; I made a lot of good ones that year. I was 19, and thought I could polish them off by age 20. Alas, I’m a little behind.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Rules for the New Year', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Rules for the New Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:45:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Maher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf1d7fc-1feb-567a-b6e3-00acb50d5894', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf1d7fc-1feb-567a-b6e3-00acb50d5894'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/weekend-fare-74/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A New Year’s Eve dinner will be served on Saturday at most restaurants, with an early seating and a later, usually pricier, one. Following are some restaurants with available seats offering various specials; some have been reviewed in The Times. Many places throw in a complimentary glass of Champagne, but be sure to make a reservation to save a spot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T17:48:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39eb46c7-4987-5b09-9f48-5c223fc414e4', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39eb46c7-4987-5b09-9f48-5c223fc414e4'}, {'abstract': 'Terrence Malick’s “Tree of Life,” which plays almost like a piece of music, doesn’t shy away from considering the divine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/movies/awardsseason/a-o-scott-on-the-musical-movement-of-the-tree-of-life.html', 'snippet': 'Terrence Malick’s “Tree of Life,” which plays almost like a piece of music, doesn’t shy away from considering the divine.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Tree of Life,” Terrence Malick’s fifth feature, the winner of the Palme d’Or at Cannes in May, encompasses the entirety of time, from the creation of the universe to a celestial vision of the afterlife. It is hardly surprising that the movie’s sweep and ambition have figured so prominently in discussions of it, whether as sources of wonder or bafflement. The dinosaurs that appear briefly and memorably about 30 minutes into the film have become conversation pieces and symbols of Mr. Malick’s audacity, even as their peculiar grace earned them an honored place in the impressively crowded cinema menagerie of 2011, alongside the loyal terrier of “The Artist” and the noble steed of “War Horse.” ', 'print_section': 'MT', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01SCOTT_SPAN/01SCOTT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01SCOTT_SPAN/01SCOTT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/arts/01SCOTT_SPAN/SCOTT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_nam