], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T21:22:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45324894-71e6-55de-a699-7a86b79f4795', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45324894-71e6-55de-a699-7a86b79f4795'}, {'abstract': 'New Jersey serves as an unlikely muse for creatives around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/30/the-fertile-ground-of-the-garden-state/', 'snippet': 'New Jersey serves as an unlikely muse for creatives around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A recent article in our magazine discussed the work of the artist Shea Hembrey, who fabricated an astounding catalog filled with the work of 100 artists of his own creation. The article by Sara Corbett discusses Hembrey’s life, his work and, briefly, his locale while completing the catalog: New Jersey. Frenchtown, to be exact. For me, as a native of the state, this small part of the story filled me with pride. The Garden State is so often mocked for everything from its accent to its aroma that my ears perk up whenever it’s mentioned without contempt. Perhaps because of this proclivity, I’ve noticed a handful of artists’ projects recently that have been influenced or directly inspired by the state, even if nowhere near it. Here are a few of my favorites:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fertile Ground of the Garden State', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-30T21:22:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 