: [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/sports/Knicks/Knicks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/sports/Knicks/Knicks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/31/sports/Knicks/Knicks-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Shortened Season, Panic Can Set In Early', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Shortened Season, Panic Can Set In Early', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Baron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stoudemire, Amare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "D'Antoni, Mike", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:10:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Beck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43a2a845-de2a-57a8-b7ce-9cc7d589da1e', 'word_count': 922, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43a2a845-de2a-57a8-b7ce-9cc7d589da1e'}, {'abstract': 'In the Midwest, the spike in farm earnings has encouraged farmers to put more land into production, including parcels once deemed unsuitable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/us/in-iowa-farmland-expands-as-crop-prices-soar.html', 'snippet': 'In the Midwest, the spike in farm earnings has encouraged farmers to put more land into production, including parcels once deemed unsuitable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHITTEMORE, Iowa — A splash of green on a solid beige horizon, the golf course at the edge of this tiny town promised residents nine modest holes of refuge from corn country. Decades earlier the spot had been farmed, too, but the rocky soil was so poor, the saying went, that you couldn’t raise hell there with a fifth of whiskey. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/us/FARMLAND-2/FARMLAND-2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/12/31/us/FARMLAND-2/FARMLAND-2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/us/FARMLAND-2/FARMLAND-2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/31/us/FARMLAND-2/FARMLAND-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/31/us/FARMLAND-2/FARMLAND-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Crop Prices Soar, Iowa Farms Add Acreage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Crop Prices Soar, Iowa Farms Expand Into Every Spare Inch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midwestern States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:13:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea2153f0-83de-5990-9839-2d8f749faaf6', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea2153f0-83de-5990-9839-2d8f749faaf6'}, {'abstract': 'The N.C.A.A. is a walking, talking antitrust violation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/opinion/nocera-the-college-sports-cartel.html', 'snippet': 'The N.C.A.A. is a walking, talking antitrust violation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twice a year in Vienna, the members of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries gather to decide on the short-term direction of oil prices. Sometimes, O.P.E.C. agrees to cut back on oil production, pushing up the price of oil. Other times, it decides to boost production. Always, the goal is to fix the price of oil, rather than allow it to be set by the competitive marketplace. Indeed, collusion and price-fixing are the main reasons cartels exist — and why they are illegal in America.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The College Sports Cartel', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The College Sports Cartel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emmert, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:33:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/257dfa16-178e-5349-b335-1450a9a9e557', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/257dfa16-178e-5349-b335-1450a9a9e557'}, {'abstract': 'A steady drumbeat of real estate deals, park restorations, reclaimed piers and new esplanades heralds the remaking of Manhattan’s East Side waterfront.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/nyregion/a-greenway-along-the-waterfront-will-beckon-from-the-east.html', 'snippet': 'A steady drumbeat of real estate deals, park restorations, reclaimed piers and new esplanades heralds the remaking of Manhattan’s East Side waterfront.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE by one, the announcements have come. Of a deal to convert Pier 42, located between the Williamsburg and Manhattan Bridges, from a storage facility into open space. Of design guidelines for Waterside Pier, a former Consolidated Edison site along the East River in Midtown, released in December by the Municipal Art Society. Of the opening of Pier 15, a block below South Street Seaport, which offers double-decker views of the East River. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reinventing the East Side Waterfront', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reinventing Waterfront On East Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Economic Development Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East River (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:37:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72812844-e773-5c3e-af2d-64f928d77a26', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72812844-e773-5c3e-af2d-64f928d77a26'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s the End-of-the-Year Republican Presidential Primary Quiz. No peeking at the answers, people, until all questions have been answered!', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/opinion/collins-a-quiz-for-all-seasons.html', 'snippet': 'Here’s the End-of-the-Year Republican Presidential Primary Quiz. No peeking at the answers, people, until all questions have been answered!', 'lead_paragraph': 'What a big week coming up! New Year’s Day and then the Iowa caucuses! Doesn’t get any better than that. And, in honor of this double-whammy of exciting events, here’s the End-of-the-Year Republican Presidential Primary Quiz:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Quiz for All Seasons', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Quiz for All Seasons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:40:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dd03400-0ab7-50bc-ab5c-1f625992c256', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dd03400-0ab7-50bc-ab5c-1f625992c256'}, {'abstract': 'When he’s not busy keeping tabs on the “crossroads of the world,” Tim Tompkins, president of the Times Square Alliance, fills his Sundays with yoga, Broadway shows and Italian dinners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/nyregion/tim-tompkins-keeping-an-eye-on-times-square-even-on-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'When he’s not busy keeping tabs on the “crossroads of the world,” Tim Tompkins, president of the Times Square Alliance, fills his Sundays with yoga, Broadway shows and Italian dinners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They’ve been dropping the New Year’s Eve ball in Times Square since 1907, and since 2002, Tim Tompkins, president of the Times Square Alliance, has been in charge of worrying about potential malfunctions and other angst-making details. Mr. Tompkins, 47, is too engrossed in logistics to socialize with headliners like Justin Bieber and Lady Gaga; he claims to be hopeless at mingling with celebrities but finds it amusing that Matthew Broderick portrays him as “a jerk” (as the boss to Hilary Swank’s character) in the romantic comedy “New Year’s Eve.” Except for an afternoon catnap, Mr. Tompkins patrols Times Square from 7 a.m. on Dec. 31 onward into the wee hours of the New Year. Jan. 1 figures to be a major blur for him, as for many. His typical Sundays are jammed with socializing, yoga, Broadway matinees, Italian dinners and multiplex action movies. He lives in the West Village.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01ROUTINE1_SPAN/01ROUTINE1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping an Eye on Times Square', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keeping an Eye on Times Square', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Times Square and 42nd Street (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Times Square Alliance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:41:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/104af011-e665-5316-822a-02d1cfc67a2a', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/104af011-e665-5316-822a-02d1cfc67a2a'}, {'abstract': 'New books describe stories of “the city’s first great civic enterprise,” recycled shuls in the boroughs outside Manhattan, and high school football.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/nyregion/books-on-an-enduring-grid-lost-synagogues-and-a-1959-tale.html', 'snippet': 'New books describe stories of “the city’s first great civic enterprise,” recycled shuls in the boroughs outside Manhattan, and high school football.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU don’t have to be a geometry major to love “The Greatest Grid: The Master Plan of Manhattan 1811-2011,”  jointly published by the Museum of the City of New York and Columbia University Press and edited by Hilary Ballon, a trustee of the museum and a professor of urban studies and architecture at New York University. It is a paean to what Professor Ballon hails as “the city’s first great civic enterprise, and a vision of brazen ambition.” After all, John Randel Jr. and three state-appointed commissioners mapped the grid all the way to 155th Street at a time when most Manhattanites lived and worked below Canal Street. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01BOOK/01BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 929, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01BOOK/01BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/nyregion/01BOOK/01BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Grid, Lost Synagogues and a 1959 Tale', 'kicker': 'Bookshelf', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Grid, Lost Synagogues and a 1959 Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levitt, Ellen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T03:44:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b2b310e-8e58-5c6b-b945-7cb943e15a73', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b2b310e-8e58-5c6b-b945-7cb943e15a7