': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lower East Side Tenement Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T06:04:05+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'Judy Tong|William Horn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': 'Tong', 'lastname': 'William', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/fb3abfe3-6035-5b39-bdbe-2541474183fd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/fb3abfe3-6035-5b39-bdbe-2541474183fd'}, {'abstract': 'Orchard Street, eight blocks in length, has one foot in Chinatown and the other in new enterprises like galleries, boutiques and “lifestyle stores.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/realestate/orchard-street-block-by-block-a-place-of-fresh-starts.html', 'snippet': 'Orchard Street, eight blocks in length, has one foot in Chinatown and the other in new enterprises like galleries, boutiques and “lifestyle stores.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a recent Wednesday, as the late morning sun shone on the Lower East Side, two gentlemen had crossed Canal and were headed north on Orchard Street. Both wearing one of the better uniforms of the day — dark washed jeans with one-inch cuffs skimming the ankle above brown leather oxfords, blouson jacket zipped up, short wool scarf in neutral tone looped around the neck, sunglasses with dark lenses and metal frame — they were out for a stroll, maybe to take in a few of Orchard Street’s new art galleries. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/realestate/01BLOCK2_SPAN/block-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/realestate/01BLOCK2_SPAN/block-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/realestate/01BLOCK2_SPAN/01BLOCK2_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/realestate/01BLOCK2_SPAN/block-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/realestate/01BLOCK2_SPAN/block-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Orchard Street, a Place of Fresh Starts', 'kicker': 'Block by Block', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Place Of Fresh Starts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chinatown (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T06:05:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christian L. Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christian', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63998bf2-7633-5633-802c-79ee546a6ced', 'word_count': 1138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63998bf2-7633-5633-802c-79ee546a6ced'}, {'abstract': 'India’s public school system is so inadequate in some areas that few want to attend.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/poor-but-attending-private-school/', 'snippet': 'India’s public school system is so inadequate in some areas that few want to attend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In India, the choice to live outside the faltering grid of government services is usually reserved for the rich or middle class, who can afford private housing compounds, private hospitals and private schools, Vikas Bajaj and Jim Yardley write in the New York Times today. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poor, But Attending Private School', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T06:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9f2b2aa-dc57-5383-b32d-d24c5a731b60', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9f2b2aa-dc57-5383-b32d-d24c5a731b60'}, {'abstract': 'A partnership between government and a not-for-profit shows promising results.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/at-school-in-a-mumbai-mill-igniting-a-desire-to-learn/', 'snippet': 'A partnership between government and a not-for-profit shows promising results.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jyoti Gupta, age 8, had never been inside a classroom when she started at the Sitaram Mill Compound Mumbai Public School this past June.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At School in a Mumbai Mill, Igniting a Desire to Learn', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 