o', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T20:49:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NAT', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natasha Singer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natasha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0ccca8d-35d8-5c1c-9d04-b0592c9450db', 'word_count': 2075, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0ccca8d-35d8-5c1c-9d04-b0592c9450db'}, {'abstract': 'Three playoff spots, most of the playoff seeds and the top pick in next year’s draft are still up for grabs as the league winds down to its final weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/sports/football/in-nfls-final-weekend-plenty-to-play-for.html', 'snippet': 'Three playoff spots, most of the playoff seeds and the top pick in next year’s draft are still up for grabs as the league winds down to its final weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A season that began in a frantic swirl of signings and practices after a lockout ended comes down to Sunday, with three playoff spots on the line, playoff pairings to be determined, coaches’ fates to be decided and one of the best teams of the past decade in the awkward position of possibly being able to secure its quarterbacking future for at least another decade — if it can finish an embarrassingly awful season with a loss. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/sports/NFL3/NFL3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/sports/NFL3/NFL3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/sp