e caucuses. But before that, a drink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES — “The only people the pundits listen to is each other,” Rick Santorum, a Republican presidential candidate, said on Friday at Legends American Grill in Marshalltown, Iowa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Des Moines, Seeking the Pulse of the People and Finding a Hall of Mirrors', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:02:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Leibovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leibovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c44334a-4582-5277-abc8-dc282bc97634', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c44334a-4582-5277-abc8-dc282bc97634'}, {'abstract': 'Among this year’s list of catchwords, “occupy” should be with us a while, but will “crankshaft” be on the tip of the tongue?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/opinion/sunday/the-words-that-defined-2011.html', 'snippet': 'Among this year’s list of catchwords, “occupy” should be with us a while, but will “crankshaft” be on the tip of the tongue?', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLLECTING the past year’s words is like sifting one’s pockets at the end of a trip. Some things you’ll keep, some you’ll discard — the dinner receipt for the expense-account meal vs. the one-peso coin. Each is a reminder of what you did, where you went and whom you were with.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/sunday-review/01BUZZWORDSsub/01BUZZWORDSsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/01/sunday-review/01BUZZWORDSsub/01BUZZWORDSsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 286}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/sunday-review/01BUZZWORDSsub/01BUZZWORDSsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 488, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/01/sunday-review/01BUZZWORDSsub/01BUZZWORDSsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/01/sunday-review/01BUZZWORDSsub/01BUZZWORDSsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Words Will Live On?', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Which Words Will Live On?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Two Thousand Eleven', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:15:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grant Barrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grant', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/669af6ed-2c4b-5401-9