22:34:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b109a0c-4fe1-570b-ba1e-9d5a027c2cfd', 'word_count': 1471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b109a0c-4fe1-570b-ba1e-9d5a027c2cfd'}, {'abstract': 'A New York county is still fighting the federal government’s efforts to upend decades of housing discrimination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/opinion/sunday/westchesters-desegregation-battle.html', 'snippet': 'A New York county is still fighting the federal government’s efforts to upend decades of housing discrimination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The struggle for racial integration is neither bygone nor exclusively Southern. In Westchester, north of New York City, county leaders are stonewalling federal authorities over a longstanding housing desegregation case. Led by County Executive Rob Astorino, they are defending an ultrawhite status quo against the federal government’s effort to upend decades of housing discrimination.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Westchester’s Desegregation Battle', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Westchester’s Desegregation Battle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Astorino, Rob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:34:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/66995863-a4ae-5464-a8a9-62087cb9382d', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66995863-a4ae-5464-a8a9-62087cb9382d'}, {'abstract': 'Is there a connection between last year’s extreme weather and global warming? If the Republicans in Congress were less hostile to climate change research we might know.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/opinion/sunday/a-tempestuous-year.html', 'snippet': 'Is there a connection between last year’s extreme weather and global warming? If the Republicans in Congress were less hostile to climate change research we might know.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a connection between last year’s extreme weather events and global warming? The answers might be a lot clearer if the Republicans in Congress were less hostile to climate change research. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tempestuous Year', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tempestuous Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:35:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2edcfcbc-8082-5773-b890-fd3f7f9741f6', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2edcfcbc-8082-5773-b890-fd3f7f9741f6'}, {'abstract': 'New Year’s Day makes you feel as though you’ve been handed a large lump of time, to be shaped as you see fit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/opinion/sunday/out-the-door-and-into-2012.html', 'snippet': 'New Year’s Day makes you feel as though you’ve been handed a large lump of time, to be shaped as you see fit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps you have a New Year’s Day of your own — a day when it suddenly feels as though you’ve truly left the old year behind. It may be the day you no longer have to think twice when putting the date on a check, if you still write checks, that is. Perhaps your new year started the moment the days began lengthening just before Christmas. Or perhaps you hold off for the vernal equinox (March 20 in 2012), when New Year used to be celebrated and when, in many places, you can feel the newness of the year about to burst out of the ground.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out the Door and Into 2012', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out the Door and Into 2012', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Times Square and 42nd Street (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:35:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/12722437-13cc-5d92-a1f1-8c91aee5bbfa', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12722437-13cc-5d92-a1f1-8c91aee5bbfa'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about leadership from students and Hollywood celebrities.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2011/12/31/the-backstory-of-my-column-on-celebrities-and-politicians/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about leadership from students and Hollywood celebrities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Okay, okay, celebrities are not my thing. I have never in my life read People Magazine, and when I’m in the grocery store passing the scandal tabloids, I have to get page one nicknames translated by my kids to know who’s supposedly having an affair with whom. But my Sunday column dips its toe into celebrity waters anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Backstory of my Column on Celebrities … and Politicians', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2011-12-31T22:38:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/223d533d-2e9a-5b14-ab81-f26ea432759e', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/223d533d-2e9a-5b14-ab81-f26ea432759e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers r